/* generated by tools/build-content.js from content-source/student - do not edit by hand */
window.LESSONS = [{"id":"ch01","short":"Ch 1","title":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","label":"Chapter 1","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Insurance moves risk from one person to a large group. Know the company types, how insurers are classified, who sells insurance (agent vs. broker), and the federal regulation timeline.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch01-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Many people pay small premiums so that the few who suffer a loss get large payouts.</li><li class=\"l0\">The exam tests <strong>labels</strong>: stock vs. mutual, domestic vs. foreign vs. alien, agent vs. broker, and which law came in which year.</li></ul>","text":"Many people pay small premiums so that the few who suffer a loss get large payouts. The exam tests labels: stock vs. mutual, domestic vs. foreign vs. alien, agent vs. broker, and which law came in which year."},{"id":"ch01-s2","title":"1. What insurance is","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Transfer of risk through a legal contract, or pooling of funds.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Spreads one person's risk across a larger group.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>How it works:</strong> The policyowner pays a small premium and gets substantial coverage.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Key benefit:</strong> Removes unplanned, large expenses.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Insurer</strong> = the company providing coverage. <strong>Insured</strong> = the person or entity protected by the policy.</li></ul>","text":"Definition: Transfer of risk through a legal contract, or pooling of funds. Purpose: Spreads one person's risk across a larger group. How it works: The policyowner pays a small premium and gets substantial coverage. Key benefit: Removes unplanned, large expenses. Insurer = the company providing coverage. Insured = the person or entity protected by the policy."},{"id":"ch01-s3","title":"2. Stock vs. mutual companies","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Stock company</th><th>Mutual company</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Owned by</td><td>Stockholders (shareholders)</td><td>Policyholders (no stockholders)</td></tr><tr><td>Typical policy</td><td>Nonparticipating</td><td>Participating</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>Go to stockholders</td><td>Paid to policyholders from divisible surplus</td></tr><tr><td>Voting</td><td>Policyholders cannot vote</td><td>Policyholders vote for the board</td></tr><tr><td>Goal</td><td>Profit for shareholders</td><td>Benefits for policyholders</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>MUTUAL = MEMBERS. Mutual company members (policyholders) get the dividends and vote.</p></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> &quot;Stock = nonparticipating&quot; is the usual pattern, not an absolute rule. A stock company can issue participating policies in some cases, and the rare &quot;Mixed plan&quot; below covers one company issuing both. Learn the usual pattern and know that exceptions exist.</p></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Divisible surplus:</strong> Earnings left after reserves and expenses are set aside. Mutuals may pay this as dividends.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Mutualization:</strong> Stock company becomes a mutual.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Demutualization:</strong> Mutual company becomes a stock company.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Mixed plan:</strong> Rare. One company issues both participating and nonparticipating policies.</li></ul><h4>Other company types</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Multi-line</td><td>Sells several kinds of insurance (life, health, property, casualty)</td></tr><tr><td>Monoline</td><td>Sells only one kind of insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Pure assessment mutual</td><td>No upfront premium; members are assessed after losses occur</td></tr><tr><td>Advance premium assessment mutual</td><td>Premiums charged upfront; extra assessments if needed</td></tr><tr><td>Reciprocal insurer</td><td>Policyholders insure each other; managed by an <strong>attorney-in-fact</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Risk retention group (RRG)</td><td>Group-owned; covers liability risks; licensed in ONE state, operates in ALL states</td></tr><tr><td>Risk purchasing group (RPG)</td><td>Members buy liability coverage together; licensed in one state, operates in all</td></tr><tr><td>Captive insurer</td><td>Created and owned by a parent company to insure its own risks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-insurer</td><td>Self-funded plan instead of transferring risk to an insurer</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial insurer</td><td>Small face amounts ($1,000-$2,000), weekly premiums; also called home service or debit insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Fraternal benefit society</td><td>Nonprofit with a lodge system; insures members only</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (Industrial):</strong> Think &quot;INDUSTRIAL = Ink-stained, weekly, Tiny face amounts, collector comes to your door (debit).&quot;</p></div><h4>Reinsurance (insurer insures the insurer)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ceding company</td><td>Original insurer that passes (cedes) part of the risk away</td></tr><tr><td>Reinsurer</td><td>Company that accepts the transferred risk</td></tr><tr><td>Primary insurer</td><td>Original insurer that first held the loss exposure</td></tr><tr><td>Net retention (net line)</td><td>Part of the risk the ceding company keeps</td></tr><tr><td>Treaty reinsurance</td><td>Automatic risk sharing agreed in advance between two insurers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>CEDE = GIVE AWAY. The ceding company gives risk away; the reinsurer takes it. &quot;Net retention&quot; is what you KEEP.</p></div><h4>Health care service providers</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>HMO (Health Maintenance Organization):</strong> Prepaid premium for comprehensive care from contracted doctors and hospitals. Emphasizes prevention. Provides both financing and delivery of care.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>PPO (Preferred Provider Organization):</strong> Negotiated discounts with providers. More flexibility in choosing providers.</li></ul><h4>Other key entities</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Lloyd's of London:</strong> NOT an insurer. It is a marketplace where individuals and companies underwrite unusual risks.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Surplus lines insurance:</strong> Non-traditional market for substandard or unusual risks (for example, hole-in-one insurance).</li></ul>","text":"Feature Stock company Mutual company Owned by Stockholders (shareholders) Policyholders (no stockholders) Typical policy Nonparticipating Participating Dividends Go to stockholders Paid to policyholders from divisible surplus Voting Policyholders cannot vote Policyholders vote for the board Goal Profit for shareholders Benefits for policyholders Memory trick: MUTUAL = MEMBERS. Mutual company members (policyholders) get the dividends and vote. Study tip: \"Stock = nonparticipating\" is the usual pattern, not an absolute rule. A stock company can issue participating policies in some cases, and the rare \"Mixed plan\" below covers one company issuing both. Learn the usual pattern and know that exceptions exist. Divisible surplus: Earnings left after reserves and expenses are set aside. Mutuals may pay this as dividends. Mutualization: Stock company becomes a mutual. Demutualization: Mutual company becomes a stock company. Mixed plan: Rare. One company issues both participating and nonparticipating policies. Other company types Term What it means Multi-line Sells several kinds of insurance (life, health, property, casualty) Monoline Sells only one kind of insurance Pure assessment mutual No upfront premium; members are assessed after losses occur Advance premium assessment mutual Premiums charged upfront; extra assessments if needed Reciprocal insurer Policyholders insure each other; managed by an attorney-in-fact Risk retention group (RRG) Group-owned; covers liability risks; licensed in ONE state, operates in ALL states Risk purchasing group (RPG) Members buy liability coverage together; licensed in one state, operates in all Captive insurer Created and owned by a parent company to insure its own risks Self-insurer Self-funded plan instead of transferring risk to an insurer Industrial insurer Small face amounts ($1,000-$2,000), weekly premiums; also called home service or debit insurance Fraternal benefit society Nonprofit with a lodge system; insures members only Memory trick (Industrial): Think \"INDUSTRIAL = Ink-stained, weekly, Tiny face amounts, collector comes to your door (debit).\" Reinsurance (insurer insures the insurer) Term Meaning Ceding company Original insurer that passes (cedes) part of the risk away Reinsurer Company that accepts the transferred risk Primary insurer Original insurer that first held the loss exposure Net retention (net line) Part of the risk the ceding company keeps Treaty reinsurance Automatic risk sharing agreed in advance between two insurers Memory trick: CEDE = GIVE AWAY. The ceding company gives risk away; the reinsurer takes it. \"Net retention\" is what you KEEP. Health care service providers HMO (Health Maintenance Organization): Prepaid premium for comprehensive care from contracted doctors and hospitals. Emphasizes prevention. Provides both financing and delivery of care. PPO (Preferred Provider Organization): Negotiated discounts with providers. More flexibility in choosing providers. Other key entities Lloyd's of London: NOT an insurer. It is a marketplace where individuals and companies underwrite unusual risks. Surplus lines insurance: Non-traditional market for substandard or unusual risks (for example, hole-in-one insurance)."},{"id":"ch01-s4","title":"3. Insurer classification (CRITICAL)","html":"<h4>By domicile (where the company's principal office is)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Domestic</strong></td><td>Principal office in the SAME state where it is authorized</td><td>State Farm (Illinois HQ) is domestic in Illinois</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Foreign</strong></td><td>Principal office in a DIFFERENT U.S. state</td><td>State Farm is foreign in New York</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alien</strong></td><td>Principal office OUTSIDE the United States</td><td>Allianz (Germany) or Manulife (Canada) operating in the U.S.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Domestic = Home state. Foreign = Foreign U.S. state. Alien = Alien country.</p></div><h4>By authorization</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Admitted (authorized) insurer:</strong> Holds a Certificate of Authority to do business in the state.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Non-admitted (unauthorized) insurer:</strong> No Certificate of Authority. Generally cannot operate in the state.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Certificate of Authority:</strong> The license from the state's insurance department that permits business.</li></ul>","text":"By domicile (where the company's principal office is) Type Meaning Example Domestic Principal office in the SAME state where it is authorized State Farm (Illinois HQ) is domestic in Illinois Foreign Principal office in a DIFFERENT U.S. state State Farm is foreign in New York Alien Principal office OUTSIDE the United States Allianz (Germany) or Manulife (Canada) operating in the U.S. Memory trick: Domestic = Home state. Foreign = Foreign U.S. state. Alien = Alien country. By authorization Admitted (authorized) insurer: Holds a Certificate of Authority to do business in the state. Non-admitted (unauthorized) insurer: No Certificate of Authority. Generally cannot operate in the state. Certificate of Authority: The license from the state's insurance department that permits business."},{"id":"ch01-s5","title":"4. Company departments and key people","html":"<h4>Departments</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Department</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Marketing / Sales</td><td>Attracts applicants, completes applications, runs sales appointments</td></tr><tr><td>Underwriting</td><td>Reviews applications, investigates applicants, assigns risk classes, approves or denies</td></tr><tr><td>Claims</td><td>Processes, investigates, and pays claims</td></tr><tr><td>Actuarial</td><td>Calculates rates, reserves, and dividends using mortality and morbidity tables</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Key people</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Who they represent / what they do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Agent</strong></td><td>Represents the INSURER</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Broker</strong></td><td>Represents the CLIENT / INSURED</td></tr><tr><td>Producer</td><td>Umbrella term for everyone who sells insurance (agents, brokers, solicitors)</td></tr><tr><td>Solicitor</td><td>Works for an agent to find and solicit applicants</td></tr><tr><td>Service representative</td><td>Insurer employee who supports customers; no commission-based selling. Must be licensed if selling</td></tr><tr><td>Underwriter</td><td>Evaluates and classifies risk; helps set the premium</td></tr><tr><td>Actuary</td><td>Statistician who calculates rates, reserves, dividends</td></tr><tr><td>Adjuster</td><td>Investigates claims; recommends settlement or denial</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Public adjuster</strong></td><td>Represents the INSURED (not the insurer) in settling claims</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Exam trap:</strong> &quot;Agent = insurer&quot; and &quot;broker = insured&quot; is the core rule. A broker does not represent the insurer in disputes; in a dispute, the agent represents the insurer.</p></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>AGENT = ATTACHED to the insurer. BROKER = BUYER's side.</p></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fiduciary responsibility:</strong> Licensed producers owe a duty of trust to BOTH the insurers they represent AND the clients they serve.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>In a dispute, the agent represents the insurer, not the insured or beneficiary.</strong></li></ul><h4>Agent authority (memorize)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Source</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Express</strong></td><td>Explicitly given (written or spoken)</td><td>Authorized to collect premiums and sell policies</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Implied</strong></td><td>Not stated, but necessary to do the job</td><td>Ordering promotional materials</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Apparent</strong></td><td>Created by the principal's conduct; others reasonably believe the agent has authority</td><td>Company gives the agent letterhead and records that make authority look real</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>EXPRESS = Said out loud or in writing. IMPLIED = Needed to do the job. APPARENT = Looks real to the public.</p></div>","text":"Departments Department Job Marketing / Sales Attracts applicants, completes applications, runs sales appointments Underwriting Reviews applications, investigates applicants, assigns risk classes, approves or denies Claims Processes, investigates, and pays claims Actuarial Calculates rates, reserves, and dividends using mortality and morbidity tables Key people Role Who they represent / what they do Agent Represents the INSURER Broker Represents the CLIENT / INSURED Producer Umbrella term for everyone who sells insurance (agents, brokers, solicitors) Solicitor Works for an agent to find and solicit applicants Service representative Insurer employee who supports customers; no commission-based selling. Must be licensed if selling Underwriter Evaluates and classifies risk; helps set the premium Actuary Statistician who calculates rates, reserves, dividends Adjuster Investigates claims; recommends settlement or denial Public adjuster Represents the INSURED (not the insurer) in settling claims Exam trap: \"Agent = insurer\" and \"broker = insured\" is the core rule. A broker does not represent the insurer in disputes; in a dispute, the agent represents the insurer. Memory trick: AGENT = ATTACHED to the insurer. BROKER = BUYER's side. Fiduciary responsibility: Licensed producers owe a duty of trust to BOTH the insurers they represent AND the clients they serve. In a dispute, the agent represents the insurer, not the insured or beneficiary. Agent authority (memorize) Type Source Example Express Explicitly given (written or spoken) Authorized to collect premiums and sell policies Implied Not stated, but necessary to do the job Ordering promotional materials Apparent Created by the principal's conduct; others reasonably believe the agent has authority Company gives the agent letterhead and records that make authority look real Memory trick: EXPRESS = Said out loud or in writing. IMPLIED = Needed to do the job. APPARENT = Looks real to the public."},{"id":"ch01-s6","title":"5. How insurance is sold","html":"<h4>Agent types</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Captive / career agent:</strong> Works exclusively for ONE insurer.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Independent agent:</strong> Represents MULTIPLE insurers under contracts.</li></ul><h4>Agency systems</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Focus</th><th>Structure / notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Career agency</strong></td><td>Recruiting, training, and supervising agents</td><td>Managed by a General Agent (GA) or branch manager</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PPGA</strong> (Personal Producing General Agency)</td><td>SELLING insurance (not recruiting or training)</td><td>A GA who also sells personally</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Independent agency</strong> (American agency system)</td><td>Selling</td><td>Represents multiple insurers through contracts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Managerial system:</strong> Branch office run by a salaried branch manager (not a GA). The manager gets a salary plus bonuses for sales and recruitment.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Direct selling:</strong> Insurer sells straight to consumers (vending machines, ads, salaried reps).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Mass marketing:</strong> Large-scale promotion through internet, TV, radio, or print.</li></ul>","text":"Agent types Captive / career agent: Works exclusively for ONE insurer. Independent agent: Represents MULTIPLE insurers under contracts. Agency systems System Focus Structure / notes Career agency Recruiting, training, and supervising agents Managed by a General Agent (GA) or branch manager PPGA (Personal Producing General Agency) SELLING insurance (not recruiting or training) A GA who also sells personally Independent agency (American agency system) Selling Represents multiple insurers through contracts Managerial system: Branch office run by a salaried branch manager (not a GA). The manager gets a salary plus bonuses for sales and recruitment. Direct selling: Insurer sells straight to consumers (vending machines, ads, salaried reps). Mass marketing: Large-scale promotion through internet, TV, radio, or print."},{"id":"ch01-s7","title":"6. Federal regulation timeline (memorize dates)","html":"<div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> For each date, learn ONE sentence.</p></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1868</td><td>*Paul v. Virginia*</td><td>Insurance is not &quot;commerce&quot;; states regulate it</td></tr><tr><td>1944</td><td>*U.S. v. SEUA* (Southeastern Underwriters Association)</td><td>Insurance IS commerce, so federal antitrust law reached it</td></tr><tr><td>1945</td><td>McCarran-Ferguson Act (Public Law 15)</td><td>States keep regulating insurance; federal laws apply only where state law does not regulate</td></tr><tr><td>1958</td><td>*FTC v. National Casualty Co.*</td><td>FTC authority is blocked only where states regulate and enforce laws against the practice</td></tr><tr><td>1959</td><td>SEC intervention (*SEC v. Variable Annuity Life Insurance Co.*)</td><td>Variable products can be securities under SEC oversight</td></tr><tr><td>1970</td><td>Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA)</td><td>Insurers must notify applicants of investigative reports; obtaining consumer info under false pretenses is a crime</td></tr><tr><td>1994</td><td>18 U.S.C. 1033 and 1034</td><td>Federal crimes and civil penalties for dishonest people in the insurance business</td></tr><tr><td>1999</td><td>Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (GLBA)</td><td>Repealed key parts of Glass-Steagall; banks, insurers, and securities firms can combine; consumer privacy notices</td></tr><tr><td>2001</td><td>USA PATRIOT Act</td><td>Amended the Bank Secrecy Act after 9/11 to fight terrorism and money laundering</td></tr><tr><td>2003</td><td>Do Not Call Implementation Act</td><td>Consumers can register to block telemarketing calls. Exempt: charities, political groups, surveys</td></tr><tr><td>2003</td><td>CAN-SPAM Act</td><td>Commercial email rules; honor opt-outs within 10 business days</td></tr><tr><td>2010</td><td>Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (ACA)</td><td>Major health insurance reform</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>1958 FTC case:</strong> The Supreme Court held that the FTC could not act against insurance advertising in states that had laws against unfair insurance practices and were enforcing them. Federal law applies only &quot;to the extent that such business is not regulated by State law&quot; (15 U.S.C. 1012(b)).</p></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>1959 SEC case:</strong> The 1959 Supreme Court decision concerned variable ANNUITIES. Variable products are treated as securities.</p></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>1970 FCRA:</strong> Obtaining consumer information under false pretenses is a federal crime.</p></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>1994 (18 U.S.C. 1033 and 1034):</strong></p><p class=\"cbullet\">• <strong>Criminal imprisonment under 1033:</strong> Generally up to 10 years. The 15-year maximum applies only when the conduct caused the insurer to be placed in conservation, rehabilitation, or liquidation.</p><p class=\"cbullet\">• <strong>Felons barred from the business (1033(e)):</strong> Up to 5 years.</p><p class=\"cbullet\">• <strong>Written consent exception:</strong> A person barred under 1033(e) may participate only with the written consent of the insurance regulator, and the consent must specifically refer to that subsection.</p><p class=\"cbullet\">• <strong>The &quot;$50,000&quot; figure is a CIVIL penalty under 1034,</strong> not a criminal fine. Civil penalty: up to $50,000 per violation OR the compensation received for the prohibited conduct, whichever is greater.</p><p class=\"cbullet\">• <strong>Threats:</strong> The statute covers threats or force used to corruptly influence regulatory proceedings (1033(d)).</p></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>1999 GLBA:</strong> GLBA requires a privacy notice and an opt-out opportunity before sharing nonpublic personal FINANCIAL information with nonaffiliated third parties. Health information is governed mainly by HIPAA and by state law.</p></div><h4>Industry oversight</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>NAIC (National Association of Insurance Commissioners):</strong> Members are the state insurance commissioners or directors. Four main objectives:</li></ul><p>  1. Encourage <strong>uniformity</strong> in state insurance laws</p><p>  2. Promote <strong>efficiency</strong> in regulation</p><p>  3. <strong>Protect consumers</strong></p><p>  4. <strong>Preserve state regulation</strong> (not federal)</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>NAIFA</strong> (National Association of Insurance and Financial Advisors) and <strong>NAHU</strong> (National Association of Health Underwriters): professional associations with Codes of Ethics.</li></ul><h4>Unfair Trade Practices Act</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Gives state insurance departments power to investigate and penalize insurers and producers.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Prohibited practices:</strong> misrepresentation and false advertising; coercion and intimidation; unfair discrimination; inequitable claim settlements.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Advertising code:</strong> Bans misleading words and phrases. Ads must disclose renewal, cancellation, and termination provisions.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Claim settlement practices:</strong> All states require prompt, fair, and equitable claim settlement.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Louisiana:</strong> R.S. 22:1964 lists the unfair and deceptive acts, including the claim-settlement practices in its paragraph (14). R.S. 22:1973 was repealed effective July 1, 2024. An insurer's good-faith duty now sits in R.S. 22:1892(I), which says an insurer owes its insured a duty of good faith and fair dealing.</p></div><h4>State guaranty associations</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Protect policyholders if an insurer becomes insolvent. Pay unpaid claims up to set limits.</li><li class=\"l0\">Funded by assessments on insurance companies.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>CRITICAL:</strong> Producers CANNOT use guaranty association protection in sales presentations.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Under R.S. 22:2083(C)(2), per life, the Louisiana association pays up to: life death benefits <strong>$300,000</strong>; life cash surrender and withdrawal values <strong>$100,000</strong>; health benefits <strong>$500,000</strong>; annuity present value <strong>$250,000</strong>. An aggregate cap of <strong>$500,000</strong> per individual applies across those coverages.</p></div><h4>Rating services and financial terms</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Rating services: A.M. Best, Fitch Ratings, Standard &amp; Poor's, Moody's. They rate insurers' financial strength.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Reserves:</strong> Accounting estimate of future obligations to policyholders.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Liquidity:</strong> The company's ability to pay unexpected claims.</li></ul>","text":"Study tip: For each date, learn ONE sentence. Year Event What to know 1868 *Paul v. Virginia* Insurance is not \"commerce\"; states regulate it 1944 *U.S. v. SEUA* (Southeastern Underwriters Association) Insurance IS commerce, so federal antitrust law reached it 1945 McCarran-Ferguson Act (Public Law 15) States keep regulating insurance; federal laws apply only where state law does not regulate 1958 *FTC v. National Casualty Co.* FTC authority is blocked only where states regulate and enforce laws against the practice 1959 SEC intervention (*SEC v. Variable Annuity Life Insurance Co.*) Variable products can be securities under SEC oversight 1970 Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA) Insurers must notify applicants of investigative reports; obtaining consumer info under false pretenses is a crime 1994 18 U.S.C. 1033 and 1034 Federal crimes and civil penalties for dishonest people in the insurance business 1999 Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (GLBA) Repealed key parts of Glass-Steagall; banks, insurers, and securities firms can combine; consumer privacy notices 2001 USA PATRIOT Act Amended the Bank Secrecy Act after 9/11 to fight terrorism and money laundering 2003 Do Not Call Implementation Act Consumers can register to block telemarketing calls. Exempt: charities, political groups, surveys 2003 CAN-SPAM Act Commercial email rules; honor opt-outs within 10 business days 2010 Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (ACA) Major health insurance reform 1958 FTC case: The Supreme Court held that the FTC could not act against insurance advertising in states that had laws against unfair insurance practices and were enforcing them. Federal law applies only \"to the extent that such business is not regulated by State law\" (15 U.S.C. 1012(b)). 1959 SEC case: The 1959 Supreme Court decision concerned variable ANNUITIES. Variable products are treated as securities. 1970 FCRA: Obtaining consumer information under false pretenses is a federal crime. 1994 (18 U.S.C. 1033 and 1034): - Criminal imprisonment under 1033: Generally up to 10 years. The 15-year maximum applies only when the conduct caused the insurer to be placed in conservation, rehabilitation, or liquidation. - Felons barred from the business (1033(e)): Up to 5 years. - Written consent exception: A person barred under 1033(e) may participate only with the written consent of the insurance regulator, and the consent must specifically refer to that subsection. - The \"$50,000\" figure is a CIVIL penalty under 1034, not a criminal fine. Civil penalty: up to $50,000 per violation OR the compensation received for the prohibited conduct, whichever is greater. - Threats: The statute covers threats or force used to corruptly influence regulatory proceedings (1033(d)). 1999 GLBA: GLBA requires a privacy notice and an opt-out opportunity before sharing nonpublic personal FINANCIAL information with nonaffiliated third parties. Health information is governed mainly by HIPAA and by state law. Industry oversight NAIC (National Association of Insurance Commissioners): Members are the state insurance commissioners or directors. Four main objectives: 1. Encourage uniformity in state insurance laws 2. Promote efficiency in regulation 3. Protect consumers 4. Preserve state regulation (not federal) NAIFA (National Association of Insurance and Financial Advisors) and NAHU (National Association of Health Underwriters): professional associations with Codes of Ethics. Unfair Trade Practices Act Gives state insurance departments power to investigate and penalize insurers and producers. Prohibited practices: misrepresentation and false advertising; coercion and intimidation; unfair discrimination; inequitable claim settlements. Advertising code: Bans misleading words and phrases. Ads must disclose renewal, cancellation, and termination provisions. Claim settlement practices: All states require prompt, fair, and equitable claim settlement. Louisiana: R.S. 22:1964 lists the unfair and deceptive acts, including the claim-settlement practices in its paragraph (14). R.S. 22:1973 was repealed effective July 1, 2024. An insurer's good-faith duty now sits in R.S. 22:1892(I), which says an insurer owes its insured a duty of good faith and fair dealing. State guaranty associations Protect policyholders if an insurer becomes insolvent. Pay unpaid claims up to set limits. Funded by assessments on insurance companies. CRITICAL: Producers CANNOT use guaranty association protection in sales presentations. Louisiana: Under R.S. 22:2083(C)(2), per life, the Louisiana association pays up to: life death benefits $300,000; life cash surrender and withdrawal values $100,000; health benefits $500,000; annuity present value $250,000. An aggregate cap of $500,000 per individual applies across those coverages. Rating services and financial terms Rating services: A.M. Best, Fitch Ratings, Standard & Poor's, Moody's. They rate insurers' financial strength. Reserves: Accounting estimate of future obligations to policyholders. Liquidity: The company's ability to pay unexpected claims."},{"id":"ch01-s8","title":"7. Ethical agent practices","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Sell to needs:</strong> Find the facts, educate the client, match the product to the need.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Suitability:</strong> The product must fit the client's needs, understanding, and financial ability.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Full disclosure:</strong> Never conceal or misrepresent the product or the company.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Documentation:</strong> Record meetings; get written consent where needed.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Client service:</strong> The sale starts the relationship. Follow up regularly.</li></ul><h4>Required disclosures</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Buyer's guide:</strong> Delivered during the sales process. Explains policy types.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Policy summary:</strong> Specific to the recommended policy. Shows premiums, benefits, cash values, and dividends.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cost indexes</strong> (compare the cost of policies):</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Net payment cost index:</strong> Compares premiums (net of dividends) over a set period, usually 10 or 20 years.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Surrender cost index:</strong> Compares the cost if the policy is surrendered at the end of that period.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> The net payment index is a cost-comparison tool built on premiums and dividends over a set period, expressed per $1,000 of death benefit. Learn the 10-year and 20-year structure.</p></div>","text":"Sell to needs: Find the facts, educate the client, match the product to the need. Suitability: The product must fit the client's needs, understanding, and financial ability. Full disclosure: Never conceal or misrepresent the product or the company. Documentation: Record meetings; get written consent where needed. Client service: The sale starts the relationship. Follow up regularly. Required disclosures Buyer's guide: Delivered during the sales process. Explains policy types. Policy summary: Specific to the recommended policy. Shows premiums, benefits, cash values, and dividends. Cost indexes (compare the cost of policies): Net payment cost index: Compares premiums (net of dividends) over a set period, usually 10 or 20 years. Surrender cost index: Compares the cost if the policy is surrendered at the end of that period. Study tip: The net payment index is a cost-comparison tool built on premiums and dividends over a set period, expressed per $1,000 of death benefit. Learn the 10-year and 20-year structure."},{"id":"ch01-s9","title":"8. Quick review: critical distinctions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Compare</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Agent vs. broker</td><td>Agent = insurer's rep. Broker = insured's rep.</td></tr><tr><td>Private vs. government insurance</td><td>Private = owned by individuals or groups. Government = Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic vs. foreign vs. alien</td><td>Home state vs. other U.S. state vs. outside the U.S.</td></tr><tr><td>Stock vs. mutual</td><td>Shareholders vs. policyholders as owners</td></tr><tr><td>Admitted vs. non-admitted</td><td>Has vs. lacks a Certificate of Authority</td></tr><tr><td>Ceding company vs. reinsurer</td><td>Gives risk vs. takes risk</td></tr><tr><td>Public adjuster vs. adjuster</td><td>Public adjuster works for the insured; the insurer's adjuster works for the insurer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Compare Key difference Agent vs. broker Agent = insurer's rep. Broker = insured's rep. Private vs. government insurance Private = owned by individuals or groups. Government = Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid Domestic vs. foreign vs. alien Home state vs. other U.S. state vs. outside the U.S. Stock vs. mutual Shareholders vs. policyholders as owners Admitted vs. non-admitted Has vs. lacks a Certificate of Authority Ceding company vs. reinsurer Gives risk vs. takes risk Public adjuster vs. adjuster Public adjuster works for the insured; the insurer's adjuster works for the insurer"},{"id":"ch01-s10","title":"9. Check yourself (active recall)","html":"<p>Try to answer each question before you look at the answer key in Section 10.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What are the two ways insurance can transfer risk?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Who owns a mutual company, and who gets its dividends?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> A company is headquartered in Ohio and is authorized in Ohio. Is it domestic, foreign, or alien in Ohio?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> A company in Germany sells policies in Texas. What is it?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> An insurer wants to give part of a large risk to another insurer. What is the first company called?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Who does a public adjuster represent?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> In a dispute, whom does the agent represent?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What are the three types of agent authority?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the NAIC's four-part mission?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Can a producer use state guaranty association protection in a sales presentation?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Maria buys a policy from Carl. Carl works only for one company and is paid by that company. Carl is a ______ agent. Maria, meanwhile, went to a different office and asked for quotes from several insurers. That person is a ______.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Ray's insurer tells him his claim is denied. Ray hires someone to fight the denial and the person is paid by Ray. Who is this person working for?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A company says it is &quot;authorized&quot; in Florida but its headquarters is in Canada. What is the Florida classification?</li></ul>","text":"Try to answer each question before you look at the answer key in Section 10. What are the two ways insurance can transfer risk? Who owns a mutual company, and who gets its dividends? A company is headquartered in Ohio and is authorized in Ohio. Is it domestic, foreign, or alien in Ohio? A company in Germany sells policies in Texas. What is it? An insurer wants to give part of a large risk to another insurer. What is the first company called? Who does a public adjuster represent? In a dispute, whom does the agent represent? What are the three types of agent authority? What is the NAIC's four-part mission? Can a producer use state guaranty association protection in a sales presentation? Scenario questions Scenario A: Maria buys a policy from Carl. Carl works only for one company and is paid by that company. Carl is a ______ agent. Maria, meanwhile, went to a different office and asked for quotes from several insurers. That person is a ______. Scenario B: Ray's insurer tells him his claim is denied. Ray hires someone to fight the denial and the person is paid by Ray. Who is this person working for? Scenario C: A company says it is \"authorized\" in Florida but its headquarters is in Canada. What is the Florida classification?"},{"id":"ch01-s11","title":"10. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Transfer of risk through a legal contract, OR pooling/collecting funds.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Policyholders own a mutual. They receive dividends from divisible surplus.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Domestic (its home state is Ohio, where it is authorized).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> An alien insurer (principal office outside the U.S.).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> The ceding company.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> The insured, not the insurer.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> The insurer.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Express, implied, apparent.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> (1) Uniformity, (2) efficiency, (3) consumer protection, (4) preserving state regulation.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> No.</p><p>Scenario A: Captive (career) agent for Carl; Maria's shopping is done through an independent agent or broker.</p><p>Scenario B: Ray hired a public adjuster, who works for the insured.</p><p>Scenario C: Alien insurer (principal office outside the U.S.).</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 1:</strong> Make a three-column card: Agent / Broker / Public Adjuster, and fill in &quot;whose side?&quot; Then do the same for Domestic / Foreign / Alien.</p></div>","text":"Transfer of risk through a legal contract, OR pooling/collecting funds. Policyholders own a mutual. They receive dividends from divisible surplus. Domestic (its home state is Ohio, where it is authorized). An alien insurer (principal office outside the U.S.). The ceding company. The insured, not the insurer. The insurer. Express, implied, apparent. (1) Uniformity, (2) efficiency, (3) consumer protection, (4) preserving state regulation. No. Scenario A: Captive (career) agent for Carl; Maria's shopping is done through an independent agent or broker. Scenario B: Ray hired a public adjuster, who works for the insured. Scenario C: Alien insurer (principal office outside the U.S.). Memory tip for Chapter 1: Make a three-column card: Agent / Broker / Public Adjuster, and fill in \"whose side?\" Then do the same for Domestic / Foreign / Alien."}]},{"id":"ch02","short":"Ch 2","title":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","label":"Chapter 2","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Four words that get mixed up on every exam: <strong>RISK</strong> (uncertainty), <strong>PERIL</strong> (cause of loss), <strong>HAZARD</strong> (condition that makes a peril more likely), <strong>LOSS</strong> (the damage). Learn these four, then the three types of hazard.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch02-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Insurance works because of the <strong>law of large numbers</strong>: big groups are predictable, single people are not.</li><li class=\"l0\">Insurers want <strong>pure risk</strong> (chance of loss only) and avoid <strong>adverse selection</strong> (the people most likely to need coverage are the ones who buy it).</li></ul>","text":"Insurance works because of the law of large numbers: big groups are predictable, single people are not. Insurers want pure risk (chance of loss only) and avoid adverse selection (the people most likely to need coverage are the ones who buy it)."},{"id":"ch02-s2","title":"1. Foundations","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Plain-English meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Transfer of risk</td><td>Moving a financial risk from one person to another through a contract or pooling</td></tr><tr><td>Risk pooling</td><td>Many people's premiums cover the losses of the few</td></tr><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Large protection in exchange for a relatively small premium</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Law of large numbers (CRITICAL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">As more similar risks are grouped together, the <strong>total</strong> loss becomes <strong>more predictable</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Insurers can predict <strong>total</strong> losses for a group, NOT the loss for one individual.</li><li class=\"l0\">This makes risk spreading, accurate premiums, and financial stability possible.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>BIG GROUP = BIG PREDICTION. One person = unpredictable.</p></div><h4>Principle of indemnity</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Put the insured back in the same financial position as before the loss.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Key rule:</strong> The insured should NOT profit from a loss.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Applies to:</strong> Contracts of indemnity (property, casualty, and expense-reimbursement health coverage).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Does NOT apply to:</strong> Life insurance, which is a valued contract (pays a fixed amount no matter the actual financial loss).</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> Expense-reimbursement health (medical expense) is indemnity. Some health products pay a set amount, such as hospital indemnity, critical illness, and individual disability income. Those behave like valued contracts. Learn: &quot;Pays the bills = indemnity. Pays a set amount = valued.&quot;</p></div><h4>Adverse selection</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Higher-risk people are MORE likely to seek and keep insurance. The selection works against the insurer.</li><li class=\"l0\">Includes policyholders who take advantage of favorable contract provisions.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Problem:</strong> One side (the applicant) knows more about the risk than the insurer.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Solution:</strong> Underwriting to assess and price risks accurately.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Examples:</strong> An 80-year-old with chronic illnesses; someone with advanced cancer applying for coverage.</li></ul>","text":"Concept Plain-English meaning Transfer of risk Moving a financial risk from one person to another through a contract or pooling Risk pooling Many people's premiums cover the losses of the few Coverage Large protection in exchange for a relatively small premium Law of large numbers (CRITICAL) As more similar risks are grouped together, the total loss becomes more predictable. Insurers can predict total losses for a group, NOT the loss for one individual. This makes risk spreading, accurate premiums, and financial stability possible. Memory trick: BIG GROUP = BIG PREDICTION. One person = unpredictable. Principle of indemnity Purpose: Put the insured back in the same financial position as before the loss. Key rule: The insured should NOT profit from a loss. Applies to: Contracts of indemnity (property, casualty, and expense-reimbursement health coverage). Does NOT apply to: Life insurance, which is a valued contract (pays a fixed amount no matter the actual financial loss). Study tip: Expense-reimbursement health (medical expense) is indemnity. Some health products pay a set amount, such as hospital indemnity, critical illness, and individual disability income. Those behave like valued contracts. Learn: \"Pays the bills = indemnity. Pays a set amount = valued.\" Adverse selection Definition: Higher-risk people are MORE likely to seek and keep insurance. The selection works against the insurer. Includes policyholders who take advantage of favorable contract provisions. Problem: One side (the applicant) knows more about the risk than the insurer. Solution: Underwriting to assess and price risks accurately. Examples: An 80-year-old with chronic illnesses; someone with advanced cancer applying for coverage."},{"id":"ch02-s3","title":"2. Risk: what it is and what can be insured","html":"<h4>Speculative vs. pure risk (EXAM CRITICAL)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Possible outcomes</th><th>Insurable?</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Speculative risk</strong></td><td>Loss OR gain</td><td><strong>NO</strong></td><td>Stock market, cryptocurrency, gambling, sports betting</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pure risk</strong></td><td>Loss ONLY</td><td><strong>YES</strong></td><td>Fire, car accident, illness, natural disaster</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>SPECULATIVE = Could Score (gain). PURE = Possible Only Loss.</p></div><h4>The 7 elements of an insurable risk</h4><p>For a pure risk to be insurable, it should meet these conditions:</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> <strong>Chance of loss:</strong> Happens by chance. The insured has no control.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> <strong>Definite and measurable:</strong> Specific time, place, and amount.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> <strong>Predictable:</strong> Frequency and severity can be estimated.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> <strong>Not catastrophic:</strong> Not so large that the insurer could not pay.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> <strong>Substantial loss exposure:</strong> Large enough for the law of large numbers to work.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> <strong>Random selection:</strong> Occurs randomly, which helps avoid adverse selection.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> <strong>Economically feasible:</strong> Premium must be affordable for the buyer.</p><h4>Risk classifications</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Standard</strong></td><td>Average loss potential</td><td>Standard premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Substandard</strong></td><td>Higher loss potential</td><td>Higher premium, reduced benefits, or coverage denied</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Preferred</strong></td><td>Lower loss potential</td><td>Lower premium</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Speculative vs. pure risk (EXAM CRITICAL) Type Possible outcomes Insurable? Examples Speculative risk Loss OR gain NO Stock market, cryptocurrency, gambling, sports betting Pure risk Loss ONLY YES Fire, car accident, illness, natural disaster Memory trick: SPECULATIVE = Could Score (gain). PURE = Possible Only Loss. The 7 elements of an insurable risk For a pure risk to be insurable, it should meet these conditions: Chance of loss: Happens by chance. The insured has no control. Definite and measurable: Specific time, place, and amount. Predictable: Frequency and severity can be estimated. Not catastrophic: Not so large that the insurer could not pay. Substantial loss exposure: Large enough for the law of large numbers to work. Random selection: Occurs randomly, which helps avoid adverse selection. Economically feasible: Premium must be affordable for the buyer. Risk classifications Class Meaning Typical outcome Standard Average loss potential Standard premium Substandard Higher loss potential Higher premium, reduced benefits, or coverage denied Preferred Lower loss potential Lower premium"},{"id":"ch02-s4","title":"3. Peril, hazard, loss: the big three","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Peril</strong></td><td>The direct, specific CAUSE of loss</td><td>Fire, theft, flood, earthquake, vandalism, explosion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hazard</strong></td><td>A condition that CREATES or INCREASES the chance a peril will happen</td><td>Icy walkways, blocked exits, high blood pressure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Loss</strong></td><td>The unforeseen decrease in value caused by a peril</td><td>The burned-down house</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>PERIL = the Pow (the event). HAZARD = the Hint (conditions that make the Pow likely). LOSS = the Lost value.</p></div><h4>Named perils vs. open perils</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Named perils:</strong> Only the listed perils are covered. If it is not listed, it is NOT covered.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Open perils (special perils):</strong> Everything is covered EXCEPT what is specifically excluded.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Common life and health exclusions:</strong> Suicide, acts of war, illegal activities.</li></ul><h4>Direct vs. indirect loss</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Direct loss:</strong> The peril directly causes the damage. No intervening factor.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Indirect (consequential) loss:</strong> A secondary result of the direct loss.</li></ul><h4>Accident vs. occurrence</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Accident:</strong> Unexpected, sudden, at a specific time and place.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Occurrence:</strong> Any loss-causing event, including long-term exposure.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exam tip:</strong> An accident is always an occurrence, but an occurrence might NOT be an accident.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> Witnesses are NOT a legal element of an accident. An accident is unexpected and unintended. Treat &quot;unexpected, sudden, specific time and place&quot; as the working definition.</p></div><h4>Loss exposure</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Any situation that could result in a loss.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Homogeneous exposure units:</strong> Similar objects exposed to the same perils. The more similar units there are, the easier it is to predict losses.</li></ul>","text":"Term Definition Example Peril The direct, specific CAUSE of loss Fire, theft, flood, earthquake, vandalism, explosion Hazard A condition that CREATES or INCREASES the chance a peril will happen Icy walkways, blocked exits, high blood pressure Loss The unforeseen decrease in value caused by a peril The burned-down house Memory trick: PERIL = the Pow (the event). HAZARD = the Hint (conditions that make the Pow likely). LOSS = the Lost value. Named perils vs. open perils Named perils: Only the listed perils are covered. If it is not listed, it is NOT covered. Open perils (special perils): Everything is covered EXCEPT what is specifically excluded. Common life and health exclusions: Suicide, acts of war, illegal activities. Direct vs. indirect loss Direct loss: The peril directly causes the damage. No intervening factor. Indirect (consequential) loss: A secondary result of the direct loss. Accident vs. occurrence Accident: Unexpected, sudden, at a specific time and place. Occurrence: Any loss-causing event, including long-term exposure. Exam tip: An accident is always an occurrence, but an occurrence might NOT be an accident. Study tip: Witnesses are NOT a legal element of an accident. An accident is unexpected and unintended. Treat \"unexpected, sudden, specific time and place\" as the working definition. Loss exposure Definition: Any situation that could result in a loss. Homogeneous exposure units: Similar objects exposed to the same perils. The more similar units there are, the easier it is to predict losses."},{"id":"ch02-s5","title":"4. The three types of hazard (memorize)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hazard</th><th>Nature</th><th>Examples</th><th>Memory cue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Physical hazard</strong></td><td>Tangible, observable condition</td><td>Icy walkways, poor lighting, high blood pressure, improperly stored chemicals</td><td>Physical = you can SEE it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Moral hazard</strong></td><td>INTENTIONAL dishonesty or fraud</td><td>Exaggerating a damage claim, lying about a pre-existing condition, leaving a car unlocked hoping it gets stolen</td><td>Moral = MALICIOUS</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Morale hazard</strong></td><td>UNINTENTIONAL carelessness that comes from having insurance</td><td>Leaving the house unlocked, speeding, neglecting health</td><td>Morale = CARELESS</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Moral = Malicious (on purpose). Morale = careless (not on purpose).</p></div>","text":"Hazard Nature Examples Memory cue Physical hazard Tangible, observable condition Icy walkways, poor lighting, high blood pressure, improperly stored chemicals Physical = you can SEE it Moral hazard INTENTIONAL dishonesty or fraud Exaggerating a damage claim, lying about a pre-existing condition, leaving a car unlocked hoping it gets stolen Moral = MALICIOUS Morale hazard UNINTENTIONAL carelessness that comes from having insurance Leaving the house unlocked, speeding, neglecting health Morale = CARELESS Memory trick: Moral = Malicious (on purpose). Morale = careless (not on purpose)."},{"id":"ch02-s6","title":"5. Risk management","html":"<h4>The four-step process</h4><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> <strong>Identify</strong> potential risks (where could a loss happen?)</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> <strong>Choose</strong> a method to handle the risk</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> <strong>Implement</strong> the plan</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> <strong>Review and adjust</strong> periodically</p><h4>Methods of handling risk</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Avoidance</strong></td><td>Eliminate the hazard completely</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reduction</strong> (loss control)</td><td>Lower how bad the loss could be</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Retention</strong></td><td>Accept and pay for the loss yourself (self-insurance)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Transfer</strong></td><td>Shift the risk to someone else, for example through insurance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sharing</strong></td><td>Divide the risk among several parties</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pooling</strong> (loss sharing)</td><td>Spread the risk across a large group</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reinsurance</strong></td><td>Insurers share risk with other insurers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prevention</strong></td><td>Actions that stop or control the loss</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Avoid and Retain are the &quot;do it yourself&quot; methods. Transfer (insurance) shifts the risk to an insurer. Sharing, pooling, and reinsurance are spreading methods.</p></div>","text":"The four-step process Identify potential risks (where could a loss happen?) Choose a method to handle the risk Implement the plan Review and adjust periodically Methods of handling risk Method Meaning Avoidance Eliminate the hazard completely Reduction (loss control) Lower how bad the loss could be Retention Accept and pay for the loss yourself (self-insurance) Transfer Shift the risk to someone else, for example through insurance Sharing Divide the risk among several parties Pooling (loss sharing) Spread the risk across a large group Reinsurance Insurers share risk with other insurers Prevention Actions that stop or control the loss Memory trick: Avoid and Retain are the \"do it yourself\" methods. Transfer (insurance) shifts the risk to an insurer. Sharing, pooling, and reinsurance are spreading methods."},{"id":"ch02-s7","title":"6. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peril vs. hazard</td><td>Peril = the event. Hazard = the condition that makes the event more likely</td></tr><tr><td>Moral vs. morale hazard</td><td>Moral = intentional. Morale = careless</td></tr><tr><td>Speculative vs. pure risk</td><td>Speculative can gain. Pure can only lose</td></tr><tr><td>Direct vs. indirect loss</td><td>Direct = immediate result. Indirect = consequence of that result</td></tr><tr><td>Named vs. open perils</td><td>Named = only what's listed. Open = everything except exclusions</td></tr><tr><td>Standard vs. substandard</td><td>Average vs. higher-than-average loss potential</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Peril vs. hazard Peril = the event. Hazard = the condition that makes the event more likely Moral vs. morale hazard Moral = intentional. Morale = careless Speculative vs. pure risk Speculative can gain. Pure can only lose Direct vs. indirect loss Direct = immediate result. Indirect = consequence of that result Named vs. open perils Named = only what's listed. Open = everything except exclusions Standard vs. substandard Average vs. higher-than-average loss potential"},{"id":"ch02-s8","title":"7. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the difference between a peril and a hazard?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Which insurable-risk element means the insured cannot control the event?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Why can an insurer predict total losses for 10,000 policies but not for one person?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> A driver texts while driving. Is this a moral or morale hazard?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> A homeowner exaggerates a storm loss to get more money. Moral or morale?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Which kind of risk can a casino gain from?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Under a named-perils policy, a fire is not listed. Is the loss covered?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Name the four steps of the risk management process.</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Jim has a history of heart disease and applies for life insurance with a plan to leave out his doctor visits. Which concept is the insurer most worried about? (Hint: think adverse selection and moral hazard.)</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Ann leaves her car door open with keys inside because she has full coverage. Which hazard is this?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A business owner installs sprinklers and fire doors. Which risk method is this?</li></ul>","text":"What is the difference between a peril and a hazard? Which insurable-risk element means the insured cannot control the event? Why can an insurer predict total losses for 10,000 policies but not for one person? A driver texts while driving. Is this a moral or morale hazard? A homeowner exaggerates a storm loss to get more money. Moral or morale? Which kind of risk can a casino gain from? Under a named-perils policy, a fire is not listed. Is the loss covered? Name the four steps of the risk management process. Scenario questions Scenario A: Jim has a history of heart disease and applies for life insurance with a plan to leave out his doctor visits. Which concept is the insurer most worried about? (Hint: think adverse selection and moral hazard.) Scenario B: Ann leaves her car door open with keys inside because she has full coverage. Which hazard is this? Scenario C: A business owner installs sprinklers and fire doors. Which risk method is this?"},{"id":"ch02-s9","title":"8. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> A peril is the event that causes loss (fire). A hazard is a condition that increases the chance the peril will happen (oily rags next to a stove).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Chance of loss (the event happens by chance and is outside the insured's control).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> The law of large numbers: large groups become predictable, individuals do not.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Morale hazard (careless, not dishonest).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Moral hazard (intentional fraud).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Speculative risk (gain or loss).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> No. Named perils cover only listed perils.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Identify, choose a method, implement, review and adjust.</p><p>Scenario A: Adverse selection (high-risk applicant who hides information) and moral hazard (intentional concealment).</p><p>Scenario B: Morale hazard (careless because insurance is in place).</p><p>Scenario C: Risk reduction (loss control).</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 2:</strong> Draw a triangle: PERIL (event) in the middle, HAZARD on one side (cause of the event), LOSS on the other side (result). Practice explaining each with a real example.</p></div>","text":"A peril is the event that causes loss (fire). A hazard is a condition that increases the chance the peril will happen (oily rags next to a stove). Chance of loss (the event happens by chance and is outside the insured's control). The law of large numbers: large groups become predictable, individuals do not. Morale hazard (careless, not dishonest). Moral hazard (intentional fraud). Speculative risk (gain or loss). No. Named perils cover only listed perils. Identify, choose a method, implement, review and adjust. Scenario A: Adverse selection (high-risk applicant who hides information) and moral hazard (intentional concealment). Scenario B: Morale hazard (careless because insurance is in place). Scenario C: Risk reduction (loss control). Memory tip for Chapter 2: Draw a triangle: PERIL (event) in the middle, HAZARD on one side (cause of the event), LOSS on the other side (result). Practice explaining each with a real example."}]},{"id":"ch03","short":"Ch 3","title":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","label":"Chapter 3","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> An insurance policy is a contract. It needs 4 essential elements, it has special features, it requires insurable interest, and it runs on good faith, agency law, and a few legal doctrines (waiver, estoppel, subrogation). Most of the traps are in the agent-vs-broker rules and the insurable-interest timing rules.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch03-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>No insurable interest = no valid contract</strong> (it becomes a wager).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Life = valued contract. Expense-reimbursement health, property, casualty = indemnity.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Agent = insurer's side. Broker = buyer's side.</strong> In a dispute, the agent represents the insurer; the broker represents the buyer.</li></ul>","text":"No insurable interest = no valid contract (it becomes a wager). Life = valued contract. Expense-reimbursement health, property, casualty = indemnity. Agent = insurer's side. Broker = buyer's side. In a dispute, the agent represents the insurer; the broker represents the buyer."},{"id":"ch03-s2","title":"1. Insurance contract basics","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Policy:</strong> Written agreement in which one party promises to compensate another for losses from unforeseen events.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Parties:</strong> The insured (policyholder) and the insurer.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Types:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Life:</strong> Insurer pays a sum if the insured dies.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Health:</strong> Insurer covers costs from illness, accident, or disability.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Property:</strong> Insurer compensates for property damage or loss.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Casualty:</strong> Insurer covers losses caused by the insured's actions (liability).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Riders and endorsements:</strong> Legal attachments that change the policy. They add benefits, change coverage, or adjust deductibles. Example: a maternity rider on a health policy.</li></ul>","text":"Policy: Written agreement in which one party promises to compensate another for losses from unforeseen events. Parties: The insured (policyholder) and the insurer. Types: Life: Insurer pays a sum if the insured dies. Health: Insurer covers costs from illness, accident, or disability. Property: Insurer compensates for property damage or loss. Casualty: Insurer covers losses caused by the insured's actions (liability). Riders and endorsements: Legal attachments that change the policy. They add benefits, change coverage, or adjust deductibles. Example: a maternity rider on a health policy."},{"id":"ch03-s3","title":"2. The 4 essential elements (CRITICAL)","html":"<p>ALL FOUR must be present for a valid, binding contract.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>What it means</th><th>Key detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Offer and acceptance</strong></td><td>Applicant proposes; insurer accepts (usually by issuing the policy)</td><td>Application WITHOUT premium = invitation, NOT an offer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Consideration</strong></td><td>Something of value exchanged</td><td>Applicant: premiums plus application statements. Insurer: promise to pay valid claims</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Legal purpose</strong></td><td>Contract must serve a lawful purpose</td><td>Insurance contracts are presumed to have legal purpose. An illegal-drug contract is void</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Competent parties</strong></td><td>Insurer must be licensed; applicant must have legal capacity</td><td>See list below</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (OCLC):</strong> <strong>O</strong>ffer, <strong>C</strong>onsideration, <strong>L</strong>egal purpose, <strong>C</strong>ompetent parties. &quot;Only Contracts Legally Count&quot; if all four are present.</p></div><h4>Counter-offers</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A counter-offer <strong>voids</strong> the original offer. No contract exists unless the applicant accepts the counter-offer.</li></ul><h4>Who is NOT competent (lacks capacity)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Minors (under the age of majority)</li><li class=\"l0\">Mentally incompetent people</li><li class=\"l0\">People under the influence of drugs or alcohol</li><li class=\"l0\">Coerced people</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>EXAM TIP:</strong> Beneficiaries and the insured (if different from the applicant) are NOT parties to the contract, so they don't need legal capacity.</p></div>","text":"ALL FOUR must be present for a valid, binding contract. Element What it means Key detail Offer and acceptance Applicant proposes; insurer accepts (usually by issuing the policy) Application WITHOUT premium = invitation, NOT an offer Consideration Something of value exchanged Applicant: premiums plus application statements. Insurer: promise to pay valid claims Legal purpose Contract must serve a lawful purpose Insurance contracts are presumed to have legal purpose. An illegal-drug contract is void Competent parties Insurer must be licensed; applicant must have legal capacity See list below Memory trick (OCLC): Offer, Consideration, Legal purpose, Competent parties. \"Only Contracts Legally Count\" if all four are present. Counter-offers A counter-offer voids the original offer. No contract exists unless the applicant accepts the counter-offer. Who is NOT competent (lacks capacity) Minors (under the age of majority) Mentally incompetent people People under the influence of drugs or alcohol Coerced people EXAM TIP: Beneficiaries and the insured (if different from the applicant) are NOT parties to the contract, so they don't need legal capacity."},{"id":"ch03-s4","title":"3. Special features of insurance contracts","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Definition</th><th>Quick example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Aleatory</strong></td><td>Unequal exchange: one party may receive far more than it gave, depending on a future event</td><td>Pay a $300 premium; the house burns; receive $200,000. Gambling is also aleatory</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Adhesion</strong></td><td>Drafted by ONE party (the insurer). &quot;Take it or leave it.&quot; Ambiguity is read AGAINST the insurer</td><td>Policy terms are unclear, so the court favors the insured</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Unilateral</strong></td><td>Only ONE party makes a binding promise (the insurer)</td><td>The applicant can stop paying premiums. The insurer must pay if the covered event happens while the policy is in force</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Personal</strong></td><td>Agreement with the individual, not with the property. Not transferable without consent</td><td>Auto and homeowners policies do not transfer to a new owner</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Conditional</strong></td><td>Coverage depends on conditions being met</td><td>Timely premiums, notice of loss, proof of loss. If conditions fail, no benefits</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Exceptions and clarifications</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Life insurance CAN be transferred</strong> (by assignment). It is NOT a personal contract in that sense.</li><li class=\"l0\">Life insurance buyers are called <strong>policyowners</strong>, not just policyholders.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Reasonable expectations doctrine:</strong> Courts give the insured the coverage a reasonable person would expect, based on the insurer's representations. It counters the advantage of the party who drafted the contract.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (AUAPC):</strong> <strong>A</strong>leatory = unequal, depends on an event. <strong>A</strong>dhesion = the insurer wrote it, so ambiguity goes AGAINST the insurer. <strong>U</strong>nilateral = only the insurer promises. <strong>P</strong>ersonal = about the person, not the property. <strong>C</strong>onditional = conditions must be met.</p></div>","text":"Feature Definition Quick example Aleatory Unequal exchange: one party may receive far more than it gave, depending on a future event Pay a $300 premium; the house burns; receive $200,000. Gambling is also aleatory Adhesion Drafted by ONE party (the insurer). \"Take it or leave it.\" Ambiguity is read AGAINST the insurer Policy terms are unclear, so the court favors the insured Unilateral Only ONE party makes a binding promise (the insurer) The applicant can stop paying premiums. The insurer must pay if the covered event happens while the policy is in force Personal Agreement with the individual, not with the property. Not transferable without consent Auto and homeowners policies do not transfer to a new owner Conditional Coverage depends on conditions being met Timely premiums, notice of loss, proof of loss. If conditions fail, no benefits Exceptions and clarifications Life insurance CAN be transferred (by assignment). It is NOT a personal contract in that sense. Life insurance buyers are called policyowners, not just policyholders. Reasonable expectations doctrine: Courts give the insured the coverage a reasonable person would expect, based on the insurer's representations. It counters the advantage of the party who drafted the contract. Memory trick (AUAPC): Aleatory = unequal, depends on an event. Adhesion = the insurer wrote it, so ambiguity goes AGAINST the insurer. Unilateral = only the insurer promises. Personal = about the person, not the property. Conditional = conditions must be met."},{"id":"ch03-s5","title":"4. Valued vs. indemnity contracts (CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>VALUED contract</th><th>INDEMNITY contract</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Payment</td><td>Predetermined amount, regardless of actual loss</td><td>Equal to the actual loss (up to limits)</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Pays a fixed sum</td><td>Restores the insured to the financial position before the loss</td></tr><tr><td>Used for</td><td><strong>Life insurance</strong> (and fixed-benefit products)</td><td><strong>Health (expense), property, casualty</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>A $300,000 death benefit pays exactly $300,000</td><td>A $15,000 surgery bill pays $15,000 (up to the policy limit)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> Expense-reimbursement health is indemnity. Fixed-benefit products, such as individual disability income and hospital indemnity, pay a set amount and behave like valued contracts.</p></div>","text":"VALUED contract INDEMNITY contract Payment Predetermined amount, regardless of actual loss Equal to the actual loss (up to limits) Purpose Pays a fixed sum Restores the insured to the financial position before the loss Used for Life insurance (and fixed-benefit products) Health (expense), property, casualty Example A $300,000 death benefit pays exactly $300,000 A $15,000 surgery bill pays $15,000 (up to the policy limit) Study tip: Expense-reimbursement health is indemnity. Fixed-benefit products, such as individual disability income and hospital indemnity, pay a set amount and behave like valued contracts."},{"id":"ch03-s6","title":"5. Insurable interest (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<h4>Definition</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A <strong>financial or economic stake</strong> in the insured person or property.</li><li class=\"l0\">The buyer must stand to suffer a financial loss from the death, disability, or damage.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Without insurable interest, the contract is a wagering contract</strong> (invalid and unenforceable).</li><li class=\"l0\">Everyone has insurable interest in their own life.</li></ul><h4>Who has it (examples)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Self:</strong> Yourself.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Family:</strong> Spouses, parents and children (husband/wife and parents/children).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Business:</strong> Key employees whose loss would hurt the business.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Debtor-creditor:</strong> A creditor in the debtor's life if repayment depends on the debtor.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Guardian:</strong> Of a minor, if financially responsible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Non-example:</strong> A store clerk with no reasonable expectation of benefit from a customer's death.</li></ul><h4>WHEN must insurable interest exist? (memorize)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>LIFE and HEALTH</th><th>PROPERTY and CASUALTY</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Required at</td><td>Time of APPLICATION</td><td>BOTH application AND claim</td></tr><tr><td>NOT required at</td><td>Time of claim or during the policy term</td><td>(Must be present throughout, for the policy's legitimate financial interest)</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Bob and Sherry, married, buy policies on each other. They divorce but keep the policies. They can still collect, because interest existed at application</td><td>Sarah owns a car with an auto policy. She cannot insure Mike's car, because she has no interest in his property</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>LIFE and HEALTH check the interest at the <strong>APPLICATION</strong> only. PROPERTY and CASUALTY check it at <strong>Application AND Claim</strong> (think: &quot;Pay Attention to Both&quot;).</p></div><h4>STOLI / IOLI</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>STOLI</strong> (stranger-originated life insurance), also called <strong>IOLI</strong> (investor-originated life insurance).</li><li class=\"l0\">Investors convince people (often seniors) to buy life policies that name the investors as beneficiaries.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Get around insurable interest law.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>How it works:</strong> Investors loan premiums to the insured. After the contestability period, the insured transfers ownership. Investors keep paying premiums and collect the death benefit.</li><li class=\"l0\">Seniors may receive an up-front payment, a loan, or small ongoing interest payments.</li><li class=\"l0\">Treat STOLI as a fraud-type scheme that the law does not allow.</li></ul>","text":"Definition A financial or economic stake in the insured person or property. The buyer must stand to suffer a financial loss from the death, disability, or damage. Without insurable interest, the contract is a wagering contract (invalid and unenforceable). Everyone has insurable interest in their own life. Who has it (examples) Self: Yourself. Family: Spouses, parents and children (husband/wife and parents/children). Business: Key employees whose loss would hurt the business. Debtor-creditor: A creditor in the debtor's life if repayment depends on the debtor. Guardian: Of a minor, if financially responsible. Non-example: A store clerk with no reasonable expectation of benefit from a customer's death. WHEN must insurable interest exist? (memorize) LIFE and HEALTH PROPERTY and CASUALTY Required at Time of APPLICATION BOTH application AND claim NOT required at Time of claim or during the policy term (Must be present throughout, for the policy's legitimate financial interest) Example Bob and Sherry, married, buy policies on each other. They divorce but keep the policies. They can still collect, because interest existed at application Sarah owns a car with an auto policy. She cannot insure Mike's car, because she has no interest in his property Memory trick: LIFE and HEALTH check the interest at the APPLICATION only. PROPERTY and CASUALTY check it at Application AND Claim (think: \"Pay Attention to Both\"). STOLI / IOLI STOLI (stranger-originated life insurance), also called IOLI (investor-originated life insurance). Investors convince people (often seniors) to buy life policies that name the investors as beneficiaries. Purpose: Get around insurable interest law. How it works: Investors loan premiums to the insured. After the contestability period, the insured transfers ownership. Investors keep paying premiums and collect the death benefit. Seniors may receive an up-front payment, a loan, or small ongoing interest payments. Treat STOLI as a fraud-type scheme that the law does not allow."},{"id":"ch03-s7","title":"6. Utmost good faith","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Both parties fully disclose all material information.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Requirements:</strong> Complete honesty. No hiding, misrepresenting, or deceiving.</li><li class=\"l0\">The policyholder relies on the insurer's explanation of coverage. The applicant must give complete and honest risk disclosure.</li></ul><h4>Warranty vs. representation vs. concealment (memorize)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>WARRANTY</th><th>REPRESENTATION</th><th>CONCEALMENT</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Definition</td><td>Statement GUARANTEED to be true</td><td>Statement BELIEVED to be true</td><td>Failure to reveal a KNOWN material fact</td></tr><tr><td>Standard</td><td>Must be true IN ALL RESPECTS</td><td>True to the best of the applicant's knowledge</td><td>Can be intentional or unintentional</td></tr><tr><td>In the contract?</td><td>YES, binding</td><td>NO, not binding</td><td>Not a statement; it is an omission</td></tr><tr><td>If false</td><td>Contract can be voided</td><td>Material misrepresentation, if it affects the risk</td><td>Contract can be rescinded (voided)</td></tr><tr><td>Insurer's burden</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Must prove BOTH concealment AND materiality</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>WARRANTY = &quot;I GUARANTEE it.&quot; REPRESENTATION = &quot;I BELIEVE it.&quot; CONCEALMENT = &quot;I didn't SAY it.&quot;</p></div><h4>Material misrepresentation, fraud, rescission</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Material misrepresentation:</strong> An inaccurate statement that could affect the insurer's decision to approve the risk or set its terms. Example: saying &quot;never diagnosed with diabetes&quot; when that is untrue.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fraud:</strong> Intentional deceit or false statements to obtain benefits. Example: exaggerating a medical history to get a lower premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Rescission:</strong> Making the contract null and void, as if it never existed. The insurer must prove both concealment and materiality. Materiality means the concealed facts would have changed the insurer's decision to issue the policy.</li></ul><h4>Contestability period</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The insurer has a limited period after policy issue to contest validity.</li><li class=\"l0\">After the contestability period, the insurer cannot deny a claim or void the contract based on fraud or misrepresentation.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Life policies are incontestable after <strong>2 years</strong> in force during the insured's lifetime (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)). Health and accident policies are incontestable after <strong>3 years</strong> (R.S. 22:975).</p></div>","text":"Definition: Both parties fully disclose all material information. Requirements: Complete honesty. No hiding, misrepresenting, or deceiving. The policyholder relies on the insurer's explanation of coverage. The applicant must give complete and honest risk disclosure. Warranty vs. representation vs. concealment (memorize) WARRANTY REPRESENTATION CONCEALMENT Definition Statement GUARANTEED to be true Statement BELIEVED to be true Failure to reveal a KNOWN material fact Standard Must be true IN ALL RESPECTS True to the best of the applicant's knowledge Can be intentional or unintentional In the contract? YES, binding NO, not binding Not a statement; it is an omission If false Contract can be voided Material misrepresentation, if it affects the risk Contract can be rescinded (voided) Insurer's burden — — Must prove BOTH concealment AND materiality Memory trick: WARRANTY = \"I GUARANTEE it.\" REPRESENTATION = \"I BELIEVE it.\" CONCEALMENT = \"I didn't SAY it.\" Material misrepresentation, fraud, rescission Material misrepresentation: An inaccurate statement that could affect the insurer's decision to approve the risk or set its terms. Example: saying \"never diagnosed with diabetes\" when that is untrue. Fraud: Intentional deceit or false statements to obtain benefits. Example: exaggerating a medical history to get a lower premium. Rescission: Making the contract null and void, as if it never existed. The insurer must prove both concealment and materiality. Materiality means the concealed facts would have changed the insurer's decision to issue the policy. Contestability period The insurer has a limited period after policy issue to contest validity. After the contestability period, the insurer cannot deny a claim or void the contract based on fraud or misrepresentation. Louisiana: Life policies are incontestable after 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)). Health and accident policies are incontestable after 3 years (R.S. 22:975)."},{"id":"ch03-s8","title":"7. Void vs. voidable contracts","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>VOID contract</th><th>VOIDABLE contract</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Status</td><td>Lacks legal effect</td><td>Valid, but one party can set it aside</td></tr><tr><td>Why</td><td>Missing one or more essential elements</td><td>One party may legally void it for a valid reason</td></tr><tr><td>Enforceable?</td><td>NOT enforceable</td><td>Binding until voided</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Contract for illegal drugs (no legal purpose)</td><td>Policyholder stops paying premiums, so the insurer may terminate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> Cancellation is a way to end a policy, not a defect that makes a contract voidable. Treat cancellation as a separate concept.</p></div>","text":"VOID contract VOIDABLE contract Status Lacks legal effect Valid, but one party can set it aside Why Missing one or more essential elements One party may legally void it for a valid reason Enforceable? NOT enforceable Binding until voided Example Contract for illegal drugs (no legal purpose) Policyholder stops paying premiums, so the insurer may terminate Study tip: Cancellation is a way to end a policy, not a defect that makes a contract voidable. Treat cancellation as a separate concept."},{"id":"ch03-s9","title":"8. Waiver and estoppel (CRITICAL)","html":"<h4>Waiver</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Voluntary giving up of a KNOWN right.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Who:</strong> Usually the insurer gives up a defense against a claim.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Key rule:</strong> Once waived, the insurer CANNOT later deny the claim based on the waived provision.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Types:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Explicit waiver:</strong> Example: an officer tells the beneficiary the insurer will waive the military-service exclusion.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Waiver by acceptance:</strong> The insurer accepts premiums or other performance while knowing of a defect or breach.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Waiver by silence (conduct):</strong> The insurer fails to object or act as the other party expects.</li></ul><h4>Estoppel</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Prevents a party from denying the consequences of its own actions. Based on the broken-promise idea.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Three requirements:</strong></li></ul><p>  1. <strong>False representation</strong> (a statement or action)</p><p>  2. <strong>Reliance</strong> (the other party acts on it)</p><p>  3. <strong>Harm</strong> (a financial loss results)</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Example:</strong> An agent says the policy covers all damages. The applicant relies on this and does not buy more coverage. The claim is denied because the policy does not cover it as stated. The insurer is <strong>estopped</strong> from denying the claim.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>WAIVER = the insurer GIVES UP a right. ESTOPPEL = the insurer is STOPPED from denying what it said or did. Both block a denial.</p></div>","text":"Waiver Definition: Voluntary giving up of a KNOWN right. Who: Usually the insurer gives up a defense against a claim. Key rule: Once waived, the insurer CANNOT later deny the claim based on the waived provision. Types: Explicit waiver: Example: an officer tells the beneficiary the insurer will waive the military-service exclusion. Waiver by acceptance: The insurer accepts premiums or other performance while knowing of a defect or breach. Waiver by silence (conduct): The insurer fails to object or act as the other party expects. Estoppel Definition: Prevents a party from denying the consequences of its own actions. Based on the broken-promise idea. Three requirements: 1. False representation (a statement or action) 2. Reliance (the other party acts on it) 3. Harm (a financial loss results) Example: An agent says the policy covers all damages. The applicant relies on this and does not buy more coverage. The claim is denied because the policy does not cover it as stated. The insurer is estopped from denying the claim. Memory trick: WAIVER = the insurer GIVES UP a right. ESTOPPEL = the insurer is STOPPED from denying what it said or did. Both block a denial."},{"id":"ch03-s10","title":"9. Law of agency","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Agent role:</strong> Authorized by the insurer to sell products, explain policies, solicit applications, collect premiums, and provide service.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Source of authority:</strong> The agency agreement (contract of agency).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Governed by:</strong> Agency law.</li></ul><h4>Core agency principles</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">An agent's actions within authority = the insurer's actions.</li><li class=\"l0\">Contracts the agent makes are binding on the principal (insurer).</li><li class=\"l0\">Payments made to the agent = payments made to the insurer.</li><li class=\"l0\">The agent's knowledge = the insurer's knowledge.</li></ul><h4>Three types of authority (MEMORIZE)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Source</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Express</strong></td><td>Explicitly granted in a written contract</td><td>Authorized to negotiate policies and collect premiums</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Implied</strong></td><td>Not specified, but necessary to do the job</td><td>Ordering promotional materials</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Apparent</strong></td><td>Created by the principal's conduct, making the agent look authorized</td><td>Company gives the agent access to materials and records, creating an appearance of authority</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The insurer is accountable for the agent's acts within authority.</li><li class=\"l0\">The insurer may be liable for unauthorized acts if the contract does not clearly define the scope of authority.</li></ul>","text":"Agent role: Authorized by the insurer to sell products, explain policies, solicit applications, collect premiums, and provide service. Source of authority: The agency agreement (contract of agency). Governed by: Agency law. Core agency principles An agent's actions within authority = the insurer's actions. Contracts the agent makes are binding on the principal (insurer). Payments made to the agent = payments made to the insurer. The agent's knowledge = the insurer's knowledge. Three types of authority (MEMORIZE) Type Source Example Express Explicitly granted in a written contract Authorized to negotiate policies and collect premiums Implied Not specified, but necessary to do the job Ordering promotional materials Apparent Created by the principal's conduct, making the agent look authorized Company gives the agent access to materials and records, creating an appearance of authority The insurer is accountable for the agent's acts within authority. The insurer may be liable for unauthorized acts if the contract does not clearly define the scope of authority."},{"id":"ch03-s11","title":"10. Agent vs. broker vs. solicitor","html":"<h4>Agent vs. broker (CRITICAL DISTINCTION)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>AGENT</th><th>BROKER</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Represents</td><td>The INSURER (in sales)</td><td>The INSURED / buyer (in sales)</td></tr><tr><td>Contract</td><td>With ONE insurer</td><td>With MULTIPLE insurers</td></tr><tr><td>Authority</td><td>Can bind the insurer to contracts</td><td>Solicits and places coverage</td></tr><tr><td>In disputes</td><td>Represents the insurer (not the insured)</td><td>Represents the insured/buyer</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Exam trap:</strong> In disputes, the AGENT represents the insurer. The BROKER represents the insured/buyer.</p></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>KEY DISTINCTION:</strong> In sales, the AGENT represents the INSURER. The BROKER represents the INSURED.</p></div><h4>Agent vs. solicitor</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>AGENT</th><th>SOLICITOR</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authority</td><td>Can BIND coverage (finalize the agreement)</td><td>Can only find and obtain applicants</td></tr><tr><td>Can bind coverage?</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Fiduciary responsibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A position of trust with financial responsibilities.</li><li class=\"l0\">Duties: properly account for premiums, give sound financial advice, manage funds responsibly.</li></ul>","text":"Agent vs. broker (CRITICAL DISTINCTION) AGENT BROKER Represents The INSURER (in sales) The INSURED / buyer (in sales) Contract With ONE insurer With MULTIPLE insurers Authority Can bind the insurer to contracts Solicits and places coverage In disputes Represents the insurer (not the insured) Represents the insured/buyer Exam trap: In disputes, the AGENT represents the insurer. The BROKER represents the insured/buyer. KEY DISTINCTION: In sales, the AGENT represents the INSURER. The BROKER represents the INSURED. Agent vs. solicitor AGENT SOLICITOR Authority Can BIND coverage (finalize the agreement) Can only find and obtain applicants Can bind coverage? Yes No Fiduciary responsibility A position of trust with financial responsibilities. Duties: properly account for premiums, give sound financial advice, manage funds responsibly."},{"id":"ch03-s12","title":"11. Other legal concepts","html":"<h4>Parol evidence rule</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A written contract prevails over oral statements.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Parol evidence</strong> = oral or spoken testimony.</li><li class=\"l0\">Verbal statements made before the contract cannot change the written terms.</li><li class=\"l0\">Once the policy is delivered, the written terms are what can be enforced in court.</li></ul><h4>Subrogation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The insurer's right to seek reimbursement from the third party who caused the loss.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Example:</strong> The insurer pays for car repairs, then pursues the at-fault driver.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>SUBROGATION = SUBSTITUTE. The insurer steps into the insured's shoes to recover from the wrongdoer.</p></div><h4>Tort law and negligence</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Tort:</strong> A civil wrong that causes harm and leads to compensation.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Contract lawsuits</strong> fall under contract law. Most other civil claims fall under tort law.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Simple negligence</strong></td><td>Failure to use reasonable care, leading to harm or loss</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gross negligence</strong></td><td>Blatant disregard for reasonable care; aware of a significant risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Willful and wanton negligence</strong></td><td>Aware of the risk and recklessly disregards it; close to intentional; often NOT covered by insurance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Errors and omissions (E&amp;O) insurance</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Professional liability insurance for agents, covering legal liability from professional services provided or neglected.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Typical covered losses:</strong> failure to provide required coverage; premium calculation mistakes; administrative errors; misidentifying a client's loss exposure; failing to make a requested policy change; inadequate explanation of policy provisions; sending incorrect information to the carrier; failing to recommend appropriate coverage; misstatements of coverage; improper claim handling.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>E&amp;O exclusions (NOT covered):</strong> fraudulent acts, criminal behavior, intentional harm, defamation, slander, unlawful acts, and deliberate breaches of law, regulation, statute, or ordinance.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> E&amp;O is strongly recommended for agents, and some employers require it.</p></div>","text":"Parol evidence rule A written contract prevails over oral statements. Parol evidence = oral or spoken testimony. Verbal statements made before the contract cannot change the written terms. Once the policy is delivered, the written terms are what can be enforced in court. Subrogation The insurer's right to seek reimbursement from the third party who caused the loss. Example: The insurer pays for car repairs, then pursues the at-fault driver. Memory trick: SUBROGATION = SUBSTITUTE. The insurer steps into the insured's shoes to recover from the wrongdoer. Tort law and negligence Tort: A civil wrong that causes harm and leads to compensation. Contract lawsuits fall under contract law. Most other civil claims fall under tort law. Level Meaning Simple negligence Failure to use reasonable care, leading to harm or loss Gross negligence Blatant disregard for reasonable care; aware of a significant risk Willful and wanton negligence Aware of the risk and recklessly disregards it; close to intentional; often NOT covered by insurance Errors and omissions (E&O) insurance Definition: Professional liability insurance for agents, covering legal liability from professional services provided or neglected. Typical covered losses: failure to provide required coverage; premium calculation mistakes; administrative errors; misidentifying a client's loss exposure; failing to make a requested policy change; inadequate explanation of policy provisions; sending incorrect information to the carrier; failing to recommend appropriate coverage; misstatements of coverage; improper claim handling. E&O exclusions (NOT covered): fraudulent acts, criminal behavior, intentional harm, defamation, slander, unlawful acts, and deliberate breaches of law, regulation, statute, or ordinance. Study tip: E&O is strongly recommended for agents, and some employers require it."},{"id":"ch03-s13","title":"12. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Name the four essential elements of a valid contract.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Is an application with no premium an offer?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What happens when an applicant makes a counter-offer?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Which kinds of contracts are valued?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> A homeowner's neighbor buys a property policy on the homeowner's house. Why is that invalid?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> At what points must insurable interest exist for a life insurance policy?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What is the difference between a warranty and a representation?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Name the three elements of estoppel.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Who represents the insured in a sale: agent or broker?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> What is the definition of subrogation?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Donna's application says she is a non-smoker. She smokes but says it was &quot;to the best of my knowledge&quot; that she does not. Is this a warranty or a representation? If it is material and false, what can the insurer do?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> An insurer's agent tells Tom that his pre-existing condition will be covered. Tom relies on this and cancels his other coverage. The insurer then denies the claim. What doctrine helps Tom?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A person wants a policy on a stranger's life and names himself as beneficiary, with no connection to the stranger. What is missing?</li></ul>","text":"Name the four essential elements of a valid contract. Is an application with no premium an offer? What happens when an applicant makes a counter-offer? Which kinds of contracts are valued? A homeowner's neighbor buys a property policy on the homeowner's house. Why is that invalid? At what points must insurable interest exist for a life insurance policy? What is the difference between a warranty and a representation? Name the three elements of estoppel. Who represents the insured in a sale: agent or broker? What is the definition of subrogation? Scenario questions Scenario A: Donna's application says she is a non-smoker. She smokes but says it was \"to the best of my knowledge\" that she does not. Is this a warranty or a representation? If it is material and false, what can the insurer do? Scenario B: An insurer's agent tells Tom that his pre-existing condition will be covered. Tom relies on this and cancels his other coverage. The insurer then denies the claim. What doctrine helps Tom? Scenario C: A person wants a policy on a stranger's life and names himself as beneficiary, with no connection to the stranger. What is missing?"},{"id":"ch03-s14","title":"13. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Offer and acceptance, consideration, legal purpose, competent parties.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> No. It is an invitation to make an offer.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> The original offer is voided. There is no contract unless the applicant accepts the counter-offer.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Life insurance (and fixed-benefit products).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> The homeowner's neighbor has no insurable interest in the house, so it is a wagering contract.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Life insurance: at the time of application (not at claim).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> A warranty must be true in all respects and is binding. A representation is believed true to the best of knowledge and is not binding.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> False representation, reliance, harm.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> The broker.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> The insurer's right to recover what it paid from the third party who caused the loss.</p><p>Scenario A: It is a representation (believed true, to the best of knowledge). If it is material and false, the insurer can rescind or void the contract, subject to the contestability period.</p><p>Scenario B: Estoppel. The insurer is stopped from denying the claim because Tom relied on the agent's promise to his harm. (Also a waiver of the pre-existing exclusion is possible.)</p><p>Scenario C: Insurable interest. Without it, the policy is a wagering contract, and the scheme resembles STOLI.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 3:</strong> Build a one-page &quot;contract checklist&quot;: 4 elements, 5 features, insurable interest timing, and warranty vs. representation vs. concealment. Quiz yourself on it daily for 5 minutes.</p></div>","text":"Offer and acceptance, consideration, legal purpose, competent parties. No. It is an invitation to make an offer. The original offer is voided. There is no contract unless the applicant accepts the counter-offer. Life insurance (and fixed-benefit products). The homeowner's neighbor has no insurable interest in the house, so it is a wagering contract. Life insurance: at the time of application (not at claim). A warranty must be true in all respects and is binding. A representation is believed true to the best of knowledge and is not binding. False representation, reliance, harm. The broker. The insurer's right to recover what it paid from the third party who caused the loss. Scenario A: It is a representation (believed true, to the best of knowledge). If it is material and false, the insurer can rescind or void the contract, subject to the contestability period. Scenario B: Estoppel. The insurer is stopped from denying the claim because Tom relied on the agent's promise to his harm. (Also a waiver of the pre-existing exclusion is possible.) Scenario C: Insurable interest. Without it, the policy is a wagering contract, and the scheme resembles STOLI. Memory tip for Chapter 3: Build a one-page \"contract checklist\": 4 elements, 5 features, insurable interest timing, and warranty vs. representation vs. concealment. Quiz yourself on it daily for 5 minutes."}]},{"id":"ch04","short":"Ch 4","title":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","label":"Chapter 4","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Underwriting = deciding WHO gets coverage, at WHAT rate. Learn the 4 risk classes, the 4 premium receipt types, the application's 3 parts, the information sources, and the free-look and replacement rules.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch04-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Underwriting fights <strong>adverse selection</strong> (high-risk people seek coverage more often).</li><li class=\"l0\">The <strong>agent collects and submits</strong>. The <strong>home office underwriter decides</strong>. Agents CANNOT issue policies.</li><li class=\"l0\">Four risk classes: <strong>Preferred (cheaper), Standard (regular), Substandard (costlier or declined), Declined (rejected).</strong></li></ul>","text":"Underwriting fights adverse selection (high-risk people seek coverage more often). The agent collects and submits. The home office underwriter decides. Agents CANNOT issue policies. Four risk classes: Preferred (cheaper), Standard (regular), Substandard (costlier or declined), Declined (rejected)."},{"id":"ch04-s2","title":"1. Purpose of underwriting","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Research and assessment of possible policyholders to decide eligibility.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Also called:</strong> Risk selection.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Goals:</strong> Protect against adverse selection; separate insurable from uninsurable risks; prevent losses from uninsurable people; set premiums based on risk.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Two fundamental questions:</strong></li></ul><p>  1. Does insurable interest exist?</p><p>  2. Is the applicant insurable (health, lifestyle, occupation, risk factors)?</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Evidence of insurability:</strong> Records or statements proving health and qualification (medical records, exams, application data).</li></ul>","text":"Definition: Research and assessment of possible policyholders to decide eligibility. Also called: Risk selection. Goals: Protect against adverse selection; separate insurable from uninsurable risks; prevent losses from uninsurable people; set premiums based on risk. Two fundamental questions: 1. Does insurable interest exist? 2. Is the applicant insurable (health, lifestyle, occupation, risk factors)? Evidence of insurability: Records or statements proving health and qualification (medical records, exams, application data)."},{"id":"ch04-s3","title":"2. Insurable interest (Chapter 4 view)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Why:</strong> Prevents the policy from becoming a wager. The death must cause a real financial impact.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Must exist ONLY at policy inception</strong> (application), not when proceeds are paid.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Insurable interest?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self</td><td>Yes, unlimited (presumed)</td></tr><tr><td>Spouse</td><td>Yes (financial interdependence)</td></tr><tr><td>Parent in child</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Child in parent or grandparent</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Business in key employee, officer, director (key person insurance)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Business partners in each other (buy-sell agreements)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Creditor in debtor</td><td>Yes, but ONLY up to the debt plus accrued interest</td></tr><tr><td>Sentimental attachment alone</td><td>NO. Must be financial dependency</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Creditor's interest is capped at <strong>&quot;the debt + interest, nothing more.&quot;</strong> Parent in child is bigger than grandparent in grandchild.</p></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Policy assignment:</strong> After issue, the owner can transfer (assign) a life policy to anyone, even someone WITHOUT insurable interest. The assignment is still valid.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Consent:</strong> You generally cannot buy insurance on another person without that person's consent. Exception: buying coverage on a minor.</li></ul>","text":"Why: Prevents the policy from becoming a wager. The death must cause a real financial impact. Must exist ONLY at policy inception (application), not when proceeds are paid. Relationship Insurable interest? Self Yes, unlimited (presumed) Spouse Yes (financial interdependence) Parent in child Yes Child in parent or grandparent Yes Business in key employee, officer, director (key person insurance) Yes Business partners in each other (buy-sell agreements) Yes Creditor in debtor Yes, but ONLY up to the debt plus accrued interest Sentimental attachment alone NO. Must be financial dependency Memory trick: Creditor's interest is capped at \"the debt + interest, nothing more.\" Parent in child is bigger than grandparent in grandchild. Policy assignment: After issue, the owner can transfer (assign) a life policy to anyone, even someone WITHOUT insurable interest. The assignment is still valid. Consent: You generally cannot buy insurance on another person without that person's consent. Exception: buying coverage on a minor."},{"id":"ch04-s4","title":"3. Elements of insurable risk (life version)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accidental loss</td><td>Loss is accidental and unintentional</td></tr><tr><td>Measurable loss</td><td>Can be quantified</td></tr><tr><td>Definable risk</td><td>Clearly defined</td></tr><tr><td>No financial gain</td><td>Risk cannot offer the insured a financial gain</td></tr><tr><td>Significant loss</td><td>Causes real financial hardship</td></tr><tr><td>Non-catastrophic</td><td>Not from catastrophes (war, natural disasters typically excluded)</td></tr><tr><td>Reasonable cost</td><td>Premium is affordable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Law of large numbers:</strong> More similar exposures = more predictable losses. Results match expected results more closely.</p></div>","text":"Element Meaning Accidental loss Loss is accidental and unintentional Measurable loss Can be quantified Definable risk Clearly defined No financial gain Risk cannot offer the insured a financial gain Significant loss Causes real financial hardship Non-catastrophic Not from catastrophes (war, natural disasters typically excluded) Reasonable cost Premium is affordable Law of large numbers: More similar exposures = more predictable losses. Results match expected results more closely."},{"id":"ch04-s5","title":"4. Who is involved","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Applicant</strong></td><td>Requests coverage and completes the application (usually with a licensed agent)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Proposed insured</strong></td><td>The person whose life is covered; health and risk factors are evaluated</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Policyowner</strong></td><td>Holds all rights once approved; pays premiums and makes policy decisions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Payor</strong></td><td>Pays the premiums (often the same as the policyowner)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Agent / producer (field underwriter)</strong></td><td>First contact. Completes the application, collects the initial premium, gives receipts and disclosures, sends the file to the home office. <strong>CANNOT issue policies</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Home office underwriter</strong></td><td>Reviews the file, assesses risk, decides whether to offer coverage, sets the rate, classifies the risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Medical examiners</strong></td><td>Give exams or tests</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reinsurers</strong></td><td>Share the risk on large or high-risk policies</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Key point:</strong> Usually the applicant, insured, policyowner, and payor are ALL THE SAME PERSON.</p></div>","text":"Role Job Applicant Requests coverage and completes the application (usually with a licensed agent) Proposed insured The person whose life is covered; health and risk factors are evaluated Policyowner Holds all rights once approved; pays premiums and makes policy decisions Payor Pays the premiums (often the same as the policyowner) Agent / producer (field underwriter) First contact. Completes the application, collects the initial premium, gives receipts and disclosures, sends the file to the home office. CANNOT issue policies Home office underwriter Reviews the file, assesses risk, decides whether to offer coverage, sets the rate, classifies the risk Medical examiners Give exams or tests Reinsurers Share the risk on large or high-risk policies Key point: Usually the applicant, insured, policyowner, and payor are ALL THE SAME PERSON."},{"id":"ch04-s6","title":"5. Agent responsibilities","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fiduciary duties:</strong> Act in the best interests of the client AND the insurer. Handle client funds and information with care and integrity.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Field underwriting duties:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\">Solicit ethically; focus on profitable business</li><li class=\"l1\">Complete the application fully and accurately</li><li class=\"l1\">Collect all required signatures</li><li class=\"l1\">Collect the initial premium and issue a receipt</li><li class=\"l1\">Submit the application and premium to the home office</li><li class=\"l1\">Keep honesty, integrity, and compliance</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>E&amp;O:</strong> Protects against an honest mistake or negligence by a producer. It is recommended, and some agents carry their own coverage.</li></ul>","text":"Fiduciary duties: Act in the best interests of the client AND the insurer. Handle client funds and information with care and integrity. Field underwriting duties: Solicit ethically; focus on profitable business Complete the application fully and accurately Collect all required signatures Collect the initial premium and issue a receipt Submit the application and premium to the home office Keep honesty, integrity, and compliance E&O: Protects against an honest mistake or negligence by a producer. It is recommended, and some agents carry their own coverage."},{"id":"ch04-s7","title":"6. The application","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> The underwriter's main tool. Once attached to the policy, it becomes part of the legal contract.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Part I</strong></td><td>General applicant information: name, address, date of birth, gender, occupation, marital status, income, beneficiary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part II</strong></td><td>Medical and health history: conditions, past illnesses and surgeries, medications, family history</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part III</strong></td><td>Agent's report: health and lifestyle observations, identity verification, financial status and character, purpose of sale, how long the agent has known the applicant</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completing the application</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The agent must make sure the application is complete and accurate.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Incomplete applications are RETURNED</strong> to the agent. Consequences: underwriting delays, delayed issue and delivery, delayed commissions, and the applicant may go elsewhere.</li><li class=\"l0\">Applicant must INITIAL any corrections.</li><li class=\"l0\">Many insurers require a parent or guardian signature when the applicant is under 18.</li><li class=\"l0\">After issue, the application cannot be changed without the applicant's written permission.</li></ul><h4>Representations vs. warranties (in applications)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Representation</th><th>Warranty</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standard</td><td>Substantially true to the best of the applicant's knowledge</td><td>Guaranteed completely true, in every detail</td></tr><tr><td>Exact?</td><td>No, not in every detail</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Innocent error</td><td>Usually does not void coverage, if not material</td><td>Can void the policy</td></tr><tr><td>Key point</td><td><strong>Application statements are REPRESENTATIONS, not warranties.</strong></td><td>Example: guaranteeing identity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Material misrepresentation:</strong> A false statement about a significant fact. Can void the policy or lead to cancellation. Premium is typically refunded. <strong>The insurer must prove materiality.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fraudulent representation:</strong> Material to the risk AND made with fraudulent intent.</li></ul><h4>Signatures</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Required:</strong> Proposed insured (an adult, or a minor 15 or older where allowed); <strong>producer/agent (REQUIRED; no application is considered without it)</strong>; policyowner (if different from the insured).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Third-party ownership:</strong> Three signatures: applicant, proposed insured, and producer.</li><li class=\"l0\">By signing, the applicant attests that everything is true. False statements can cost coverage.</li></ul><h4>Minors on the application</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">General rule: under 18 is a minor in most states (can void contracts).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana:</strong> The age of majority is 18 (Civil Code art. 29). Under R.S. 22:852(B), a minor 15 or older may contract for life, health, or accident insurance.</li></ul>","text":"Purpose: The underwriter's main tool. Once attached to the policy, it becomes part of the legal contract. Part Contents Part I General applicant information: name, address, date of birth, gender, occupation, marital status, income, beneficiary Part II Medical and health history: conditions, past illnesses and surgeries, medications, family history Part III Agent's report: health and lifestyle observations, identity verification, financial status and character, purpose of sale, how long the agent has known the applicant Completing the application The agent must make sure the application is complete and accurate. Incomplete applications are RETURNED to the agent. Consequences: underwriting delays, delayed issue and delivery, delayed commissions, and the applicant may go elsewhere. Applicant must INITIAL any corrections. Many insurers require a parent or guardian signature when the applicant is under 18. After issue, the application cannot be changed without the applicant's written permission. Representations vs. warranties (in applications) Representation Warranty Standard Substantially true to the best of the applicant's knowledge Guaranteed completely true, in every detail Exact? No, not in every detail Yes Innocent error Usually does not void coverage, if not material Can void the policy Key point Application statements are REPRESENTATIONS, not warranties. Example: guaranteeing identity Material misrepresentation: A false statement about a significant fact. Can void the policy or lead to cancellation. Premium is typically refunded. The insurer must prove materiality. Fraudulent representation: Material to the risk AND made with fraudulent intent. Signatures Required: Proposed insured (an adult, or a minor 15 or older where allowed); producer/agent (REQUIRED; no application is considered without it); policyowner (if different from the insured). Third-party ownership: Three signatures: applicant, proposed insured, and producer. By signing, the applicant attests that everything is true. False statements can cost coverage. Minors on the application General rule: under 18 is a minor in most states (can void contracts). Louisiana: The age of majority is 18 (Civil Code art. 29). Under R.S. 22:852(B), a minor 15 or older may contract for life, health, or accident insurance."},{"id":"ch04-s8","title":"7. Premium receipts (and when coverage starts)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Consideration:</strong> The applicant's consideration is the first premium plus the application. The insurer will NOT issue the policy without an initial premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Trial application:</strong> An application submitted WITHOUT the initial premium. Not effective until a premium is collected.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Producer MUST give a receipt</strong> when the premium is collected. The receipt type determines when coverage begins.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Receipt</th><th>Coverage</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Conditional receipt</strong> (most common; also called temporary receipt)</td><td>Effective only IF the applicant proves insurable. Starts on the application date or exam date</td><td>If uninsurable: no coverage, premium refunded. Date on the receipt always comes before the issue date</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Binding receipt</strong> (unconditional)</td><td>Guarantees coverage until the insurer formally rejects the application</td><td>Insurer pays if death occurs before approval or rejection. Mostly for experienced agents; more common in auto and homeowners</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Temporary insurance agreement</strong></td><td>Immediate coverage throughout underwriting</td><td>Insurer pays death claims during underwriting, regardless of outcome</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Inspection receipt</strong></td><td>Applicant reviews the policy first; pays the first premium after</td><td>Largely obsolete because of the free-look period</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Conditional receipt subtypes</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Insurability type:</strong> Insurer makes a conditional offer; applicant accepts by paying. Coverage begins as of application or exam date if insurable. <strong>Policy delivery is NOT required.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Approval type:</strong> More restrictive. Coverage starts only AFTER the insurer approves. Rarely used today.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (receipts, strongest to weakest):</strong> BINDING = &quot;guaranteed until we say NO.&quot; TEMPORARY = &quot;covered while we decide.&quot; CONDITIONAL = &quot;covered IF you're insurable.&quot; INSPECTION = &quot;look first, pay later&quot; (obsolete).</p></div>","text":"Consideration: The applicant's consideration is the first premium plus the application. The insurer will NOT issue the policy without an initial premium. Trial application: An application submitted WITHOUT the initial premium. Not effective until a premium is collected. Producer MUST give a receipt when the premium is collected. The receipt type determines when coverage begins. Receipt Coverage Key facts Conditional receipt (most common; also called temporary receipt) Effective only IF the applicant proves insurable. Starts on the application date or exam date If uninsurable: no coverage, premium refunded. Date on the receipt always comes before the issue date Binding receipt (unconditional) Guarantees coverage until the insurer formally rejects the application Insurer pays if death occurs before approval or rejection. Mostly for experienced agents; more common in auto and homeowners Temporary insurance agreement Immediate coverage throughout underwriting Insurer pays death claims during underwriting, regardless of outcome Inspection receipt Applicant reviews the policy first; pays the first premium after Largely obsolete because of the free-look period Conditional receipt subtypes Insurability type: Insurer makes a conditional offer; applicant accepts by paying. Coverage begins as of application or exam date if insurable. Policy delivery is NOT required. Approval type: More restrictive. Coverage starts only AFTER the insurer approves. Rarely used today. Memory trick (receipts, strongest to weakest): BINDING = \"guaranteed until we say NO.\" TEMPORARY = \"covered while we decide.\" CONDITIONAL = \"covered IF you're insurable.\" INSPECTION = \"look first, pay later\" (obsolete)."},{"id":"ch04-s9","title":"8. Underwriting information sources","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>What it is</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Application</strong></td><td>The primary source</td><td>First and most important document</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Medical report</strong></td><td>Exam or medical info</td><td>Nonmedical limits: small policies (for example, under $50,000) need no extra medical info. Higher amounts or concerns trigger a medical report. Can be done by paramedics or registered nurses, not only physicians. Reviewed by the medical director</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Attending Physician's Statement (APS)</strong></td><td>Physician's report on specific conditions</td><td>Requested when there is a question. The applicant must sign a release</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MIB (Medical Information Bureau)</strong></td><td>Database of health info from past applications</td><td>Helps check for undisclosed history. Provided to member insurers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Inspection report</strong> (investigative consumer report)</td><td>Character, reputation, lifestyle, finances, hazards</td><td>For large amounts. Interviews may include employees, neighbors, associates. May include a credit check. Applicant has the right to a copy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Credit report</strong></td><td>Financial reliability</td><td>Three bureaus: Experian, Equifax, TransUnion. Poor credit may mean a higher chance of lapse</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Special questionnaire</strong></td><td>Targeted questions</td><td>For hazardous hobbies or dangerous occupations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mortality table</strong></td><td>Death probability by age and sex</td><td>Older people = higher cost. Women tend to live longer = lower cost</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (MIB):</strong> MIB = &quot;Medical Information Bureau,&quot; the insurance industry's shared health history database.</p></div>","text":"Source What it is Key facts Application The primary source First and most important document Medical report Exam or medical info Nonmedical limits: small policies (for example, under $50,000) need no extra medical info. Higher amounts or concerns trigger a medical report. Can be done by paramedics or registered nurses, not only physicians. Reviewed by the medical director Attending Physician's Statement (APS) Physician's report on specific conditions Requested when there is a question. The applicant must sign a release MIB (Medical Information Bureau) Database of health info from past applications Helps check for undisclosed history. Provided to member insurers Inspection report (investigative consumer report) Character, reputation, lifestyle, finances, hazards For large amounts. Interviews may include employees, neighbors, associates. May include a credit check. Applicant has the right to a copy Credit report Financial reliability Three bureaus: Experian, Equifax, TransUnion. Poor credit may mean a higher chance of lapse Special questionnaire Targeted questions For hazardous hobbies or dangerous occupations Mortality table Death probability by age and sex Older people = higher cost. Women tend to live longer = lower cost Memory trick (MIB): MIB = \"Medical Information Bureau,\" the insurance industry's shared health history database."},{"id":"ch04-s10","title":"9. Federal laws in underwriting","html":"<h4>Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA), 1970</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Protects consumers when inspection or credit reports are used.</li><li class=\"l0\">Applicants must be NOTIFIED when an inspection report is requested, and told what nonpublic information will be used.</li><li class=\"l0\">The applicant must be allowed to opt out.</li><li class=\"l0\">If coverage is denied because of a report, the insurer must give the name and address of the reporting agency so the applicant can request a copy.</li><li class=\"l0\">Life applicants are told of their FCRA rights at application.</li></ul><h4>USA PATRIOT Act, 2001</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Detects and deters terrorism by preventing money laundering.</li><li class=\"l0\">Insurers must have formal anti-money-laundering (AML) programs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Life insurance vulnerability:</strong> cash-value policies that can be surrendered can be used to launder money.</li></ul>","text":"Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA), 1970 Protects consumers when inspection or credit reports are used. Applicants must be NOTIFIED when an inspection report is requested, and told what nonpublic information will be used. The applicant must be allowed to opt out. If coverage is denied because of a report, the insurer must give the name and address of the reporting agency so the applicant can request a copy. Life applicants are told of their FCRA rights at application. USA PATRIOT Act, 2001 Detects and deters terrorism by preventing money laundering. Insurers must have formal anti-money-laundering (AML) programs. Life insurance vulnerability: cash-value policies that can be surrendered can be used to launder money."},{"id":"ch04-s11","title":"10. Required disclosures","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Disclosure statement:</strong> Where mandated, the agent signs a form confirming the disclosure was given.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Authorization form:</strong> The proposed insured and the agent (as witness) sign to allow reports and medical information to be obtained.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Application details:</strong> Must show the insurer's name, the agent's name, and the agent's license ID, legibly.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>When</th><th>What it covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Life Insurance Buyer's Guide</strong></td><td>REQUIRED; often before the initial premium is accepted</td><td>General consumer education. Does not cover a specific policy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Policy summary</strong></td><td>Delivered for the recommended product</td><td>Agent, insurer, policy, riders, premiums, dividends, benefit amounts, cash surrender values, policy loan interest rates, cost indexes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Disclosure statement: Where mandated, the agent signs a form confirming the disclosure was given. Authorization form: The proposed insured and the agent (as witness) sign to allow reports and medical information to be obtained. Application details: Must show the insurer's name, the agent's name, and the agent's license ID, legibly. Document When What it covers Life Insurance Buyer's Guide REQUIRED; often before the initial premium is accepted General consumer education. Does not cover a specific policy Policy summary Delivered for the recommended product Agent, insurer, policy, riders, premiums, dividends, benefit amounts, cash surrender values, policy loan interest rates, cost indexes"},{"id":"ch04-s12","title":"11. Risk classification (the 4 classes)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Premium result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Preferred</strong></td><td>Low risk; superior health, favorable lifestyle or occupation. Examples: non-smoker, ideal weight, abstains from alcohol</td><td>REDUCED premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Standard</strong></td><td>Meets guidelines without restrictions; average risk</td><td>REGULAR premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Substandard (impaired)</strong></td><td>Below standard. Poor health, dangerous occupation, hazardous habits or hobbies (scuba diving, skydiving can make a healthy person substandard)</td><td>Rejected OR higher premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Declined (uninsurable)</strong></td><td>Too risky. Severe health issues outside the insurer's limits</td><td>APPLICATION REJECTED</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Substandard options</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Rated policy (rating up):</strong> An extra charge added to the standard premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Special class:</strong> Does not qualify for standard, but may get coverage with an exclusion rider for a pre-existing condition.</li></ul><h4>Unfair discrimination</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Prohibited underwriting factors: sexual orientation, religious preference.</li></ul>","text":"Class Meaning Premium result Preferred Low risk; superior health, favorable lifestyle or occupation. Examples: non-smoker, ideal weight, abstains from alcohol REDUCED premium Standard Meets guidelines without restrictions; average risk REGULAR premium Substandard (impaired) Below standard. Poor health, dangerous occupation, hazardous habits or hobbies (scuba diving, skydiving can make a healthy person substandard) Rejected OR higher premium Declined (uninsurable) Too risky. Severe health issues outside the insurer's limits APPLICATION REJECTED Substandard options Rated policy (rating up): An extra charge added to the standard premium. Special class: Does not qualify for standard, but may get coverage with an exclusion rider for a pre-existing condition. Unfair discrimination Prohibited underwriting factors: sexual orientation, religious preference."},{"id":"ch04-s13","title":"12. Policy issue and delivery","html":"<h4>Effective date</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Sets when coverage begins and when future premiums are due.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>With a receipt:</strong> The date on the conditional or binding receipt is the effective date.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Without an initial premium:</strong> The insurer sets the date (often the issue date).</li><li class=\"l0\">The policy is generally not truly effective until (1) it is delivered, (2) the first premium is paid, and (3) a Statement of Good Health is obtained.</li></ul><h4>Backdating</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Setting an EARLIER effective date than the application date to lower the applicant's age and premium on paper.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Maximum:</strong> Typically 6 months, as limited by state regulation.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Prohibited</strong> for variable contracts (investment risk).</li></ul><h4>Age change (age at issue)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The age changes on the date midway between birthdays.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Nearest birthday method:</strong> Age changes based on the closer birthday.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Last birthday method:</strong> Uses the actual last birthday.</li></ul><h4>Policy delivery</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Most applicants do not keep all the policy details. In-person delivery lets the agent explain provisions, exclusions, and riders.</li><li class=\"l0\">Some states and most insurers require in-person delivery.</li><li class=\"l0\">Delivery can be completed without physically handing over the policy.</li></ul><h4>Explaining a substandard rating</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Prepare the applicant in advance if a substandard rating is possible.</li><li class=\"l0\">If it is unexpected, explain it.</li><li class=\"l0\">Stress that coverage is MORE needed because of the impairment, and that the applicant should buy now, since worsening health could make them uninsurable.</li></ul><h4>Statement of good health</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Used when the initial premium is not paid until the agent delivers the policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">The agent collects the premium, then gets a signed statement that the insured's health is unchanged, and submits both to the insurer.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Confirms the risk is the same as when assessed.</li></ul><h4>Free-look period</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sale type</th><th>Free-look period</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Individual life (Louisiana)</td><td>10 days from receipt of the policy (R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a))</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> Free look = return the policy for a full refund. In Louisiana the 10 days run from receipt of the policy.</p></div><h4>Replacement</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> The producer encourages a client to cancel or surrender an existing policy to buy a new one.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Requirements:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\">Give the consumer a &quot;Notice Regarding Replacement.&quot;</li><li class=\"l1\">Inform the existing insurer about the replacement.</li><li class=\"l1\">Use the replacement disclosure form mandated by the state.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Replacement is governed by Regulation 70 (LAC 37:XIII ch. 89), which requires a notice regarding replacement signed by the applicant and producer.</li></ul>","text":"Effective date Sets when coverage begins and when future premiums are due. With a receipt: The date on the conditional or binding receipt is the effective date. Without an initial premium: The insurer sets the date (often the issue date). The policy is generally not truly effective until (1) it is delivered, (2) the first premium is paid, and (3) a Statement of Good Health is obtained. Backdating Definition: Setting an EARLIER effective date than the application date to lower the applicant's age and premium on paper. Maximum: Typically 6 months, as limited by state regulation. Prohibited for variable contracts (investment risk). Age change (age at issue) The age changes on the date midway between birthdays. Nearest birthday method: Age changes based on the closer birthday. Last birthday method: Uses the actual last birthday. Policy delivery Most applicants do not keep all the policy details. In-person delivery lets the agent explain provisions, exclusions, and riders. Some states and most insurers require in-person delivery. Delivery can be completed without physically handing over the policy. Explaining a substandard rating Prepare the applicant in advance if a substandard rating is possible. If it is unexpected, explain it. Stress that coverage is MORE needed because of the impairment, and that the applicant should buy now, since worsening health could make them uninsurable. Statement of good health Used when the initial premium is not paid until the agent delivers the policy. The agent collects the premium, then gets a signed statement that the insured's health is unchanged, and submits both to the insurer. Purpose: Confirms the risk is the same as when assessed. Free-look period Sale type Free-look period Individual life (Louisiana) 10 days from receipt of the policy (R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a)) Study tip: Free look = return the policy for a full refund. In Louisiana the 10 days run from receipt of the policy. Replacement Definition: The producer encourages a client to cancel or surrender an existing policy to buy a new one. Requirements: Give the consumer a \"Notice Regarding Replacement.\" Inform the existing insurer about the replacement. Use the replacement disclosure form mandated by the state. Louisiana: Replacement is governed by Regulation 70 (LAC 37:XIII ch. 89), which requires a notice regarding replacement signed by the applicant and producer."},{"id":"ch04-s14","title":"13. Exam quick reference","html":"<h4>Critical definitions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Underwriting</strong> = risk selection.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Adverse selection</strong> = higher-risk people more likely to seek insurance.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Field underwriter</strong> = agent or producer (CANNOT issue policies).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Trial application</strong> = application submitted without the initial premium.</li></ul><h4>Key parties (usually the same person)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Applicant (requests and completes the application)</li><li class=\"l0\">Proposed insured (the life covered)</li><li class=\"l0\">Policyowner (holds all rights)</li><li class=\"l0\">Payor (pays premiums)</li></ul><h4>Application parts</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Part I: general information</li><li class=\"l0\">Part II: medical and health history</li><li class=\"l0\">Part III: agent's report</li></ul><h4>Federal laws</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>FCRA (1970):</strong> consumer rights for reports</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>USA PATRIOT Act (2001):</strong> anti-money laundering programs</li></ul><h4>Other critical points</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Insurable interest is required ONLY at policy inception.</li><li class=\"l0\">A policy can be assigned to someone WITHOUT insurable interest.</li><li class=\"l0\">Application statements are REPRESENTATIONS (not warranties).</li><li class=\"l0\">Unfair discrimination is prohibited.</li><li class=\"l0\">The incontestable clause takes effect after the contestable period ends.</li><li class=\"l0\">Only the home office underwriter issues policies. Agents do not.</li></ul>","text":"Critical definitions Underwriting = risk selection. Adverse selection = higher-risk people more likely to seek insurance. Field underwriter = agent or producer (CANNOT issue policies). Trial application = application submitted without the initial premium. Key parties (usually the same person) Applicant (requests and completes the application) Proposed insured (the life covered) Policyowner (holds all rights) Payor (pays premiums) Application parts Part I: general information Part II: medical and health history Part III: agent's report Federal laws FCRA (1970): consumer rights for reports USA PATRIOT Act (2001): anti-money laundering programs Other critical points Insurable interest is required ONLY at policy inception. A policy can be assigned to someone WITHOUT insurable interest. Application statements are REPRESENTATIONS (not warranties). Unfair discrimination is prohibited. The incontestable clause takes effect after the contestable period ends. Only the home office underwriter issues policies. Agents do not."},{"id":"ch04-s15","title":"14. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What are the four risk classifications, in order from best to worst?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Which premium receipt guarantees coverage until the insurer formally rejects the application?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> How much insurable interest can a creditor have in a debtor's life?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Is an application statement a representation or a warranty?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Who must sign the application, and what happens if the producer does not sign?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What is the free-look period for an individual life policy in Louisiana, and when does it start?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Which federal law requires notice when an inspection report is requested?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Name three information sources an underwriter may use.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is backdating, and what is its typical limit?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Can an agent issue a policy?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Ellen pays the first premium with her application. The receipt says &quot;conditional.&quot; She dies three weeks later, before the underwriter decides. Is she covered? (Hint: think about the condition.)</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Sam, a business owner, wants to insure his key employee. Does his company have insurable interest? What kind of coverage is this?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> Grace has a heart condition and wants a policy. The underwriter offers coverage at a higher premium. What class is Grace in?</li></ul>","text":"What are the four risk classifications, in order from best to worst? Which premium receipt guarantees coverage until the insurer formally rejects the application? How much insurable interest can a creditor have in a debtor's life? Is an application statement a representation or a warranty? Who must sign the application, and what happens if the producer does not sign? What is the free-look period for an individual life policy in Louisiana, and when does it start? Which federal law requires notice when an inspection report is requested? Name three information sources an underwriter may use. What is backdating, and what is its typical limit? Can an agent issue a policy? Scenario questions Scenario A: Ellen pays the first premium with her application. The receipt says \"conditional.\" She dies three weeks later, before the underwriter decides. Is she covered? (Hint: think about the condition.) Scenario B: Sam, a business owner, wants to insure his key employee. Does his company have insurable interest? What kind of coverage is this? Scenario C: Grace has a heart condition and wants a policy. The underwriter offers coverage at a higher premium. What class is Grace in?"},{"id":"ch04-s16","title":"15. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Preferred, standard, substandard, declined.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> The binding receipt (unconditional receipt).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Up to the debt plus accrued interest, and no more.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> A representation.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> The producer must sign (required). The proposed insured and policyowner also sign when applicable. Without the producer's signature the application will not be considered.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> 10 days. It starts from receipt of the policy.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> The Fair Credit Reporting Act (1970).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Any three of: application, medical report, APS, MIB, inspection report, credit report, special questionnaire, mortality table.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Backdating sets an earlier effective date to lower the applicant's age and premium. Typical limit: 6 months, as limited by state regulation.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> No. Only the home office underwriter (insurer) issues policies.</p><p>Scenario A: Ellen is probably covered as of the application or exam date, if she is found insurable. This is an insurability-type conditional receipt. If the underwriter later finds her uninsurable, there is no coverage and the premium is refunded. Review the actual receipt wording.</p><p>Scenario B: Yes. A business has insurable interest in key employees. This is key person insurance.</p><p>Scenario C: Substandard (impaired) risk, with a rated policy.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 4:</strong> Make a single flowchart: Application → Premium receipt (which type?) → Underwriter (which class?) → Policy issue → Delivery → Free look. Walk through it out loud.</p></div>","text":"Preferred, standard, substandard, declined. The binding receipt (unconditional receipt). Up to the debt plus accrued interest, and no more. A representation. The producer must sign (required). The proposed insured and policyowner also sign when applicable. Without the producer's signature the application will not be considered. 10 days. It starts from receipt of the policy. The Fair Credit Reporting Act (1970). Any three of: application, medical report, APS, MIB, inspection report, credit report, special questionnaire, mortality table. Backdating sets an earlier effective date to lower the applicant's age and premium. Typical limit: 6 months, as limited by state regulation. No. Only the home office underwriter (insurer) issues policies. Scenario A: Ellen is probably covered as of the application or exam date, if she is found insurable. This is an insurability-type conditional receipt. If the underwriter later finds her uninsurable, there is no coverage and the premium is refunded. Review the actual receipt wording. Scenario B: Yes. A business has insurable interest in key employees. This is key person insurance. Scenario C: Substandard (impaired) risk, with a rated policy. Memory tip for Chapter 4: Make a single flowchart: Application → Premium receipt (which type?) → Underwriter (which class?) → Policy issue → Delivery → Free look. Walk through it out loud."}]},{"id":"ch05_1","short":"Ch 5.1","title":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","label":"Chapter 5.1","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> This is the biggest &quot;which policy is this?&quot; chapter. Sort every product into TERM (temporary, no cash value) or PERMANENT (cash value). Then learn the flexible and variable products, and the tax traps (MEC, endowment).</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch05_1-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Term</strong> = death protection only. No cash value. Cheapest up front.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Whole life</strong> = permanent, fixed premium, fixed death benefit, guaranteed cash value.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Universal life</strong> = permanent, flexible premium and death benefit, interest-based cash value.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Variable products</strong> = policyowner bears the investment risk. Securities licenses required.</li></ul>","text":"Term = death protection only. No cash value. Cheapest up front. Whole life = permanent, fixed premium, fixed death benefit, guaranteed cash value. Universal life = permanent, flexible premium and death benefit, interest-based cash value. Variable products = policyowner bears the investment risk. Securities licenses required."},{"id":"ch05_1-s2","title":"1. Three primary categories of life insurance","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Ordinary life</strong></td><td>Individual coverage, term or permanent. Premiums monthly, quarterly, semiannual, or annual. The main form in the U.S.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Industrial life</strong> (home service)</td><td>Small face amounts, typically $1,000 to $2,000. Premiums paid weekly or monthly to an agent who visits the home. Often sold as burial insurance. Also called combination or debit companies, which assign agents to areas (debits). Now a small share of the market</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Group life</strong></td><td>Covers members of a group (employees, union members, association members) under one master contract. Underwriting looks at the group as a whole. Usually NO proof of insurability. Company pays the premium</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>INDUSTRIAL = &quot;I&quot; for &quot;In-home, weekly&quot; (the agent comes to the house). GROUP = one master contract, everyone covered.</p></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Monthly debit ordinary</strong> is a blend: higher face amounts than pure industrial, paid monthly.</p></div>","text":"Category Key facts Ordinary life Individual coverage, term or permanent. Premiums monthly, quarterly, semiannual, or annual. The main form in the U.S. Industrial life (home service) Small face amounts, typically $1,000 to $2,000. Premiums paid weekly or monthly to an agent who visits the home. Often sold as burial insurance. Also called combination or debit companies, which assign agents to areas (debits). Now a small share of the market Group life Covers members of a group (employees, union members, association members) under one master contract. Underwriting looks at the group as a whole. Usually NO proof of insurability. Company pays the premium Memory trick: INDUSTRIAL = \"I\" for \"In-home, weekly\" (the agent comes to the house). GROUP = one master contract, everyone covered. Monthly debit ordinary is a blend: higher face amounts than pure industrial, paid monthly."},{"id":"ch05_1-s3","title":"2. Term life insurance","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Pure death protection for a set period. Pays only if the insured dies during the term. <strong>No cash value.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Also called:</strong> Temporary life insurance.</li></ul><h4>Advantages and disadvantages</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lower initial premiums</td><td>Coverage ends when the term ends</td></tr><tr><td>Highest death benefit per premium dollar</td><td>Premiums rise on renewal</td></tr><tr><td>Level premiums for the term</td><td>Most policies pay no claim (most outlive the policy)</td></tr><tr><td>Protects insurability (renewable and convertible options)</td><td>No equity or cash value</td></tr><tr><td>Covers debts and mortgages</td><td>Does not return premiums</td></tr><tr><td>Can supplement whole life</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Term durations</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>By years:</strong> 1, 5, 10, 20, or 30 years.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>By age:</strong> Term to 45, to 55, or to 70.</li></ul><h4>Three term structures (memorize)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Death benefit</th><th>Premium</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Level term</strong></td><td>Stays the same for the whole term</td><td>Stays the same for the term (higher than annual renewable term)</td><td>General family protection. Example: $200,000 20-year level term</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Decreasing term</strong></td><td>Falls on a set schedule to $0</td><td>Stays level</td><td>Mortgage protection, credit life</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Increasing term</strong></td><td>Grows at set intervals (fixed amounts, percentage, or cost-of-living index)</td><td>Rises with the benefit</td><td>Protection against inflation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Mortgage protection (mortgage redemption):</strong> Pays off the mortgage balance if the breadwinner dies.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Credit life insurance:</strong> Covers a loan balance. The term matches the loan. The face amount can be no more than the outstanding debt.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Legal note:</strong> A creditor CANNOT tell a debtor where to buy the insurance. That is coercion and illegal.</p></div>","text":"Definition: Pure death protection for a set period. Pays only if the insured dies during the term. No cash value. Also called: Temporary life insurance. Advantages and disadvantages Advantages Disadvantages Lower initial premiums Coverage ends when the term ends Highest death benefit per premium dollar Premiums rise on renewal Level premiums for the term Most policies pay no claim (most outlive the policy) Protects insurability (renewable and convertible options) No equity or cash value Covers debts and mortgages Does not return premiums Can supplement whole life Term durations By years: 1, 5, 10, 20, or 30 years. By age: Term to 45, to 55, or to 70. Three term structures (memorize) Type Death benefit Premium Typical use Level term Stays the same for the whole term Stays the same for the term (higher than annual renewable term) General family protection. Example: $200,000 20-year level term Decreasing term Falls on a set schedule to $0 Stays level Mortgage protection, credit life Increasing term Grows at set intervals (fixed amounts, percentage, or cost-of-living index) Rises with the benefit Protection against inflation Mortgage protection (mortgage redemption): Pays off the mortgage balance if the breadwinner dies. Credit life insurance: Covers a loan balance. The term matches the loan. The face amount can be no more than the outstanding debt. Legal note: A creditor CANNOT tell a debtor where to buy the insurance. That is coercion and illegal."},{"id":"ch05_1-s4","title":"3. Key features of term life","html":"<h4>Renewable term</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Renew before expiration WITHOUT proof of insurability.</li><li class=\"l0\">Premiums go up with each renewal (reflecting the older age).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annually renewable term (ART) / yearly renewable term (YRT):</strong> One-year coverage, renewed each year without evidence of insurability. Insurers often cap the number of renewals or the upper age. Renewal options commonly run to age 65 or beyond.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Re-entry (revertible) term:</strong> At renewal, the premium depends on the insured's current insurability. To keep the lowest rate, the insured may have to show insurability again. If the insured is not insurable, coverage continues at a higher premium.</li><li class=\"l0\">Every term policy has a final termination date.</li></ul><h4>Convertible term</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The insured can convert a term policy to a permanent (whole life) policy WITHOUT evidence of insurability.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Attained-age method:</strong> Premium is based on the age at conversion. Higher premium. No extra payment.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Original-age method:</strong> Premium is based on the age when the term policy was issued. Lower premium. May require an additional payment to make up the difference. Builds cash value faster.</li><li class=\"l0\">The conversion window is usually limited (e.g., within 10 years or by age 55).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Group conversion:</strong> Lets a group term insured convert to an individual plan without proving insurability.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>RENEWABLE = keep the same coverage, pay more. CONVERTIBLE = switch to permanent, no health questions.</p></div><h4>Term riders</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Extend term coverage to family members under the primary insured's policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">Always level term; usually cheaper than separate policies.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Family plan policies:</strong> Permanent coverage for the family head, with term riders for the spouse and children. Future children are usually added automatically.</li></ul>","text":"Renewable term Renew before expiration WITHOUT proof of insurability. Premiums go up with each renewal (reflecting the older age). Annually renewable term (ART) / yearly renewable term (YRT): One-year coverage, renewed each year without evidence of insurability. Insurers often cap the number of renewals or the upper age. Renewal options commonly run to age 65 or beyond. Re-entry (revertible) term: At renewal, the premium depends on the insured's current insurability. To keep the lowest rate, the insured may have to show insurability again. If the insured is not insurable, coverage continues at a higher premium. Every term policy has a final termination date. Convertible term The insured can convert a term policy to a permanent (whole life) policy WITHOUT evidence of insurability. Attained-age method: Premium is based on the age at conversion. Higher premium. No extra payment. Original-age method: Premium is based on the age when the term policy was issued. Lower premium. May require an additional payment to make up the difference. Builds cash value faster. The conversion window is usually limited (e.g., within 10 years or by age 55). Group conversion: Lets a group term insured convert to an individual plan without proving insurability. Memory trick: RENEWABLE = keep the same coverage, pay more. CONVERTIBLE = switch to permanent, no health questions. Term riders Extend term coverage to family members under the primary insured's policy. Always level term; usually cheaper than separate policies. Family plan policies: Permanent coverage for the family head, with term riders for the spouse and children. Future children are usually added automatically."},{"id":"ch05_1-s5","title":"4. Whole life insurance","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Pays the death benefit whenever death occurs, as long as premiums are paid.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Also known as:</strong> Straight life, continuous premium life, permanent life, ordinary life.</li></ul><h4>Characteristics</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fixed death benefit</strong> (face amount does not change).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fixed premium</strong>, set at issue.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cash value</strong> (savings component) that grows at a guaranteed rate.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Lifetime protection</strong>. No renewal needed.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Endowment at age 100:</strong> Cash value equals the face amount.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Living benefits:</strong> Cash value can be borrowed against or withdrawn.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Analogy:</strong> Whole life = buying a house. Once paid off, you own it for life.</p></div><h4>How premiums are split</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Early years:</strong> More of the premium pays for the cost of death protection.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Later years:</strong> More of the premium builds cash value.</li><li class=\"l0\">At age 100, cash value = face amount.</li></ul><h4>Cash value rules</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Cash value is NOT paid to the beneficiary on top of the death benefit, unless a &quot;return of cash value&quot; rider is added.</li><li class=\"l0\">You can surrender the policy or borrow against the cash value.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Taxes</strong> may apply on surrender (gains over premiums paid).</li></ul>","text":"Definition: Pays the death benefit whenever death occurs, as long as premiums are paid. Also known as: Straight life, continuous premium life, permanent life, ordinary life. Characteristics Fixed death benefit (face amount does not change). Fixed premium, set at issue. Cash value (savings component) that grows at a guaranteed rate. Lifetime protection. No renewal needed. Endowment at age 100: Cash value equals the face amount. Living benefits: Cash value can be borrowed against or withdrawn. Analogy: Whole life = buying a house. Once paid off, you own it for life. How premiums are split Early years: More of the premium pays for the cost of death protection. Later years: More of the premium builds cash value. At age 100, cash value = face amount. Cash value rules Cash value is NOT paid to the beneficiary on top of the death benefit, unless a \"return of cash value\" rider is added. You can surrender the policy or borrow against the cash value. Taxes may apply on surrender (gains over premiums paid)."},{"id":"ch05_1-s6","title":"5. Whole life payment options","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>How premiums are paid</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Straight (traditional) whole life</strong></td><td>Level premiums for life (to age 100)</td><td>Default meaning of &quot;whole life&quot; on the exam</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Limited payment</strong></td><td>Level premiums for a shorter period (10-pay, 20-pay, paid-up at 65)</td><td>Higher premium; faster cash value; coverage continues for life</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Single premium</strong></td><td>One lump sum at issue</td><td>Immediate cash value; fully paid-up; <strong>ALWAYS a MEC</strong> (see Section 9)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Modified whole life</strong> (modified premium)</td><td>Lower premiums for the first 5 years, then a one-time increase in year 6, then level</td><td>Good for people who need lower early payments (for example, a college student)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Graded premium</strong></td><td>Lower premiums that rise each year for 5 to 10 years, then level</td><td>Expects future income growth</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Option How premiums are paid Notes Straight (traditional) whole life Level premiums for life (to age 100) Default meaning of \"whole life\" on the exam Limited payment Level premiums for a shorter period (10-pay, 20-pay, paid-up at 65) Higher premium; faster cash value; coverage continues for life Single premium One lump sum at issue Immediate cash value; fully paid-up; ALWAYS a MEC (see Section 9) Modified whole life (modified premium) Lower premiums for the first 5 years, then a one-time increase in year 6, then level Good for people who need lower early payments (for example, a college student) Graded premium Lower premiums that rise each year for 5 to 10 years, then level Expects future income growth"},{"id":"ch05_1-s7","title":"6. Endowment policies","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Cash value grows quickly so the policy matures BEFORE age 100.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Two benefits:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Death benefit</strong> if the insured dies during the endowment period.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Maturity benefit</strong> (face amount paid) if the insured is alive at the end.</li><li class=\"l0\">Endowments do NOT pay cash value on death. Premiums are higher because the term is shorter.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Living benefit at a set time (retirement or college).</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Payout</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Semi-endowment</strong></td><td>Half the sum if the insured survives; full amount if the insured dies</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pure endowment</strong></td><td>Pays nothing on death; pays only if the insured survives</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Juvenile endowment</strong></td><td>For children; matures at an age for education</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Tax note:</strong> Endowments generally no longer qualify for favorable life insurance tax treatment.</p></div>","text":"Definition: Cash value grows quickly so the policy matures BEFORE age 100. Two benefits: Death benefit if the insured dies during the endowment period. Maturity benefit (face amount paid) if the insured is alive at the end. Endowments do NOT pay cash value on death. Premiums are higher because the term is shorter. Purpose: Living benefit at a set time (retirement or college). Type Payout Semi-endowment Half the sum if the insured survives; full amount if the insured dies Pure endowment Pays nothing on death; pays only if the insured survives Juvenile endowment For children; matures at an age for education Tax note: Endowments generally no longer qualify for favorable life insurance tax treatment."},{"id":"ch05_1-s8","title":"7. Family policies","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>What it combines</th><th>How it pays</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Family plan</strong></td><td>Permanent coverage for the breadwinner; term riders for spouse and children</td><td>Units of coverage; future children included at no extra cost</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Family income</strong></td><td>Whole life plus decreasing term</td><td>Monthly income to the beneficiary if death occurs in the set period (10 to 20 years from issue). Payments run from the policy start date, not the death date. After the period, only the whole life face value is paid</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Family maintenance</strong></td><td>Whole life plus level term</td><td>Lump sum PLUS income for a set number of years FROM the date of death. After the income period, the beneficiary receives the full face amount. If death comes after the selected period, only the face amount is paid</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>INCOME policy = income FROM the start date (decreasing term). MAINTENANCE policy = income FROM the date of death, plus a lump sum.</p></div>","text":"Policy What it combines How it pays Family plan Permanent coverage for the breadwinner; term riders for spouse and children Units of coverage; future children included at no extra cost Family income Whole life plus decreasing term Monthly income to the beneficiary if death occurs in the set period (10 to 20 years from issue). Payments run from the policy start date, not the death date. After the period, only the whole life face value is paid Family maintenance Whole life plus level term Lump sum PLUS income for a set number of years FROM the date of death. After the income period, the beneficiary receives the full face amount. If death comes after the selected period, only the face amount is paid Memory trick: INCOME policy = income FROM the start date (decreasing term). MAINTENANCE policy = income FROM the date of death, plus a lump sum."},{"id":"ch05_1-s9","title":"8. Joint life policies","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>When it pays</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>First-to-die (joint life)</strong></td><td>At the death of the FIRST insured</td><td>Usually permanent. Survivors may buy individual policies without evidence of insurability. Premium lower than two separate policies (ages averaged)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Second-to-die (survivorship)</strong></td><td>At the death of the LAST surviving insured</td><td>Lower premium than two separate policies. Common in estate planning to pay estate taxes and expenses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Type When it pays Notes First-to-die (joint life) At the death of the FIRST insured Usually permanent. Survivors may buy individual policies without evidence of insurability. Premium lower than two separate policies (ages averaged) Second-to-die (survivorship) At the death of the LAST surviving insured Lower premium than two separate policies. Common in estate planning to pay estate taxes and expenses"},{"id":"ch05_1-s10","title":"9. Universal and flexible products","html":"<h4>Current assumption whole life (CAWL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Also called interest-sensitive or excess interest whole life.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Variable premiums</strong> based on the insurer's current assumptions (death rates, investment returns, expenses).</li><li class=\"l0\">Favorable assumptions: lower premiums or higher cash values. Unfavorable: higher premiums or a reduced face amount.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Accumulation account:</strong> Premium minus expenses and mortality charges, credited with interest.</li><li class=\"l0\">Has a guaranteed minimum cash value and rate of return, and a maximum annual premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Two types:</strong> Low-premium type (indeterminate premium) and high-premium type (high initial premium with a pay-up or vanishing-premium provision).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Partial surrender provision:</strong> Lets the owner withdraw cash value. Withdrawals are generally tax-free only up to the owner's cost basis.</li></ul><h4>Adjustable life</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Also called blended or combination policies.</li><li class=\"l0\">Merges term and permanent coverage.</li><li class=\"l0\">The owner can adjust the face amount, the premium, and the payment period.</li><li class=\"l0\">Increasing the face amount requires proof of insurability.</li><li class=\"l0\">Can convert between term and whole life, and switch between high and low premium or limited pay.</li><li class=\"l0\">Usually more expensive than standard term or whole life.</li></ul><h4>Universal life (UL)</h4><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> Universal life is a PERMANENT policy with a cash value account, not a term policy. Remember: UL = permanent + flexible.</p></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Flexible premiums:</strong> The owner sets the amount and frequency (within limits).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Adjustable death benefit:</strong> Can be changed without a new policy.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Investment component:</strong> Part of the premium goes to a cash value account that earns interest (current or guaranteed minimum).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Partial withdrawals</strong> are allowed (unlike whole life).</li></ul><h5>Unbundled components</h5><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Insurance</strong></td><td>Monthly mortality charge is deducted from the cash value (rises with age)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Savings</strong></td><td>Cash value account earns interest</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Expense</strong></td><td>Front-end load (at issue) or back-end load (at surrender)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The policy continues as long as the cash value covers the monthly costs. If cash value runs out, the policy terminates.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Target premium:</strong> A recommended premium to keep the policy in force. It does NOT guarantee funds for the entire duration.</li><li class=\"l0\">Face amount changes at set intervals (usually with proof of insurability).</li></ul><h5>Universal life death benefit options</h5><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Death benefit</th><th>Cash value effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Option A</strong> (level)</td><td>Stated amount (cash value + pure insurance)</td><td>As cash value grows, pure insurance shrinks. Total stays level</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Option B</strong> (increasing)</td><td>Face amount of pure insurance PLUS cash value</td><td>Death benefit grows as cash value grows</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Corridor:</strong> A minimum ratio of death benefit to cash value that federal law requires. It is added if the cash value grows too large relative to the death benefit.</li></ul>","text":"Current assumption whole life (CAWL) Also called interest-sensitive or excess interest whole life. Variable premiums based on the insurer's current assumptions (death rates, investment returns, expenses). Favorable assumptions: lower premiums or higher cash values. Unfavorable: higher premiums or a reduced face amount. Accumulation account: Premium minus expenses and mortality charges, credited with interest. Has a guaranteed minimum cash value and rate of return, and a maximum annual premium. Two types: Low-premium type (indeterminate premium) and high-premium type (high initial premium with a pay-up or vanishing-premium provision). Partial surrender provision: Lets the owner withdraw cash value. Withdrawals are generally tax-free only up to the owner's cost basis. Adjustable life Also called blended or combination policies. Merges term and permanent coverage. The owner can adjust the face amount, the premium, and the payment period. Increasing the face amount requires proof of insurability. Can convert between term and whole life, and switch between high and low premium or limited pay. Usually more expensive than standard term or whole life. Universal life (UL) Study tip: Universal life is a PERMANENT policy with a cash value account, not a term policy. Remember: UL = permanent + flexible. Flexible premiums: The owner sets the amount and frequency (within limits). Adjustable death benefit: Can be changed without a new policy. Investment component: Part of the premium goes to a cash value account that earns interest (current or guaranteed minimum). Partial withdrawals are allowed (unlike whole life). Unbundled components Element What it does Insurance Monthly mortality charge is deducted from the cash value (rises with age) Savings Cash value account earns interest Expense Front-end load (at issue) or back-end load (at surrender) The policy continues as long as the cash value covers the monthly costs. If cash value runs out, the policy terminates. Target premium: A recommended premium to keep the policy in force. It does NOT guarantee funds for the entire duration. Face amount changes at set intervals (usually with proof of insurability). Universal life death benefit options Option Death benefit Cash value effect Option A (level) Stated amount (cash value + pure insurance) As cash value grows, pure insurance shrinks. Total stays level Option B (increasing) Face amount of pure insurance PLUS cash value Death benefit grows as cash value grows Corridor: A minimum ratio of death benefit to cash value that federal law requires. It is added if the cash value grows too large relative to the death benefit."},{"id":"ch05_1-s11","title":"10. Variable and equity-indexed products","html":"<h4>Variable whole life</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Fixed premium, with part of the premium invested in separate accounts (stocks, bonds, money market funds).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cash value</strong> fluctuates with the market. The death benefit may be guaranteed as a minimum and can increase if accounts perform well.</li><li class=\"l0\">The policyowner bears ALL investment risk. No guaranteed interest rate or minimum cash value.</li></ul><h4>Variable universal life (VUL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Combines variable whole life with universal life features.</li><li class=\"l0\">Flexible premiums and a guaranteed minimum death benefit despite market swings.</li><li class=\"l0\">Expense charges are deducted from premiums.</li></ul><h4>Regulation of variable products</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Regulated by state insurance departments AND the SEC.</li><li class=\"l0\">The agent needs a life insurance license AND a FINRA registered representative license.</li><li class=\"l0\">Some states also require a special variable insurance license or addendum.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana variable products:</strong> R.S. 22:1547(A)(3) lists &quot;variable life and variable annuity&quot; as a separate line of authority.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>CRITICAL REMINDER:</strong> Variable policies are LIFE INSURANCE contracts first. Their primary purpose is the death benefit, not investment.</p></div><h4>Equity-indexed universal life (EIUL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Links the return to an equity index (for example, the S&amp;P 500).</li><li class=\"l0\">Premiums are mostly placed in fixed-income securities (the actual split varies by product).</li><li class=\"l0\">A <strong>guaranteed minimum interest rate</strong> means the cash value won't fall because of poor markets.</li><li class=\"l0\">Tax-deferred interest growth; policy loans are available.</li></ul>","text":"Variable whole life Fixed premium, with part of the premium invested in separate accounts (stocks, bonds, money market funds). Cash value fluctuates with the market. The death benefit may be guaranteed as a minimum and can increase if accounts perform well. The policyowner bears ALL investment risk. No guaranteed interest rate or minimum cash value. Variable universal life (VUL) Combines variable whole life with universal life features. Flexible premiums and a guaranteed minimum death benefit despite market swings. Expense charges are deducted from premiums. Regulation of variable products Regulated by state insurance departments AND the SEC. The agent needs a life insurance license AND a FINRA registered representative license. Some states also require a special variable insurance license or addendum. Louisiana variable products: R.S. 22:1547(A)(3) lists \"variable life and variable annuity\" as a separate line of authority. CRITICAL REMINDER: Variable policies are LIFE INSURANCE contracts first. Their primary purpose is the death benefit, not investment. Equity-indexed universal life (EIUL) Links the return to an equity index (for example, the S&P 500). Premiums are mostly placed in fixed-income securities (the actual split varies by product). A guaranteed minimum interest rate means the cash value won't fall because of poor markets. Tax-deferred interest growth; policy loans are available."},{"id":"ch05_1-s12","title":"11. Modified endowment contracts (MECs) (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> A life policy that is overfunded under IRS rules.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Background:</strong> Technical and Miscellaneous Revenue Act (TAMRA) of 1988. Its goal is to stop life insurance being used as a tax shelter.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>7-pay test:</strong> The total premiums paid during the first 7 years must not exceed the amount needed to pay up the policy in 7 annual premiums. Failing the test = MEC.</li><li class=\"l0\">Applies to policies bought after June 20, 1988.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>All single premium whole life policies are automatically MECs.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Once a MEC, always a MEC. It cannot go back to a standard life policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">If the policy changes materially (for example, a benefit increase), the 7-pay test is run again.</li></ul><h4>MEC tax treatment</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Withdrawals and loans</strong> are taxed <strong>LIFO</strong> (last in, first out): gains come out first and are taxed first.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>10% penalty</strong> if taken before age 59½.</li><li class=\"l0\">Tax applies only when cash is taken out (surrender, loan, or dividend).</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>MEC = &quot;Money Early Cost.&quot; Overfund it in the first 7 years and the tax benefits shrink.</p></div>","text":"Definition: A life policy that is overfunded under IRS rules. Background: Technical and Miscellaneous Revenue Act (TAMRA) of 1988. Its goal is to stop life insurance being used as a tax shelter. 7-pay test: The total premiums paid during the first 7 years must not exceed the amount needed to pay up the policy in 7 annual premiums. Failing the test = MEC. Applies to policies bought after June 20, 1988. All single premium whole life policies are automatically MECs. Once a MEC, always a MEC. It cannot go back to a standard life policy. If the policy changes materially (for example, a benefit increase), the 7-pay test is run again. MEC tax treatment Withdrawals and loans are taxed LIFO (last in, first out): gains come out first and are taxed first. 10% penalty if taken before age 59½. Tax applies only when cash is taken out (surrender, loan, or dividend). Memory trick: MEC = \"Money Early Cost.\" Overfund it in the first 7 years and the tax benefits shrink."},{"id":"ch05_1-s13","title":"12. Participating vs. nonparticipating","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Participating</th><th>Nonparticipating</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>YES. Shares the company's excess earnings</td><td>NO dividends</td></tr><tr><td>Dividend uses</td><td>Reduce premiums, buy more coverage, or take as cash</td><td>Not applicable</td></tr><tr><td>Premium</td><td>Can change with the dividend</td><td>Stable, not affected by performance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Participating Nonparticipating Dividends YES. Shares the company's excess earnings NO dividends Dividend uses Reduce premiums, buy more coverage, or take as cash Not applicable Premium Can change with the dividend Stable, not affected by performance"},{"id":"ch05_1-s14","title":"13. Specialized policies and features","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>AD&amp;D</strong> (Accidental death and dismemberment)</td><td>Pays if the insured dies in an accident. Also pays for loss of limb, blindness, or paralysis from a covered accident</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-medical life</strong></td><td>No medical exam. Usually costs more. Insurer prices across all applicants. Still asks about medical history and lifestyle</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Juvenile insurance</strong></td><td>Covers a child, usually under 15. An adult applies and owns the policy. Child's consent is NOT needed. Owner pays premiums until adulthood. Ownership does not transfer automatically; the owner must request it in writing. Insurable interest required only at application</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Jumping juvenile (junior estate builder)</strong></td><td>Starts as low as $1,000. The face amount can jump (usually 5 times) at age of majority or a set age (e.g., 21) with no new proof of insurability and no premium increase</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Minimum deposit</strong></td><td>A way to FINANCE a whole life policy, not a separate product. The owner borrows cash value growth to pay part of the premium. Useful in higher tax brackets</td></tr><tr><td><strong>STOLI / IOLI</strong></td><td>STOLI: buyer intends to sell the policy to a third party with no insurable interest. IOLI: investor finances and owns the policy and pays premiums. Most states prohibit these arrangements</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Feature Key facts AD&D (Accidental death and dismemberment) Pays if the insured dies in an accident. Also pays for loss of limb, blindness, or paralysis from a covered accident Non-medical life No medical exam. Usually costs more. Insurer prices across all applicants. Still asks about medical history and lifestyle Juvenile insurance Covers a child, usually under 15. An adult applies and owns the policy. Child's consent is NOT needed. Owner pays premiums until adulthood. Ownership does not transfer automatically; the owner must request it in writing. Insurable interest required only at application Jumping juvenile (junior estate builder) Starts as low as $1,000. The face amount can jump (usually 5 times) at age of majority or a set age (e.g., 21) with no new proof of insurability and no premium increase Minimum deposit A way to FINANCE a whole life policy, not a separate product. The owner borrows cash value growth to pay part of the premium. Useful in higher tax brackets STOLI / IOLI STOLI: buyer intends to sell the policy to a third party with no insurable interest. IOLI: investor finances and owns the policy and pays premiums. Most states prohibit these arrangements"},{"id":"ch05_1-s15","title":"14. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Renewable vs. convertible term</td><td>Renewable = keep term coverage, premiums go up. Convertible = switch to permanent with no proof of insurability</td></tr><tr><td>Attained-age vs. original-age conversion</td><td>Attained = current age, higher premium. Original = issue age, lower premium, may pay a difference</td></tr><tr><td>Family income vs. family maintenance</td><td>Income = payments from the policy start. Maintenance = lump sum plus payments from the date of death</td></tr><tr><td>First-to-die vs. second-to-die</td><td>First = pays at first death. Second = pays at last death (estate planning)</td></tr><tr><td>Whole life vs. universal life</td><td>Whole = fixed premium and death benefit. Universal = flexible premium and death benefit</td></tr><tr><td>Option A vs. Option B (UL)</td><td>A = level total death benefit. B = face amount plus cash value</td></tr><tr><td>Participating vs. nonparticipating</td><td>Participating gets dividends. Nonparticipating does not</td></tr><tr><td>Semi-endowment vs. pure endowment</td><td>Semi = half pays if the insured dies. Pure = nothing pays at death</td></tr><tr><td>Variable whole vs. variable universal</td><td>Variable whole = fixed premium. VUL = flexible premium</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Renewable vs. convertible term Renewable = keep term coverage, premiums go up. Convertible = switch to permanent with no proof of insurability Attained-age vs. original-age conversion Attained = current age, higher premium. Original = issue age, lower premium, may pay a difference Family income vs. family maintenance Income = payments from the policy start. Maintenance = lump sum plus payments from the date of death First-to-die vs. second-to-die First = pays at first death. Second = pays at last death (estate planning) Whole life vs. universal life Whole = fixed premium and death benefit. Universal = flexible premium and death benefit Option A vs. Option B (UL) A = level total death benefit. B = face amount plus cash value Participating vs. nonparticipating Participating gets dividends. Nonparticipating does not Semi-endowment vs. pure endowment Semi = half pays if the insured dies. Pure = nothing pays at death Variable whole vs. variable universal Variable whole = fixed premium. VUL = flexible premium"},{"id":"ch05_1-s16","title":"15. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What are the three primary categories of life insurance?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Does term insurance accrue cash value?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What are the three types of level, decreasing, and increasing term? Which one is used for a mortgage?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> What does a re-entry (revertible) term policy require at renewal?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> What is the difference between the attained-age and original-age conversion methods?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> In a family income policy, when does the income start?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Who receives the death benefit on a second-to-die policy, and when?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What is the difference between Option A and Option B in universal life?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the 7-pay test?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Are all single premium whole life policies MECs?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Ben wants the lowest possible premium for 20 years of protection while his kids are young, and he does not need cash value. Which product fits?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Carla is 40 and buys a 20-year term policy with a conversion option. At age 50, she wants permanent coverage. Her health has declined. Can she convert, and which conversion method is more expensive?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A creditor tells a borrower, &quot;You must buy credit life insurance from our affiliated agency.&quot; What is the problem?</li></ul>","text":"What are the three primary categories of life insurance? Does term insurance accrue cash value? What are the three types of level, decreasing, and increasing term? Which one is used for a mortgage? What does a re-entry (revertible) term policy require at renewal? What is the difference between the attained-age and original-age conversion methods? In a family income policy, when does the income start? Who receives the death benefit on a second-to-die policy, and when? What is the difference between Option A and Option B in universal life? What is the 7-pay test? Are all single premium whole life policies MECs? Scenario questions Scenario A: Ben wants the lowest possible premium for 20 years of protection while his kids are young, and he does not need cash value. Which product fits? Scenario B: Carla is 40 and buys a 20-year term policy with a conversion option. At age 50, she wants permanent coverage. Her health has declined. Can she convert, and which conversion method is more expensive? Scenario C: A creditor tells a borrower, \"You must buy credit life insurance from our affiliated agency.\" What is the problem?"},{"id":"ch05_1-s17","title":"16. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Ordinary, industrial, and group.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> No. Term has no cash value.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Level (stays the same), decreasing (falls to $0), increasing (grows). Decreasing term is used for mortgage protection.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Evidence of insurability (the insured may need to prove insurability to keep the lowest premium).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Attained-age: premium based on age at conversion (higher, no extra payment). Original-age: premium based on issue age (lower, may need an extra payment).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> The policy start date (not the death date).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> The beneficiary receives the death benefit when the LAST surviving insured dies.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Option A: total death benefit stays level; pure insurance shrinks as cash value grows. Option B: pure insurance stays level; total death benefit grows with cash value.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> A test of whether premiums in the first 7 years exceed the amount needed to fully pay up the policy in 7 level payments. Failing it makes the policy a MEC.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Yes.</p><p>Scenario A: Level term (20-year term), since no cash value is wanted.</p><p>Scenario B: Yes, she can convert without evidence of insurability. The attained-age method is more expensive (her current age).</p><p>Scenario C: The creditor cannot require the borrower to buy from a specific source. That is coercion and illegal.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 5.1:</strong> Build a three-column chart: Term / Whole / Universal. Fill in cash value, premium, death benefit, and who bears the investment risk. Test yourself by covering one column at a time.</p></div>","text":"Ordinary, industrial, and group. No. Term has no cash value. Level (stays the same), decreasing (falls to $0), increasing (grows). Decreasing term is used for mortgage protection. Evidence of insurability (the insured may need to prove insurability to keep the lowest premium). Attained-age: premium based on age at conversion (higher, no extra payment). Original-age: premium based on issue age (lower, may need an extra payment). The policy start date (not the death date). The beneficiary receives the death benefit when the LAST surviving insured dies. Option A: total death benefit stays level; pure insurance shrinks as cash value grows. Option B: pure insurance stays level; total death benefit grows with cash value. A test of whether premiums in the first 7 years exceed the amount needed to fully pay up the policy in 7 level payments. Failing it makes the policy a MEC. Yes. Scenario A: Level term (20-year term), since no cash value is wanted. Scenario B: Yes, she can convert without evidence of insurability. The attained-age method is more expensive (her current age). Scenario C: The creditor cannot require the borrower to buy from a specific source. That is coercion and illegal. Memory tip for Chapter 5.1: Build a three-column chart: Term / Whole / Universal. Fill in cash value, premium, death benefit, and who bears the investment risk. Test yourself by covering one column at a time."}]},{"id":"ch05_2","short":"Ch 5.2","title":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","label":"Chapter 5.2","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Three groups to master. (1) <strong>Standard provisions</strong> (the rules every policy has, such as the grace period, reinstatement, incontestable clause). (2) <strong>Options and riders</strong> (cash value options, dividend options, add-ons). (3) <strong>Exclusions</strong> (when the insurer does NOT pay). Know the key numbers in the Quick Numbers table.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch05_2-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Two years</strong> matters for many rules: the incontestable clause and the suicide exclusion.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Nonforfeiture</strong> options are what happen when premiums stop and cash value exists.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Riders</strong> add coverage for a price. <strong>Exclusions</strong> remove coverage.</li></ul>","text":"Two years matters for many rules: the incontestable clause and the suicide exclusion. Nonforfeiture options are what happen when premiums stop and cash value exists. Riders add coverage for a price. Exclusions remove coverage."},{"id":"ch05_2-s2","title":"1. Standard policy provisions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Entire contract</strong></td><td>The policy + application + riders = the whole contract. Nothing outside the documents counts. Changes must be in writing and approved by an authorized officer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Execution clause</strong></td><td>Contract is fully enacted once both sides meet their obligations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Modification</strong></td><td>Changes must be written, attached to the policy, and made by an insurer officer or authorized home-office staff. The agent does NOT need to countersign</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Privilege of change</strong></td><td>Owner's right to change coverage. Premium increase with face constant: no proof of insurability. Premium decrease or face increase: proof of insurability required (prevents adverse selection)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Insuring clause</strong></td><td>On the policy face. States the promise to pay. Signed by the company president and secretary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Consideration clause</strong></td><td>Owner gives the application and first premium; insurer promises the death benefit. Without consideration, no contract</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Incontestable clause</strong></td><td>After 2 years, the insurer cannot contest the policy (even for fraud, misstatement, or concealment)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Physical exam and autopsy</strong></td><td>Insurer may examine the insured during a claim (at its expense) and may request an autopsy to find the cause of death</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Misstatement of age or sex</strong></td><td>Benefits or premiums are adjusted to the correct facts. Policy is NOT canceled for misstatement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Suicide clause</strong></td><td>Excluded for the first 2 years. Death in that window = premiums refunded (no interest). After 2 years, fully covered</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Owner's provision</strong></td><td>Owner may change beneficiaries, manage dividends, alter premium frequency (not amount), exercise options, choose settlement options, surrender, assign, and convert or renew term</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Applicant control (ownership)</strong></td><td>If the insured is a minor, the parent or guardian controls the policy until the minor reaches the specified age</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Assignment</strong></td><td>Owner transfers rights. See the assignment types below</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Exemption of proceeds from creditors</strong></td><td>Beneficiaries and assignees have priority over the insured's creditors. Exception: transfers made to defraud creditors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Free look</strong></td><td>Right to return the policy for a full premium refund. See the free look note below</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mode of premium</strong></td><td>Premiums must be paid for coverage to stay active. Annual payment is cheapest; monthly, quarterly, and semiannual cost more (higher admin costs). Insurer may add a small charge for non-annual modes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Grace period</strong></td><td>In Louisiana, 30 days (or one month at the insurer's option) after the due date for life insurance. Coverage stays in force. If the insured dies during grace, the death benefit is paid minus the unpaid premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reinstatement</strong></td><td>Reactivates a lapsed policy. See details below</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Incontestable clause: exceptions (can still contest after 2 years)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Impersonation or identity fraud</li><li class=\"l0\">No insurable interest at application</li><li class=\"l0\">Intent to murder the insured</li><li class=\"l0\">Age or sex misstatements (benefits adjusted at any time)</li><li class=\"l0\">Nonpayment of premiums</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> The incontestable clause does NOT apply to accidental death benefits or disability provisions.</p></div><h4>Assignment types</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Absolute</strong></td><td>Complete, permanent transfer of all ownership rights</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collateral</strong></td><td>Temporary, partial transfer, usually as security for a loan</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Assignor</strong> = original owner. <strong>Assignee</strong> = new owner.</li><li class=\"l0\">No insurable interest is needed between the insured and the assignee.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must notify the insurer in writing. If an irrevocable beneficiary exists, that beneficiary must consent.</li></ul><h4>Reinstatement details</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Request within a set window that varies by state. <strong>Louisiana life policies: reinstatement is allowed within 3 years of default.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Requirements: submit an application, give proof of insurability, pay back premiums plus interest.</li><li class=\"l0\">Benefits: original premium rate and original coverage are restored.</li><li class=\"l0\">A SURRENDERED policy CANNOT be reinstated.</li></ul><h4>Free look: a Louisiana note</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The free look period starts when the owner RECEIVES the policy.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana: 10 days</strong> from receipt of the policy.</li></ul><h4>Grace period in Louisiana</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">For life insurance, the grace period is 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option.</li><li class=\"l0\">For health insurance, the grace period is 30 days from the due date.</li></ul>","text":"Provision What it does Entire contract The policy + application + riders = the whole contract. Nothing outside the documents counts. Changes must be in writing and approved by an authorized officer Execution clause Contract is fully enacted once both sides meet their obligations Modification Changes must be written, attached to the policy, and made by an insurer officer or authorized home-office staff. The agent does NOT need to countersign Privilege of change Owner's right to change coverage. Premium increase with face constant: no proof of insurability. Premium decrease or face increase: proof of insurability required (prevents adverse selection) Insuring clause On the policy face. States the promise to pay. Signed by the company president and secretary Consideration clause Owner gives the application and first premium; insurer promises the death benefit. Without consideration, no contract Incontestable clause After 2 years, the insurer cannot contest the policy (even for fraud, misstatement, or concealment) Physical exam and autopsy Insurer may examine the insured during a claim (at its expense) and may request an autopsy to find the cause of death Misstatement of age or sex Benefits or premiums are adjusted to the correct facts. Policy is NOT canceled for misstatement Suicide clause Excluded for the first 2 years. Death in that window = premiums refunded (no interest). After 2 years, fully covered Owner's provision Owner may change beneficiaries, manage dividends, alter premium frequency (not amount), exercise options, choose settlement options, surrender, assign, and convert or renew term Applicant control (ownership) If the insured is a minor, the parent or guardian controls the policy until the minor reaches the specified age Assignment Owner transfers rights. See the assignment types below Exemption of proceeds from creditors Beneficiaries and assignees have priority over the insured's creditors. Exception: transfers made to defraud creditors Free look Right to return the policy for a full premium refund. See the free look note below Mode of premium Premiums must be paid for coverage to stay active. Annual payment is cheapest; monthly, quarterly, and semiannual cost more (higher admin costs). Insurer may add a small charge for non-annual modes Grace period In Louisiana, 30 days (or one month at the insurer's option) after the due date for life insurance. Coverage stays in force. If the insured dies during grace, the death benefit is paid minus the unpaid premium Reinstatement Reactivates a lapsed policy. See details below Incontestable clause: exceptions (can still contest after 2 years) Impersonation or identity fraud No insurable interest at application Intent to murder the insured Age or sex misstatements (benefits adjusted at any time) Nonpayment of premiums Note: The incontestable clause does NOT apply to accidental death benefits or disability provisions. Assignment types Type Meaning Absolute Complete, permanent transfer of all ownership rights Collateral Temporary, partial transfer, usually as security for a loan Assignor = original owner. Assignee = new owner. No insurable interest is needed between the insured and the assignee. Must notify the insurer in writing. If an irrevocable beneficiary exists, that beneficiary must consent. Reinstatement details Request within a set window that varies by state. Louisiana life policies: reinstatement is allowed within 3 years of default. Requirements: submit an application, give proof of insurability, pay back premiums plus interest. Benefits: original premium rate and original coverage are restored. A SURRENDERED policy CANNOT be reinstated. Free look: a Louisiana note The free look period starts when the owner RECEIVES the policy. Louisiana: 10 days from receipt of the policy. Grace period in Louisiana For life insurance, the grace period is 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option. For health insurance, the grace period is 30 days from the due date."},{"id":"ch05_2-s3","title":"2. Cash value provisions","html":"<h4>Excess interest provision</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">If the insurer earns more than the guaranteed rate, the extra interest is credited to the cash value.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Index-linked method:</strong> Based on an economic indicator (for example, CPI).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Portfolio method:</strong> Based on the company's overall investment earnings.</li></ul><h4>Nonforfeiture options (when premiums stop)</h4><p>These require accumulated cash value. Once the policy is surrendered, it CANNOT be reinstated.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What happens</th><th>Key points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cash surrender value</strong></td><td>Receive the cash value minus loans; coverage ends</td><td>Insurer can delay payment up to 6 months. Early surrender charges may apply. Tax: premiums are a tax-free return; gains above premiums are taxable (cost recovery rule)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reduced paid-up insurance</strong></td><td>Smaller whole life policy, no more premiums</td><td>Lasts for life. Based on the insured's attained age. No proof of insurability. Riders and accidental death benefits do NOT carry over</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Extended term insurance</strong></td><td>Cash value buys term coverage for a set period</td><td>Same face amount (if no loans). Loans reduce both cash value and face amount. Usually the default option when a policy lapses. Gives the HIGHEST face amount of the three options</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>CASH = money in hand, coverage ends. REDUCED = smaller permanent policy. EXTENDED = same amount, for a limited time (the default).</p></div><h4>Policy loan provisions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">An advance on the policy proceeds. It is NOT a traditional loan. No credit check or proof of income.</li><li class=\"l0\">Only for permanent (whole life) policies.</li><li class=\"l0\">Maximum: cash value minus projected interest.</li><li class=\"l0\">Repayment is NOT required, but unpaid loans reduce the death benefit.</li><li class=\"l0\">If an irrevocable beneficiary exists, that beneficiary's permission is needed.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Interest:</strong> Rate stated in the policy (fixed, often 5-10%, or variable tied to Moody's corporate bond index). Due annually. Unpaid interest is added to the loan balance. If loan plus interest exceeds cash value, the policy LAPSES. Loan interest is NOT tax deductible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Taxes:</strong> Loans are NOT taxable while the policy is active. If the policy lapses or is surrendered with a loan outstanding and gains above premiums paid, the gains are taxable.</li></ul><h4>Right to defer</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The insurer may defer a policy loan or cash value payment, usually up to 6 months.</li><li class=\"l0\">Rarely used. It protects the insurer if many owners ask for cash at once.</li><li class=\"l0\">Does NOT apply to death benefit claims or automatic premium loans.</li></ul><h4>Automatic premium loan (APL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Must be elected by the owner (usually at application).</li><li class=\"l0\">Borrows from cash value to pay an overdue premium. Only for permanent policies with enough cash value.</li><li class=\"l0\">Treated like any other policy loan: interest accrues and the death benefit is reduced.</li></ul>","text":"Excess interest provision If the insurer earns more than the guaranteed rate, the extra interest is credited to the cash value. Index-linked method: Based on an economic indicator (for example, CPI). Portfolio method: Based on the company's overall investment earnings. Nonforfeiture options (when premiums stop) These require accumulated cash value. Once the policy is surrendered, it CANNOT be reinstated. Option What happens Key points Cash surrender value Receive the cash value minus loans; coverage ends Insurer can delay payment up to 6 months. Early surrender charges may apply. Tax: premiums are a tax-free return; gains above premiums are taxable (cost recovery rule) Reduced paid-up insurance Smaller whole life policy, no more premiums Lasts for life. Based on the insured's attained age. No proof of insurability. Riders and accidental death benefits do NOT carry over Extended term insurance Cash value buys term coverage for a set period Same face amount (if no loans). Loans reduce both cash value and face amount. Usually the default option when a policy lapses. Gives the HIGHEST face amount of the three options Memory trick: CASH = money in hand, coverage ends. REDUCED = smaller permanent policy. EXTENDED = same amount, for a limited time (the default). Policy loan provisions An advance on the policy proceeds. It is NOT a traditional loan. No credit check or proof of income. Only for permanent (whole life) policies. Maximum: cash value minus projected interest. Repayment is NOT required, but unpaid loans reduce the death benefit. If an irrevocable beneficiary exists, that beneficiary's permission is needed. Interest: Rate stated in the policy (fixed, often 5-10%, or variable tied to Moody's corporate bond index). Due annually. Unpaid interest is added to the loan balance. If loan plus interest exceeds cash value, the policy LAPSES. Loan interest is NOT tax deductible. Taxes: Loans are NOT taxable while the policy is active. If the policy lapses or is surrendered with a loan outstanding and gains above premiums paid, the gains are taxable. Right to defer The insurer may defer a policy loan or cash value payment, usually up to 6 months. Rarely used. It protects the insurer if many owners ask for cash at once. Does NOT apply to death benefit claims or automatic premium loans. Automatic premium loan (APL) Must be elected by the owner (usually at application). Borrows from cash value to pay an overdue premium. Only for permanent policies with enough cash value. Treated like any other policy loan: interest accrues and the death benefit is reduced."},{"id":"ch05_2-s4","title":"3. Policy dividends","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Only for <strong>participating (par)</strong> policies, usually from mutual insurers.</li><li class=\"l0\">Dividends are a return of overpaid premium, so they are <strong>NOT taxable</strong> (interest earned on them is taxable).</li><li class=\"l0\">NOT guaranteed. The policy must say so.</li><li class=\"l0\">Usually paid after the first or second policy year, on anniversary dates.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Producers cannot guarantee dividends</strong> to applicants.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dividend option</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cash</strong></td><td>Direct payment; tax-exempt</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reduce or suspend premiums</strong></td><td>Applied to the next premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Paid-up permanent additions (PUA)</strong></td><td>Buys more whole life with no proof of insurability. Increases death benefit AND cash value. <strong>Maximizes total death coverage</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>One-year term</strong></td><td>Buys term coverage up to the base policy's cash value. May need proof of insurability if added later</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accumulate at interest</strong></td><td>Left with insurer to earn interest. Dividends stay tax-free; the interest is taxable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Paid-up policy</strong></td><td>Used to pay up the policy early. Face stays the same</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Only for participating (par) policies, usually from mutual insurers. Dividends are a return of overpaid premium, so they are NOT taxable (interest earned on them is taxable). NOT guaranteed. The policy must say so. Usually paid after the first or second policy year, on anniversary dates. Producers cannot guarantee dividends to applicants. Dividend option What happens Cash Direct payment; tax-exempt Reduce or suspend premiums Applied to the next premium Paid-up permanent additions (PUA) Buys more whole life with no proof of insurability. Increases death benefit AND cash value. Maximizes total death coverage One-year term Buys term coverage up to the base policy's cash value. May need proof of insurability if added later Accumulate at interest Left with insurer to earn interest. Dividends stay tax-free; the interest is taxable Paid-up policy Used to pay up the policy early. Face stays the same"},{"id":"ch05_2-s5","title":"4. Riders (add-ons; usually extra premium)","html":"<h4>Disability riders</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rider</th><th>What it does</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Waiver of premium</strong></td><td>Waives premiums if the insured is totally disabled for 6+ months</td><td>Must be requested; extra premium. Expires at age 60-65 (disabilities that start before the cutoff continue). 6-month waiting period, with premiums refunded after it. Excludes war, self-inflicted injury, and criminal acts. Provides NO income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Waiver of cost of insurance</strong></td><td>Waives the monthly cost of insurance</td><td>Mainly for universal life. Cash value stays level and keeps earning interest</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disability income benefit</strong></td><td>Monthly income if totally and permanently disabled</td><td>Typically 1% of face per month (max $1,000/month). 6-month waiting period. Pays income, not just premiums</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Accidental death riders</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rider</th><th>What it does</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Accidental death benefit (ADB)</strong></td><td>Pays 2x (double indemnity) or 3x (triple indemnity) the face amount</td><td>Death must occur within 90 days of the accident. Must be purely accidental. Excludes self-inflicted injury, war, riots, and private aviation. Lowest-cost rider. Available to about age 55-60; expires at 60-65</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AD&amp;D</strong> (accidental death and dismemberment)</td><td>Principal sum: full benefit for accidental death</td><td>Capital sum: typically one-half of the principal sum for dismemberment (loss of limb, use, sight, or hearing). May pay the principal sum for combination losses (for example, a hand and a foot)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Coverage increase riders</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rider</th><th>What it does</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Guaranteed insurability option (GIO)</strong></td><td>Buy more coverage on set dates WITHOUT a medical exam</td><td>Also called insurance protection rider (IPR) or future increase option (FIO). May allow purchases at life events (&quot;stork provision&quot; for birth). Options do NOT roll over (use it or lose it). Premium based on attained age at purchase</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cost of living (COL / COLA)</strong></td><td>Face amount rises with CPI</td><td>A CPI decrease does NOT reduce the face amount. No proof of insurability. Premium rises with coverage. Owner may decline an increase</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Payor rider</strong></td><td>Waives premiums if the payor (parent or guardian) dies or becomes totally disabled</td><td>Covers the child until age 18, 21, or 25. Covers only premiums, not income or a death benefit</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Term insurance riders</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Give temporary extra coverage on a PERMANENT policy (not on term).</li><li class=\"l0\">Cannot run past the base policy's premium-paying period.</li><li class=\"l0\">Dropped if the policy lapses or a nonforfeiture option is used.</li><li class=\"l0\">Do NOT affect cash value or dividends.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Types:</strong> Level, decreasing (often for mortgage coverage), increasing.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Return of premium rider:</strong> At death, pays face amount PLUS all premiums paid.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Return of cash value rider:</strong> At death, pays face amount PLUS cash value.</li></ul><h4>Family coverage riders</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Family (term) rider:</strong> Covers the entire family except the primary insured.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Spousal (term) rider:</strong> Spouse only.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Children's (term) rider:</strong> Children only.</li></ul><h4>Exchange privilege (substitute insured) rider</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Lets the insured person be changed on a policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">Typically used for business policies on key employees or executives.</li><li class=\"l0\">Face amount stays the same. Premiums are recalculated for the new insured's age, sex, and insurability.</li></ul>","text":"Disability riders Rider What it does Key facts Waiver of premium Waives premiums if the insured is totally disabled for 6+ months Must be requested; extra premium. Expires at age 60-65 (disabilities that start before the cutoff continue). 6-month waiting period, with premiums refunded after it. Excludes war, self-inflicted injury, and criminal acts. Provides NO income Waiver of cost of insurance Waives the monthly cost of insurance Mainly for universal life. Cash value stays level and keeps earning interest Disability income benefit Monthly income if totally and permanently disabled Typically 1% of face per month (max $1,000/month). 6-month waiting period. Pays income, not just premiums Accidental death riders Rider What it does Key facts Accidental death benefit (ADB) Pays 2x (double indemnity) or 3x (triple indemnity) the face amount Death must occur within 90 days of the accident. Must be purely accidental. Excludes self-inflicted injury, war, riots, and private aviation. Lowest-cost rider. Available to about age 55-60; expires at 60-65 AD&D (accidental death and dismemberment) Principal sum: full benefit for accidental death Capital sum: typically one-half of the principal sum for dismemberment (loss of limb, use, sight, or hearing). May pay the principal sum for combination losses (for example, a hand and a foot) Coverage increase riders Rider What it does Key facts Guaranteed insurability option (GIO) Buy more coverage on set dates WITHOUT a medical exam Also called insurance protection rider (IPR) or future increase option (FIO). May allow purchases at life events (\"stork provision\" for birth). Options do NOT roll over (use it or lose it). Premium based on attained age at purchase Cost of living (COL / COLA) Face amount rises with CPI A CPI decrease does NOT reduce the face amount. No proof of insurability. Premium rises with coverage. Owner may decline an increase Payor rider Waives premiums if the payor (parent or guardian) dies or becomes totally disabled Covers the child until age 18, 21, or 25. Covers only premiums, not income or a death benefit Term insurance riders Give temporary extra coverage on a PERMANENT policy (not on term). Cannot run past the base policy's premium-paying period. Dropped if the policy lapses or a nonforfeiture option is used. Do NOT affect cash value or dividends. Types: Level, decreasing (often for mortgage coverage), increasing. Return of premium rider: At death, pays face amount PLUS all premiums paid. Return of cash value rider: At death, pays face amount PLUS cash value. Family coverage riders Family (term) rider: Covers the entire family except the primary insured. Spousal (term) rider: Spouse only. Children's (term) rider: Children only. Exchange privilege (substitute insured) rider Lets the insured person be changed on a policy. Typically used for business policies on key employees or executives. Face amount stays the same. Premiums are recalculated for the new insured's age, sex, and insurability."},{"id":"ch05_2-s6","title":"5. Living benefits and settlement options","html":"<h4>Living benefits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Give the owner access to part of the death benefit while the insured is alive.</li><li class=\"l0\">The insurer requires a physician's certification of a qualifying condition.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Accelerated benefits</strong></td><td>Terminal illness, usually expected to cause death within 12 to 24 months. Percentage of the death benefit varies by company. The amount paid is subtracted from the face amount. Lump sum or monthly installments. Generally tax-free for the terminally ill. Usually offered at no extra premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Catastrophic illness coverage</strong></td><td>Pays for listed diseases such as cancer, heart disease, renal failure, and stroke</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Long-term care rider</strong></td><td>Pays when the insured cannot perform at least 2 of the 6 Activities of Daily Living (ADLs): bathing, continence, dressing, eating, toileting, and transferring (moving in and out of bed or a chair). The insured is also confined. Generally tax-free. Two designs: <strong>generalized (independent)</strong>, which does not change the base policy, and <strong>integrated</strong>, which reduces the base face amount or cash value</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Beneficiary designations</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The owner designates the beneficiary who receives the death benefit. Can be set at application or changed later, unless the designation is irrevocable.</li><li class=\"l0\">The beneficiary does NOT need to sign the application or be notified.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Revocable:</strong> The owner can change it at any time without consent.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Irrevocable:</strong> Cannot be changed without the beneficiary's consent.</li></ul><h4>Settlement options</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Specify how the death benefit is paid.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Lump sum:</strong> Principal method. The entire proceeds paid at once, generally income-tax-free.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Installments or interest-only:</strong> If the beneficiary (or owner) chooses another option, the proceeds stay with the insurer. The insurer pays interest on the retained proceeds, and that interest is taxable as ordinary income.</li><li class=\"l0\">The owner may choose at application, or the beneficiary may choose after death.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Spendthrift clause:</strong> Lets the owner protect the proceeds from being squandered. The owner sets the settlement option at application, so funds are used as intended.</li></ul>","text":"Living benefits Give the owner access to part of the death benefit while the insured is alive. The insurer requires a physician's certification of a qualifying condition. Benefit Key facts Accelerated benefits Terminal illness, usually expected to cause death within 12 to 24 months. Percentage of the death benefit varies by company. The amount paid is subtracted from the face amount. Lump sum or monthly installments. Generally tax-free for the terminally ill. Usually offered at no extra premium Catastrophic illness coverage Pays for listed diseases such as cancer, heart disease, renal failure, and stroke Long-term care rider Pays when the insured cannot perform at least 2 of the 6 Activities of Daily Living (ADLs): bathing, continence, dressing, eating, toileting, and transferring (moving in and out of bed or a chair). The insured is also confined. Generally tax-free. Two designs: generalized (independent), which does not change the base policy, and integrated, which reduces the base face amount or cash value Beneficiary designations The owner designates the beneficiary who receives the death benefit. Can be set at application or changed later, unless the designation is irrevocable. The beneficiary does NOT need to sign the application or be notified. Revocable: The owner can change it at any time without consent. Irrevocable: Cannot be changed without the beneficiary's consent. Settlement options Specify how the death benefit is paid. Lump sum: Principal method. The entire proceeds paid at once, generally income-tax-free. Installments or interest-only: If the beneficiary (or owner) chooses another option, the proceeds stay with the insurer. The insurer pays interest on the retained proceeds, and that interest is taxable as ordinary income. The owner may choose at application, or the beneficiary may choose after death. Spendthrift clause: Lets the owner protect the proceeds from being squandered. The owner sets the settlement option at application, so funds are used as intended."},{"id":"ch05_2-s7","title":"6. Policy exclusions (when the insurer does NOT pay)","html":"<h4>Common exclusions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Exclusion</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Suicide</strong></td><td>Excluded for the first 2 years. Only premiums are refunded. After the period, fully covered. This exclusion &quot;falls off&quot;; the other exclusions below remain for the life of the policy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>War</strong></td><td>Covers declared and undeclared wars. <strong>Status clause:</strong> no coverage while in the military, even on furlough. <strong>Results clause:</strong> no coverage if death results from military activity; covered on furlough</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aviation</strong></td><td>Commercial passengers, crew, and pilots are usually covered. Excluded: stunt, test, student, and flight-instructor flying, and agricultural aviation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hazardous occupations, hobbies, avocations</strong></td><td>Insurers may exclude these activities. Today most insurers charge an extra premium (rate-up) instead. An exclusion, if written in, lasts for the policy's life</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Change of occupation</strong></td><td>If the insured moves to a more hazardous job, the insurer can reduce the maximum benefit. A less hazardous job may lower the premium</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Other exclusions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Exclusion</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Commission of a felony</strong></td><td>Excludes death or injury from the insured's own felony. Victims and innocent bystanders are still covered</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Illegal occupation</strong></td><td>Insurer not responsible for losses while the insured is engaged in a felony or illegal occupation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Intoxicants and narcotics</strong></td><td>Claims are typically denied if the insured was intoxicated or under narcotics at the time of loss</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Self-inflicted injuries</strong></td><td>Not covered if the insured intentionally harmed themselves to collect insurance. Intent to collect is the key factor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Foreign travel or residence</strong></td><td>Not universally excluded. Extended stays overseas or residence in a foreign country may end benefits</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Impairment rider</strong></td><td>Waives coverage for a specific condition the insured has. If the condition improves, the insurer may remove the waiver and restore full coverage for it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Common exclusions Exclusion Key facts Suicide Excluded for the first 2 years. Only premiums are refunded. After the period, fully covered. This exclusion \"falls off\"; the other exclusions below remain for the life of the policy War Covers declared and undeclared wars. Status clause: no coverage while in the military, even on furlough. Results clause: no coverage if death results from military activity; covered on furlough Aviation Commercial passengers, crew, and pilots are usually covered. Excluded: stunt, test, student, and flight-instructor flying, and agricultural aviation Hazardous occupations, hobbies, avocations Insurers may exclude these activities. Today most insurers charge an extra premium (rate-up) instead. An exclusion, if written in, lasts for the policy's life Change of occupation If the insured moves to a more hazardous job, the insurer can reduce the maximum benefit. A less hazardous job may lower the premium Other exclusions Exclusion Key facts Commission of a felony Excludes death or injury from the insured's own felony. Victims and innocent bystanders are still covered Illegal occupation Insurer not responsible for losses while the insured is engaged in a felony or illegal occupation Intoxicants and narcotics Claims are typically denied if the insured was intoxicated or under narcotics at the time of loss Self-inflicted injuries Not covered if the insured intentionally harmed themselves to collect insurance. Intent to collect is the key factor Foreign travel or residence Not universally excluded. Extended stays overseas or residence in a foreign country may end benefits Impairment rider Waives coverage for a specific condition the insured has. If the condition improves, the insurer may remove the waiver and restore full coverage for it"},{"id":"ch05_2-s8","title":"7. Quick numbers (memorize)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incontestable period</td><td>Life: 2 years. Health and accident: 3 years</td></tr><tr><td>Suicide exclusion</td><td>2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Grace period</td><td>Life: 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option. Health: 30 days</td></tr><tr><td>Reinstatement window</td><td>Life: within 3 years of default. Health: accepting the defaulted premium reinstates; sickness is covered again after 10 days</td></tr><tr><td>Free look</td><td>10 days from receipt of the policy</td></tr><tr><td>ADB death window</td><td>Within 90 days of the accident</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term care trigger</td><td>2 of the 6 ADLs</td></tr><tr><td>Insurer may defer payment</td><td>Up to 6 months</td></tr><tr><td>Waiver of premium wait</td><td>6 months</td></tr><tr><td>Disability income rider</td><td>1% of face per month, max $1,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Item Number Incontestable period Life: 2 years. Health and accident: 3 years Suicide exclusion 2 years Grace period Life: 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option. Health: 30 days Reinstatement window Life: within 3 years of default. Health: accepting the defaulted premium reinstates; sickness is covered again after 10 days Free look 10 days from receipt of the policy ADB death window Within 90 days of the accident Long-term care trigger 2 of the 6 ADLs Insurer may defer payment Up to 6 months Waiver of premium wait 6 months Disability income rider 1% of face per month, max $1,000"},{"id":"ch05_2-s9","title":"8. Critical concepts and vocabulary","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Extended term = highest face amount</strong> of the nonforfeiture options.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Paid-up additions = maximizes death coverage.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Policy loans are NOT taxable while the policy is active.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Dividends are NOT taxable. Interest on accumulated dividends IS taxable.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Principal sum = accidental death benefit. Capital sum = dismemberment benefit.</strong></li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assignor / assignee</td><td>Original owner / new owner</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute / collateral assignment</td><td>Full transfer / temporary (for loans)</td></tr><tr><td>Attained age</td><td>The insured's current age</td></tr><tr><td>Contestable period</td><td>First 2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Par / non-par</td><td>Participating (pays dividends) / nonparticipating (no dividends)</td></tr><tr><td>PUA</td><td>Paid-up additions</td></tr><tr><td>GIO</td><td>Guaranteed insurability option</td></tr><tr><td>COL / COLA</td><td>Cost of living (adjustment)</td></tr><tr><td>ADL</td><td>Activity of daily living</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Extended term = highest face amount of the nonforfeiture options. Paid-up additions = maximizes death coverage. Policy loans are NOT taxable while the policy is active. Dividends are NOT taxable. Interest on accumulated dividends IS taxable. Principal sum = accidental death benefit. Capital sum = dismemberment benefit. Term Meaning Assignor / assignee Original owner / new owner Absolute / collateral assignment Full transfer / temporary (for loans) Attained age The insured's current age Contestable period First 2 years Par / non-par Participating (pays dividends) / nonparticipating (no dividends) PUA Paid-up additions GIO Guaranteed insurability option COL / COLA Cost of living (adjustment) ADL Activity of daily living"},{"id":"ch05_2-s10","title":"9. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Waiver of premium vs. disability income rider</td><td>Waiver = premiums waived (no income). Disability income = monthly income paid</td></tr><tr><td>Double vs. triple indemnity</td><td>Double = 2x face. Triple = 3x face</td></tr><tr><td>Principal sum vs. capital sum</td><td>Principal = full accidental death. Capital = dismemberment (usually half)</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute vs. collateral assignment</td><td>Absolute = permanent full transfer. Collateral = temporary, for a loan</td></tr><tr><td>Revocable vs. irrevocable beneficiary</td><td>Revocable = owner can change anytime. Irrevocable = needs beneficiary consent</td></tr><tr><td>Status vs. results war clause</td><td>Status = no coverage while in the military. Results = no coverage only if death results from military activity</td></tr><tr><td>Suicide exclusion vs. other exclusions</td><td>Suicide &quot;falls off&quot; after 2 years. Other exclusions last the life of the policy</td></tr><tr><td>Cash surrender vs. reduced paid-up vs. extended term</td><td>Cash = money, coverage ends. Reduced = smaller permanent policy. Extended = same amount, limited time</td></tr><tr><td>Accelerated benefits vs. life settlement</td><td>Accelerated = insured gets part of the death benefit while alive (terminal illness). Settlement = a third party buys the policy</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Waiver of premium vs. disability income rider Waiver = premiums waived (no income). Disability income = monthly income paid Double vs. triple indemnity Double = 2x face. Triple = 3x face Principal sum vs. capital sum Principal = full accidental death. Capital = dismemberment (usually half) Absolute vs. collateral assignment Absolute = permanent full transfer. Collateral = temporary, for a loan Revocable vs. irrevocable beneficiary Revocable = owner can change anytime. Irrevocable = needs beneficiary consent Status vs. results war clause Status = no coverage while in the military. Results = no coverage only if death results from military activity Suicide exclusion vs. other exclusions Suicide \"falls off\" after 2 years. Other exclusions last the life of the policy Cash surrender vs. reduced paid-up vs. extended term Cash = money, coverage ends. Reduced = smaller permanent policy. Extended = same amount, limited time Accelerated benefits vs. life settlement Accelerated = insured gets part of the death benefit while alive (terminal illness). Settlement = a third party buys the policy"},{"id":"ch05_2-s11","title":"10. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> How long is the incontestable period, and what are its main exceptions?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> What happens to premiums if the insured dies by suicide in month 18?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Which nonforfeiture option gives the highest face amount?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> What is the difference between a waiver of premium rider and a disability income benefit rider?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> How is an accidental death benefit different from double indemnity?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Are dividends taxable? Is the interest on accumulated dividends taxable?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Can a policy loan be repaid? What happens if the loan plus interest exceeds the cash value?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> In a settlement option, who pays interest on retained proceeds, and is it taxable?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the difference between a status and a results war clause?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Which type of assignment is temporary?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Tom's policy lapses after he stops paying. He has cash value and wants to keep the original face amount for as long as possible, without more premiums. Which nonforfeiture option? (Hint: &quot;same amount, limited time.&quot;)</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Lisa's husband is injured in a car accident and dies 120 days later. The policy has an ADB rider. Does the rider pay double?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> Kevin's life insurance policy names his daughter as beneficiary. He wants to be able to change the beneficiary later without asking her. Is the designation revocable or irrevocable?</li></ul>","text":"How long is the incontestable period, and what are its main exceptions? What happens to premiums if the insured dies by suicide in month 18? Which nonforfeiture option gives the highest face amount? What is the difference between a waiver of premium rider and a disability income benefit rider? How is an accidental death benefit different from double indemnity? Are dividends taxable? Is the interest on accumulated dividends taxable? Can a policy loan be repaid? What happens if the loan plus interest exceeds the cash value? In a settlement option, who pays interest on retained proceeds, and is it taxable? What is the difference between a status and a results war clause? Which type of assignment is temporary? Scenario questions Scenario A: Tom's policy lapses after he stops paying. He has cash value and wants to keep the original face amount for as long as possible, without more premiums. Which nonforfeiture option? (Hint: \"same amount, limited time.\") Scenario B: Lisa's husband is injured in a car accident and dies 120 days later. The policy has an ADB rider. Does the rider pay double? Scenario C: Kevin's life insurance policy names his daughter as beneficiary. He wants to be able to change the beneficiary later without asking her. Is the designation revocable or irrevocable?"},{"id":"ch05_2-s12","title":"11. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Two years. Exceptions: impersonation or identity fraud, no insurable interest at application, intent to murder the insured, age or sex misstatement, and nonpayment of premiums.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Premiums are refunded (no interest). No death benefit, because the suicide exclusion applies in the first 2 years.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Extended term insurance.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Waiver of premium waives the premiums and gives no income. Disability income pays a monthly income.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> ADB is a general term for the accidental-death rider. Double indemnity specifically pays 2x the face amount.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Dividends are NOT taxable (return of overpaid premium). Interest on accumulated dividends IS taxable.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Yes. Repayment is not required, but unpaid loans reduce the death benefit. If loan plus interest exceeds cash value, the policy lapses.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> The insurer pays interest on the proceeds it holds under an installment settlement. That interest is taxable as ordinary income.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Status: no coverage while in military service. Results: no coverage only if death results from military activity.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> A collateral assignment.</p><p>Scenario A: Extended term insurance (same face amount, limited period).</p><p>Scenario B: No. The ADB requires death within 90 days of the accident. Death at 120 days is outside the window, so the rider does not pay double. The ordinary death benefit is still paid.</p><p>Scenario C: Revocable. The owner can change the beneficiary at any time without consent.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick for Chapter 5.2:</strong> Make three cards: (1) Provisions with numbers (2 years, 30 days, 10 days free look, 6 months deferral); (2) Nonforfeiture and dividend options; (3) Exclusions. Recite each card before bed for a week.</p></div>","text":"Two years. Exceptions: impersonation or identity fraud, no insurable interest at application, intent to murder the insured, age or sex misstatement, and nonpayment of premiums. Premiums are refunded (no interest). No death benefit, because the suicide exclusion applies in the first 2 years. Extended term insurance. Waiver of premium waives the premiums and gives no income. Disability income pays a monthly income. ADB is a general term for the accidental-death rider. Double indemnity specifically pays 2x the face amount. Dividends are NOT taxable (return of overpaid premium). Interest on accumulated dividends IS taxable. Yes. Repayment is not required, but unpaid loans reduce the death benefit. If loan plus interest exceeds cash value, the policy lapses. The insurer pays interest on the proceeds it holds under an installment settlement. That interest is taxable as ordinary income. Status: no coverage while in military service. Results: no coverage only if death results from military activity. A collateral assignment. Scenario A: Extended term insurance (same face amount, limited period). Scenario B: No. The ADB requires death within 90 days of the accident. Death at 120 days is outside the window, so the rider does not pay double. The ordinary death benefit is still paid. Scenario C: Revocable. The owner can change the beneficiary at any time without consent. Memory trick for Chapter 5.2: Make three cards: (1) Provisions with numbers (2 years, 30 days, 10 days free look, 6 months deferral); (2) Nonforfeiture and dividend options; (3) Exclusions. Recite each card before bed for a week."}]},{"id":"ch06","short":"Ch 6","title":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","label":"Chapter 6","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Group life = one master contract (employer = contract holder/owner), many certificate holders (employees). Know the participation rules, the conversion rules, the tax thresholds, and the special groups (SGLI, FSGLI, VGLI, FEGLI, franchise, credit, blanket).</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch06-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employer owns the master policy. Employee holds a certificate.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Underwriting looks at the group</strong>, not each person. Large groups usually skip proof of insurability.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Conversion</strong> lets a leaving employee turn group term into an individual policy within <strong>31 days</strong>, without proof of insurability.</li></ul>","text":"Employer owns the master policy. Employee holds a certificate. Underwriting looks at the group, not each person. Large groups usually skip proof of insurability. Conversion lets a leaving employee turn group term into an individual policy within 31 days, without proof of insurability."},{"id":"ch06-s2","title":"1. Group vs. individual insurance","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Group</th><th>Individual</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Two-party contract: employer (policyholder/contract holder) and insurer</td><td>Insurer and the insured/owner</td></tr><tr><td>Document</td><td>Master policy held by the employer; employees get a <strong>certificate</strong> (booklet)</td><td>Policy issued to the owner</td></tr><tr><td>Coverage type</td><td>Annually renewable term (temporary coverage)</td><td>Term or permanent</td></tr><tr><td>Underwriting</td><td>The GROUP is assessed; individuals usually not underwritten</td><td>Each person must prove insurability</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Lower (lower admin costs); employer may pay all or part</td><td>Higher (underwriting, commissions, billing); individual pays all</td></tr><tr><td>Ownership</td><td>Employer owns the master policy; employee is the certificate holder</td><td>Insured usually owns the policy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Employer responsibilities</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Select the group coverages.</li><li class=\"l0\">Keep records of coverage and participants.</li><li class=\"l0\">Enroll employees.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Avoid discrimination</strong>, especially in noncontributory plans.</li></ul>","text":"Feature Group Individual Structure Two-party contract: employer (policyholder/contract holder) and insurer Insurer and the insured/owner Document Master policy held by the employer; employees get a certificate (booklet) Policy issued to the owner Coverage type Annually renewable term (temporary coverage) Term or permanent Underwriting The GROUP is assessed; individuals usually not underwritten Each person must prove insurability Cost Lower (lower admin costs); employer may pay all or part Higher (underwriting, commissions, billing); individual pays all Ownership Employer owns the master policy; employee is the certificate holder Insured usually owns the policy Employer responsibilities Select the group coverages. Keep records of coverage and participants. Enroll employees. Avoid discrimination, especially in noncontributory plans."},{"id":"ch06-s3","title":"2. Contributory vs. noncontributory plans (CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>NONCONTRIBUTORY</th><th>CONTRIBUTORY</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who pays</td><td>Employer pays 100%</td><td>Employer and employees share the cost</td></tr><tr><td>Employee contribution</td><td>None</td><td>Usually payroll deduction</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum participation required</td><td><strong>100%</strong> of eligible employees</td><td><strong>75%</strong> of eligible employees</td></tr><tr><td>Why the rule exists</td><td>Everyone included, so less adverse selection</td><td>Insurer needs enough participation to limit adverse selection</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>NON-contributory = NO employee pays = 100% must join. CONtributory = CONTRIBUTE = 75% is enough.</p></div>","text":"NONCONTRIBUTORY CONTRIBUTORY Who pays Employer pays 100% Employer and employees share the cost Employee contribution None Usually payroll deduction Minimum participation required 100% of eligible employees 75% of eligible employees Why the rule exists Everyone included, so less adverse selection Insurer needs enough participation to limit adverse selection Memory trick: NON-contributory = NO employee pays = 100% must join. CONtributory = CONTRIBUTE = 75% is enough."},{"id":"ch06-s4","title":"3. Eligible groups and eligible employees","html":"<h4>Eligible groups</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A group CANNOT be formed just to get insurance. Insurance must be a <strong>benefit</strong>, not the reason the group exists.</li><li class=\"l0\">Businesses that make products or provide services are eligible.</li><li class=\"l0\">Individuals in the same industry may form an association and then buy group coverage.</li><li class=\"l0\">An employment or professional relationship is usually required, but not always.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>States</strong> may require the group to exist for 2+ years or have a minimum number of members.</li></ul><p>Eligible group types: single employer; multiple employer; labor unions; credit or debit groups; fraternal organizations; trade associations; customer groups (such as credit union members); trustee groups (2 or more employers or unions).</p><h4>Employee eligibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Must be full-time and actively working.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Probationary period:</strong> 1 to 6 months for new employees.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Enrollment period:</strong> 31 days to sign up.</li><li class=\"l0\">Missing the enrollment period may require <strong>evidence of insurability</strong> (proof of insurability).</li><li class=\"l0\">Contributory plans: the employee must consent to payroll deductions.</li></ul>","text":"Eligible groups A group CANNOT be formed just to get insurance. Insurance must be a benefit, not the reason the group exists. Businesses that make products or provide services are eligible. Individuals in the same industry may form an association and then buy group coverage. An employment or professional relationship is usually required, but not always. States may require the group to exist for 2+ years or have a minimum number of members. Eligible group types: single employer; multiple employer; labor unions; credit or debit groups; fraternal organizations; trade associations; customer groups (such as credit union members); trustee groups (2 or more employers or unions). Employee eligibility Must be full-time and actively working. Probationary period: 1 to 6 months for new employees. Enrollment period: 31 days to sign up. Missing the enrollment period may require evidence of insurability (proof of insurability). Contributory plans: the employee must consent to payroll deductions."},{"id":"ch06-s5","title":"4. Underwriting group life","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Goal:</strong> Control adverse selection (also called anti-selection).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Adverse selection risk:</strong> People in poorer health are more likely to buy coverage. If the insurer approves too many high-risk people, the plan loses money.</li><li class=\"l0\">Insurers require a <strong>minimum participation</strong> so that acceptable risks balance the bad risks.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Large groups:</strong> Proof of insurability usually NOT required. The law of large numbers helps.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Small groups:</strong> May require some proof of insurability, because adverse selection is a bigger risk.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Physical impairments do NOT exclude</strong> an employee from a group plan.</li></ul><h4>Persistency</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Persistency</strong> = the share of policies that stay in force over time.</li><li class=\"l0\">Measured at 1, 3, or 5 years after issue.</li><li class=\"l0\">Insurers avoid groups that switch insurers often. Lapses and replacements cost the insurer money to acquire and issue new policies.</li></ul><h4>Risk classification (group version)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Premium</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Preferred</strong></td><td>Low risk; excellent health; healthy lifestyle</td><td>Lower</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Standard</strong></td><td>Average risk; normal life expectancy</td><td>Standard</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Substandard (rated up)</strong></td><td>High risk; health problems or pre-existing conditions</td><td>Higher</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Declined</strong></td><td>Not insurable</td><td>No coverage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Goal: Control adverse selection (also called anti-selection). Adverse selection risk: People in poorer health are more likely to buy coverage. If the insurer approves too many high-risk people, the plan loses money. Insurers require a minimum participation so that acceptable risks balance the bad risks. Large groups: Proof of insurability usually NOT required. The law of large numbers helps. Small groups: May require some proof of insurability, because adverse selection is a bigger risk. Physical impairments do NOT exclude an employee from a group plan. Persistency Persistency = the share of policies that stay in force over time. Measured at 1, 3, or 5 years after issue. Insurers avoid groups that switch insurers often. Lapses and replacements cost the insurer money to acquire and issue new policies. Risk classification (group version) Class Meaning Premium Preferred Low risk; excellent health; healthy lifestyle Lower Standard Average risk; normal life expectancy Standard Substandard (rated up) High risk; health problems or pre-existing conditions Higher Declined Not insurable No coverage"},{"id":"ch06-s6","title":"5. Conversion privileges (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>ALL group life policies</strong> include a conversion privilege.</li><li class=\"l0\">It lets an employee who leaves (layoff or voluntary) convert group term to an individual policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">Most insurers limit conversion to an <strong>individual whole life</strong> policy.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Conversion period:</strong> <strong>31 days</strong> after termination.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>No proof of insurability</strong> is required.</li><li class=\"l0\">If the employee dies during the 31 days, the death benefit is paid under the group policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">Coverage continues during the conversion period, even if the employee does not intend to convert.</li><li class=\"l0\">New premium is usually based on the <strong>attained age</strong> (current age). The original-age method is also possible.</li><li class=\"l0\">Employees AND dependents may convert.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Master policy terminated:</strong> Members insured for <strong>5 or more years</strong> may convert. The new coverage is up to the face value of the original group policy.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>31 days for enrollment, 31 days for conversion. Both are &quot;one month and a day&quot; windows.</p></div>","text":"ALL group life policies include a conversion privilege. It lets an employee who leaves (layoff or voluntary) convert group term to an individual policy. Most insurers limit conversion to an individual whole life policy. Conversion period: 31 days after termination. No proof of insurability is required. If the employee dies during the 31 days, the death benefit is paid under the group policy. Coverage continues during the conversion period, even if the employee does not intend to convert. New premium is usually based on the attained age (current age). The original-age method is also possible. Employees AND dependents may convert. Master policy terminated: Members insured for 5 or more years may convert. The new coverage is up to the face value of the original group policy. Memory trick: 31 days for enrollment, 31 days for conversion. Both are \"one month and a day\" windows."},{"id":"ch06-s7","title":"6. Beneficiary designation and ownership","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employer</strong> = contract holder (owns the master policy; keeps most ownership rights).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employee</strong> = certificate holder. The employee has the <strong>incident of ownership</strong>, which is the right to name and change the beneficiary.</li><li class=\"l0\">An employee may name the employer as beneficiary ONLY if the employer has an insurable interest.</li></ul>","text":"Employer = contract holder (owns the master policy; keeps most ownership rights). Employee = certificate holder. The employee has the incident of ownership, which is the right to name and change the beneficiary. An employee may name the employer as beneficiary ONLY if the employer has an insurable interest."},{"id":"ch06-s8","title":"7. Special types of group life","html":"<h4>Military and federal programs</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SGLI</strong> (Servicemembers' Group Life Insurance)</td><td>Group term life for full-time service members, available in set increments up to the program maximum. Automatic unless the member opts out</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FSGLI</strong> (Family SGLI)</td><td>Spouse: automatically covered for the lesser of $100,000 or the member's SGLI amount. Premium depends on the spouse's age and the coverage amount. Children: $10,000 each at no cost</td></tr><tr><td><strong>VGLI</strong> (Veterans' Group Life)</td><td>Lets a separated service member keep coverage as renewable term. No proof of insurability</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FEGLI</strong> (Federal Employees Group Life)</td><td>Group term life for federal employees and civil service workers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Other special groups</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Franchise life</strong></td><td>For employees of a common employer or association members. Each participant gets an INDIVIDUAL policy (not a certificate). Uniform provisions, but benefits may vary. Often used for small groups that do not meet the state's minimums for regular group insurance. Also called wholesale insurance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Credit life and credit disability</strong></td><td>Help a borrower repay a loan if they die (lump sum to the creditor) or become disabled (monthly benefits equal to loan payments). Set up by banks and finance companies. Premiums are based on claims experience and expenses, NOT on the borrower's age. Usually decreasing term that matches the loan balance. The insured usually pays</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Blanket life</strong></td><td>Covers a group exposed to the same hazard (for example, airline passengers). NO specific individuals named, NO certificates issued</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Blanket health</strong></td><td>Covers groups with constantly changing membership (for example, students at a school or airline passengers). No individual certificates</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (blanket):</strong> BLANKET = a whole group under one cover, no names. Think &quot;a blanket covers everyone under it; you don't need your name on it.&quot;</p></div><h4>Retired lives reserve (RLR) and qualified retirement plans</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>RLR:</strong> Provides life insurance after retirement. It is annually renewable term. A reserve account is built before retirement and used to pay premiums afterward. Employer contributions are tax-deductible for the employer, not for the employee. A life insurer or a trust may manage the fund.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Qualified retirement plans</strong> may buy life insurance if the plan document allows it. The plan administrator or the participant may decide. In a defined contribution plan, the policy is part of the participant's account. In a defined benefit plan, the death benefit is part of the benefit. The life insurance must be INCIDENTAL to the retirement purpose.</li></ul>","text":"Military and federal programs Program Key facts SGLI (Servicemembers' Group Life Insurance) Group term life for full-time service members, available in set increments up to the program maximum. Automatic unless the member opts out FSGLI (Family SGLI) Spouse: automatically covered for the lesser of $100,000 or the member's SGLI amount. Premium depends on the spouse's age and the coverage amount. Children: $10,000 each at no cost VGLI (Veterans' Group Life) Lets a separated service member keep coverage as renewable term. No proof of insurability FEGLI (Federal Employees Group Life) Group term life for federal employees and civil service workers Other special groups Type Key facts Franchise life For employees of a common employer or association members. Each participant gets an INDIVIDUAL policy (not a certificate). Uniform provisions, but benefits may vary. Often used for small groups that do not meet the state's minimums for regular group insurance. Also called wholesale insurance Credit life and credit disability Help a borrower repay a loan if they die (lump sum to the creditor) or become disabled (monthly benefits equal to loan payments). Set up by banks and finance companies. Premiums are based on claims experience and expenses, NOT on the borrower's age. Usually decreasing term that matches the loan balance. The insured usually pays Blanket life Covers a group exposed to the same hazard (for example, airline passengers). NO specific individuals named, NO certificates issued Blanket health Covers groups with constantly changing membership (for example, students at a school or airline passengers). No individual certificates Memory trick (blanket): BLANKET = a whole group under one cover, no names. Think \"a blanket covers everyone under it; you don't need your name on it.\" Retired lives reserve (RLR) and qualified retirement plans RLR: Provides life insurance after retirement. It is annually renewable term. A reserve account is built before retirement and used to pay premiums afterward. Employer contributions are tax-deductible for the employer, not for the employee. A life insurer or a trust may manage the fund. Qualified retirement plans may buy life insurance if the plan document allows it. The plan administrator or the participant may decide. In a defined contribution plan, the policy is part of the participant's account. In a defined benefit plan, the death benefit is part of the benefit. The life insurance must be INCIDENTAL to the retirement purpose."},{"id":"ch06-s9","title":"8. Taxation of group life","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Plan must cover at least 70% of all employees</strong></td><td>For favorable tax treatment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>At least 85% of participants must NOT be key employees</strong></td><td>So no more than 15% can be key employees</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Employee-paid premiums</strong></td><td>NOT tax-deductible</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Employer-paid premiums</strong></td><td>Tax-deductible business expense. Exception: a sole proprietor or partner cannot deduct premiums for their own coverage</td></tr><tr><td><strong>First $50,000 of coverage</strong></td><td>Tax-free to the employee</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coverage over $50,000</strong></td><td>May be taxable as ordinary income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Benefit schedules</strong></td><td>Based on earnings (salary), job title, or a flat amount for everyone</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Proceeds: lump sum</strong></td><td>Tax-free</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Proceeds: installments</strong></td><td>The interest portion of each installment is taxable</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Rule Detail Plan must cover at least 70% of all employees For favorable tax treatment At least 85% of participants must NOT be key employees So no more than 15% can be key employees Employee-paid premiums NOT tax-deductible Employer-paid premiums Tax-deductible business expense. Exception: a sole proprietor or partner cannot deduct premiums for their own coverage First $50,000 of coverage Tax-free to the employee Coverage over $50,000 May be taxable as ordinary income Benefit schedules Based on earnings (salary), job title, or a flat amount for everyone Proceeds: lump sum Tax-free Proceeds: installments The interest portion of each installment is taxable"},{"id":"ch06-s10","title":"9. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Contributory vs. noncontributory</td><td>Employees pay (75% participation) vs. employer pays all (100% participation)</td></tr><tr><td>Master policy vs. certificate</td><td>Employer holds the master policy. Employee holds the certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Contract holder vs. certificate holder</td><td>Employer vs. employee</td></tr><tr><td>Attained age vs. original age</td><td>Current age (higher premium) vs. issue age (lower premium)</td></tr><tr><td>Group conversion vs. master policy termination conversion</td><td>Leaving employee: 31 days. Master policy ends: members insured 5+ years</td></tr><tr><td>Franchise vs. group</td><td>Franchise = individual policies. Group = certificates under one master policy</td></tr><tr><td>Blanket vs. group</td><td>Blanket = no names, no certificates. Group = named members with certificates</td></tr><tr><td>Credit life vs. group life</td><td>Credit: pays the creditor; decreasing term; premium not based on age. Group: pays the named beneficiary; annual renewable term</td></tr><tr><td>SGLI vs. FSGLI</td><td>SGLI = service member. FSGLI = spouse and children</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Contributory vs. noncontributory Employees pay (75% participation) vs. employer pays all (100% participation) Master policy vs. certificate Employer holds the master policy. Employee holds the certificate Contract holder vs. certificate holder Employer vs. employee Attained age vs. original age Current age (higher premium) vs. issue age (lower premium) Group conversion vs. master policy termination conversion Leaving employee: 31 days. Master policy ends: members insured 5+ years Franchise vs. group Franchise = individual policies. Group = certificates under one master policy Blanket vs. group Blanket = no names, no certificates. Group = named members with certificates Credit life vs. group life Credit: pays the creditor; decreasing term; premium not based on age. Group: pays the named beneficiary; annual renewable term SGLI vs. FSGLI SGLI = service member. FSGLI = spouse and children"},{"id":"ch06-s11","title":"10. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Who owns the master group policy, and who holds the certificate?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> What minimum participation applies to a contributory plan?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> How long is the enrollment period for group life?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> How long is the probationary period for new employees?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> How long is the conversion period after employment ends?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Does conversion require proof of insurability?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What is the largest tax-free amount of group term coverage per employee?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What must be true about the eligible-employee group for the plan to get favorable tax treatment?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the difference between blanket life and franchise life?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Who receives the proceeds from a credit life policy?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Dana leaves her job in March. She has group life coverage and wants a permanent policy. The coverage was group term. What is her deadline, and does she need a medical exam?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> A company offers a noncontributory group plan. Only 80% of eligible employees sign up. Is this acceptable? Explain.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A bank loans money to Jordan for a car and requires a policy that pays off the loan if Jordan dies. Which type of group coverage is this?</li></ul>","text":"Who owns the master group policy, and who holds the certificate? What minimum participation applies to a contributory plan? How long is the enrollment period for group life? How long is the probationary period for new employees? How long is the conversion period after employment ends? Does conversion require proof of insurability? What is the largest tax-free amount of group term coverage per employee? What must be true about the eligible-employee group for the plan to get favorable tax treatment? What is the difference between blanket life and franchise life? Who receives the proceeds from a credit life policy? Scenario questions Scenario A: Dana leaves her job in March. She has group life coverage and wants a permanent policy. The coverage was group term. What is her deadline, and does she need a medical exam? Scenario B: A company offers a noncontributory group plan. Only 80% of eligible employees sign up. Is this acceptable? Explain. Scenario C: A bank loans money to Jordan for a car and requires a policy that pays off the loan if Jordan dies. Which type of group coverage is this?"},{"id":"ch06-s12","title":"11. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> The employer (contract holder) owns the master policy. The employee holds the certificate.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> 75% of eligible employees.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> 31 days.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> 1 to 6 months.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> 31 days after termination.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> No.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> $50,000. Coverage above that may be taxable.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> At least 70% of all employees covered, and no more than 15% key employees (at least 85% of participants must NOT be key employees).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Blanket life covers a group exposed to a common hazard with no names and no certificates. Franchise life issues an individual policy to each participant.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> The creditor (the bank), up to the loan balance.</p><p>Scenario A: Dana has 31 days from termination to convert. No medical exam is required. She will convert to an individual whole life policy (most insurers limit conversion to whole life).</p><p>Scenario B: Not acceptable for a noncontributory plan. Noncontributory plans require 100% participation.</p><p>Scenario C: Credit life insurance (decreasing term, paid to the creditor).</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick for Chapter 6:</strong> Make two cards: (1) Numbers card: 100% vs. 75%, 31 days, 70%/85%, 15%, $50,000. (2) Program card: SGLI, FSGLI, VGLI, FEGLI, franchise, credit, blanket. Quiz yourself daily.</p></div>","text":"The employer (contract holder) owns the master policy. The employee holds the certificate. 75% of eligible employees. 31 days. 1 to 6 months. 31 days after termination. No. $50,000. Coverage above that may be taxable. At least 70% of all employees covered, and no more than 15% key employees (at least 85% of participants must NOT be key employees). Blanket life covers a group exposed to a common hazard with no names and no certificates. Franchise life issues an individual policy to each participant. The creditor (the bank), up to the loan balance. Scenario A: Dana has 31 days from termination to convert. No medical exam is required. She will convert to an individual whole life policy (most insurers limit conversion to whole life). Scenario B: Not acceptable for a noncontributory plan. Noncontributory plans require 100% participation. Scenario C: Credit life insurance (decreasing term, paid to the creditor). Memory trick for Chapter 6: Make two cards: (1) Numbers card: 100% vs. 75%, 31 days, 70%/85%, 15%, $50,000. (2) Program card: SGLI, FSGLI, VGLI, FEGLI, franchise, credit, blanket. Quiz yourself daily."}]},{"id":"ch07","short":"Ch 7","title":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","label":"Chapter 7","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Three things drive premiums (mortality, interest, expenses). Then: how premiums are funded, how policies are compared, who gets the money (beneficiaries), how the money is paid (settlement options), and how it is taxed. Most points are &quot;which way does it go?&quot; questions.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch07-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Higher mortality or expenses = higher premium. Higher interest = lower premium.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annual payment = cheapest. Monthly = most expensive.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Lump sum = default settlement. Death benefit proceeds are generally income-tax-free.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Irrevocable beneficiary = needs consent to change.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Higher mortality or expenses = higher premium. Higher interest = lower premium. Annual payment = cheapest. Monthly = most expensive. Lump sum = default settlement. Death benefit proceeds are generally income-tax-free. Irrevocable beneficiary = needs consent to change."},{"id":"ch07-s2","title":"1. Premium basics","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A premium is the owner's consideration (the payment that keeps the contract binding).</li><li class=\"l0\">Due on the due date. The grace period gives extra time. Non-payment past the grace period = lapse.</li></ul><h4>The three premium factors</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect on premium</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Mortality</strong> (most significant)</td><td>Higher death rates = HIGHER premium</td><td>Based on mortality tables. Expressed as deaths per 1,000. Morbidity = frequency of disease; mortality = frequency of death</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interest</strong></td><td>Higher earnings on premiums = LOWER premium</td><td>Premiums are based on an assumed interest rate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Expenses</strong> (loading)</td><td>Higher costs = HIGHER premium</td><td>Commissions, salaries, admin costs, required reserves, and profit margin (surplus)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Additional premium factors</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Age:</strong> Older = higher premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Sex:</strong> Women live longer = lower premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Health, occupation, hobbies, habits:</strong> Poor health, risky jobs or hobbies, and tobacco use = higher premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Benefits:</strong> Bigger death benefit = higher cost. Cash value makes the policy more expensive.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Riders:</strong> Each rider adds premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Premium mode:</strong> More frequent payments = higher total cost.</li></ul>","text":"A premium is the owner's consideration (the payment that keeps the contract binding). Due on the due date. The grace period gives extra time. Non-payment past the grace period = lapse. The three premium factors Factor Effect on premium Notes Mortality (most significant) Higher death rates = HIGHER premium Based on mortality tables. Expressed as deaths per 1,000. Morbidity = frequency of disease; mortality = frequency of death Interest Higher earnings on premiums = LOWER premium Premiums are based on an assumed interest rate Expenses (loading) Higher costs = HIGHER premium Commissions, salaries, admin costs, required reserves, and profit margin (surplus) Additional premium factors Age: Older = higher premium. Sex: Women live longer = lower premium. Health, occupation, hobbies, habits: Poor health, risky jobs or hobbies, and tobacco use = higher premium. Benefits: Bigger death benefit = higher cost. Cash value makes the policy more expensive. Riders: Each rider adds premium. Premium mode: More frequent payments = higher total cost."},{"id":"ch07-s3","title":"2. Net vs. gross premiums","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Net single premium</strong></td><td>Covers mortality and interest only; one payment funds the whole policy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Net level annual premium</strong></td><td>Same idea, spread into level annual payments</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gross premium</strong></td><td>The total charged: mortality + interest + expenses. What the owner actually pays</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gross annual premium</strong></td><td>Gross premium adjusted for an annual payment schedule</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Formula:</strong> Gross premium = net premium + expenses. The net premium is the present value of expected death benefits (mortality cost discounted by interest).</p></div>","text":"Term Meaning Net single premium Covers mortality and interest only; one payment funds the whole policy Net level annual premium Same idea, spread into level annual payments Gross premium The total charged: mortality + interest + expenses. What the owner actually pays Gross annual premium Gross premium adjusted for an annual payment schedule Formula: Gross premium = net premium + expenses. The net premium is the present value of expected death benefits (mortality cost discounted by interest)."},{"id":"ch07-s4","title":"3. Premium funding methods","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th><th>Key fact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Single premium</strong></td><td>One payment covers the whole policy</td><td>High upfront cost. Tied to single-pay whole life</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Periodic</strong></td><td>Monthly, quarterly, semiannual, or annual</td><td>More affordable. Spreads cost over time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fixed (level)</strong></td><td>Level premium for life</td><td>Early years pay more than the actual cost, which offsets later years</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Modified</strong></td><td>Lower premium for 3 to 5 years, then one increase to a higher level</td><td>Good if income is expected to grow</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Graded</strong></td><td>Premium rises each year during an initial period, then levels off</td><td>Starts lower than standard whole life</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Flexible</strong></td><td>Owner adjusts payments within the contract</td><td>Maximum control; typical of universal life</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Premium mode ranking (lowest to highest cost)</h4><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> <strong>Annual</strong> (lowest cost; the standard basis)</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> <strong>Semiannual</strong></p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> <strong>Quarterly</strong></p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> <strong>Monthly</strong> (highest cost)</p><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Why:</strong> Less interest is earned on money paid early, and admin costs are higher for frequent billing.</p></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>ANNUAL = ALL in one go = cheapest. MONTHLY = MOST often = most expensive.</p></div>","text":"Method How it works Key fact Single premium One payment covers the whole policy High upfront cost. Tied to single-pay whole life Periodic Monthly, quarterly, semiannual, or annual More affordable. Spreads cost over time Fixed (level) Level premium for life Early years pay more than the actual cost, which offsets later years Modified Lower premium for 3 to 5 years, then one increase to a higher level Good if income is expected to grow Graded Premium rises each year during an initial period, then levels off Starts lower than standard whole life Flexible Owner adjusts payments within the contract Maximum control; typical of universal life Premium mode ranking (lowest to highest cost) Annual (lowest cost; the standard basis) Semiannual Quarterly Monthly (highest cost) Why: Less interest is earned on money paid early, and admin costs are higher for frequent billing. Memory trick: ANNUAL = ALL in one go = cheapest. MONTHLY = MOST often = most expensive."},{"id":"ch07-s5","title":"4. Paying premiums from policy values","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Dividends:</strong> Can pay premiums, buy paid-up additions, or buy one-year term.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cash value:</strong> Can pay premiums, but it reduces the policy's cash value and equity. If the cash value runs out, the policy may lapse.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Minimum deposit (financed insurance):</strong> A FINANCING method, NOT a policy type. Best for people in high tax brackets. The owner borrows against the cash value each year to pay part of the premium.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>IRS rule:</strong> At least <strong>4 of the first 7 premiums</strong> must come from non-loan sources. This keeps the policy from becoming a MEC.</li></ul>","text":"Dividends: Can pay premiums, buy paid-up additions, or buy one-year term. Cash value: Can pay premiums, but it reduces the policy's cash value and equity. If the cash value runs out, the policy may lapse. Minimum deposit (financed insurance): A FINANCING method, NOT a policy type. Best for people in high tax brackets. The owner borrows against the cash value each year to pay part of the premium. IRS rule: At least 4 of the first 7 premiums must come from non-loan sources. This keeps the policy from becoming a MEC."},{"id":"ch07-s6","title":"5. Reserves and premium collection","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Initial premium:</strong> Collected by the producer at application.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Future premiums:</strong> Billed by the insurer and paid by the insured.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Premium financing:</strong> Available, similar to an installment loan.</li></ul><h4>Earned vs. unearned premium</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Earned:</strong> Portion the insurer is entitled to for coverage already provided.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Unearned:</strong> Portion paid for coverage not yet provided. If the policy is canceled, the unearned portion is returned.</li></ul><h4>Reserves</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Funds legally required to cover current and future claims. Also called unpaid claim reserves.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Backs the insurer's promise to pay. Critical for solvency.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>State rules:</strong> Each state specifies required reserve amounts. Funded by future premiums and interest.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Legal reserve:</strong> The amount the insurance commissioner requires the insurer to hold, based on the mortality table and assumed interest rate. The commissioner sets both factors.</li></ul>","text":"Initial premium: Collected by the producer at application. Future premiums: Billed by the insurer and paid by the insured. Premium financing: Available, similar to an installment loan. Earned vs. unearned premium Earned: Portion the insurer is entitled to for coverage already provided. Unearned: Portion paid for coverage not yet provided. If the policy is canceled, the unearned portion is returned. Reserves Definition: Funds legally required to cover current and future claims. Also called unpaid claim reserves. Purpose: Backs the insurer's promise to pay. Critical for solvency. State rules: Each state specifies required reserve amounts. Funded by future premiums and interest. Legal reserve: The amount the insurance commissioner requires the insurer to hold, based on the mortality table and assumed interest rate. The commissioner sets both factors."},{"id":"ch07-s7","title":"6. Comparing policy costs","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cost</strong> = premiums paid minus benefits received.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Lower premium does NOT mean lower overall cost.</strong> Consider dividends, cash value, and other benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Interest-adjusted net cost method:</strong> Evaluates four variables: premiums, death benefits, cash value, and dividends.</li></ul><h4>NAIC required indexes</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Index</th><th>What it measures</th><th>When to use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Surrender cost index</strong></td><td>Average net cost per $1,000 if the policy is surrendered for cash at a set point (10 or 20 years)</td><td>For buyers focused on cash value growth, planning to surrender in 10 to 20 years</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Net payment cost index</strong></td><td>Average annual premium outlay. Assumes the policy is NOT surrendered. Excludes cash value</td><td>For buyers focused on the death benefit</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Comparative interest rate (CIR) method</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The &quot;buy term and invest the rest&quot; strategy.</li><li class=\"l0\">Finds the rate of return the investment must earn to match the cash value policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">The face amounts of the two products must be the same.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Higher CIR = the permanent policy is more cost-effective.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Cost = premiums paid minus benefits received. Lower premium does NOT mean lower overall cost. Consider dividends, cash value, and other benefits. Interest-adjusted net cost method: Evaluates four variables: premiums, death benefits, cash value, and dividends. NAIC required indexes Index What it measures When to use Surrender cost index Average net cost per $1,000 if the policy is surrendered for cash at a set point (10 or 20 years) For buyers focused on cash value growth, planning to surrender in 10 to 20 years Net payment cost index Average annual premium outlay. Assumes the policy is NOT surrendered. Excludes cash value For buyers focused on the death benefit Comparative interest rate (CIR) method The \"buy term and invest the rest\" strategy. Finds the rate of return the investment must earn to match the cash value policy. The face amounts of the two products must be the same. Higher CIR = the permanent policy is more cost-effective."},{"id":"ch07-s8","title":"7. Living benefits","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Access to part of the death benefit before death. Only the policy owner can start one. Beneficiaries do not receive it.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cash value</strong></td><td>Main living benefit of whole life. Can be borrowed against or used as collateral. May carry fees, taxes, interest, or a reduced death benefit. Early years: cash value is less than total premiums paid. Different from the company's reserves</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accelerated (death) benefit</strong></td><td>Also called a terminal illness rider. Physician certifies death is expected within <strong>2 years</strong>. Usually capped at <strong>50%</strong> of face value. The amount paid is deducted from the death benefit. Tax-free if terminally ill</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Policy dividends</strong></td><td>Refund of excess premium, from mutual (participating) insurers. Uses: cash, premiums, paid-up additions, one-year term, or accumulate at interest. Dividends do NOT reduce the death benefit</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Definition: Access to part of the death benefit before death. Only the policy owner can start one. Beneficiaries do not receive it. Benefit Key facts Cash value Main living benefit of whole life. Can be borrowed against or used as collateral. May carry fees, taxes, interest, or a reduced death benefit. Early years: cash value is less than total premiums paid. Different from the company's reserves Accelerated (death) benefit Also called a terminal illness rider. Physician certifies death is expected within 2 years. Usually capped at 50% of face value. The amount paid is deducted from the death benefit. Tax-free if terminally ill Policy dividends Refund of excess premium, from mutual (participating) insurers. Uses: cash, premiums, paid-up additions, one-year term, or accumulate at interest. Dividends do NOT reduce the death benefit"},{"id":"ch07-s9","title":"8. Viatical and life settlements","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>VIATICAL settlement</th><th>LIFE settlement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who sells</td><td>Policyholder with a chronic or terminal illness</td><td>Policyholder for any reason</td></tr><tr><td>Illness required?</td><td>YES</td><td>NO</td></tr><tr><td>Terms</td><td><strong>Viator</strong> = seller (policyholder); <strong>Viatical/viatee</strong> = buyer</td><td>Seller receives MORE than cash surrender value but LESS than the death benefit</td></tr><tr><td>Licensing</td><td>Many states require a special license</td><td>Brokers must be licensed</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>Tax-free if chronically or terminally ill</td><td>Not covered in this chapter</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Chronically ill:</strong> Needs supervision due to cognitive impairment, OR cannot perform 2 or more ADLs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Terminally ill:</strong> Expected to die within <strong>24 months</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Life settlement exclusions:</strong> Loan collateral assignments, policy loans and surrender benefits, 1035 exchanges, related-party agreements, and corporate or pension plans.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>VIATICAL = Vital illness required. LIFE settlement = Life, no illness needed.</p></div>","text":"VIATICAL settlement LIFE settlement Who sells Policyholder with a chronic or terminal illness Policyholder for any reason Illness required? YES NO Terms Viator = seller (policyholder); Viatical/viatee = buyer Seller receives MORE than cash surrender value but LESS than the death benefit Licensing Many states require a special license Brokers must be licensed Tax Tax-free if chronically or terminally ill Not covered in this chapter Chronically ill: Needs supervision due to cognitive impairment, OR cannot perform 2 or more ADLs. Terminally ill: Expected to die within 24 months. Life settlement exclusions: Loan collateral assignments, policy loans and surrender benefits, 1035 exchanges, related-party agreements, and corporate or pension plans. Memory trick: VIATICAL = Vital illness required. LIFE settlement = Life, no illness needed."},{"id":"ch07-s10","title":"9. Death benefit settlement options (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The insurer must pay on receipt of proof of death (a death certificate).</li><li class=\"l0\">The <strong>owner</strong> picks the option when applying and can change it during the insured's life.</li><li class=\"l0\">If the owner picked an option, the beneficiary cannot change it.</li><li class=\"l0\">If no option was picked, the <strong>beneficiary</strong> chooses at death.</li></ul><h4>Policy proceeds</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Benefit type</th><th>Amount paid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Death benefit</td><td>Face value + earned dividends − loans and interest</td></tr><tr><td>Surrender benefit</td><td>Cash value − surrender charges and loans</td></tr><tr><td>Maturity benefit</td><td>Cash value − loans and interest</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>The five options</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>How it pays</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1. Lump sum</strong></td><td>One payment</td><td>Most common and the default. Paid after outstanding loans and overdue premiums are deducted</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. Interest only</strong></td><td>Insurer keeps the principal; pays interest</td><td>Minimum interest rate guaranteed. Paid at least annually. Interest is taxable. Beneficiary can withdraw principal later unless restricted</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Fixed amount</strong></td><td>Set payment amount until principal and interest run out</td><td>Beneficiary picks the payment. Larger payments = shorter payout. Focus: the amount of income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4. Fixed period</strong> (period certain)</td><td>Equal payments over a set number of years</td><td>Beneficiary picks the period (e.g., 10 years). Ignores life expectancy. Focus: the time period</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5. Life income</strong></td><td>Payments for the beneficiary's lifetime</td><td>Based on life expectancy. Cannot outlive the income</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Life income sub-options</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sub-option</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Single (pure, straight) life</strong></td><td>Payments for the beneficiary's lifetime. Stops at death, no refund. Highest income, riskiest</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Refund life income</strong></td><td>Guarantees total payments at least equal to the principal. The rest goes to survivors (installments or lump sum)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Life income with period certain</strong></td><td>Lifetime income. If the beneficiary dies before the period ends, payments go to a secondary beneficiary. If they survive the period, payments continue for life</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Joint and survivor</strong></td><td>Lifetime payments for two or more people. Continues to the survivor. Amounts depend on both ages; may reduce after the first death</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (ranking the life income options):</strong> SINGLE = highest income, no refund. REFUND = gets its money back. PERIOD CERTAIN = guaranteed years. JOINT = two lives.</p></div>","text":"The insurer must pay on receipt of proof of death (a death certificate). The owner picks the option when applying and can change it during the insured's life. If the owner picked an option, the beneficiary cannot change it. If no option was picked, the beneficiary chooses at death. Policy proceeds Benefit type Amount paid Death benefit Face value + earned dividends − loans and interest Surrender benefit Cash value − surrender charges and loans Maturity benefit Cash value − loans and interest The five options Option How it pays Key facts 1. Lump sum One payment Most common and the default. Paid after outstanding loans and overdue premiums are deducted 2. Interest only Insurer keeps the principal; pays interest Minimum interest rate guaranteed. Paid at least annually. Interest is taxable. Beneficiary can withdraw principal later unless restricted 3. Fixed amount Set payment amount until principal and interest run out Beneficiary picks the payment. Larger payments = shorter payout. Focus: the amount of income 4. Fixed period (period certain) Equal payments over a set number of years Beneficiary picks the period (e.g., 10 years). Ignores life expectancy. Focus: the time period 5. Life income Payments for the beneficiary's lifetime Based on life expectancy. Cannot outlive the income Life income sub-options Sub-option What happens Single (pure, straight) life Payments for the beneficiary's lifetime. Stops at death, no refund. Highest income, riskiest Refund life income Guarantees total payments at least equal to the principal. The rest goes to survivors (installments or lump sum) Life income with period certain Lifetime income. If the beneficiary dies before the period ends, payments go to a secondary beneficiary. If they survive the period, payments continue for life Joint and survivor Lifetime payments for two or more people. Continues to the survivor. Amounts depend on both ages; may reduce after the first death Memory trick (ranking the life income options): SINGLE = highest income, no refund. REFUND = gets its money back. PERIOD CERTAIN = guaranteed years. JOINT = two lives."},{"id":"ch07-s11","title":"10. Beneficiaries","html":"<h4>Who can be named</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Individuals (by name and relationship).</li><li class=\"l0\">Class designations (such as &quot;children of the insured&quot;).</li><li class=\"l0\">Businesses (key person insurance).</li><li class=\"l0\">Charities.</li><li class=\"l0\">Trusts (to manage proceeds).</li><li class=\"l0\">Estate: possible but NOT advisable (probate, taxes, creditors).</li></ul><h4>Changing beneficiaries</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How</th><th>When it takes effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Filing method</strong></td><td>Owner notifies the insurer in writing; may need a witness</td><td>Date of the request</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Endorsement method</strong></td><td>Policy is sent to the insurer to be endorsed</td><td>Date the new policy or endorsement is issued</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Revocable vs. irrevocable</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>REVOCABLE</th><th>IRREVOCABLE</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Change</td><td>Owner, anytime, no consent</td><td>Needs the beneficiary's consent</td></tr><tr><td>Beneficiary's interest</td><td>No vested interest; most common</td><td>Vested interest</td></tr><tr><td>Consent needed for</td><td>Nothing</td><td>Changing beneficiaries, policy loans, assignment, changing settlement options</td></tr><tr><td>Right to a policy copy</td><td>No</td><td>YES</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Primary, contingent, tertiary</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Primary:</strong> Gets the benefit first.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Contingent (secondary):</strong> Gets it if the primary dies before the insured.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Tertiary:</strong> Gets it if both the primary and contingent die before the insured.</li><li class=\"l0\">Multiple levels are possible.</li></ul><h4>Class beneficiaries</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A group, such as &quot;all my children.&quot;</li><li class=\"l0\">New members (births, adoptions) are added automatically.</li><li class=\"l0\">Deceased members are removed automatically.</li></ul><h4>Beneficiary rights</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Unless an irrevocable settlement option was chosen, the beneficiary can withdraw proceeds at any time.</li><li class=\"l0\">Proceeds left with the insurer under an interest option are protected from the beneficiary's creditors.</li></ul><h4>Special distribution situations</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Minor:</strong> Insurers usually do not pay minors directly. A guardian or trustee manages the money. The insurer may hold the proceeds and pay interest until the minor reaches majority.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Trust:</strong> Good for minor children. <strong>Testamentary</strong> trusts are set up by the will at death. <strong>Inter vivos</strong> (living) trusts are set up during life. Trust fees may reduce proceeds.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Estate:</strong> Used only if no beneficiary survives. Subject to probate, death taxes, and creditor claims. Keep beneficiary designations current to avoid this.</li></ul>","text":"Who can be named Individuals (by name and relationship). Class designations (such as \"children of the insured\"). Businesses (key person insurance). Charities. Trusts (to manage proceeds). Estate: possible but NOT advisable (probate, taxes, creditors). Changing beneficiaries Method How When it takes effect Filing method Owner notifies the insurer in writing; may need a witness Date of the request Endorsement method Policy is sent to the insurer to be endorsed Date the new policy or endorsement is issued Revocable vs. irrevocable REVOCABLE IRREVOCABLE Change Owner, anytime, no consent Needs the beneficiary's consent Beneficiary's interest No vested interest; most common Vested interest Consent needed for Nothing Changing beneficiaries, policy loans, assignment, changing settlement options Right to a policy copy No YES Primary, contingent, tertiary Primary: Gets the benefit first. Contingent (secondary): Gets it if the primary dies before the insured. Tertiary: Gets it if both the primary and contingent die before the insured. Multiple levels are possible. Class beneficiaries A group, such as \"all my children.\" New members (births, adoptions) are added automatically. Deceased members are removed automatically. Beneficiary rights Unless an irrevocable settlement option was chosen, the beneficiary can withdraw proceeds at any time. Proceeds left with the insurer under an interest option are protected from the beneficiary's creditors. Special distribution situations Minor: Insurers usually do not pay minors directly. A guardian or trustee manages the money. The insurer may hold the proceeds and pay interest until the minor reaches majority. Trust: Good for minor children. Testamentary trusts are set up by the will at death. Inter vivos (living) trusts are set up during life. Trust fees may reduce proceeds. Estate: Used only if no beneficiary survives. Subject to probate, death taxes, and creditor claims. Keep beneficiary designations current to avoid this."},{"id":"ch07-s12","title":"11. How shares are divided","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example (3 children; one dies with 2 kids)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Per capita</strong></td><td>&quot;By the head.&quot; Equal shares among living members. A deceased member's share is redistributed</td><td>The 2 surviving children each receive 50%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Per stirpes</strong></td><td>&quot;By the bloodline.&quot; A deceased member's share goes to their descendants</td><td>The 2 surviving children each receive 33.3%. The deceased child's 2 children split their parent's 33.3% (16.65% each)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>PER CAPITA = &quot;per CAPITA&quot; = per head (each living person gets the same). PER STIRPES = &quot;stirps&quot; = family branch (the deceased's branch keeps its share).</p></div>","text":"Method Meaning Example (3 children; one dies with 2 kids) Per capita \"By the head.\" Equal shares among living members. A deceased member's share is redistributed The 2 surviving children each receive 50% Per stirpes \"By the bloodline.\" A deceased member's share goes to their descendants The 2 surviving children each receive 33.3%. The deceased child's 2 children split their parent's 33.3% (16.65% each) Memory trick: PER CAPITA = \"per CAPITA\" = per head (each living person gets the same). PER STIRPES = \"stirps\" = family branch (the deceased's branch keeps its share)."},{"id":"ch07-s13","title":"12. Policy provisions affecting beneficiaries","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Facility of payment</strong></td><td>Lets the insurer pay a surviving relative when no beneficiary is alive. Usually limited to a small dollar amount set by the policy or state law</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Uniform Simultaneous Death Act</strong></td><td>Applies when no one can determine who died first. The beneficiary is presumed to have died first, so proceeds go to the contingent beneficiary.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Common disaster provision</strong></td><td>Primary beneficiary must outlive the insured by a set period. If both die within that period, benefits go to the contingent. Protects the contingent and can avoid probate and estate taxes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Spendthrift clause</strong></td><td>Protects the beneficiary's proceeds from creditors while the insurer holds them. Prevents commuting (taking a lump sum) or encumbering (using as collateral). Prevents imprudent spending through installments. Does NOT apply to a lump sum</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Provision Key facts Facility of payment Lets the insurer pay a surviving relative when no beneficiary is alive. Usually limited to a small dollar amount set by the policy or state law Uniform Simultaneous Death Act Applies when no one can determine who died first. The beneficiary is presumed to have died first, so proceeds go to the contingent beneficiary. Common disaster provision Primary beneficiary must outlive the insured by a set period. If both die within that period, benefits go to the contingent. Protects the contingent and can avoid probate and estate taxes Spendthrift clause Protects the beneficiary's proceeds from creditors while the insurer holds them. Prevents commuting (taking a lump sum) or encumbering (using as collateral). Prevents imprudent spending through installments. Does NOT apply to a lump sum"},{"id":"ch07-s14","title":"13. Tax consequences","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Tax treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Premiums (individual)</strong></td><td>NOT deductible (personal expense). Exceptions include employer-paid premiums (business deduction) and some special arrangements</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Death proceeds: lump sum</strong></td><td>Income-tax-free to the beneficiary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Transfer-for-value rule</strong></td><td>Exception to tax-free proceeds when the policy was sold or transferred for value before death</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Estate tax</strong></td><td>Proceeds may be subject to federal estate tax if the policy is in the owner's gross estate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Death proceeds: installments</strong></td><td>Principal is tax-free; interest is taxable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cash value</strong></td><td>Interest is tax-deferred. Cost basis = total premiums paid − dividends received. Gains above cost basis are taxable (cost recovery rule). No tax while the cash value stays in the policy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Policy loans</strong></td><td>Usually NOT taxable. Exception: MECs (interest-first rule). Loan interest NOT deductible. If the policy is surrendered with a loan outstanding and the loan exceeds premiums paid, the gains are taxable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accelerated death benefit</strong></td><td>Tax-free if terminally ill; physician certifies death expected within 2 years</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dividends</strong></td><td>Generally NOT taxable (return of excess premium). Any amount above total premiums paid is taxable. Interest on accumulated dividends is taxable. MEC: dividends may be taxable; 10% penalty before age 59½</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1035 exchange</strong></td><td>Moving a policy to another insurer for a new policy is NOT a taxable event; tax deferral carries over</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Economic benefit doctrine</strong></td><td>A benefit with economic value may be taxable compensation. Exception: substantial risk of forfeiture</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Item Tax treatment Premiums (individual) NOT deductible (personal expense). Exceptions include employer-paid premiums (business deduction) and some special arrangements Death proceeds: lump sum Income-tax-free to the beneficiary Transfer-for-value rule Exception to tax-free proceeds when the policy was sold or transferred for value before death Estate tax Proceeds may be subject to federal estate tax if the policy is in the owner's gross estate Death proceeds: installments Principal is tax-free; interest is taxable Cash value Interest is tax-deferred. Cost basis = total premiums paid − dividends received. Gains above cost basis are taxable (cost recovery rule). No tax while the cash value stays in the policy Policy loans Usually NOT taxable. Exception: MECs (interest-first rule). Loan interest NOT deductible. If the policy is surrendered with a loan outstanding and the loan exceeds premiums paid, the gains are taxable Accelerated death benefit Tax-free if terminally ill; physician certifies death expected within 2 years Dividends Generally NOT taxable (return of excess premium). Any amount above total premiums paid is taxable. Interest on accumulated dividends is taxable. MEC: dividends may be taxable; 10% penalty before age 59½ 1035 exchange Moving a policy to another insurer for a new policy is NOT a taxable event; tax deferral carries over Economic benefit doctrine A benefit with economic value may be taxable compensation. Exception: substantial risk of forfeiture"},{"id":"ch07-s15","title":"14. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mortality vs. morbidity</td><td>Mortality = death. Morbidity = disease</td></tr><tr><td>Net premium vs. gross premium</td><td>Net = mortality + interest. Gross = net + expenses</td></tr><tr><td>Surrender cost index vs. net payment cost index</td><td>Surrender = assumes cash value is taken. Net payment = assumes the policy stays in force</td></tr><tr><td>Viatical vs. life settlement</td><td>Viatical = illness required. Life = no illness required</td></tr><tr><td>Filing vs. endorsement (beneficiary change)</td><td>Filing = effective on request date. Endorsement = effective when the endorsement is issued</td></tr><tr><td>Per capita vs. per stirpes</td><td>Per capita = equal among living members. Per stirpes = deceased's share passes to descendants</td></tr><tr><td>Revocable vs. irrevocable beneficiary</td><td>Revocable = owner can change. Irrevocable = consent needed</td></tr><tr><td>Primary vs. contingent vs. tertiary</td><td>Primary first, contingent second, tertiary third</td></tr><tr><td>Common disaster vs. simultaneous death</td><td>Common disaster = survival period. Simultaneous = cannot tell who died first</td></tr><tr><td>Spendthrift vs. lump sum</td><td>Spendthrift protects installments. Lump sum is not protected</td></tr><tr><td>Dividend vs. interest on dividends</td><td>Dividend = tax-free. Interest on dividend = taxable</td></tr><tr><td>Interest-only vs. fixed period</td><td>Interest-only = principal stays. Fixed period = principal and interest paid out over years</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Mortality vs. morbidity Mortality = death. Morbidity = disease Net premium vs. gross premium Net = mortality + interest. Gross = net + expenses Surrender cost index vs. net payment cost index Surrender = assumes cash value is taken. Net payment = assumes the policy stays in force Viatical vs. life settlement Viatical = illness required. Life = no illness required Filing vs. endorsement (beneficiary change) Filing = effective on request date. Endorsement = effective when the endorsement is issued Per capita vs. per stirpes Per capita = equal among living members. Per stirpes = deceased's share passes to descendants Revocable vs. irrevocable beneficiary Revocable = owner can change. Irrevocable = consent needed Primary vs. contingent vs. tertiary Primary first, contingent second, tertiary third Common disaster vs. simultaneous death Common disaster = survival period. Simultaneous = cannot tell who died first Spendthrift vs. lump sum Spendthrift protects installments. Lump sum is not protected Dividend vs. interest on dividends Dividend = tax-free. Interest on dividend = taxable Interest-only vs. fixed period Interest-only = principal stays. Fixed period = principal and interest paid out over years"},{"id":"ch07-s16","title":"15. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Which premium factor is most significant?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Does a higher interest rate raise or lower the premium?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What is the cheapest premium mode?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> What is the difference between net premium and gross premium?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Which NAIC index assumes the policy is surrendered?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Who is the viator in a viatical settlement?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> How much of its face value can a policy usually pay as an accelerated benefit?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Who chooses the settlement option if the owner made no choice?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Under which settlement option does the beneficiary receive payments for life?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> If a beneficiary dies and there are two surviving children and two grandchildren from the deceased, how does per stirpes divide the share?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Helen is 35 and wants a policy she can surrender in 15 years for cash. Which cost index helps her compare policies?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Paul's policy has an irrevocable beneficiary. He wants to switch the beneficiary to his new spouse. What is required?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> Mark is terminally ill, with an expected life of 18 months. He sells his policy to an investor. Is this a viatical or life settlement?</li></ul>","text":"Which premium factor is most significant? Does a higher interest rate raise or lower the premium? What is the cheapest premium mode? What is the difference between net premium and gross premium? Which NAIC index assumes the policy is surrendered? Who is the viator in a viatical settlement? How much of its face value can a policy usually pay as an accelerated benefit? Who chooses the settlement option if the owner made no choice? Under which settlement option does the beneficiary receive payments for life? If a beneficiary dies and there are two surviving children and two grandchildren from the deceased, how does per stirpes divide the share? Scenario questions Scenario A: Helen is 35 and wants a policy she can surrender in 15 years for cash. Which cost index helps her compare policies? Scenario B: Paul's policy has an irrevocable beneficiary. He wants to switch the beneficiary to his new spouse. What is required? Scenario C: Mark is terminally ill, with an expected life of 18 months. He sells his policy to an investor. Is this a viatical or life settlement?"},{"id":"ch07-s17","title":"16. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Mortality.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Lower. Higher interest = lower premium.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Annual.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Net premium covers mortality and interest. Gross premium is net premium plus expenses (what the owner pays).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> The surrender cost index.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> The policyholder who sells the policy because of chronic or terminal illness.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Usually up to 50%.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> The beneficiary.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Life income (single or joint and survivor, depending on the sub-option).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Per stirpes: each surviving child gets one-third (33.3%). The deceased child's two children split their parent's one-third (16.65% each).</p><p>Scenario A: The surrender cost index (planning to surrender in 10 to 20 years).</p><p>Scenario B: The irrevocable beneficiary must consent to the change.</p><p>Scenario C: A viatical settlement, because Mark is terminally ill (expected death within 24 months).</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick for Chapter 7:</strong> Draw three arrows: MORTALITY, INTEREST, EXPENSES, each pointing up or down to PREMIUM. Then draw a flowchart: Death → Proof → Owner's option → Beneficiary's option → Proceeds taxed how?</p></div>","text":"Mortality. Lower. Higher interest = lower premium. Annual. Net premium covers mortality and interest. Gross premium is net premium plus expenses (what the owner pays). The surrender cost index. The policyholder who sells the policy because of chronic or terminal illness. Usually up to 50%. The beneficiary. Life income (single or joint and survivor, depending on the sub-option). Per stirpes: each surviving child gets one-third (33.3%). The deceased child's two children split their parent's one-third (16.65% each). Scenario A: The surrender cost index (planning to surrender in 10 to 20 years). Scenario B: The irrevocable beneficiary must consent to the change. Scenario C: A viatical settlement, because Mark is terminally ill (expected death within 24 months). Memory trick for Chapter 7: Draw three arrows: MORTALITY, INTEREST, EXPENSES, each pointing up or down to PREMIUM. Then draw a flowchart: Death → Proof → Owner's option → Beneficiary's option → Proceeds taxed how?"}]},{"id":"ch08","short":"Ch 8","title":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","label":"Chapter 8","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Three big parts. (1) <strong>How much life insurance</strong> a family needs (the Needs Approach is the one to know). (2) <strong>Business uses</strong> (key person, buy-sell agreements, COLI, executive bonus). (3) <strong>Annuity basics</strong> (deferred vs. immediate, fixed vs. variable, payout options).</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch08-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Needs Approach = best method.</strong> It adds the family's lump-sum needs and income needs, then subtracts liquid assets.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Key person</strong> insurance protects the BUSINESS from losing an employee. The business owns and is the beneficiary.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Buy-sell agreements</strong> fund the purchase of a deceased owner's share. Know the cross-purchase vs. entity math.</li></ul>","text":"Needs Approach = best method. It adds the family's lump-sum needs and income needs, then subtracts liquid assets. Key person insurance protects the BUSINESS from losing an employee. The business owns and is the beneficiary. Buy-sell agreements fund the purchase of a deceased owner's share. Know the cross-purchase vs. entity math."},{"id":"ch08-s2","title":"1. Planning for survivor financial needs","html":"<h4>Five planning steps</h4><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> <strong>Information gathering:</strong> personal details, financial data, assets, investments.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> <strong>Identifying objectives:</strong> determine and rank the client's goals.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> <strong>Financial analysis:</strong> evaluate the current situation.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> <strong>Plan development:</strong> create and carry out a tailored plan.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> <strong>Periodic review:</strong> reassess and adjust regularly.</p>","text":"Five planning steps Information gathering: personal details, financial data, assets, investments. Identifying objectives: determine and rank the client's goals. Financial analysis: evaluate the current situation. Plan development: create and carry out a tailored plan. Periodic review: reassess and adjust regularly."},{"id":"ch08-s3","title":"2. Methods for determining the amount of coverage","html":"<h4>Human life value approach</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Calculates the present value of future net earnings.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Formula:</strong> (annual earned income − expenses) × years until retirement.</li><li class=\"l0\">Purpose: replace lost income for dependents; gives a clear dollar figure.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Limitation:</strong> Does NOT account for people who financially benefit from the insured's life.</li></ul><h4>Needs approach (MOST EFFECTIVE)</h4><p>Based on the family's financial goals: education fund, emergency fund, bequests and charitable gifts, spouse's retirement income, family members' ages, current and projected wages, and health history.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Identify <strong>lump-sum needs</strong>: funeral, medical bills, debts, education, emergency fund, bequests</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Calculate <strong>liquid assets</strong> available: savings, pension or profit-sharing benefits, existing life proceeds, Social Security income, bond interest, dividends, rental income, other income</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Consider the <strong>Social Security blackout period</strong>: time from death until the spouse is eligible for retirement benefits. Child benefits continue until age 18</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sum the <strong>total capital needs</strong></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Subtract</strong> liquid assets from capital needs. The result is the amount of insurance needed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (needs formula):</strong> NEEDS = Lump sums + Income gap − Liquid assets.</p></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Charitable gifting:</strong> Transferring an existing policy to a charity can produce an income tax deduction. A new policy owned and payable to a charity can have its premiums deducted.</li></ul><h4>Other approaches</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Multiple earnings</strong></td><td>Replace a set number of years of salary (for example, 5 times annual income)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interest-only</strong></td><td>Enough coverage to produce income that covers living costs without spending the principal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Single needs</strong></td><td>Covers one specific need: loan repayment, education, death taxes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capital needs analysis</strong></td><td>Covers immediate needs (final expenses, medical, probate, living costs, debts, emergency, education), death taxes, and continuing needs (readjustment, dependency period, life income, retirement)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Seat of the pants</strong></td><td>Arbitrary or intuitive. Quick estimate only; less reliable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Tax timing:</strong> The federal estate tax return and payment are generally due 9 months after death. State rules vary.</p></div>","text":"Human life value approach Calculates the present value of future net earnings. Formula: (annual earned income − expenses) × years until retirement. Purpose: replace lost income for dependents; gives a clear dollar figure. Limitation: Does NOT account for people who financially benefit from the insured's life. Needs approach (MOST EFFECTIVE) Based on the family's financial goals: education fund, emergency fund, bequests and charitable gifts, spouse's retirement income, family members' ages, current and projected wages, and health history. Step What to do 1 Identify lump-sum needs: funeral, medical bills, debts, education, emergency fund, bequests 2 Calculate liquid assets available: savings, pension or profit-sharing benefits, existing life proceeds, Social Security income, bond interest, dividends, rental income, other income 3 Consider the Social Security blackout period: time from death until the spouse is eligible for retirement benefits. Child benefits continue until age 18 4 Sum the total capital needs 5 Subtract liquid assets from capital needs. The result is the amount of insurance needed Memory trick (needs formula): NEEDS = Lump sums + Income gap − Liquid assets. Charitable gifting: Transferring an existing policy to a charity can produce an income tax deduction. A new policy owned and payable to a charity can have its premiums deducted. Other approaches Method How it works Multiple earnings Replace a set number of years of salary (for example, 5 times annual income) Interest-only Enough coverage to produce income that covers living costs without spending the principal Single needs Covers one specific need: loan repayment, education, death taxes Capital needs analysis Covers immediate needs (final expenses, medical, probate, living costs, debts, emergency, education), death taxes, and continuing needs (readjustment, dependency period, life income, retirement) Seat of the pants Arbitrary or intuitive. Quick estimate only; less reliable Tax timing: The federal estate tax return and payment are generally due 9 months after death. State rules vary."},{"id":"ch08-s4","title":"3. Individual uses of life insurance","html":"<p>Life insurance can provide immediate funds for:</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Final expenses (funeral, burial)</li><li class=\"l0\">Estate protection and conservation (covers taxes and expenses)</li><li class=\"l0\">Survivor protection and monthly income</li><li class=\"l0\">Education costs</li><li class=\"l0\">Debt repayment (mortgage)</li><li class=\"l0\">Retirement income supplement</li><li class=\"l0\">Charitable gifts</li><li class=\"l0\">Emergency funds</li><li class=\"l0\">Cash accumulation and liquidity</li></ul><h4>Key benefits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Survivor protection</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Estate creation:</strong> an immediate estate at death</li><li class=\"l0\">Cash accumulation</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Liquidity:</strong> easy access to funds</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Estate conservation:</strong> pays taxes and expenses</li></ul><h4>Life insurance as property</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The policy is a piece of property (like a house).</li><li class=\"l0\">Its value is included in the owner's estate at death. It may be subject to estate tax.</li><li class=\"l0\">Main advantage: creates an immediate estate for beneficiaries.</li></ul>","text":"Life insurance can provide immediate funds for: Final expenses (funeral, burial) Estate protection and conservation (covers taxes and expenses) Survivor protection and monthly income Education costs Debt repayment (mortgage) Retirement income supplement Charitable gifts Emergency funds Cash accumulation and liquidity Key benefits Survivor protection Estate creation: an immediate estate at death Cash accumulation Liquidity: easy access to funds Estate conservation: pays taxes and expenses Life insurance as property The policy is a piece of property (like a house). Its value is included in the owner's estate at death. It may be subject to estate tax. Main advantage: creates an immediate estate for beneficiaries."},{"id":"ch08-s5","title":"4. Business uses: key person insurance","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Protects the business from the financial impact of losing a key employee (someone with essential skills or knowledge).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Covers the EMPLOYEE, not the business owner.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Covers:</strong> lost earnings, increased costs (hiring and training a replacement), and business continuity.</li></ul><h4>Third-party ownership</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The BUSINESS is the applicant, owner, premium payor, and beneficiary.</li><li class=\"l0\">The EMPLOYEE is the insured.</li><li class=\"l0\">The business has insurable interest in the key employee.</li><li class=\"l0\">The business retains all owner rights: naming or changing beneficiaries, borrowing against cash value, receiving dividends, assigning benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Policy types:</strong> Whole life or universal life for long-term needs. Term for short-term needs.</li></ul><h4>Tax considerations</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Premiums: generally NOT tax-deductible.</li><li class=\"l0\">Death benefit: NOT taxable if it is not included in the employee's estate.</li><li class=\"l0\">The employee must hold no ownership rights in the contract.</li></ul><h4>Policy loan uses</h4><p>Policy loans are a useful tool for funding buy-sell agreements, deferred compensation, and split-dollar arrangements.</p>","text":"Purpose: Protects the business from the financial impact of losing a key employee (someone with essential skills or knowledge). Covers the EMPLOYEE, not the business owner. Covers: lost earnings, increased costs (hiring and training a replacement), and business continuity. Third-party ownership The BUSINESS is the applicant, owner, premium payor, and beneficiary. The EMPLOYEE is the insured. The business has insurable interest in the key employee. The business retains all owner rights: naming or changing beneficiaries, borrowing against cash value, receiving dividends, assigning benefits. Policy types: Whole life or universal life for long-term needs. Term for short-term needs. Tax considerations Premiums: generally NOT tax-deductible. Death benefit: NOT taxable if it is not included in the employee's estate. The employee must hold no ownership rights in the contract. Policy loan uses Policy loans are a useful tool for funding buy-sell agreements, deferred compensation, and split-dollar arrangements."},{"id":"ch08-s6","title":"5. Buy-sell agreements (business continuation)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Keep the business running. Give the deceased owner's family financial security. Prevent a forced sale of assets.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> A legal contract that ensures orderly transfer of the business and pays the deceased's survivors. Often funded with life insurance.</li></ul><h4>Sole proprietorship</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Two steps: (1) buy-sell plan drafted by an attorney; (2) insurance policy bought by the employee on the proprietor's life.</li><li class=\"l0\">The employee is owner, beneficiary, and premium payor.</li><li class=\"l0\">On death, the proceeds buy the business from the estate.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>When a sole proprietor dies, the business typically ceases.</strong></li></ul><h4>Partnership</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A partner's death legally dissolves the partnership.</li><li class=\"l0\">The agreement obligates the survivors to buy the deceased's interest at a set price. The estate must sell.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Who buys and owns the policies</th><th>Policy count (4 partners)</th><th>Who buys the deceased's share</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Entity plan</strong></td><td>The partnership buys and owns one policy on each partner</td><td>4 policies</td><td>The partnership, using the proceeds</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cross-purchase plan</strong></td><td>Each partner buys policies on the others</td><td>12 policies (each buys 3)</td><td>Surviving partners, directly</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Close corporation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A close corporation is a legally separate entity and continues after an owner dies.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Entity plan (stock redemption plan):</strong> The corporation buys policies on each stockholder (4 stockholders = 4 policies). Proceeds buy back the deceased's shares.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cross-purchase plan:</strong> Each stockholder buys policies on the others (4 stockholders = 12 policies). Survivors buy the shares directly.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Section 303 redemption plan:</strong> For estates where stock makes up a large share of the estate and estate taxes are a concern. The stock must be more than 35% of the adjusted gross estate. Allows the corporation to redeem stock to pay estate taxes without forcing a sale of business assets.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Wait-and-see agreement:</strong> Combines cross-purchase and entity elements for flexibility.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (cross-purchase vs. entity):</strong> CROSS-PURCHASE = the owners buy policies on EACH OTHER (many policies, n × (n − 1)). ENTITY = the business buys one policy per owner (n policies).</p></div>","text":"Purpose: Keep the business running. Give the deceased owner's family financial security. Prevent a forced sale of assets. Definition: A legal contract that ensures orderly transfer of the business and pays the deceased's survivors. Often funded with life insurance. Sole proprietorship Two steps: (1) buy-sell plan drafted by an attorney; (2) insurance policy bought by the employee on the proprietor's life. The employee is owner, beneficiary, and premium payor. On death, the proceeds buy the business from the estate. When a sole proprietor dies, the business typically ceases. Partnership A partner's death legally dissolves the partnership. The agreement obligates the survivors to buy the deceased's interest at a set price. The estate must sell. Plan Who buys and owns the policies Policy count (4 partners) Who buys the deceased's share Entity plan The partnership buys and owns one policy on each partner 4 policies The partnership, using the proceeds Cross-purchase plan Each partner buys policies on the others 12 policies (each buys 3) Surviving partners, directly Close corporation A close corporation is a legally separate entity and continues after an owner dies. Entity plan (stock redemption plan): The corporation buys policies on each stockholder (4 stockholders = 4 policies). Proceeds buy back the deceased's shares. Cross-purchase plan: Each stockholder buys policies on the others (4 stockholders = 12 policies). Survivors buy the shares directly. Section 303 redemption plan: For estates where stock makes up a large share of the estate and estate taxes are a concern. The stock must be more than 35% of the adjusted gross estate. Allows the corporation to redeem stock to pay estate taxes without forcing a sale of business assets. Wait-and-see agreement: Combines cross-purchase and entity elements for flexibility. Memory trick (cross-purchase vs. entity): CROSS-PURCHASE = the owners buy policies on EACH OTHER (many policies, n × (n − 1)). ENTITY = the business buys one policy per owner (n policies)."},{"id":"ch08-s7","title":"6. Deferred compensation and salary continuation","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Deferred compensation</strong></td><td>Executive benefit. The employer promises a future payment on disability, retirement, or death. Non-qualified (no special tax advantages). Often funded with cash value life or annuities</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Salary continuation</strong></td><td>Similar, but the employer fully funds it. The employee receives ongoing income on death, disability, or retirement. A formal agreement sets terms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Plan Key facts Deferred compensation Executive benefit. The employer promises a future payment on disability, retirement, or death. Non-qualified (no special tax advantages). Often funded with cash value life or annuities Salary continuation Similar, but the employer fully funds it. The employee receives ongoing income on death, disability, or retirement. A formal agreement sets terms"},{"id":"ch08-s8","title":"7. Corporate-owned life insurance (COLI)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The corporation owns a policy on a key employee. Premiums are generally treated as a deductible business expense.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Tax treatment on death:</strong> COLI death proceeds are generally income-tax-free to the corporation, subject to special employer-owned life insurance notice and consent rules. The $50,000 group-term rule does not apply to business-owned life insurance.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Change of insured:</strong> Allows the employer to replace a retired or departed employee with a new one, subject to insurability. Avoids the cost of writing a new policy.</li></ul><h4>Corporate-owned annuities</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Natural person as annuitant: interest is tax-deferred.</li><li class=\"l0\">Non-natural person (for example, a corporation) as annuitant: interest is taxable as ordinary income.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exceptions where interest stays tax-deferred:</strong> annuity held by a trust or corporation as agent for a natural person; annuity held by an estate after death; qualified plans, TSAs, and IRAs; immediate annuities (single premium, payments start within a year).</li></ul>","text":"The corporation owns a policy on a key employee. Premiums are generally treated as a deductible business expense. Tax treatment on death: COLI death proceeds are generally income-tax-free to the corporation, subject to special employer-owned life insurance notice and consent rules. The $50,000 group-term rule does not apply to business-owned life insurance. Change of insured: Allows the employer to replace a retired or departed employee with a new one, subject to insurability. Avoids the cost of writing a new policy. Corporate-owned annuities Natural person as annuitant: interest is tax-deferred. Non-natural person (for example, a corporation) as annuitant: interest is taxable as ordinary income. Exceptions where interest stays tax-deferred: annuity held by a trust or corporation as agent for a natural person; annuity held by an estate after death; qualified plans, TSAs, and IRAs; immediate annuities (single premium, payments start within a year)."},{"id":"ch08-s9","title":"8. Executive benefit plans","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Split-dollar</strong></td><td>The employer and employee share the cost of a life policy. A METHOD of acquiring insurance, NOT a policy type. Often combines term and whole life. The employee usually chooses the beneficiary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Executive bonus (Section 162 plan)</strong></td><td>Non-qualified. Employer gives the employee a bonus, which the employee uses to pay premiums on a policy the employee owns. Employer's bonus is tax-deductible. Bonus is taxable income to the employee. Death benefit is tax-free. The employee controls the policy (withdrawals, surrenders, loans). Benefits: employer gets deductible pay and a retention tool; employee gets funded life insurance and cash value access</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Plan Key facts Split-dollar The employer and employee share the cost of a life policy. A METHOD of acquiring insurance, NOT a policy type. Often combines term and whole life. The employee usually chooses the beneficiary Executive bonus (Section 162 plan) Non-qualified. Employer gives the employee a bonus, which the employee uses to pay premiums on a policy the employee owns. Employer's bonus is tax-deductible. Bonus is taxable income to the employee. Death benefit is tax-free. The employee controls the policy (withdrawals, surrenders, loans). Benefits: employer gets deductible pay and a retention tool; employee gets funded life insurance and cash value access"},{"id":"ch08-s10","title":"9. Annuity basics","html":"<h4>Key annuity terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>403(b) plan</strong></td><td>Retirement plan for public school employees, nonprofit workers, and some ministers. Similar to a 401(k)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1035 exchange</strong></td><td>Moves an annuity or life policy to another contract without triggering tax on the gain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accumulation period</strong></td><td>&quot;Saving&quot; phase: contributions and growth</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accumulation units</strong></td><td>In a variable annuity, contributions plus growth minus expenses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Annuitant</strong></td><td>The person whose life determines how long payments last</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Annuity units</strong></td><td>Accumulation units convert to these at payout; used to calculate monthly income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Principal</strong></td><td>The original amount invested</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Exclusion ratio</strong></td><td>Determines how much of each payment is tax-free (return of principal) vs. taxable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Market value adjustment (MVA)</strong></td><td>Adjusts the payout if money is taken out early, based on interest rate changes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Types of annuities</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Deferred</strong></td><td>&quot;Save now, get paid later.&quot; Payments start in the future</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Immediate</strong></td><td>Income starts within about a month of purchase</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fixed</strong></td><td>Guaranteed interest rate. The insurer takes the investment risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Variable</strong></td><td>Invested in portfolios (stocks, bonds, etc.). Payouts vary with performance. <strong>Requires a FINRA license and a state insurance license.</strong> In Louisiana, &quot;variable life and variable annuity&quot; is a separate line of authority</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Equity-indexed</strong></td><td>Hybrid. Guaranteed minimum return plus a bonus tied to a stock index (such as the S&amp;P 500)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Single premium</strong></td><td>One payment up front; payments start immediately or later</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Periodic payment (flexible premium)</strong></td><td>Ongoing contributions over time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Payout options</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Straight life annuity</strong></td><td>Income for the annuitant's life only. Stops at death. No refund</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Joint life and survivor</strong></td><td>Income for two people. Continues to the survivor after the first death</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Life with period certain</strong></td><td>Lifetime income plus a guaranteed minimum period. If the annuitant dies before the period ends, the beneficiary gets the remaining payments</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Period certain</strong></td><td>Guaranteed payments for a set number of years, regardless of life or death</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cash refund</strong></td><td>If the annuitant dies early, the remainder is paid to the beneficiary as a lump sum</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Key annuity terms Term Meaning 403(b) plan Retirement plan for public school employees, nonprofit workers, and some ministers. Similar to a 401(k) 1035 exchange Moves an annuity or life policy to another contract without triggering tax on the gain Accumulation period \"Saving\" phase: contributions and growth Accumulation units In a variable annuity, contributions plus growth minus expenses Annuitant The person whose life determines how long payments last Annuity units Accumulation units convert to these at payout; used to calculate monthly income Principal The original amount invested Exclusion ratio Determines how much of each payment is tax-free (return of principal) vs. taxable Market value adjustment (MVA) Adjusts the payout if money is taken out early, based on interest rate changes Types of annuities Type Key facts Deferred \"Save now, get paid later.\" Payments start in the future Immediate Income starts within about a month of purchase Fixed Guaranteed interest rate. The insurer takes the investment risk Variable Invested in portfolios (stocks, bonds, etc.). Payouts vary with performance. Requires a FINRA license and a state insurance license. In Louisiana, \"variable life and variable annuity\" is a separate line of authority Equity-indexed Hybrid. Guaranteed minimum return plus a bonus tied to a stock index (such as the S&P 500) Single premium One payment up front; payments start immediately or later Periodic payment (flexible premium) Ongoing contributions over time Payout options Option Key facts Straight life annuity Income for the annuitant's life only. Stops at death. No refund Joint life and survivor Income for two people. Continues to the survivor after the first death Life with period certain Lifetime income plus a guaranteed minimum period. If the annuitant dies before the period ends, the beneficiary gets the remaining payments Period certain Guaranteed payments for a set number of years, regardless of life or death Cash refund If the annuitant dies early, the remainder is paid to the beneficiary as a lump sum"},{"id":"ch08-s11","title":"10. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Needs approach vs. human life value</td><td>Needs = goals and liquid assets (best). Human life value = lost earnings only</td></tr><tr><td>Key person vs. buy-sell</td><td>Key person = protects the business from an employee's loss. Buy-sell = funds the purchase of an owner's share</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-purchase vs. entity plan</td><td>Cross-purchase = owners buy policies on each other (more policies). Entity = business buys policies (fewer policies)</td></tr><tr><td>Stock redemption vs. entity plan</td><td>Same concept in a close corporation (stock redemption is the corporate name)</td></tr><tr><td>Deferred vs. immediate annuity</td><td>Deferred = payments later. Immediate = payments within about a month</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed vs. variable annuity</td><td>Fixed = guaranteed rate, insurer bears risk. Variable = market-based, owner bears risk</td></tr><tr><td>Straight life vs. life with period certain</td><td>Straight = stops at death. Period certain = guaranteed minimum years</td></tr><tr><td>Split-dollar vs. executive bonus</td><td>Split-dollar = cost sharing (method). Executive bonus = employer pays a bonus that the employee uses for premiums</td></tr><tr><td>Deferred compensation vs. salary continuation</td><td>Deferred = future payment promised. Salary continuation = ongoing income, fully employer-funded</td></tr><tr><td>Section 303 vs. buy-sell</td><td>Section 303 = corporate stock redemption to pay estate taxes. Buy-sell = funds an owner's buyout</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Needs approach vs. human life value Needs = goals and liquid assets (best). Human life value = lost earnings only Key person vs. buy-sell Key person = protects the business from an employee's loss. Buy-sell = funds the purchase of an owner's share Cross-purchase vs. entity plan Cross-purchase = owners buy policies on each other (more policies). Entity = business buys policies (fewer policies) Stock redemption vs. entity plan Same concept in a close corporation (stock redemption is the corporate name) Deferred vs. immediate annuity Deferred = payments later. Immediate = payments within about a month Fixed vs. variable annuity Fixed = guaranteed rate, insurer bears risk. Variable = market-based, owner bears risk Straight life vs. life with period certain Straight = stops at death. Period certain = guaranteed minimum years Split-dollar vs. executive bonus Split-dollar = cost sharing (method). Executive bonus = employer pays a bonus that the employee uses for premiums Deferred compensation vs. salary continuation Deferred = future payment promised. Salary continuation = ongoing income, fully employer-funded Section 303 vs. buy-sell Section 303 = corporate stock redemption to pay estate taxes. Buy-sell = funds an owner's buyout"},{"id":"ch08-s12","title":"11. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the most effective method for determining the amount of life insurance?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> What is the human life value formula?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> In the needs approach, what do you subtract from total capital needs?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Does a key person policy cover the business owner or the employee?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> In a three-partner cross-purchase plan, how many policies are needed?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> In an entity plan with four partners, how many policies are needed?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What is the main purpose of a Section 303 redemption plan?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Who owns a key person policy?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the difference between an executive bonus plan and split-dollar insurance?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Are variable annuities sold with only an insurance license?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Ana is the sole proprietor of a bakery. She wants the business to continue after her death. Her employee is named as buyer. Is a buy-sell plan the right tool? What happens to the business when a sole proprietor dies?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Three equal partners use a cross-purchase plan. Partner A dies. How do the survivors fund the buyout?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A corporation buys life insurance on its top salesperson to protect against the loss of her sales. What type of insurance is this, and who receives the proceeds?</li></ul>","text":"What is the most effective method for determining the amount of life insurance? What is the human life value formula? In the needs approach, what do you subtract from total capital needs? Does a key person policy cover the business owner or the employee? In a three-partner cross-purchase plan, how many policies are needed? In an entity plan with four partners, how many policies are needed? What is the main purpose of a Section 303 redemption plan? Who owns a key person policy? What is the difference between an executive bonus plan and split-dollar insurance? Are variable annuities sold with only an insurance license? Scenario questions Scenario A: Ana is the sole proprietor of a bakery. She wants the business to continue after her death. Her employee is named as buyer. Is a buy-sell plan the right tool? What happens to the business when a sole proprietor dies? Scenario B: Three equal partners use a cross-purchase plan. Partner A dies. How do the survivors fund the buyout? Scenario C: A corporation buys life insurance on its top salesperson to protect against the loss of her sales. What type of insurance is this, and who receives the proceeds?"},{"id":"ch08-s13","title":"12. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> The Needs Approach.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> (Annual earned income − expenses) × years until retirement.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Liquid assets (savings, pensions, existing life proceeds, Social Security, income-producing assets).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> The employee (the insured). The business is the owner and beneficiary.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Six policies. Each of the three partners buys policies on the other two (3 × 2 = 6).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Four policies, one on each partner, owned by the partnership.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> To let the corporation redeem stock to pay estate taxes, so the business is not forced to sell assets.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> The business (third-party ownership).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Split-dollar is a method of sharing the cost of a policy. An executive bonus plan is a non-qualified bonus that the employee uses to pay premiums on a policy the employee owns.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> No. They require a FINRA license and a state insurance license.</p><p>Scenario A: Yes, a buy-sell can work, but a sole proprietorship usually ends when the owner dies. The plan would have an employee buy the business from the estate. Ana should confirm with an attorney.</p><p>Scenario B: The surviving partners each buy a share of the deceased partner's interest, using the policies they each own on the deceased partner (each survivor owns one policy on A).</p><p>Scenario C: Key person insurance. The corporation is the owner and the beneficiary.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick for Chapter 8:</strong> Build one table with three columns (Needs / Key person / Buy-sell) and fill in: who owns, who is insured, who is paid, and why. Then do the 4-partner math out loud: 4 entity policies vs. 12 cross-purchase policies.</p></div>","text":"The Needs Approach. (Annual earned income − expenses) × years until retirement. Liquid assets (savings, pensions, existing life proceeds, Social Security, income-producing assets). The employee (the insured). The business is the owner and beneficiary. Six policies. Each of the three partners buys policies on the other two (3 × 2 = 6). Four policies, one on each partner, owned by the partnership. To let the corporation redeem stock to pay estate taxes, so the business is not forced to sell assets. The business (third-party ownership). Split-dollar is a method of sharing the cost of a policy. An executive bonus plan is a non-qualified bonus that the employee uses to pay premiums on a policy the employee owns. No. They require a FINRA license and a state insurance license. Scenario A: Yes, a buy-sell can work, but a sole proprietorship usually ends when the owner dies. The plan would have an employee buy the business from the estate. Ana should confirm with an attorney. Scenario B: The surviving partners each buy a share of the deceased partner's interest, using the policies they each own on the deceased partner (each survivor owns one policy on A). Scenario C: Key person insurance. The corporation is the owner and the beneficiary. Memory trick for Chapter 8: Build one table with three columns (Needs / Key person / Buy-sell) and fill in: who owns, who is insured, who is paid, and why. Then do the 4-partner math out loud: 4 entity policies vs. 12 cross-purchase policies."}]},{"id":"ch09","short":"Ch 9","title":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","label":"Chapter 9","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Life insurance CREATES an estate. An annuity LIQUIDATES one (it turns savings into income). Learn the two phases, fixed vs. variable, immediate vs. deferred, the payout options, the tax rules, and the suitability duties.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch09-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Accumulation phase</strong> (saving, tax-deferred) → <strong>annuity (payout) phase</strong> (income).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fixed</strong> = insurer's general account, guaranteed, insurer takes the risk. <strong>Variable</strong> = separate account, owner takes the risk, securities license required.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Period certain = guaranteed TIME. Refund = guaranteed MONEY.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Early withdrawals can trigger BOTH a surrender charge AND a 10% tax penalty.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Accumulation phase (saving, tax-deferred) → annuity (payout) phase (income). Fixed = insurer's general account, guaranteed, insurer takes the risk. Variable = separate account, owner takes the risk, securities license required. Period certain = guaranteed TIME. Refund = guaranteed MONEY. Early withdrawals can trigger BOTH a surrender charge AND a 10% tax penalty."},{"id":"ch09-s2","title":"1. What an annuity is","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A contract that provides periodic payments to protect against outliving your income.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Life insurance</strong> creates an estate (lump sum at death). <strong>Annuity</strong> liquidates an estate (converts savings into payments).</li></ul><h4>Regulation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fixed annuities:</strong> State insurance department.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Variable annuities:</strong> State insurance department AND the SEC and FINRA. Must provide a <strong>prospectus</strong> before sale. The seller needs a life insurance license plus a <strong>Series 6 or Series 7</strong> securities license.</li></ul>","text":"A contract that provides periodic payments to protect against outliving your income. Life insurance creates an estate (lump sum at death). Annuity liquidates an estate (converts savings into payments). Regulation Fixed annuities: State insurance department. Variable annuities: State insurance department AND the SEC and FINRA. Must provide a prospectus before sale. The seller needs a life insurance license plus a Series 6 or Series 7 securities license."},{"id":"ch09-s3","title":"2. The two phases","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>What happens</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Accumulation (pay-in)</strong></td><td>Owner pays premiums; interest grows tax-deferred</td><td>Can surrender for current value. If the owner dies, the beneficiary gets contributions plus interest (NOT a life-insurance-style face amount). The phase does not end just because premiums stop</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Annuity (payout)</strong></td><td>The owner becomes the &quot;annuitant&quot; and receives regular income</td><td>Cannot surrender once annuitized. Each payment = principal + interest</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Phase What happens Key facts Accumulation (pay-in) Owner pays premiums; interest grows tax-deferred Can surrender for current value. If the owner dies, the beneficiary gets contributions plus interest (NOT a life-insurance-style face amount). The phase does not end just because premiums stop Annuity (payout) The owner becomes the \"annuitant\" and receives regular income Cannot surrender once annuitized. Each payment = principal + interest"},{"id":"ch09-s4","title":"3. Key parties","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Insurer</strong></td><td>Issues the contract and makes the payments</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contract owner</strong></td><td>Buys and funds the annuity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Annuitant</strong></td><td>Receives the income (often the same as the owner). Whose life determines payment length</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Beneficiary</strong></td><td>Receives the funds if the owner dies during accumulation. If none is named, the proceeds go to the owner's estate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Principal</strong></td><td>The amount invested through premiums</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Party Role Insurer Issues the contract and makes the payments Contract owner Buys and funds the annuity Annuitant Receives the income (often the same as the owner). Whose life determines payment length Beneficiary Receives the funds if the owner dies during accumulation. If none is named, the proceeds go to the owner's estate Principal The amount invested through premiums"},{"id":"ch09-s5","title":"4. Tax classification","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>NON-QUALIFIED</th><th>QUALIFIED</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Funded with</td><td>After-tax dollars</td><td>Pre-tax dollars through an employer plan (TSA, IRA, 401(k), pension)</td></tr><tr><td>Premium deduction</td><td>None</td><td>May be deductible; salary reduction allowed</td></tr><tr><td>Growth</td><td>Tax-deferred</td><td>Tax-deferred</td></tr><tr><td>Who can buy</td><td>Anyone</td><td>Through a qualified plan</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> ALL annuities grow tax-deferred. Only QUALIFIED annuities offer tax-deductible contributions.</p></div>","text":"NON-QUALIFIED QUALIFIED Funded with After-tax dollars Pre-tax dollars through an employer plan (TSA, IRA, 401(k), pension) Premium deduction None May be deductible; salary reduction allowed Growth Tax-deferred Tax-deferred Who can buy Anyone Through a qualified plan Exam tip: ALL annuities grow tax-deferred. Only QUALIFIED annuities offer tax-deductible contributions."},{"id":"ch09-s6","title":"5. Funding methods","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Single premium</strong></td><td>One lump sum. No more contributions allowed. Immediate or deferred</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Periodic premium, level</strong></td><td>Same amount each year</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Periodic premium, flexible</strong></td><td>Varies as the budget allows</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Annual premium retirement annuity</strong></td><td>Older contracts with high loads. Largely replaced by flexible premium deferred annuities (FPDAs)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Method Key facts Single premium One lump sum. No more contributions allowed. Immediate or deferred Periodic premium, level Same amount each year Periodic premium, flexible Varies as the budget allows Annual premium retirement annuity Older contracts with high loads. Largely replaced by flexible premium deferred annuities (FPDAs)"},{"id":"ch09-s7","title":"6. Timing of benefits","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>IMMEDIATE (SPIA)</th><th>DEFERRED (SPDA, FPDA)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Funding</td><td>Single lump sum</td><td>Single or periodic</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulation period</td><td>None</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>When payments start</td><td>First payment within 30 days (must start within 12 months)</td><td>Future date</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Income needed now (disabled, retiring)</td><td>Planning ahead</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Deferred annuity withdrawal options:</strong> (1) lump sum, (2) periodic withdrawals, (3) convert to the annuity phase.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Bailout provision (SPDA):</strong> Lets the owner withdraw without penalty if interest rates drop.</li></ul>","text":"IMMEDIATE (SPIA) DEFERRED (SPDA, FPDA) Funding Single lump sum Single or periodic Accumulation period None Yes When payments start First payment within 30 days (must start within 12 months) Future date Best for Income needed now (disabled, retiring) Planning ahead Deferred annuity withdrawal options: (1) lump sum, (2) periodic withdrawals, (3) convert to the annuity phase. Bailout provision (SPDA): Lets the owner withdraw without penalty if interest rates drop."},{"id":"ch09-s8","title":"7. Investment types: fixed vs. variable (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>FIXED</th><th>VARIABLE</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Account</td><td><strong>General account</strong></td><td><strong>Separate account</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Returns</td><td>Guaranteed minimum; predetermined</td><td>Not guaranteed; depends on investments</td></tr><tr><td>Investment risk</td><td>Insurer</td><td>Owner</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td>Fixed amount</td><td>Vary with performance</td></tr><tr><td>Inflation</td><td>Vulnerable</td><td>Better protection</td></tr><tr><td>Creditors</td><td>Insurer's general creditors can reach these assets</td><td>Separate account is protected from the insurer's creditors</td></tr><tr><td>License needed</td><td>Insurance only</td><td>Insurance AND Series 6 or 7</td></tr><tr><td>Regulation</td><td>State insurance</td><td>State insurance, SEC, FINRA; must be registered under the Investment Company Act of 1940</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>GENERAL account = FIXED. SEPARATE account = VARIABLE.</p></div><h4>Variable annuity subaccounts</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The separate account holds subaccounts that work like mutual funds (large-cap stock, bonds, money market).</li><li class=\"l0\">The owner can allocate payments and transfer between subaccounts.</li><li class=\"l0\">Some subaccounts may offer fixed rates guaranteed by the insurer.</li></ul><h4>Units</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Accumulation phase:</strong> Contributions buy accumulation units (value fluctuates).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annuity phase:</strong> Accumulation units convert to annuity units. The NUMBER of units is fixed; the value per unit varies.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Payment = fixed number of units × variable unit value.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Feature FIXED VARIABLE Account General account Separate account Returns Guaranteed minimum; predetermined Not guaranteed; depends on investments Investment risk Insurer Owner Payments Fixed amount Vary with performance Inflation Vulnerable Better protection Creditors Insurer's general creditors can reach these assets Separate account is protected from the insurer's creditors License needed Insurance only Insurance AND Series 6 or 7 Regulation State insurance State insurance, SEC, FINRA; must be registered under the Investment Company Act of 1940 Memory trick: GENERAL account = FIXED. SEPARATE account = VARIABLE. Variable annuity subaccounts The separate account holds subaccounts that work like mutual funds (large-cap stock, bonds, money market). The owner can allocate payments and transfer between subaccounts. Some subaccounts may offer fixed rates guaranteed by the insurer. Units Accumulation phase: Contributions buy accumulation units (value fluctuates). Annuity phase: Accumulation units convert to annuity units. The NUMBER of units is fixed; the value per unit varies. Payment = fixed number of units × variable unit value."},{"id":"ch09-s9","title":"8. Newer annuity types","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Equity-indexed annuity (EIA)</strong></td><td>A FIXED annuity. Interest linked to a stock index (such as the S&amp;P 500). Principal guaranteed. Has a guaranteed minimum rate. A participation rate sets how much index gain is credited. Upside, without downside</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Market value-adjusted annuity (MVA)</strong></td><td>Guaranteed rate for a set period. Held to maturity = works like a fixed annuity. Surrendered early = value adjusted for current interest rates. Higher rates than traditional annuities. Owner assumes some risk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Type Key facts Equity-indexed annuity (EIA) A FIXED annuity. Interest linked to a stock index (such as the S&P 500). Principal guaranteed. Has a guaranteed minimum rate. A participation rate sets how much index gain is credited. Upside, without downside Market value-adjusted annuity (MVA) Guaranteed rate for a set period. Held to maturity = works like a fixed annuity. Surrendered early = value adjusted for current interest rates. Higher rates than traditional annuities. Owner assumes some risk"},{"id":"ch09-s10","title":"9. Mortality factors","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Annuities use their own mortality tables (not the life insurance tables).</li><li class=\"l0\">The <strong>survivorship factor</strong> is used to calculate income payments from life expectancy.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Women receive smaller payments</strong> because they live longer.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Occupation and hobbies are NOT considered</strong> (unlike life insurance).</li></ul>","text":"Annuities use their own mortality tables (not the life insurance tables). The survivorship factor is used to calculate income payments from life expectancy. Women receive smaller payments because they live longer. Occupation and hobbies are NOT considered (unlike life insurance)."},{"id":"ch09-s11","title":"10. Payout options","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>How it pays</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Straight life (pure life)</strong></td><td>Lifetime payments only</td><td>Highest payment. NO refund to beneficiaries. Most risk for the annuitant</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Period certain</strong></td><td>Payments for a set period (10 or 20 years)</td><td>If the annuitant dies early, the beneficiary gets the rest. Payments stop after the period</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Life with period certain</strong></td><td>Payments for life, or for the minimum period if longer</td><td>If the annuitant dies within the period, the beneficiary gets the rest. If they outlive it, payments continue for life</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Life with refund</strong></td><td>Guarantees return of principal</td><td><strong>Installment refund:</strong> monthly payments continue to the beneficiary. <strong>Cash refund:</strong> remaining balance paid as a lump sum. Payments are LOWER (the guarantee reduces the payout)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Joint and survivor</strong></td><td>Payments for two people (usually spouses) until both die</td><td>Survivor option: 100% (full), two-thirds (66.7%), or one-half (50%)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>PERIOD CERTAIN guarantees TIME. REFUND guarantees MONEY. STRAIGHT LIFE pays the most, and stops at death.</p></div>","text":"Option How it pays Key facts Straight life (pure life) Lifetime payments only Highest payment. NO refund to beneficiaries. Most risk for the annuitant Period certain Payments for a set period (10 or 20 years) If the annuitant dies early, the beneficiary gets the rest. Payments stop after the period Life with period certain Payments for life, or for the minimum period if longer If the annuitant dies within the period, the beneficiary gets the rest. If they outlive it, payments continue for life Life with refund Guarantees return of principal Installment refund: monthly payments continue to the beneficiary. Cash refund: remaining balance paid as a lump sum. Payments are LOWER (the guarantee reduces the payout) Joint and survivor Payments for two people (usually spouses) until both die Survivor option: 100% (full), two-thirds (66.7%), or one-half (50%) Memory trick: PERIOD CERTAIN guarantees TIME. REFUND guarantees MONEY. STRAIGHT LIFE pays the most, and stops at death."},{"id":"ch09-s12","title":"11. Riders","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rider</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Long-term care rider</strong></td><td>Pays if the insured needs long-term care. No terminal illness required</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GMWB</strong> (guaranteed minimum withdrawal benefit)</td><td>Steady income regardless of the market. Withdraw a set percentage of the initial investment each year until it is depleted</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GMIB</strong> (guaranteed minimum income benefit)</td><td>Guaranteed minimum income after annuitization. MUST annuitize to trigger. Independent of market performance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GMAB</strong> (guaranteed minimum accumulation benefit)</td><td>Guaranteed minimum value after a set period. Does NOT require annuitization</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Rider Key facts Long-term care rider Pays if the insured needs long-term care. No terminal illness required GMWB (guaranteed minimum withdrawal benefit) Steady income regardless of the market. Withdraw a set percentage of the initial investment each year until it is depleted GMIB (guaranteed minimum income benefit) Guaranteed minimum income after annuitization. MUST annuitize to trigger. Independent of market performance GMAB (guaranteed minimum accumulation benefit) Guaranteed minimum value after a set period. Does NOT require annuitization"},{"id":"ch09-s13","title":"12. Surrender charges, penalties, and non-forfeiture","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Surrender charge</strong> (insurer)</td><td>Applies when withdrawing more than the free-withdrawal percentage. Declines over several years. Waived for death, disability, or nursing home care. A back-end load</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Federal 10% tax penalty</strong></td><td>On the EARNINGS portion of withdrawals before age 59½. Exceptions: death, disability, age 59½, immediate annuity, and qualified plans</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-forfeiture value</strong></td><td>Annuity value minus surrender charges</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>EXAM TIP:</strong> You can face BOTH a surrender charge AND the 10% tax penalty.</p></div>","text":"Item Key facts Surrender charge (insurer) Applies when withdrawing more than the free-withdrawal percentage. Declines over several years. Waived for death, disability, or nursing home care. A back-end load Federal 10% tax penalty On the EARNINGS portion of withdrawals before age 59½. Exceptions: death, disability, age 59½, immediate annuity, and qualified plans Non-forfeiture value Annuity value minus surrender charges EXAM TIP: You can face BOTH a surrender charge AND the 10% tax penalty."},{"id":"ch09-s14","title":"13. Taxation","html":"<h4>During accumulation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Earnings are tax-deferred. No tax until withdrawal.</li></ul><h4>Withdrawals</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>LIFO</strong> (last in, first out): earnings come out first and are taxed first. Then the principal comes back tax-free.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exception:</strong> Contracts issued BEFORE August 14, 1982 use FIFO.</li></ul><h4>At annuitization</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The <strong>exclusion ratio</strong> sets the tax-free share of each payment.</li><li class=\"l0\">Formula: <strong>Total investment ÷ expected return.</strong> That percentage of each payment is tax-free. The rest is ordinary income.</li></ul><h4>If the owner dies during accumulation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The beneficiary gets the greater of the accumulated value or total contributions.</li><li class=\"l0\">The amount above contributions is taxable.</li><li class=\"l0\">The beneficiary can choose installments to spread the tax.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Must decide within 60 days.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Annuity proceeds are generally included in the deceased's estate.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> The annuity death payment is contributions plus interest. It is NOT like a life insurance face amount.</p></div><h4>1035 exchanges (tax-free)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Allowed (no tax)</th><th>NOT allowed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Annuity → annuity</td><td>Annuity → life insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Life insurance → annuity</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Endowment → annuity</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">No money is received during the exchange. Cost basis carries over. Taxes are deferred, not eliminated.</li><li class=\"l0\">Can be within the same company or between companies.</li></ul>","text":"During accumulation Earnings are tax-deferred. No tax until withdrawal. Withdrawals LIFO (last in, first out): earnings come out first and are taxed first. Then the principal comes back tax-free. Exception: Contracts issued BEFORE August 14, 1982 use FIFO. At annuitization The exclusion ratio sets the tax-free share of each payment. Formula: Total investment ÷ expected return. That percentage of each payment is tax-free. The rest is ordinary income. If the owner dies during accumulation The beneficiary gets the greater of the accumulated value or total contributions. The amount above contributions is taxable. The beneficiary can choose installments to spread the tax. Must decide within 60 days. Annuity proceeds are generally included in the deceased's estate. Exam tip: The annuity death payment is contributions plus interest. It is NOT like a life insurance face amount. 1035 exchanges (tax-free) Allowed (no tax) NOT allowed Annuity → annuity Annuity → life insurance Life insurance → annuity Endowment → annuity No money is received during the exchange. Cost basis carries over. Taxes are deferred, not eliminated. Can be within the same company or between companies."},{"id":"ch09-s15","title":"14. Qualified plans using annuities","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Who it covers / key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>403(b) / TSA</strong></td><td>Employees of public schools and Section 501(c)(3) nonprofits. Contributions excluded from taxable income; payouts taxed as ordinary income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>401(k)</strong></td><td>Corporate defined contribution plan</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IRA</strong></td><td>Individual retirement annuity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pension plans</strong></td><td>Defined benefit plans</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Keogh and SEP</strong></td><td>Self-employed and small-business retirement plans</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Defined contribution:</strong> Sets the contribution amount (like a 401(k)).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Defined benefit:</strong> Sets the benefit amount (like a pension).</li></ul>","text":"Plan Who it covers / key facts 403(b) / TSA Employees of public schools and Section 501(c)(3) nonprofits. Contributions excluded from taxable income; payouts taxed as ordinary income 401(k) Corporate defined contribution plan IRA Individual retirement annuity Pension plans Defined benefit plans Keogh and SEP Self-employed and small-business retirement plans Defined contribution: Sets the contribution amount (like a 401(k)). Defined benefit: Sets the benefit amount (like a pension)."},{"id":"ch09-s16","title":"15. Corporate-owned annuities","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The corporation must name a <strong>natural person</strong> as annuitant.</li><li class=\"l0\">A non-natural annuitant (such as a corporation): interest is taxed as ordinary income each year.</li><li class=\"l0\">A natural-person annuitant: interest is tax-deferred. Also called a &quot;measurable life.&quot;</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exceptions to the natural person rule:</strong> annuity held by a trust or corporation as agent for a natural person; annuity acquired by an estate after death; annuity held under a qualified plan (TSA, IRA); immediate annuity with a single premium.</li></ul>","text":"The corporation must name a natural person as annuitant. A non-natural annuitant (such as a corporation): interest is taxed as ordinary income each year. A natural-person annuitant: interest is tax-deferred. Also called a \"measurable life.\" Exceptions to the natural person rule: annuity held by a trust or corporation as agent for a natural person; annuity acquired by an estate after death; annuity held under a qualified plan (TSA, IRA); immediate annuity with a single premium."},{"id":"ch09-s17","title":"16. Other uses","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Structured settlements:</strong> Lawsuit payments or lottery winnings paid over time instead of a lump sum. Buyers purchase them at a discount (present value).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Education funds:</strong> Steady income for a child's education.</li></ul>","text":"Structured settlements: Lawsuit payments or lottery winnings paid over time instead of a lump sum. Buyers purchase them at a discount (present value). Education funds: Steady income for a child's education."},{"id":"ch09-s18","title":"17. Suitability","html":"<p>Agents must make sure the recommendation fits:</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Age of the applicant and spouse</li><li class=\"l0\">Annual household income</li><li class=\"l0\">Financial situation, needs, experience, and objectives</li><li class=\"l0\">Intended use and time horizon</li><li class=\"l0\">Existing assets and insurance</li><li class=\"l0\">Liquidity needs</li><li class=\"l0\">Risk tolerance</li><li class=\"l0\">Tax status</li></ul><p><strong>Senior consumers (age 65+):</strong> Special suitability standards apply. The agent must make reasonable efforts to collect information and take extra care with the recommendation.</p><p><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Regulation 89 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 117) sets a best-interest standard for annuity sales and recommendations. It was amended effective September 20, 2024. Producers who sell annuities must complete a one-time 4-hour annuity training course.</p>","text":"Agents must make sure the recommendation fits: Age of the applicant and spouse Annual household income Financial situation, needs, experience, and objectives Intended use and time horizon Existing assets and insurance Liquidity needs Risk tolerance Tax status Senior consumers (age 65+): Special suitability standards apply. The agent must make reasonable efforts to collect information and take extra care with the recommendation. Louisiana: Regulation 89 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 117) sets a best-interest standard for annuity sales and recommendations. It was amended effective September 20, 2024. Producers who sell annuities must complete a one-time 4-hour annuity training course."},{"id":"ch09-s19","title":"18. Quick comparison tables","html":"<h4>Fixed vs. variable</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>FIXED</th><th>VARIABLE</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Account</td><td>General</td><td>Separate</td></tr><tr><td>Returns</td><td>Guaranteed</td><td>Not guaranteed</td></tr><tr><td>Investment risk</td><td>Insurer</td><td>Owner</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Vary</td></tr><tr><td>Inflation</td><td>Vulnerable</td><td>Protected</td></tr><tr><td>License</td><td>Insurance only</td><td>Insurance + Series 6/7</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Immediate vs. deferred</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>IMMEDIATE</th><th>DEFERRED</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Funding</td><td>Single premium</td><td>Single or periodic</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulation</td><td>None</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Payments start</td><td>Within 30 days</td><td>Future date</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Income now</td><td>Planning ahead</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Fixed vs. variable Feature FIXED VARIABLE Account General Separate Returns Guaranteed Not guaranteed Investment risk Insurer Owner Payments Fixed Vary Inflation Vulnerable Protected License Insurance only Insurance + Series 6/7 Immediate vs. deferred Feature IMMEDIATE DEFERRED Funding Single premium Single or periodic Accumulation None Yes Payments start Within 30 days Future date Best for Income now Planning ahead"},{"id":"ch09-s20","title":"19. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Annuity vs. life insurance</td><td>Annuity liquidates an estate (income). Life insurance creates an estate (death benefit)</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed vs. variable</td><td>General account, guaranteed (insurer risk) vs. separate account, not guaranteed (owner risk)</td></tr><tr><td>Period certain vs. refund</td><td>Period certain = guaranteed time. Refund = guaranteed money returned</td></tr><tr><td>Straight life vs. life with period certain</td><td>Straight life = payments stop at death. Period certain = guaranteed minimum years</td></tr><tr><td>GMWB vs. GMIB</td><td>GMWB = withdrawals, no annuitization needed. GMIB = minimum income, must annuitize</td></tr><tr><td>GMIB vs. GMAB</td><td>GMIB needs annuitization. GMAB does not</td></tr><tr><td>Surrender charge vs. 10% tax penalty</td><td>Surrender charge = insurer's fee. 10% penalty = IRS tax on earnings before 59½</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified vs. non-qualified</td><td>Pre-tax employer plan vs. after-tax personal purchase</td></tr><tr><td>Immediate vs. deferred</td><td>Income now vs. income later</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulation unit vs. annuity unit</td><td>Accumulation = value fluctuates during savings. Annuity = number of units fixed at payout</td></tr><tr><td>General vs. separate account</td><td>Fixed/insurer's assets vs. variable/protected from insurer creditors</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Annuity vs. life insurance Annuity liquidates an estate (income). Life insurance creates an estate (death benefit) Fixed vs. variable General account, guaranteed (insurer risk) vs. separate account, not guaranteed (owner risk) Period certain vs. refund Period certain = guaranteed time. Refund = guaranteed money returned Straight life vs. life with period certain Straight life = payments stop at death. Period certain = guaranteed minimum years GMWB vs. GMIB GMWB = withdrawals, no annuitization needed. GMIB = minimum income, must annuitize GMIB vs. GMAB GMIB needs annuitization. GMAB does not Surrender charge vs. 10% tax penalty Surrender charge = insurer's fee. 10% penalty = IRS tax on earnings before 59½ Qualified vs. non-qualified Pre-tax employer plan vs. after-tax personal purchase Immediate vs. deferred Income now vs. income later Accumulation unit vs. annuity unit Accumulation = value fluctuates during savings. Annuity = number of units fixed at payout General vs. separate account Fixed/insurer's assets vs. variable/protected from insurer creditors"},{"id":"ch09-s21","title":"20. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the key difference between a life insurance policy and an annuity?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> What are the two phases of an annuity?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Which account holds a variable annuity's assets?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Which license does a variable annuity seller need, in addition to the insurance license?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> In what order are withdrawals taxed (LIFO)?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What does the 10% penalty apply to?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What is the exclusion ratio formula?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Within what time must a beneficiary decide how to take the death proceeds from an annuity?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the difference between &quot;period certain&quot; and &quot;refund&quot; guarantees?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Can an annuity be exchanged for life insurance tax-free?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Ruth is 68 and wants a lifetime income with no refund. Which payout option fits, and what is its drawback?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Dan, age 45, withdraws $20,000 from a deferred annuity. Half of that is earnings. He is penalized by the surrender charge and the IRS. Explain both.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A corporation buys an annuity. The annuitant is the corporation itself. How is the interest taxed?</li></ul>","text":"What is the key difference between a life insurance policy and an annuity? What are the two phases of an annuity? Which account holds a variable annuity's assets? Which license does a variable annuity seller need, in addition to the insurance license? In what order are withdrawals taxed (LIFO)? What does the 10% penalty apply to? What is the exclusion ratio formula? Within what time must a beneficiary decide how to take the death proceeds from an annuity? What is the difference between \"period certain\" and \"refund\" guarantees? Can an annuity be exchanged for life insurance tax-free? Scenario questions Scenario A: Ruth is 68 and wants a lifetime income with no refund. Which payout option fits, and what is its drawback? Scenario B: Dan, age 45, withdraws $20,000 from a deferred annuity. Half of that is earnings. He is penalized by the surrender charge and the IRS. Explain both. Scenario C: A corporation buys an annuity. The annuitant is the corporation itself. How is the interest taxed?"},{"id":"ch09-s22","title":"21. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Life insurance creates an estate (death benefit). An annuity liquidates an estate (turns savings into income).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Accumulation (pay-in) and annuity (payout).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> The separate account.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Series 6 or Series 7.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Earnings first (taxable), then principal (tax-free).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> The earnings portion of withdrawals before age 59½.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Total investment ÷ expected return.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Within 60 days.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Period certain guarantees the number of years. Refund guarantees the return of money.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> No. Annuity-to-life-insurance exchanges are not allowed. Life insurance or an endowment to an annuity is allowed.</p><p>Scenario A: Straight life annuity. Drawback: payments stop at death and there is no refund to beneficiaries.</p><p>Scenario B: The surrender charge is the insurer's fee for withdrawing more than the free amount. The IRS 10% penalty applies to the earnings portion of a withdrawal before 59½. Dan may face both.</p><p>Scenario C: The interest is taxed as ordinary income each year, because a non-natural person is the annuitant.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick for Chapter 9:</strong> Make a two-column card: FIXED (general account, guaranteed) and VARIABLE (separate account, owner risk). Then list the five payout options with one sentence each. Quiz yourself on the 1035 exchange chart until you can say it from memory.</p></div>","text":"Life insurance creates an estate (death benefit). An annuity liquidates an estate (turns savings into income). Accumulation (pay-in) and annuity (payout). The separate account. Series 6 or Series 7. Earnings first (taxable), then principal (tax-free). The earnings portion of withdrawals before age 59½. Total investment ÷ expected return. Within 60 days. Period certain guarantees the number of years. Refund guarantees the return of money. No. Annuity-to-life-insurance exchanges are not allowed. Life insurance or an endowment to an annuity is allowed. Scenario A: Straight life annuity. Drawback: payments stop at death and there is no refund to beneficiaries. Scenario B: The surrender charge is the insurer's fee for withdrawing more than the free amount. The IRS 10% penalty applies to the earnings portion of a withdrawal before 59½. Dan may face both. Scenario C: The interest is taxed as ordinary income each year, because a non-natural person is the annuitant. Memory trick for Chapter 9: Make a two-column card: FIXED (general account, guaranteed) and VARIABLE (separate account, owner risk). Then list the five payout options with one sentence each. Quiz yourself on the 1035 exchange chart until you can say it from memory."}]},{"id":"ch10","short":"Ch 10","title":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","label":"Chapter 10","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Social Security (OASDHI/OASDI) is a federal social insurance program paid for by FICA taxes. Learn the three big ideas: <strong>insured status</strong> (who qualifies), <strong>benefit math</strong> (PIA, FRA, early and delayed claiming), and <strong>survivor and disability rules</strong>. Use the 2026 figures in this chapter.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch10-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fully insured = 40 quarters (10 years). Currently insured = 6 of the last 13 quarters.</strong> Currently insured gets survivor benefits ONLY.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>PIA</strong> = the full benefit at Full Retirement Age (FRA). <strong>AIME</strong> = the earnings average used to compute PIA.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Disability</strong> = TOTAL disability, lasting 12+ months (or resulting in death), with a 5-month waiting period.</li></ul>","text":"Fully insured = 40 quarters (10 years). Currently insured = 6 of the last 13 quarters. Currently insured gets survivor benefits ONLY. PIA = the full benefit at Full Retirement Age (FRA). AIME = the earnings average used to compute PIA. Disability = TOTAL disability, lasting 12+ months (or resulting in death), with a 5-month waiting period."},{"id":"ch10-s2","title":"1. What Social Security is","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Official names:</strong> OASDHI (Old Age, Survivors, Disability, and Health Insurance) or OASDI (same program without the health piece in the name).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Created:</strong> 1935 under the Social Security Act, during the Great Depression.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Amendments:</strong> 1939 added survivor benefits and benefits for a retiree's spouse and children. 1956 added disability benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Nature:</strong> An ENTITLEMENT program (you pay in, you receive benefits later). Not a welfare program.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Supplements personal insurance and savings. It does not replace them. The Act covers unemployment, disability, survivors, and old age, not only welfare for people aged 65 and older.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Benefits:</strong> Retirement income, disability income, survivor benefits, and a small lump-sum death benefit.</li></ul>","text":"Official names: OASDHI (Old Age, Survivors, Disability, and Health Insurance) or OASDI (same program without the health piece in the name). Created: 1935 under the Social Security Act, during the Great Depression. Amendments: 1939 added survivor benefits and benefits for a retiree's spouse and children. 1956 added disability benefits. Nature: An ENTITLEMENT program (you pay in, you receive benefits later). Not a welfare program. Purpose: Supplements personal insurance and savings. It does not replace them. The Act covers unemployment, disability, survivors, and old age, not only welfare for people aged 65 and older. Benefits: Retirement income, disability income, survivor benefits, and a small lump-sum death benefit."},{"id":"ch10-s3","title":"2. Funding: FICA taxes","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Who pays</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employee</td><td>7.65%</td></tr><tr><td>Employer</td><td>7.65%</td></tr><tr><td>Self-employed</td><td>15.3% (pays both halves)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">FICA Social Security tax applies only up to the <strong>taxable wage base</strong>, indexed each year.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>2026 taxable maximum: $184,500</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare tax has NO maximum.</strong> All earned income is subject to Medicare tax (Part A).</li><li class=\"l0\">If you don't pay FICA through payroll or self-employment, you don't earn Social Security benefits.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Self-employed = &quot;double&quot; (15.3%) because you are both the employer and the employee.</p></div>","text":"Who pays Rate Employee 7.65% Employer 7.65% Self-employed 15.3% (pays both halves) FICA Social Security tax applies only up to the taxable wage base, indexed each year. 2026 taxable maximum: $184,500. Medicare tax has NO maximum. All earned income is subject to Medicare tax (Part A). If you don't pay FICA through payroll or self-employment, you don't earn Social Security benefits. Memory trick: Self-employed = \"double\" (15.3%) because you are both the employer and the employee."},{"id":"ch10-s4","title":"3. Who is covered?","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Almost all employed and self-employed Americans are covered through FICA.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>NOT covered:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\">Most federal employees hired before 1984 (they have the Civil Service Retirement System).</li><li class=\"l1\">About 25% of state and local government employees (they have a state pension).</li><li class=\"l1\">Railroad workers (they have Railroad Retirement).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Covered does NOT mean eligible.</strong> You must meet insured status requirements for each benefit.</li></ul>","text":"Almost all employed and self-employed Americans are covered through FICA. NOT covered: Most federal employees hired before 1984 (they have the Civil Service Retirement System). About 25% of state and local government employees (they have a state pension). Railroad workers (they have Railroad Retirement). Covered does NOT mean eligible. You must meet insured status requirements for each benefit."},{"id":"ch10-s5","title":"4. Insured status (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>FULLY INSURED</th><th>CURRENTLY INSURED</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Requirement</td><td><strong>40 quarters</strong> (credits), about 10 years of work</td><td><strong>6 of the last 13 quarters</strong>, about 3 years</td></tr><tr><td>Quarters</td><td>Need not be consecutive. Max <strong>4 per year</strong></td><td>Recent work</td></tr><tr><td>Retirement benefits</td><td>YES</td><td>NO</td></tr><tr><td>Disability benefits</td><td>YES (if the disability criteria are met)</td><td>NO</td></tr><tr><td>Survivor benefits</td><td>YES</td><td>YES (survivor benefits ONLY)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>FULLY = FULL retirement and disability. CURRENTLY = only the family gets death benefits.</p></div>","text":"FULLY INSURED CURRENTLY INSURED Requirement 40 quarters (credits), about 10 years of work 6 of the last 13 quarters, about 3 years Quarters Need not be consecutive. Max 4 per year Recent work Retirement benefits YES NO Disability benefits YES (if the disability criteria are met) NO Survivor benefits YES YES (survivor benefits ONLY) Memory trick: FULLY = FULL retirement and disability. CURRENTLY = only the family gets death benefits."},{"id":"ch10-s6","title":"5. Benefit calculations","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>AIME</strong> (Average Indexed Monthly Earnings)</td><td>Average monthly earnings during working years, adjusted to current dollars. Used to calculate the PIA</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PIA</strong> (Primary Insurance Amount)</td><td>The FULL benefit at FRA (100%). All other benefits are calculated from the PIA</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>AIME = the INPUT (earnings). PIA = the OUTPUT (benefit).</p></div>","text":"Term Meaning AIME (Average Indexed Monthly Earnings) Average monthly earnings during working years, adjusted to current dollars. Used to calculate the PIA PIA (Primary Insurance Amount) The FULL benefit at FRA (100%). All other benefits are calculated from the PIA Memory trick: AIME = the INPUT (earnings). PIA = the OUTPUT (benefit)."},{"id":"ch10-s7","title":"6. Retirement benefits","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Must be fully insured.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Must APPLY.</strong> Benefits are not automatic.</li><li class=\"l0\">Payments begin the first day of the month you reach eligibility.</li></ul><h4>Full retirement age (FRA)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Birth year</th><th>FRA</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Before 1938</td><td>65</td></tr><tr><td>1938 to 1959</td><td>Increases gradually, a few months per year</td></tr><tr><td>1960 or later</td><td><strong>67</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Early and delayed claiming</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Claim at 62:</strong> Benefit is permanently reduced. For FRA 67 (born 1960 or later), claiming at 62 pays about <strong>70% of PIA</strong>. (The 80% figure applies only to FRA 65.) The reduction is permanent.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Delayed retirement credits:</strong> Each month past FRA, up to age 70, adds to the benefit. Benefits stop growing at 70.</li></ul>","text":"Must be fully insured. Must APPLY. Benefits are not automatic. Payments begin the first day of the month you reach eligibility. Full retirement age (FRA) Birth year FRA Before 1938 65 1938 to 1959 Increases gradually, a few months per year 1960 or later 67 Early and delayed claiming Claim at 62: Benefit is permanently reduced. For FRA 67 (born 1960 or later), claiming at 62 pays about 70% of PIA. (The 80% figure applies only to FRA 65.) The reduction is permanent. Delayed retirement credits: Each month past FRA, up to age 70, adds to the benefit. Benefits stop growing at 70."},{"id":"ch10-s8","title":"7. Earnings test (retirement)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation in 2026</th><th>Rule</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Under FRA for the whole year</td><td>$1 withheld for every $2 earned above the limit</td><td><strong>$24,480</strong> a year</td></tr><tr><td>Reaching FRA in 2026</td><td>$1 withheld for every $3 above the limit. Only earnings BEFORE the FRA month count</td><td><strong>$65,160</strong> a year</td></tr><tr><td>From the FRA month onward</td><td><strong>No earnings limit</strong></td><td>None</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The year you reach FRA uses a higher limit and a $1-for-$3 rule, and counts only months before FRA.</li><li class=\"l0\">The earnings test is about the WORK income of someone receiving benefits. It is not a tax.</li></ul><h4>Worked example (2026 limit)</h4><p>Jane is 63 and gets a $1,200 monthly benefit. She earns $25,000 in 2026.</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Limit: $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $25,000 − $24,480 = $520.</li><li class=\"l0\">Reduction: $520 ÷ 2 = $260 for the year.</li><li class=\"l0\">Annual benefit without reduction: $14,400. After reduction: $14,400 − $260 = $14,140.</li></ul>","text":"Situation in 2026 Rule Limit Under FRA for the whole year $1 withheld for every $2 earned above the limit $24,480 a year Reaching FRA in 2026 $1 withheld for every $3 above the limit. Only earnings BEFORE the FRA month count $65,160 a year From the FRA month onward No earnings limit None The year you reach FRA uses a higher limit and a $1-for-$3 rule, and counts only months before FRA. The earnings test is about the WORK income of someone receiving benefits. It is not a tax. Worked example (2026 limit) Jane is 63 and gets a $1,200 monthly benefit. She earns $25,000 in 2026. Limit: $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $25,000 − $24,480 = $520. Reduction: $520 ÷ 2 = $260 for the year. Annual benefit without reduction: $14,400. After reduction: $14,400 − $260 = $14,140."},{"id":"ch10-s9","title":"8. Survivor and death benefits","html":"<h4>Lump-sum death benefit</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>$255</strong>. This amount has not changed for decades.</li><li class=\"l0\">Paid to an eligible spouse, or in some cases to children.</li><li class=\"l0\">Requires a fully or currently insured deceased worker.</li></ul><h4>Monthly survivor benefits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The survivor benefit equals the deceased worker's PIA.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Surviving spouse with no children:</strong> Can start at <strong>age 60</strong>. Reduced if taken before 65.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Spouse of any age with children under 16</strong> (or a disabled child over 15): can receive benefits.</li></ul><h4>Who can receive survivor benefits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Spouse (see above).</li><li class=\"l0\">Unmarried children under 18 (or under 19 if still in high school).</li><li class=\"l0\">Disabled children of any age, if disabled before age 22 and still disabled.</li><li class=\"l0\">Parents age 62 or older, if the deceased provided at least half their support.</li></ul><h4>Benefit amounts</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Recipient</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Child</td><td>75% of the worker's PIA</td></tr><tr><td>Parent (one dependent parent)</td><td>82.5% of PIA</td></tr><tr><td>Each of two dependent parents</td><td>75% of PIA each</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The 82.5% figure applies to ONE dependent parent. When two parents both qualify, each receives 75%.</li></ul>","text":"Lump-sum death benefit $255. This amount has not changed for decades. Paid to an eligible spouse, or in some cases to children. Requires a fully or currently insured deceased worker. Monthly survivor benefits The survivor benefit equals the deceased worker's PIA. Surviving spouse with no children: Can start at age 60. Reduced if taken before 65. Spouse of any age with children under 16 (or a disabled child over 15): can receive benefits. Who can receive survivor benefits Spouse (see above). Unmarried children under 18 (or under 19 if still in high school). Disabled children of any age, if disabled before age 22 and still disabled. Parents age 62 or older, if the deceased provided at least half their support. Benefit amounts Recipient Amount Child 75% of the worker's PIA Parent (one dependent parent) 82.5% of PIA Each of two dependent parents 75% of PIA each The 82.5% figure applies to ONE dependent parent. When two parents both qualify, each receives 75%."},{"id":"ch10-s10","title":"9. Blackout period","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> A time when a surviving spouse receives NO Social Security benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Starts:</strong> When the youngest child turns <strong>16</strong>, OR immediately if there are no children.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Ends:</strong> When the surviving spouse reaches <strong>age 60</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">During the blackout, the children's benefits continue until 18 (or 19 if in high school).</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Blackout starts at 16 (spouse loses benefits), not 18. Children's benefits run to 18.</p></div>","text":"Definition: A time when a surviving spouse receives NO Social Security benefits. Starts: When the youngest child turns 16, OR immediately if there are no children. Ends: When the surviving spouse reaches age 60. During the blackout, the children's benefits continue until 18 (or 19 if in high school). Memory trick: Blackout starts at 16 (spouse loses benefits), not 18. Children's benefits run to 18."},{"id":"ch10-s11","title":"10. Maximum family benefit","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A cap on the total benefits payable to one family when several members qualify.</li><li class=\"l0\">Calculated from the worker's PIA.</li></ul>","text":"A cap on the total benefits payable to one family when several members qualify. Calculated from the worker's PIA."},{"id":"ch10-s12","title":"11. Dual benefit rule","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A person eligible for two benefits can collect only ONE.</li><li class=\"l0\">They receive the higher amount. Example: a surviving spouse can receive their own retirement or the survivor benefit, not both at the same time.</li></ul>","text":"A person eligible for two benefits can collect only ONE. They receive the higher amount. Example: a surviving spouse can receive their own retirement or the survivor benefit, not both at the same time."},{"id":"ch10-s13","title":"12. Disability benefits","html":"<h4>Two federal programs</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Based on</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SSDI</strong> (Social Security Disability Insurance)</td><td>Work credits</td><td>Covered workers who are insured with sufficient work history</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SSI</strong> (Supplemental Security Income)</td><td>Financial need</td><td>Low income. No work history needed. Must still prove disability</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Other sources</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">State-sponsored disability programs.</li><li class=\"l0\">Workers' compensation.</li></ul><h4>SSDI eligibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Must be <strong>fully insured</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Work test: <strong>20 of the last 40 quarters</strong> (for workers age 31 and older).</li><li class=\"l0\">Must meet SSA's strict definition of disability.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>5-month waiting period.</strong> Benefits begin in the 6th month after disability is determined.</li></ul><h4>Definition of disability (SSA)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>TOTAL disability:</strong> Cannot perform substantial gainful activity (SGA) in any job that exists in the national economy.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must be a medically determinable physical or mental impairment.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must last <strong>at least 12 months</strong>, or be expected to result in death.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>No partial disability and no short-term disability benefits.</strong></li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>2026 SGA:</strong> <strong>$1,690 a month</strong> (non-blind); <strong>$2,830 a month</strong> (blind).</p></div><h4>Important limits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">SSDI benefits end the month you reach FRA and convert to retirement benefits. FRA is 66 or 67 for most people now, not 65.</li><li class=\"l0\">Based on the worker's PIA. Paid to the worker, spouse, and children.</li></ul>","text":"Two federal programs Program Based on Key facts SSDI (Social Security Disability Insurance) Work credits Covered workers who are insured with sufficient work history SSI (Supplemental Security Income) Financial need Low income. No work history needed. Must still prove disability Other sources State-sponsored disability programs. Workers' compensation. SSDI eligibility Must be fully insured. Work test: 20 of the last 40 quarters (for workers age 31 and older). Must meet SSA's strict definition of disability. 5-month waiting period. Benefits begin in the 6th month after disability is determined. Definition of disability (SSA) TOTAL disability: Cannot perform substantial gainful activity (SGA) in any job that exists in the national economy. Must be a medically determinable physical or mental impairment. Must last at least 12 months, or be expected to result in death. No partial disability and no short-term disability benefits. 2026 SGA: $1,690 a month (non-blind); $2,830 a month (blind). Important limits SSDI benefits end the month you reach FRA and convert to retirement benefits. FRA is 66 or 67 for most people now, not 65. Based on the worker's PIA. Paid to the worker, spouse, and children."},{"id":"ch10-s14","title":"13. Medicare (health insurance piece)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Optional?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Part A</strong> (Hospital Insurance)</td><td>Inpatient hospital, skilled nursing facility, hospice, some home health</td><td>Mandatory (generally premium-free with work history)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part B</strong> (Supplementary Medical Insurance)</td><td>Outpatient care, doctors, preventive services, medical supplies</td><td>Optional. Must elect and pay a premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part C</strong> (Medicare Advantage)</td><td>Private plans approved by Medicare that replace Parts A and B</td><td>Optional</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part D</strong> (Prescription drug plans)</td><td>Prescription drugs through private plans</td><td>Optional</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Eligibility:</strong> Age 65+ or disabled.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Part B standard premium:</strong> set each year by CMS.</li><li class=\"l0\">Medicare is administered by the Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (CMS). Part A is funded through payroll taxes.</li></ul>","text":"Part What it covers Optional? Part A (Hospital Insurance) Inpatient hospital, skilled nursing facility, hospice, some home health Mandatory (generally premium-free with work history) Part B (Supplementary Medical Insurance) Outpatient care, doctors, preventive services, medical supplies Optional. Must elect and pay a premium Part C (Medicare Advantage) Private plans approved by Medicare that replace Parts A and B Optional Part D (Prescription drug plans) Prescription drugs through private plans Optional Eligibility: Age 65+ or disabled. Part B standard premium: set each year by CMS. Medicare is administered by the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS). Part A is funded through payroll taxes."},{"id":"ch10-s15","title":"14. Taxation of Social Security benefits","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Filing status</th><th>Combined income threshold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Individual</td><td>Over $25,000: part of the benefit may be taxable</td></tr><tr><td>Joint</td><td>Over $32,000: part of the benefit may be taxable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">These thresholds are fixed by law and are not indexed for inflation.</li><li class=\"l0\">Up to 85% of benefits can be taxable at higher income levels.</li></ul>","text":"Filing status Combined income threshold Individual Over $25,000: part of the benefit may be taxable Joint Over $32,000: part of the benefit may be taxable These thresholds are fixed by law and are not indexed for inflation. Up to 85% of benefits can be taxable at higher income levels."},{"id":"ch10-s16","title":"15. 2026 figures reference","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>2026 figure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>COLA</td><td>2.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Taxable maximum (Social Security tax)</td><td>$184,500</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings test, under FRA all year</td><td>$24,480 a year ($1 for $2)</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings test, year you reach FRA</td><td>$65,160 a year ($1 for $3, months before FRA only)</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI SGA (non-blind)</td><td>$1,690 a month</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI SGA (blind)</td><td>$2,830 a month</td></tr><tr><td>SSI federal benefit rate (individual)</td><td>$994 a month</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare Part B standard premium</td><td>Set each year by CMS</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Item 2026 figure COLA 2.8% Taxable maximum (Social Security tax) $184,500 Earnings test, under FRA all year $24,480 a year ($1 for $2) Earnings test, year you reach FRA $65,160 a year ($1 for $3, months before FRA only) SSDI SGA (non-blind) $1,690 a month SSDI SGA (blind) $2,830 a month SSI federal benefit rate (individual) $994 a month Medicare Part B standard premium Set each year by CMS"},{"id":"ch10-s17","title":"16. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fully vs. currently insured</td><td>40 quarters (all benefits) vs. 6 of last 13 quarters (survivor only)</td></tr><tr><td>AIME vs. PIA</td><td>AIME = average earnings (input). PIA = benefit at FRA (output)</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI vs. SSI</td><td>SSDI = work credits. SSI = financial need</td></tr><tr><td>OASDI vs. OASDHI</td><td>Same program; OASDHI adds the health insurance (Medicare) piece</td></tr><tr><td>Part A vs. Part B</td><td>Hospital (mandatory) vs. medical (optional)</td></tr><tr><td>Part C vs. Part D</td><td>Medicare Advantage (replaces A and B) vs. prescription drug coverage</td></tr><tr><td>Early vs. full vs. delayed</td><td>Reduced permanently before FRA, full at FRA, increased after FRA (to age 70)</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings test before vs. after FRA</td><td>Limit and $1-for-$2 rule before FRA. $1-for-$3 rule in the year of FRA. No limit after FRA</td></tr><tr><td>Blackout vs. child benefit</td><td>Spouse loses benefits from child's 16th birthday to spouse's 60th. Child benefits run to 18</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Fully vs. currently insured 40 quarters (all benefits) vs. 6 of last 13 quarters (survivor only) AIME vs. PIA AIME = average earnings (input). PIA = benefit at FRA (output) SSDI vs. SSI SSDI = work credits. SSI = financial need OASDI vs. OASDHI Same program; OASDHI adds the health insurance (Medicare) piece Part A vs. Part B Hospital (mandatory) vs. medical (optional) Part C vs. Part D Medicare Advantage (replaces A and B) vs. prescription drug coverage Early vs. full vs. delayed Reduced permanently before FRA, full at FRA, increased after FRA (to age 70) Earnings test before vs. after FRA Limit and $1-for-$2 rule before FRA. $1-for-$3 rule in the year of FRA. No limit after FRA Blackout vs. child benefit Spouse loses benefits from child's 16th birthday to spouse's 60th. Child benefits run to 18"},{"id":"ch10-s18","title":"17. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> How many quarters are needed to be fully insured?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> What does a currently insured person receive?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What is the difference between AIME and PIA?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> At what age does a surviving spouse with no children first get benefits?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> What is the lump-sum death benefit?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> When does the blackout period start?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Can a person collect both their own retirement and a survivor benefit?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What is the SSA's definition of total disability?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the waiting period for SSDI?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Which Medicare part is optional and covers outpatient care?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Priya is 40 and has worked 30 quarters. Is she fully insured? Currently insured?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> A widow has a child who is 15. She is 45 and not working. Are her benefits paid now? When does the blackout end?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> Tom is 64 in 2026 (FRA 67) and earns $30,000 while receiving benefits. Is he under the earnings test, and what is the limit?</li></ul>","text":"How many quarters are needed to be fully insured? What does a currently insured person receive? What is the difference between AIME and PIA? At what age does a surviving spouse with no children first get benefits? What is the lump-sum death benefit? When does the blackout period start? Can a person collect both their own retirement and a survivor benefit? What is the SSA's definition of total disability? What is the waiting period for SSDI? Which Medicare part is optional and covers outpatient care? Scenario questions Scenario A: Priya is 40 and has worked 30 quarters. Is she fully insured? Currently insured? Scenario B: A widow has a child who is 15. She is 45 and not working. Are her benefits paid now? When does the blackout end? Scenario C: Tom is 64 in 2026 (FRA 67) and earns $30,000 while receiving benefits. Is he under the earnings test, and what is the limit?"},{"id":"ch10-s19","title":"18. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> 40 quarters (about 10 years). Quarters need not be consecutive. Max 4 per year.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Survivor benefits only.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> AIME is the average indexed monthly earnings used as input. PIA is the full benefit at FRA.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Age 60.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> $255.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> When the youngest child turns 16 (or immediately if no children).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> No. The dual benefit rule allows only the higher benefit.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Inability to do any substantial gainful activity in the national economy, due to a medically determinable impairment expected to last at least 12 months or to result in death.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> 5 months. Benefits start in the 6th month.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Part B.</p><p>Scenario A: She has 30 quarters, which is fewer than 40, so she is NOT fully insured. Currently insured status depends on whether she has 6 quarters in the last 13 quarters. If she has recent work, she is currently insured.</p><p>Scenario B: Yes, she receives survivor benefits now, because she is caring for a child under 16. The blackout starts when the child turns 16 (no benefits for her then, although the child's benefits continue to 18). The blackout ends when she reaches age 60.</p><p>Scenario C: Yes, he is under the earnings test because he is under FRA (67). The 2026 limit is $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $30,000 − $24,480 = $5,520. Withholding: $5,520 ÷ 2 = $2,760 for the year.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 10:</strong> Make a single card with 10 numbers: 40 quarters, 6 of 13, 5-month wait, 12 months, $255, 75%, 82.5%, age 60, age 16, FRA 67. Recite them out loud each morning this week.</p></div>","text":"40 quarters (about 10 years). Quarters need not be consecutive. Max 4 per year. Survivor benefits only. AIME is the average indexed monthly earnings used as input. PIA is the full benefit at FRA. Age 60. $255. When the youngest child turns 16 (or immediately if no children). No. The dual benefit rule allows only the higher benefit. Inability to do any substantial gainful activity in the national economy, due to a medically determinable impairment expected to last at least 12 months or to result in death. 5 months. Benefits start in the 6th month. Part B. Scenario A: She has 30 quarters, which is fewer than 40, so she is NOT fully insured. Currently insured status depends on whether she has 6 quarters in the last 13 quarters. If she has recent work, she is currently insured. Scenario B: Yes, she receives survivor benefits now, because she is caring for a child under 16. The blackout starts when the child turns 16 (no benefits for her then, although the child's benefits continue to 18). The blackout ends when she reaches age 60. Scenario C: Yes, he is under the earnings test because he is under FRA (67). The 2026 limit is $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $30,000 − $24,480 = $5,520. Withholding: $5,520 ÷ 2 = $2,760 for the year. Memory tip for Chapter 10: Make a single card with 10 numbers: 40 quarters, 6 of 13, 5-month wait, 12 months, $255, 75%, 82.5%, age 60, age 16, FRA 67. Recite them out loud each morning this week."}]},{"id":"ch11","short":"Ch 11","title":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","label":"Chapter 11","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Retirement plans are sorted by three questions: (1) <strong>Qualified or nonqualified?</strong> (2) <strong>Defined contribution or defined benefit?</strong> (3) <strong>Who is the employer?</strong> Then learn the IRA family (Traditional vs. Roth vs. rollover) and the small-business plans (SEP, SIMPLE, Keogh).</p></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> Limits, age rules, and inherited-IRA rules changed with the SECURE Act (2019) and SECURE 2.0 (2022). This chapter uses the current rules.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch11-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Qualified</strong> = meets IRS and ERISA rules, gets tax breaks. <strong>Nonqualified</strong> = no tax breaks, can favor executives.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Defined contribution</strong> = the CONTRIBUTION is set (the account is what it is). <strong>Defined benefit</strong> = the BENEFIT is set (like a pension).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Traditional IRA</strong> = tax-deferred, RMDs later. <strong>Roth IRA</strong> = after-tax, tax-free qualified withdrawals, NO RMDs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Direct rollover = no withholding. Indirect rollover = 20% withheld.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Qualified = meets IRS and ERISA rules, gets tax breaks. Nonqualified = no tax breaks, can favor executives. Defined contribution = the CONTRIBUTION is set (the account is what it is). Defined benefit = the BENEFIT is set (like a pension). Traditional IRA = tax-deferred, RMDs later. Roth IRA = after-tax, tax-free qualified withdrawals, NO RMDs. Direct rollover = no withholding. Indirect rollover = 20% withheld."},{"id":"ch11-s2","title":"1. Qualified vs. nonqualified plans","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>QUALIFIED</th><th>NONQUALIFIED</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rules</td><td>Meets IRS requirements and follows ERISA</td><td>Does not meet federal requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Tax</td><td>Employer contributions are deductible; employee contributions are not taxed in the year made; earnings grow tax-deferred</td><td>No favorable tax treatment; employer deduction not allowed until paid out</td></tr><tr><td>Discrimination</td><td>Cannot favor highly paid employees</td><td>CAN favor highly paid employees (often executives)</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>401(k), 403(b), pension, profit-sharing</td><td>NQDCs, SERPs, incentive compensation plans</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>NQDC (nonqualified deferred compensation):</strong> Pay deferred until retirement. Employee is not taxed until paid. Employer deducts when paid out.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>SERP (supplemental executive retirement plan):</strong> Extra retirement benefits for key executives.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Funded</strong> nonqualified plan: assets held in a trust or escrow to secure payments. <strong>Unfunded:</strong> only the employer's unsecured promise.</li></ul>","text":"QUALIFIED NONQUALIFIED Rules Meets IRS requirements and follows ERISA Does not meet federal requirements Tax Employer contributions are deductible; employee contributions are not taxed in the year made; earnings grow tax-deferred No favorable tax treatment; employer deduction not allowed until paid out Discrimination Cannot favor highly paid employees CAN favor highly paid employees (often executives) Examples 401(k), 403(b), pension, profit-sharing NQDCs, SERPs, incentive compensation plans NQDC (nonqualified deferred compensation): Pay deferred until retirement. Employee is not taxed until paid. Employer deducts when paid out. SERP (supplemental executive retirement plan): Extra retirement benefits for key executives. Funded nonqualified plan: assets held in a trust or escrow to secure payments. Unfunded: only the employer's unsecured promise."},{"id":"ch11-s3","title":"2. ERISA (1974)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employee Retirement Income Security Act:</strong> Federal law protecting retirement benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Why:</strong> Before ERISA, workers with many years at one employer could be fired shortly before retirement and lose the pension they expected.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Key rules:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Participation:</strong> Age 21 with 1 year of service. If the plan gives 100% immediate vesting, it may require 2 years of service instead.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Coverage:</strong> Plans must benefit a broad group, not just the highly paid.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Vesting:</strong> Sets when employees own their benefits.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Funding:</strong> Plans must be adequately funded, with contributions managed by a third party.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Reporting:</strong> Annual <strong>Form 5500</strong>.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Summary plan description:</strong> Given to new participants within 90 days.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Also governs</strong> group health insurance disclosure and reporting.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exempt from ERISA:</strong> church plans and governmental plans. Collectively bargained (union) plans are generally NOT exempt; ERISA covers most union pension plans.</li></ul>","text":"Employee Retirement Income Security Act: Federal law protecting retirement benefits. Why: Before ERISA, workers with many years at one employer could be fired shortly before retirement and lose the pension they expected. Key rules: Participation: Age 21 with 1 year of service. If the plan gives 100% immediate vesting, it may require 2 years of service instead. Coverage: Plans must benefit a broad group, not just the highly paid. Vesting: Sets when employees own their benefits. Funding: Plans must be adequately funded, with contributions managed by a third party. Reporting: Annual Form 5500. Summary plan description: Given to new participants within 90 days. Also governs group health insurance disclosure and reporting. Exempt from ERISA: church plans and governmental plans. Collectively bargained (union) plans are generally NOT exempt; ERISA covers most union pension plans."},{"id":"ch11-s4","title":"3. Key ERISA concepts","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Vesting</strong></td><td>Employee's right to the retirement funds. Employee's OWN contributions are ALWAYS 100% vested. Employer contributions vest on a schedule</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alienation of benefits</strong></td><td>Transferring benefits to someone else is generally restricted. Exceptions: certain loans and QDROs (qualified domestic relations orders in divorce)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Top-heavy plan</strong></td><td>More than <strong>60%</strong> of assets belong to key employees, measured on the last day of the prior plan year</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Exclusive benefit rule</strong></td><td>Plan assets are only for employees and their beneficiaries. Survivor benefits can be waived only with the spouse's written consent</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Summary plan description</strong></td><td>Given to new participants within 90 days</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Concept Meaning Vesting Employee's right to the retirement funds. Employee's OWN contributions are ALWAYS 100% vested. Employer contributions vest on a schedule Alienation of benefits Transferring benefits to someone else is generally restricted. Exceptions: certain loans and QDROs (qualified domestic relations orders in divorce) Top-heavy plan More than 60% of assets belong to key employees, measured on the last day of the prior plan year Exclusive benefit rule Plan assets are only for employees and their beneficiaries. Survivor benefits can be waived only with the spouse's written consent Summary plan description Given to new participants within 90 days"},{"id":"ch11-s5","title":"4. Two main plan categories (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>DEFINED CONTRIBUTION</th><th>DEFINED BENEFIT</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What is defined</td><td>The CONTRIBUTIONS</td><td>The BENEFIT at retirement</td></tr><tr><td>Final benefit</td><td>Depends on contributions plus investment results</td><td>Set by a formula (usually years of service and salary)</td></tr><tr><td>Investment risk</td><td>Employee</td><td>Employer</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td>Account balance</td><td>Usually for life</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>401(k), 403(b), profit-sharing, stock bonus, money purchase, ESOP</td><td>Traditional pension</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>DC = Dollars Contributed (the account is what's left). DB = Dollars Benefit (the pension is promised).</p></div><h4>Four requirements for defined benefit plans</h4><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> <strong>Definite, determinable benefits</strong> (set by formula or actuarial computation)</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> <strong>Systematic payment of benefits</strong> (detailed payment terms, typically for life)</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> <strong>Primary retirement benefits</strong> (death or disability benefits allowed only if incidental)</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> <strong>Maximum annual benefit</strong> capped by tax law</p>","text":"DEFINED CONTRIBUTION DEFINED BENEFIT What is defined The CONTRIBUTIONS The BENEFIT at retirement Final benefit Depends on contributions plus investment results Set by a formula (usually years of service and salary) Investment risk Employee Employer Payments Account balance Usually for life Examples 401(k), 403(b), profit-sharing, stock bonus, money purchase, ESOP Traditional pension Memory trick: DC = Dollars Contributed (the account is what's left). DB = Dollars Benefit (the pension is promised). Four requirements for defined benefit plans Definite, determinable benefits (set by formula or actuarial computation) Systematic payment of benefits (detailed payment terms, typically for life) Primary retirement benefits (death or disability benefits allowed only if incidental) Maximum annual benefit capped by tax law"},{"id":"ch11-s6","title":"5. Specific plan types","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>401(k)</strong></td><td>Salary reduction (cash or deferred arrangement). Deferred amounts are not in current income. Earnings grow tax-free until distribution. Often has employer match. <strong>2026 employee limit: $24,500</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>403(b)</strong> (tax-sheltered annuity)</td><td>ONLY for 501(c)(3) nonprofits, public school employees, and some ministers. Contributions excluded from current income; growth tax-deferred</td></tr><tr><td><strong>457</strong></td><td>For state and local government and some nonprofit employees. Deferred compensation without current taxation. Life insurance and annuities are allowed investments</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Profit-sharing</strong></td><td>Contributions based on profits. Can vary each year. Must be &quot;recurring and substantial&quot; (IRS). Early withdrawal before 59½: 10% penalty plus income tax</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stock bonus</strong></td><td>Like profit-sharing, but not tied to profits. Benefits paid in company stock</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Money purchase</strong></td><td>FIXED contribution formula. Distributions only based on amounts credited to accounts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ESOP</strong> (employee stock ownership plan)</td><td>Employees own company stock, held in a trust until retirement or departure</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Plan Key facts 401(k) Salary reduction (cash or deferred arrangement). Deferred amounts are not in current income. Earnings grow tax-free until distribution. Often has employer match. 2026 employee limit: $24,500 403(b) (tax-sheltered annuity) ONLY for 501(c)(3) nonprofits, public school employees, and some ministers. Contributions excluded from current income; growth tax-deferred 457 For state and local government and some nonprofit employees. Deferred compensation without current taxation. Life insurance and annuities are allowed investments Profit-sharing Contributions based on profits. Can vary each year. Must be \"recurring and substantial\" (IRS). Early withdrawal before 59½: 10% penalty plus income tax Stock bonus Like profit-sharing, but not tied to profits. Benefits paid in company stock Money purchase FIXED contribution formula. Distributions only based on amounts credited to accounts ESOP (employee stock ownership plan) Employees own company stock, held in a trust until retirement or departure"},{"id":"ch11-s7","title":"6. Plans for small employers","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Keogh (HR-10)</strong></td><td>For self-employed and unincorporated businesses. Owner can participate. Must include eligible employees. Can be defined contribution or defined benefit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SEP</strong> (simplified employee pension)</td><td>Introduced 1978. Employer contributes to employee-owned IRAs. Contributions not in gross income. Much higher limits than traditional IRAs. Cannot discriminate in favor of highly compensated employees</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SARSEP</strong> (salary reduction SEP)</td><td>For employers with 25 or fewer employees. Had to be set up BEFORE 1997. NO new SARSEPs. Existing plans may continue. Employees choose SEP contributions or taxable cash</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SIMPLE</strong> (Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees)</td><td>For employers with 100 or fewer employees. Employees must have earned at least $5,000 the prior year. Cannot have another qualified plan. SIMPLE IRA or SIMPLE 401(k). <strong>IMMEDIATE 100% vesting</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Catch-up contributions (age 50+):</strong> Allowed in SARSEP and SIMPLE plans. Can be tax-deductible or go to a Roth IRA.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (small-business plans):</strong> SEP = Simple Employer Pension. SARSEP = &quot;Salary&quot; + &quot;pre-1997.&quot; SIMPLE = 100 or fewer employees, instant 100% vesting.</p></div>","text":"Plan Key facts Keogh (HR-10) For self-employed and unincorporated businesses. Owner can participate. Must include eligible employees. Can be defined contribution or defined benefit SEP (simplified employee pension) Introduced 1978. Employer contributes to employee-owned IRAs. Contributions not in gross income. Much higher limits than traditional IRAs. Cannot discriminate in favor of highly compensated employees SARSEP (salary reduction SEP) For employers with 25 or fewer employees. Had to be set up BEFORE 1997. NO new SARSEPs. Existing plans may continue. Employees choose SEP contributions or taxable cash SIMPLE (Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees) For employers with 100 or fewer employees. Employees must have earned at least $5,000 the prior year. Cannot have another qualified plan. SIMPLE IRA or SIMPLE 401(k). IMMEDIATE 100% vesting Catch-up contributions (age 50+): Allowed in SARSEP and SIMPLE plans. Can be tax-deductible or go to a Roth IRA. Memory trick (small-business plans): SEP = Simple Employer Pension. SARSEP = \"Salary\" + \"pre-1997.\" SIMPLE = 100 or fewer employees, instant 100% vesting."},{"id":"ch11-s8","title":"7. Traditional IRA","html":"<h4>Eligibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Anyone with earned income. No age limit for contributions (SECURE Act, 2019).</li><li class=\"l0\">Contribution: up to the annual limit or 100% of compensation, whichever is less.</li><li class=\"l0\">A non-working spouse can also contribute (<strong>spousal IRA</strong>).</li></ul><h4>Tax treatment</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Contributions grow tax-deferred.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Deductibility:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\">No employer plan: fully deductible regardless of income.</li><li class=\"l1\">Employer plan: deduction phases out at higher incomes.</li></ul><h4>Investment options</h4><p>Flexible premium deferred annuity, bank time deposits and CDs, insured credit union accounts, mutual funds, face amount certificates, REIT units, and certain U.S. gold and silver coins.</p><h4>Required minimum distributions (RMDs)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>RMD age:</strong> <strong>73</strong> for people born 1951 to 1959, and <strong>75</strong> for people born 1960 or later.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>RMD penalty:</strong> <strong>25%</strong> of the shortfall, or <strong>10%</strong> if it is corrected quickly.</li></ul><h4>Early withdrawals (before 59½)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Subject to income tax PLUS a <strong>10% penalty</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Penalty exceptions: death; disability; qualifying medical expenses; higher education; first-time home purchase (up to <strong>$10,000</strong> lifetime); health insurance premiums while unemployed; excess contributions.</li></ul><h4>Distributions at or after 59½</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Lump sum or periodic payments.</li><li class=\"l0\">Nondeductible contributions come back <strong>tax-free</strong>. Earnings and deductible contributions are <strong>taxable</strong>.</li></ul><h4>Inherited IRAs (after death)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Remaining funds go to the designated beneficiary.</li><li class=\"l0\">Under the SECURE Act (2019), most non-spouse beneficiaries must empty the inherited IRA within <strong>10 years</strong>. There are narrow exceptions, such as minor children of the owner (until they reach the age of majority), disabled or chronically ill beneficiaries, and beneficiaries not more than 10 years younger than the owner.</li></ul>","text":"Eligibility Anyone with earned income. No age limit for contributions (SECURE Act, 2019). Contribution: up to the annual limit or 100% of compensation, whichever is less. A non-working spouse can also contribute (spousal IRA). Tax treatment Contributions grow tax-deferred. Deductibility: No employer plan: fully deductible regardless of income. Employer plan: deduction phases out at higher incomes. Investment options Flexible premium deferred annuity, bank time deposits and CDs, insured credit union accounts, mutual funds, face amount certificates, REIT units, and certain U.S. gold and silver coins. Required minimum distributions (RMDs) RMD age: 73 for people born 1951 to 1959, and 75 for people born 1960 or later. RMD penalty: 25% of the shortfall, or 10% if it is corrected quickly. Early withdrawals (before 59½) Subject to income tax PLUS a 10% penalty. Penalty exceptions: death; disability; qualifying medical expenses; higher education; first-time home purchase (up to $10,000 lifetime); health insurance premiums while unemployed; excess contributions. Distributions at or after 59½ Lump sum or periodic payments. Nondeductible contributions come back tax-free. Earnings and deductible contributions are taxable. Inherited IRAs (after death) Remaining funds go to the designated beneficiary. Under the SECURE Act (2019), most non-spouse beneficiaries must empty the inherited IRA within 10 years. There are narrow exceptions, such as minor children of the owner (until they reach the age of majority), disabled or chronically ill beneficiaries, and beneficiaries not more than 10 years younger than the owner."},{"id":"ch11-s9","title":"8. Roth IRA","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Introduced by the <strong>Taxpayer Relief Act of 1997</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Contributions are made with AFTER-TAX dollars. Not deductible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Qualified withdrawals are tax-free.</strong> No age limit for contributions.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>NO required minimum distributions</strong> during the owner's life. Can be left to heirs.</li><li class=\"l0\">Income limits can phase out or eliminate eligibility for high earners.</li></ul><h4>Qualified withdrawal (tax-free)</h4><p>Must meet BOTH:</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> The account is at least <strong>5 years old</strong>, AND</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> One qualifying event: age <strong>59½</strong>, death, disability, or a first home purchase (<strong>$10,000</strong> lifetime limit).</p><h4>Nonqualified withdrawals</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Earnings are taxable. Under 59½, a 10% penalty also applies to the earnings.</li><li class=\"l0\">Contributions always come out tax-free (already taxed).</li></ul>","text":"Introduced by the Taxpayer Relief Act of 1997. Contributions are made with AFTER-TAX dollars. Not deductible. Qualified withdrawals are tax-free. No age limit for contributions. NO required minimum distributions during the owner's life. Can be left to heirs. Income limits can phase out or eliminate eligibility for high earners. Qualified withdrawal (tax-free) Must meet BOTH: The account is at least 5 years old, AND One qualifying event: age 59½, death, disability, or a first home purchase ($10,000 lifetime limit). Nonqualified withdrawals Earnings are taxable. Under 59½, a 10% penalty also applies to the earnings. Contributions always come out tax-free (already taxed)."},{"id":"ch11-s10","title":"9. Rollover IRA","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Moves funds from one qualified plan to another. Keeps the tax-deferred status.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>No dollar limit</strong> on rollovers.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>60-day rule:</strong> A rollover must be completed within 60 days to avoid taxes and penalties.</li></ul><h4>The 20% withholding rule (CRITICAL)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Direct rollover</strong> (trustee to trustee)</td><td>No withholding</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Indirect rollover</strong> (funds touch the owner's hands)</td><td>20% is withheld for taxes. To roll over the full amount, the owner must replace the 20% from other money. Otherwise the withheld amount is taxable, and a 10% penalty may apply before 59½</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Partial rollovers:</strong> The part not rolled over is taxed as ordinary income.</li></ul><h4>Who can roll over</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Surviving spouse:</strong> Can roll the deceased's plan into their own IRA. Not subject to estate tax at death because of the unlimited marital deduction.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Non-spouse beneficiaries:</strong> See the inherited IRA rules above (generally 10 years).</li></ul>","text":"Moves funds from one qualified plan to another. Keeps the tax-deferred status. No dollar limit on rollovers. 60-day rule: A rollover must be completed within 60 days to avoid taxes and penalties. The 20% withholding rule (CRITICAL) Method Result Direct rollover (trustee to trustee) No withholding Indirect rollover (funds touch the owner's hands) 20% is withheld for taxes. To roll over the full amount, the owner must replace the 20% from other money. Otherwise the withheld amount is taxable, and a 10% penalty may apply before 59½ Partial rollovers: The part not rolled over is taxed as ordinary income. Who can roll over Surviving spouse: Can roll the deceased's plan into their own IRA. Not subject to estate tax at death because of the unlimited marital deduction. Non-spouse beneficiaries: See the inherited IRA rules above (generally 10 years)."},{"id":"ch11-s11","title":"10. Other IRA types","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Spousal IRA</strong></td><td>For a non-working spouse. Can contribute up to the annual maximum, regardless of whether the working spouse has an employer plan</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Conduit IRA</strong></td><td>Temporary holding account for funds moving between qualified plans. No withholding if the money is not distributed to the individual</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Type Key facts Spousal IRA For a non-working spouse. Can contribute up to the annual maximum, regardless of whether the working spouse has an employer plan Conduit IRA Temporary holding account for funds moving between qualified plans. No withholding if the money is not distributed to the individual"},{"id":"ch11-s12","title":"11. Pension Protection Act (2006)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Called the most significant pension reform in more than 30 years.</li><li class=\"l0\">Encourages more contributions. Introduces <strong>automatic enrollment</strong> in 401(k) plans.</li><li class=\"l0\">From 2008: automatic deferrals into investment funds and automatic annual increases in deferral rates.</li><li class=\"l0\">From 2007: plans may offer fund-specific investment advice through providers or fiduciary advisers.</li></ul>","text":"Called the most significant pension reform in more than 30 years. Encourages more contributions. Introduces automatic enrollment in 401(k) plans. From 2008: automatic deferrals into investment funds and automatic annual increases in deferral rates. From 2007: plans may offer fund-specific investment advice through providers or fiduciary advisers."},{"id":"ch11-s13","title":"12. Education savings plans","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Coverdell Education Savings Account</strong> (education IRA)</td><td>Nondeductible contributions up to <strong>$2,000 per child</strong> a year. Child must be under 18. Can be used for primary, secondary, and higher education. Can roll over to another beneficiary before age 30</td></tr><tr><td><strong>529 plans</strong></td><td>State-operated. Introduced by the Economic Growth and Tax Relief Reconciliation Act of 2001. Two types: <strong>college savings</strong> (any college) and <strong>prepaid tuition</strong> (lock in current rates). Earnings are tax-free for qualified education expenses. Non-qualified withdrawals: 10% penalty plus income tax on earnings. Account owner keeps control, even after the child turns 18. Owner can change the beneficiary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Plan Key facts Coverdell Education Savings Account (education IRA) Nondeductible contributions up to $2,000 per child a year. Child must be under 18. Can be used for primary, secondary, and higher education. Can roll over to another beneficiary before age 30 529 plans State-operated. Introduced by the Economic Growth and Tax Relief Reconciliation Act of 2001. Two types: college savings (any college) and prepaid tuition (lock in current rates). Earnings are tax-free for qualified education expenses. Non-qualified withdrawals: 10% penalty plus income tax on earnings. Account owner keeps control, even after the child turns 18. Owner can change the beneficiary"},{"id":"ch11-s14","title":"13. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Qualified vs. nonqualified</td><td>Tax-favored and ERISA-governed vs. no tax benefit and can discriminate</td></tr><tr><td>Defined contribution vs. defined benefit</td><td>Contributions are defined (employee bears risk) vs. benefit is defined (employer bears risk)</td></tr><tr><td>401(k) vs. 403(b) vs. 457</td><td>Any private employer vs. nonprofits, public schools, ministers vs. state and local government</td></tr><tr><td>Profit-sharing vs. money purchase</td><td>Variable contributions based on profits vs. fixed contributions</td></tr><tr><td>Stock bonus vs. profit-sharing</td><td>Stock bonus pays in company stock. Not tied to profits</td></tr><tr><td>Keogh vs. SEP</td><td>Self-employed/unincorporated (defined contribution or benefit) vs. simplified, employer contributes to employee IRAs</td></tr><tr><td>SARSEP vs. SIMPLE</td><td>Pre-1997 only, 25 or fewer employees vs. 100 or fewer, immediate 100% vesting</td></tr><tr><td>Traditional vs. Roth IRA</td><td>Deductible/tax-deferred, RMDs vs. after-tax, tax-free qualified withdrawals, no RMDs</td></tr><tr><td>Direct vs. indirect rollover</td><td>No withholding vs. 20% withheld</td></tr><tr><td>Coverdell vs. 529</td><td>$2,000 a year, under 18 vs. state plan, large limits</td></tr><tr><td>Surviving spouse vs. non-spouse beneficiary</td><td>Spouse can roll over to own IRA vs. non-spouse generally must withdraw within 10 years</td></tr><tr><td>Conduit vs. rollover IRA</td><td>Temporary holding (no withholding) vs. long-term rollover</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Qualified vs. nonqualified Tax-favored and ERISA-governed vs. no tax benefit and can discriminate Defined contribution vs. defined benefit Contributions are defined (employee bears risk) vs. benefit is defined (employer bears risk) 401(k) vs. 403(b) vs. 457 Any private employer vs. nonprofits, public schools, ministers vs. state and local government Profit-sharing vs. money purchase Variable contributions based on profits vs. fixed contributions Stock bonus vs. profit-sharing Stock bonus pays in company stock. Not tied to profits Keogh vs. SEP Self-employed/unincorporated (defined contribution or benefit) vs. simplified, employer contributes to employee IRAs SARSEP vs. SIMPLE Pre-1997 only, 25 or fewer employees vs. 100 or fewer, immediate 100% vesting Traditional vs. Roth IRA Deductible/tax-deferred, RMDs vs. after-tax, tax-free qualified withdrawals, no RMDs Direct vs. indirect rollover No withholding vs. 20% withheld Coverdell vs. 529 $2,000 a year, under 18 vs. state plan, large limits Surviving spouse vs. non-spouse beneficiary Spouse can roll over to own IRA vs. non-spouse generally must withdraw within 10 years Conduit vs. rollover IRA Temporary holding (no withholding) vs. long-term rollover"},{"id":"ch11-s15","title":"14. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What makes a plan qualified?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Can a nonqualified plan favor highly paid employees?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Which plans are for nonprofits and public school employees only?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> What is the difference between defined contribution and defined benefit?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Which ERISA form is filed each year?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> How long after joining must a participant receive the summary plan description?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What is the early withdrawal penalty for an IRA before age 59½?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What must happen for a Roth withdrawal to be tax-free?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> How does a direct rollover differ from an indirect rollover?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Which small-business plan has immediate 100% vesting?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Lee leaves his job and takes a check payable to himself for his 401(k). He puts the money into an IRA 30 days later. What happened to the 20%, and what should he have done?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> A 45-year-old employee of a for-profit company asks whether she can use a 403(b) plan. What is the answer?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A self-employed consultant with no employees wants a plan with high contribution limits and simple administration. Which plan fits best?</li></ul>","text":"What makes a plan qualified? Can a nonqualified plan favor highly paid employees? Which plans are for nonprofits and public school employees only? What is the difference between defined contribution and defined benefit? Which ERISA form is filed each year? How long after joining must a participant receive the summary plan description? What is the early withdrawal penalty for an IRA before age 59½? What must happen for a Roth withdrawal to be tax-free? How does a direct rollover differ from an indirect rollover? Which small-business plan has immediate 100% vesting? Scenario questions Scenario A: Lee leaves his job and takes a check payable to himself for his 401(k). He puts the money into an IRA 30 days later. What happened to the 20%, and what should he have done? Scenario B: A 45-year-old employee of a for-profit company asks whether she can use a 403(b) plan. What is the answer? Scenario C: A self-employed consultant with no employees wants a plan with high contribution limits and simple administration. Which plan fits best?"},{"id":"ch11-s16","title":"15. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> It meets IRS requirements and follows ERISA, and gets favorable tax treatment.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Yes. Nonqualified plans can favor highly paid employees (qualified plans cannot).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> 403(b) plans.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Defined contribution sets the contribution and the final benefit depends on investment results (employee bears risk). Defined benefit sets the benefit by formula (employer bears risk).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Form 5500.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Within 90 days of enrollment.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> 10%, plus income tax on the taxable portion.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> The account must be at least 5 years old AND a qualifying event must occur (age 59½, death, disability, or first home purchase up to $10,000).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> A direct rollover moves money trustee to trustee with no withholding. An indirect rollover pays the money to the owner, who has 20% withheld and must replace it within 60 days to avoid tax.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> SIMPLE plans.</p><p>Scenario A: The plan withheld 20% for taxes. To keep the full amount tax-deferred, Lee had to replace the withheld 20% from other money within 60 days. He should have asked for a direct rollover (trustee to trustee), which has no withholding.</p><p>Scenario B: No. A 403(b) is only for 501(c)(3) nonprofits, public schools, and some ministers. A for-profit employer uses a 401(k) or similar plan.</p><p>Scenario C: A SEP or a Solo 401(k)-type plan. The Keogh is possible but more complex. The SEP is the simplest and has high limits.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 11:</strong> Make a one-page table: Plan name, who can sponsor it, contribution or benefit defined, and vesting. Then write the Roth vs. Traditional comparison in four words each.</p></div>","text":"It meets IRS requirements and follows ERISA, and gets favorable tax treatment. Yes. Nonqualified plans can favor highly paid employees (qualified plans cannot). 403(b) plans. Defined contribution sets the contribution and the final benefit depends on investment results (employee bears risk). Defined benefit sets the benefit by formula (employer bears risk). Form 5500. Within 90 days of enrollment. 10%, plus income tax on the taxable portion. The account must be at least 5 years old AND a qualifying event must occur (age 59½, death, disability, or first home purchase up to $10,000). A direct rollover moves money trustee to trustee with no withholding. An indirect rollover pays the money to the owner, who has 20% withheld and must replace it within 60 days to avoid tax. SIMPLE plans. Scenario A: The plan withheld 20% for taxes. To keep the full amount tax-deferred, Lee had to replace the withheld 20% from other money within 60 days. He should have asked for a direct rollover (trustee to trustee), which has no withholding. Scenario B: No. A 403(b) is only for 501(c)(3) nonprofits, public schools, and some ministers. A for-profit employer uses a 401(k) or similar plan. Scenario C: A SEP or a Solo 401(k)-type plan. The Keogh is possible but more complex. The SEP is the simplest and has high limits. Memory tip for Chapter 11: Make a one-page table: Plan name, who can sponsor it, contribution or benefit defined, and vesting. Then write the Roth vs. Traditional comparison in four words each."}]},{"id":"ch12","short":"Ch 12","title":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","label":"Chapter 12","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Health insurance has three main types (medical expense, disability income, AD&amp;D) and a lot of rules. The big exam topics: <strong>renewability</strong> (least to most favorable), <strong>COBRA</strong> (who gets how many months), <strong>HIPAA and ACA</strong> rules, <strong>group health</strong> basics, <strong>AD&amp;D</strong>, <strong>HSA</strong>, and <strong>disability income</strong> planning.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch12-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medical expense</strong> = reimbursement (indemnity). <strong>Disability income</strong> = replaces income (valued). <strong>AD&amp;D</strong> = pays for death or dismemberment by accident (valued).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>The more favorable to the insured, the higher the cost.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>COBRA</strong> = keep group health coverage after leaving a job, at your own expense (up to 102%).</li></ul>","text":"Medical expense = reimbursement (indemnity). Disability income = replaces income (valued). AD&D = pays for death or dismemberment by accident (valued). The more favorable to the insured, the higher the cost. COBRA = keep group health coverage after leaving a job, at your own expense (up to 102%)."},{"id":"ch12-s2","title":"1. The three main categories","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>What it pays</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Medical expense</strong></td><td>Doctors, surgery, hospital stays</td><td>Reimbursement (indemnity)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disability income</strong></td><td>Replaces income when you can't work</td><td>Valued (fixed benefit)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AD&amp;D</strong> (accidental death and dismemberment)</td><td>Death or severe injury from an accident</td><td>Valued (fixed schedule)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Category What it pays Basis Medical expense Doctors, surgery, hospital stays Reimbursement (indemnity) Disability income Replaces income when you can't work Valued (fixed benefit) AD&D (accidental death and dismemberment) Death or severe injury from an accident Valued (fixed schedule)"},{"id":"ch12-s3","title":"2. Renewability provisions (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The MORE favorable to the insured, the HIGHER the cost.</p></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Can the insurer cancel or change?</th><th>Premium</th><th>Cost to insured</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cancellable</strong></td><td>Insurer can cancel ANYTIME</td><td>Can change</td><td>Lowest (and prohibited in most states)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Optionally renewable</strong></td><td>Insurer can decline renewal on the anniversary date</td><td>Can change at anniversary</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Conditionally renewable</strong></td><td>Insurer can cancel only if specific conditions are met (age, employment)</td><td>Can change at anniversary</td><td>Moderate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guaranteed renewable</strong></td><td>Insurer must renew until a set age; cannot change the individual's terms</td><td>Can increase for the class</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Noncancellable (noncancelable)</strong></td><td>Insurer cannot cancel or change terms</td><td>Stays the same</td><td>Highest</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Nonrenewable policies:</strong> Issued for a set term (usually 1 year or less), such as short-term health insurance.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Most common for disability income:</strong> noncancellable.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (5 steps, least to most favorable):</strong> CAN (cancel anytime) → OPTIONAL (insurer picks) → CONDITIONAL (if conditions) → GUARANTEED (renewal promised) → NON-CANCELLABLE (locked in). The more locked-in, the more it costs.</p></div>","text":"Rule: The MORE favorable to the insured, the HIGHER the cost. Type Can the insurer cancel or change? Premium Cost to insured Cancellable Insurer can cancel ANYTIME Can change Lowest (and prohibited in most states) Optionally renewable Insurer can decline renewal on the anniversary date Can change at anniversary Low Conditionally renewable Insurer can cancel only if specific conditions are met (age, employment) Can change at anniversary Moderate Guaranteed renewable Insurer must renew until a set age; cannot change the individual's terms Can increase for the class High Noncancellable (noncancelable) Insurer cannot cancel or change terms Stays the same Highest Nonrenewable policies: Issued for a set term (usually 1 year or less), such as short-term health insurance. Most common for disability income: noncancellable. Memory trick (5 steps, least to most favorable): CAN (cancel anytime) → OPTIONAL (insurer picks) → CONDITIONAL (if conditions) → GUARANTEED (renewal promised) → NON-CANCELLABLE (locked in). The more locked-in, the more it costs."},{"id":"ch12-s4","title":"3. Cost sharing","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Deductible</strong></td><td>Amount the insured pays before insurance starts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Copayment</strong></td><td>Fixed amount per service</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coinsurance</strong></td><td>Percentage the insured pays after the deductible. Example: an 80/20 plan pays 80%; the insured pays 20%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Out-of-pocket maximum</strong></td><td>Most the insured pays in a plan year. After that, insurance pays 100% of covered costs. Premiums and non-covered services do NOT count toward it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Policy provisions that limit coverage</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Pre-authorization (prior approval):</strong> Required for some services, such as hospital admissions, elective surgery, and high-cost tests.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Usual, reasonable, and customary (UCR) charges:</strong> Insurer pays only up to the typical charge in the area. The insured pays the difference if the provider charges more.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Maximum benefit limits:</strong> Lifetime, annual, or per-cause caps. Rare for essential health benefits under the ACA.</li></ul>","text":"Term Meaning Deductible Amount the insured pays before insurance starts Copayment Fixed amount per service Coinsurance Percentage the insured pays after the deductible. Example: an 80/20 plan pays 80%; the insured pays 20% Out-of-pocket maximum Most the insured pays in a plan year. After that, insurance pays 100% of covered costs. Premiums and non-covered services do NOT count toward it Policy provisions that limit coverage Pre-authorization (prior approval): Required for some services, such as hospital admissions, elective surgery, and high-cost tests. Usual, reasonable, and customary (UCR) charges: Insurer pays only up to the typical charge in the area. The insured pays the difference if the provider charges more. Maximum benefit limits: Lifetime, annual, or per-cause caps. Rare for essential health benefits under the ACA."},{"id":"ch12-s5","title":"4. Individual vs. group health insurance","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>INDIVIDUAL</th><th>GROUP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Contract</td><td>Individual and insurer</td><td>Employer or organization and insurer</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>Full application plus evidence of insurability</td><td>Enrollment form only (no individual underwriting)</td></tr><tr><td>Premium</td><td>Higher</td><td>Lower</td></tr><tr><td>Underwriting</td><td>Individual</td><td>Group</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Interim coverage:</strong> Short-term policy used to fill a gap, such as between jobs.</li></ul><h4>Premium factors</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Interest (insurer's expected return), expenses, types of benefits, <strong>morbidity</strong> (expected illness rate), claims experience, age (older = higher), sex (may vary), occupation (riskier = higher).</li></ul>","text":"INDIVIDUAL GROUP Contract Individual and insurer Employer or organization and insurer Application Full application plus evidence of insurability Enrollment form only (no individual underwriting) Premium Higher Lower Underwriting Individual Group Interim coverage: Short-term policy used to fill a gap, such as between jobs. Premium factors Interest (insurer's expected return), expenses, types of benefits, morbidity (expected illness rate), claims experience, age (older = higher), sex (may vary), occupation (riskier = higher)."},{"id":"ch12-s6","title":"5. Group health essentials","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Master policy:</strong> Issued to the employer (policyowner).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Certificate of insurance:</strong> Given to each employee. Not a separate policy.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Enrollment period:</strong> Usually 30 or 31 days.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Probationary period:</strong> A waiting period before coverage starts. It applies to SICKNESS, not accidents.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Minimum group size:</strong> Usually 10 employees. Requirements vary by insurer and state.</li></ul><h4>Contributory vs. noncontributory (same rules as life)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>NONCONTRIBUTORY</th><th>CONTRIBUTORY</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who pays</td><td>Employer pays 100%</td><td>Employer and employees share</td></tr><tr><td>Participation required</td><td>100% of eligible employees</td><td>75% of eligible employees</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Premium rating factors for group disability</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Average age of the group (older = higher).</li><li class=\"l0\">Waiting period for disability benefits (shorter = higher).</li><li class=\"l0\">Benefit period (longer = higher).</li><li class=\"l0\">Occupation (hazardous = higher).</li></ul><h4>Small employer plans</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Insurers may offer plans to employers with about 2 to 50 employees.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare coordination:</strong> Employers with fewer than 20 employees: Medicare is PRIMARY. Employers with 20 or more: group plan is primary and Medicare is secondary.</li></ul><h4>Participating vs. nonparticipating</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Nonparticipating:</strong> No share of divisible surplus. Most individual plans.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Participating:</strong> Share of divisible surplus, often as dividends. Typical for group plans.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Experience-rated plans:</strong> Premiums adjust to the group's claims.</li></ul><h4>Group underwriting factors</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Reason the group exists:</strong> Its primary purpose must NOT be to buy insurance.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Stability:</strong> Groups with stable membership and low turnover are preferred.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Persistency:</strong> Groups that switch insurers often are higher risk.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Benefit method:</strong> A set schedule prevents adverse selection.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Eligibility rules:</strong> Time at the employer, probationary periods, full-time status.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Premium source:</strong> Contributory vs. noncontributory affects risk.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Claims history, size and diversity, and industry hazard</strong> (hazardous industries have higher morbidity).</li></ul><h4>Eligible groups</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Employers; trade associations in the same industry; labor unions; creditor-debtor groups; lodges; multiple-employer trusts in the same industry.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Negotiated trusteeships (Taft-Hartley trusts):</strong> Created by collective bargaining. Employers pay into a trust fund, not directly to the union.</li></ul><h4>Individual eligibility and election</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Often 1 to 3 months of work, full-time status.</li><li class=\"l0\">Employees 65+ who keep working must be offered the same benefits as younger employees.</li><li class=\"l0\">Election window: about 30 to 31 days after becoming eligible. Missing it usually means individual application and possibly a medical exam.</li></ul>","text":"Master policy: Issued to the employer (policyowner). Certificate of insurance: Given to each employee. Not a separate policy. Enrollment period: Usually 30 or 31 days. Probationary period: A waiting period before coverage starts. It applies to SICKNESS, not accidents. Minimum group size: Usually 10 employees. Requirements vary by insurer and state. Contributory vs. noncontributory (same rules as life) NONCONTRIBUTORY CONTRIBUTORY Who pays Employer pays 100% Employer and employees share Participation required 100% of eligible employees 75% of eligible employees Premium rating factors for group disability Average age of the group (older = higher). Waiting period for disability benefits (shorter = higher). Benefit period (longer = higher). Occupation (hazardous = higher). Small employer plans Insurers may offer plans to employers with about 2 to 50 employees. Medicare coordination: Employers with fewer than 20 employees: Medicare is PRIMARY. Employers with 20 or more: group plan is primary and Medicare is secondary. Participating vs. nonparticipating Nonparticipating: No share of divisible surplus. Most individual plans. Participating: Share of divisible surplus, often as dividends. Typical for group plans. Experience-rated plans: Premiums adjust to the group's claims. Group underwriting factors Reason the group exists: Its primary purpose must NOT be to buy insurance. Stability: Groups with stable membership and low turnover are preferred. Persistency: Groups that switch insurers often are higher risk. Benefit method: A set schedule prevents adverse selection. Eligibility rules: Time at the employer, probationary periods, full-time status. Premium source: Contributory vs. noncontributory affects risk. Claims history, size and diversity, and industry hazard (hazardous industries have higher morbidity). Eligible groups Employers; trade associations in the same industry; labor unions; creditor-debtor groups; lodges; multiple-employer trusts in the same industry. Negotiated trusteeships (Taft-Hartley trusts): Created by collective bargaining. Employers pay into a trust fund, not directly to the union. Individual eligibility and election Often 1 to 3 months of work, full-time status. Employees 65+ who keep working must be offered the same benefits as younger employees. Election window: about 30 to 31 days after becoming eligible. Missing it usually means individual application and possibly a medical exam."},{"id":"ch12-s7","title":"6. COBRA (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Full name:</strong> Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1985.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>What it does:</strong> Lets people who lose group coverage keep it, at their own expense, for a set time.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Who must offer it:</strong> Employers with 20 or more employees.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cost:</strong> Up to <strong>102%</strong> of the premium (includes administrative costs).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Time to elect:</strong> <strong>60 days.</strong></li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Qualifying event</th><th>Coverage period</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employment terminated (not for gross misconduct)</td><td><strong>18 months</strong> (29 if disabled)</td></tr><tr><td>Reduction in hours</td><td><strong>18 months</strong> (29 if disabled)</td></tr><tr><td>Employee dies</td><td><strong>36 months</strong> (for dependents)</td></tr><tr><td>Divorce or legal separation</td><td><strong>36 months</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Dependent child no longer qualifies</td><td><strong>36 months</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Employee becomes eligible for Medicare</td><td><strong>36 months</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>18 for the EMPLOYEE leaving. 36 for the FAMILY (death, divorce, child aging out, Medicare). Add 11 months if disabled (18 → 29).</p></div>","text":"Full name: Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1985. What it does: Lets people who lose group coverage keep it, at their own expense, for a set time. Who must offer it: Employers with 20 or more employees. Cost: Up to 102% of the premium (includes administrative costs). Time to elect: 60 days. Qualifying event Coverage period Employment terminated (not for gross misconduct) 18 months (29 if disabled) Reduction in hours 18 months (29 if disabled) Employee dies 36 months (for dependents) Divorce or legal separation 36 months Dependent child no longer qualifies 36 months Employee becomes eligible for Medicare 36 months Memory trick: 18 for the EMPLOYEE leaving. 36 for the FAMILY (death, divorce, child aging out, Medicare). Add 11 months if disabled (18 → 29)."},{"id":"ch12-s8","title":"7. HIPAA and the ACA","html":"<h4>HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Makes it possible to transfer or continue coverage when changing or losing jobs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Privacy Rule:</strong> Protects personal health information.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Security Rule:</strong> Technical safeguards for electronic health data.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cannot deny coverage</strong> based on health status or past health problems.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Immediate coverage</strong> when switching jobs for eligible employees.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Does NOT apply to disability income plans.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Preexisting conditions:</strong> The ACA (effective 2014) bans preexisting-condition exclusions in most individual and group health plans. The older HIPAA rules (6-month lookback, 12-month exclusion for regular enrollees and 18 months for late enrollees, creditable coverage, and the 63-day break rule) are history only.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employer size:</strong> HIPAA's portability rules apply to group health plans generally. The 20-employee threshold belongs to COBRA (20+ employees = COBRA).</li></ul><h4>ACA (Affordable Care Act) essential benefits</h4><p>Ten essential health benefits must be covered with NO lifetime or annual dollar limits:</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Ambulatory (outpatient) patient services</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Emergency services</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Hospitalization</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Pregnancy, maternity, and newborn care</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Mental health and substance use disorder services</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Prescription drugs</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Rehabilitative and habilitative services and devices</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Laboratory services</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Preventive and wellness services and chronic disease management</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Pediatric services (including dental and vision for children)</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Dependent coverage:</strong> Children can stay on a parent's plan until <strong>age 26</strong> (married or unmarried).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Metal tiers (actuarial value):</strong></li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Plan pays</th><th>Insured pays</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bronze</td><td>60%</td><td>40%</td></tr><tr><td>Silver</td><td>70%</td><td>30%</td></tr><tr><td>Gold</td><td>80%</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>Platinum</td><td>90%</td><td>10%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Special enrollment period:</strong> Losing minimum essential coverage opens a special enrollment period. It starts <strong>60 days BEFORE</strong> the termination date.</li></ul>","text":"HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act) Makes it possible to transfer or continue coverage when changing or losing jobs. Privacy Rule: Protects personal health information. Security Rule: Technical safeguards for electronic health data. Cannot deny coverage based on health status or past health problems. Immediate coverage when switching jobs for eligible employees. Does NOT apply to disability income plans. Preexisting conditions: The ACA (effective 2014) bans preexisting-condition exclusions in most individual and group health plans. The older HIPAA rules (6-month lookback, 12-month exclusion for regular enrollees and 18 months for late enrollees, creditable coverage, and the 63-day break rule) are history only. Employer size: HIPAA's portability rules apply to group health plans generally. The 20-employee threshold belongs to COBRA (20+ employees = COBRA). ACA (Affordable Care Act) essential benefits Ten essential health benefits must be covered with NO lifetime or annual dollar limits: Ambulatory (outpatient) patient services Emergency services Hospitalization Pregnancy, maternity, and newborn care Mental health and substance use disorder services Prescription drugs Rehabilitative and habilitative services and devices Laboratory services Preventive and wellness services and chronic disease management Pediatric services (including dental and vision for children) Dependent coverage: Children can stay on a parent's plan until age 26 (married or unmarried). Metal tiers (actuarial value): Tier Plan pays Insured pays Bronze 60% 40% Silver 70% 30% Gold 80% 20% Platinum 90% 10% Special enrollment period: Losing minimum essential coverage opens a special enrollment period. It starts 60 days BEFORE the termination date."},{"id":"ch12-s9","title":"8. Coordination of benefits (COB)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Prevents duplicate payment when covered by more than one plan. Total benefits cannot exceed actual expenses.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Primary plan</strong> pays first. <strong>Secondary plan</strong> pays the remaining eligible costs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Married couples:</strong> Each spouse's plan is primary for that spouse.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Dependent children:</strong> The parent whose birthday falls earlier in the year is primary (birthday rule).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Workers 65+ with a group plan:</strong> The group plan is primary when the employer has 20+ employees, and Medicare is secondary.</li></ul>","text":"Prevents duplicate payment when covered by more than one plan. Total benefits cannot exceed actual expenses. Primary plan pays first. Secondary plan pays the remaining eligible costs. Married couples: Each spouse's plan is primary for that spouse. Dependent children: The parent whose birthday falls earlier in the year is primary (birthday rule). Workers 65+ with a group plan: The group plan is primary when the employer has 20+ employees, and Medicare is secondary."},{"id":"ch12-s10","title":"9. Conversion privilege (group health)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">An employee leaving the group can convert to an individual policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">Application window: typically <strong>31 days</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>No proof of insurability</strong> required.</li><li class=\"l0\">Usually limited to employees covered for a set period (such as 3 months).</li><li class=\"l0\">A converted policy cannot give more benefits than the group plan.</li><li class=\"l0\">The insurer may decline renewal for overinsurance, fraud, misrepresentation, or Medicare eligibility.</li></ul>","text":"An employee leaving the group can convert to an individual policy. Application window: typically 31 days. No proof of insurability required. Usually limited to employees covered for a set period (such as 3 months). A converted policy cannot give more benefits than the group plan. The insurer may decline renewal for overinsurance, fraud, misrepresentation, or Medicare eligibility."},{"id":"ch12-s11","title":"10. Waiting period vs. probationary period","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Waiting period</th><th>Probationary period</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it applies to</td><td>Preexisting conditions (usually 12 months)</td><td>Sickness (accidents are covered)</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Limit adverse selection</td><td>Limit adverse selection</td></tr><tr><td>Status today</td><td>Under the ACA, preexisting-condition exclusions are banned for most plans. The rule is history only</td><td>Still used for group sickness coverage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Waiting period Probationary period What it applies to Preexisting conditions (usually 12 months) Sickness (accidents are covered) Purpose Limit adverse selection Limit adverse selection Status today Under the ACA, preexisting-condition exclusions are banned for most plans. The rule is history only Still used for group sickness coverage"},{"id":"ch12-s12","title":"11. Health Savings Accounts (HSA)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Eligibility</strong></td><td>Must have a high-deductible health plan (HDHP)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contributions</strong></td><td>Tax-deductible. Limited by the IRS annual limit (NOT 100% of the deductible). <strong>2026 limits: $4,400 self-only; $8,750 family</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Catch-up</strong></td><td>Age <strong>55 and older</strong> (no upper age limit), $1,000 a year</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Earnings</strong></td><td>Grow tax-free</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Withdrawals</strong></td><td>Tax-free for qualified medical expenses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Penalty</strong></td><td>20% penalty plus income tax on nonqualified withdrawals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Portability</strong></td><td>Fully portable; funds stay with the account owner</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Death</strong></td><td>Can pass to a spouse tax-free</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Qualified expenses</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Physician fees, prescription drugs (including non-prescription), hospital care.</li><li class=\"l0\">Health insurance premiums for retirees, and Medicare premiums (Parts A, B, C, D). <strong>NOT Medigap.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Qualified long-term care services.</li><li class=\"l0\">COBRA coverage.</li></ul>","text":"Feature Key facts Eligibility Must have a high-deductible health plan (HDHP) Contributions Tax-deductible. Limited by the IRS annual limit (NOT 100% of the deductible). 2026 limits: $4,400 self-only; $8,750 family Catch-up Age 55 and older (no upper age limit), $1,000 a year Earnings Grow tax-free Withdrawals Tax-free for qualified medical expenses Penalty 20% penalty plus income tax on nonqualified withdrawals Portability Fully portable; funds stay with the account owner Death Can pass to a spouse tax-free Qualified expenses Physician fees, prescription drugs (including non-prescription), hospital care. Health insurance premiums for retirees, and Medicare premiums (Parts A, B, C, D). NOT Medigap. Qualified long-term care services. COBRA coverage."},{"id":"ch12-s13","title":"12. Cafeteria plans (Section 125)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Employees choose benefits from a menu.</li><li class=\"l0\">Regulated by Section 125 of the Internal Revenue Code.</li><li class=\"l0\">Key benefit: <strong>pre-tax</strong> contributions to benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\">Common benefits: health, life, disability, AD&amp;D, dependent care.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Not eligible:</strong> S-corporation owners with more than a 2% stake.</li></ul>","text":"Employees choose benefits from a menu. Regulated by Section 125 of the Internal Revenue Code. Key benefit: pre-tax contributions to benefits. Common benefits: health, life, disability, AD&D, dependent care. Not eligible: S-corporation owners with more than a 2% stake."},{"id":"ch12-s14","title":"13. Tax treatment","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Premiums</th><th>Benefits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Individual disability income</strong></td><td>NOT deductible</td><td>TAX-FREE</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Individual medical expense</strong></td><td>Individuals generally cannot deduct health insurance premiums, except as allowed for self-employed persons. Medical expenses are deductible only above a percentage of AGI set by the IRS</td><td>TAX-FREE</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Group (employer pays)</strong></td><td>Employer deducts; NOT taxable to employee</td><td>Medical: tax-free. Disability from EMPLOYEE contributions: tax-free. Disability from EMPLOYER contributions: TAXABLE. Death proceeds: generally not subject to federal income tax</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Plan Premiums Benefits Individual disability income NOT deductible TAX-FREE Individual medical expense Individuals generally cannot deduct health insurance premiums, except as allowed for self-employed persons. Medical expenses are deductible only above a percentage of AGI set by the IRS TAX-FREE Group (employer pays) Employer deducts; NOT taxable to employee Medical: tax-free. Disability from EMPLOYEE contributions: tax-free. Disability from EMPLOYER contributions: TAXABLE. Death proceeds: generally not subject to federal income tax"},{"id":"ch12-s15","title":"14. Disability income insurance","html":"<h4>Why it matters</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Often overlooked in financial planning. Without it, a person is self-insuring against disability.</li><li class=\"l0\">Social Security disability has a strict definition and limited benefits. It should be supplemental, not the main protection.</li></ul><h4>The 60% rule</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A plan that replaces about <strong>60%</strong> of pre-disability gross earnings is generally sufficient.</li><li class=\"l0\">Benefits are tax-free when the insured pays the premiums.</li><li class=\"l0\">Example: earns $3,500 a month gross, takes home $2,300 after taxes. A $2,100 tax-free benefit (60% of $3,500) is sufficient.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Malingering prevention:</strong> Insurers limit total benefits from two policies with the same company.</li></ul><h4>Group disability plans</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Short-term</th><th>Long-term</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maximum benefit period</td><td>13 or 26 weeks</td><td>Exceeds 2 years; sometimes to retirement age</td></tr><tr><td>Benefit</td><td>Weekly; 50% to 100% of income</td><td>Generally capped at about 60% of income</td></tr><tr><td>Definition of disability</td><td>—</td><td>Often &quot;own occupation&quot; for the first 1 to 2 years, then &quot;any occupation&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Coordination</td><td>Long-term begins after short-term ends</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Group benefits are typically COORDINATED with other income: Social Security disability, workers' compensation, pensions, and other social insurance can reduce them.</li><li class=\"l0\">Individual policies usually pay the full benefit on top of other income.</li></ul><h4>Business insurance plans</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Business continuity plan</strong></td><td>Keeps the business running if an owner or key employee dies or becomes disabled</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Business overhead expense (BOE)</strong></td><td>Pays business overhead (rent, utilities, employee salaries, equipment leases, phone) during an owner's disability. Does NOT pay the owner's compensation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disability buy-sell (buy-out) agreement</strong></td><td>When one co-owner is disabled, the shares are sold to the other owners or the business. Funded by disability income insurance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key person disability</strong></td><td>Monthly benefit to the business to cover the loss of a key employee's skills. Benefits received tax-free; premiums not deductible</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Why it matters Often overlooked in financial planning. Without it, a person is self-insuring against disability. Social Security disability has a strict definition and limited benefits. It should be supplemental, not the main protection. The 60% rule A plan that replaces about 60% of pre-disability gross earnings is generally sufficient. Benefits are tax-free when the insured pays the premiums. Example: earns $3,500 a month gross, takes home $2,300 after taxes. A $2,100 tax-free benefit (60% of $3,500) is sufficient. Malingering prevention: Insurers limit total benefits from two policies with the same company. Group disability plans Short-term Long-term Maximum benefit period 13 or 26 weeks Exceeds 2 years; sometimes to retirement age Benefit Weekly; 50% to 100% of income Generally capped at about 60% of income Definition of disability — Often \"own occupation\" for the first 1 to 2 years, then \"any occupation\" Coordination Long-term begins after short-term ends Group benefits are typically COORDINATED with other income: Social Security disability, workers' compensation, pensions, and other social insurance can reduce them. Individual policies usually pay the full benefit on top of other income. Business insurance plans Plan What it does Business continuity plan Keeps the business running if an owner or key employee dies or becomes disabled Business overhead expense (BOE) Pays business overhead (rent, utilities, employee salaries, equipment leases, phone) during an owner's disability. Does NOT pay the owner's compensation Disability buy-sell (buy-out) agreement When one co-owner is disabled, the shares are sold to the other owners or the business. Funded by disability income insurance Key person disability Monthly benefit to the business to cover the loss of a key employee's skills. Benefits received tax-free; premiums not deductible"},{"id":"ch12-s16","title":"15. Funding arrangements (group health)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Modified fully insured</strong> (for medium and large employers)</td><td><strong>Premium-delay:</strong> premium paid after the 30-day grace period (often 60 to 90 days). <strong>Reserve reduction:</strong> employer keeps part of the premium to match its claims reserve. <strong>Retrospective premium:</strong> provisional premium adjusted at year-end to actual losses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Shared funding</strong></td><td>Employer self-funds claims up to a limit it chooses; insurer covers claims above the limit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Minimum premium</strong></td><td>Employer self-insures routine claims and pays up to a set amount; insurer covers costs above it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>501(c)(9) trust</strong> (VEBA)</td><td>Funding vehicle for employee benefits. Contributions tax-deductible. Must provide eligible benefits only, with a single purpose, and be controlled by its members, an independent trustee, or a fiduciary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Self-funding</strong></td><td>Large employers pay all claims and carry the risk. Small and medium employers usually avoid it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ASO</strong> (administrative services only)</td><td>Insurer or TPA provides administration only. Employer keeps the claims risk. Services: claims administration, COBRA administration, prescription cards, employee communications. Common with self-funded plans</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Arrangement Key facts Modified fully insured (for medium and large employers) Premium-delay: premium paid after the 30-day grace period (often 60 to 90 days). Reserve reduction: employer keeps part of the premium to match its claims reserve. Retrospective premium: provisional premium adjusted at year-end to actual losses Shared funding Employer self-funds claims up to a limit it chooses; insurer covers claims above the limit Minimum premium Employer self-insures routine claims and pays up to a set amount; insurer covers costs above it 501(c)(9) trust (VEBA) Funding vehicle for employee benefits. Contributions tax-deductible. Must provide eligible benefits only, with a single purpose, and be controlled by its members, an independent trustee, or a fiduciary Self-funding Large employers pay all claims and carry the risk. Small and medium employers usually avoid it ASO (administrative services only) Insurer or TPA provides administration only. Employer keeps the claims risk. Services: claims administration, COBRA administration, prescription cards, employee communications. Common with self-funded plans"},{"id":"ch12-s17","title":"16. Other group and special health plans","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Blanket health</strong></td><td>Covers groups whose membership changes often but face similar risks (airline passengers, students, volunteer rescue workers). No individual certificates or underwriting</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Student health</strong></td><td>Colleges and universities may provide coverage; some extend it after graduation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Franchise (wholesale) health</strong></td><td>Individual policies issued to members of an association, which acts only as sponsor. Each person completes a separate application. Often discounted premiums. Contributory or noncontributory</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Credit disability</strong></td><td>Monthly benefits equal to loan payments during disability. Lump sum to the creditor on death. Cannot exceed the loan amount. Coverage declines as the balance declines. Individual or group</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Credit life</strong></td><td>Protects the lender if the borrower dies. Usually decreasing term. Lender holds the policy, pays premiums, and is beneficiary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nonoccupational disability</strong></td><td>Excludes work-related injuries, which are covered by workers' compensation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Plan Key facts Blanket health Covers groups whose membership changes often but face similar risks (airline passengers, students, volunteer rescue workers). No individual certificates or underwriting Student health Colleges and universities may provide coverage; some extend it after graduation Franchise (wholesale) health Individual policies issued to members of an association, which acts only as sponsor. Each person completes a separate application. Often discounted premiums. Contributory or noncontributory Credit disability Monthly benefits equal to loan payments during disability. Lump sum to the creditor on death. Cannot exceed the loan amount. Coverage declines as the balance declines. Individual or group Credit life Protects the lender if the borrower dies. Usually decreasing term. Lender holds the policy, pays premiums, and is beneficiary Nonoccupational disability Excludes work-related injuries, which are covered by workers' compensation"},{"id":"ch12-s18","title":"17. Dental and vision","html":"<h4>Dental</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scheduled (basic) plans:</strong> No deductible or coinsurance. First-dollar coverage on a fee schedule.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Nonscheduled (comprehensive) plans:</strong> Most common. Usual-customary-reasonable (UCR) basis. Deductibles and coinsurance apply.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Typical coinsurance:</strong> 80% for basic services, 50% for major services.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Preventive services:</strong> Often 100% with no deductible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Maximums:</strong> Calendar-year maximum (example: $3,000) or lifetime maximums for specific services.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Predetermination:</strong> Often required for major procedures.</li></ul><p>Covered service types: endodontics (root canals), pediatric dentistry, oral surgery, restorative (fillings, crowns), orthodontics (braces), prosthodontics (dentures, bridges), periodontics (gum disease), oral pathology (biopsies).</p><h4>Vision</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Covers eye exams by ophthalmologists and optometrists. Partial coverage for glasses and contact lenses.</li><li class=\"l0\">Frames may be limited (for example, once every 2 years).</li><li class=\"l0\">Typically EXCLUDED: eye disease treatment, safety lenses, essential eye surgery, prescription sunglasses, LASIK, plastic lenses, frame upgrades.</li></ul><h4>Hearing aids</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Some private plans cover hearing evaluations and partial or full coverage of hearing aids.</li></ul>","text":"Dental Scheduled (basic) plans: No deductible or coinsurance. First-dollar coverage on a fee schedule. Nonscheduled (comprehensive) plans: Most common. Usual-customary-reasonable (UCR) basis. Deductibles and coinsurance apply. Typical coinsurance: 80% for basic services, 50% for major services. Preventive services: Often 100% with no deductible. Maximums: Calendar-year maximum (example: $3,000) or lifetime maximums for specific services. Predetermination: Often required for major procedures. Covered service types: endodontics (root canals), pediatric dentistry, oral surgery, restorative (fillings, crowns), orthodontics (braces), prosthodontics (dentures, bridges), periodontics (gum disease), oral pathology (biopsies). Vision Covers eye exams by ophthalmologists and optometrists. Partial coverage for glasses and contact lenses. Frames may be limited (for example, once every 2 years). Typically EXCLUDED: eye disease treatment, safety lenses, essential eye surgery, prescription sunglasses, LASIK, plastic lenses, frame upgrades. Hearing aids Some private plans cover hearing evaluations and partial or full coverage of hearing aids."},{"id":"ch12-s19","title":"18. Limited-risk policies (require a \"Notice to Insured\")","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>What it pays</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Specified disease (dread disease)</strong></td><td>Fixed dollar amount for named diseases (examples: multiple sclerosis, kidney failure)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Critical illness</strong></td><td>Lump sum on diagnosis of a covered critical illness (organ transplant, heart attack, stroke). The insured must survive a set period (for example, 30 days)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hospital indemnity</strong></td><td>Fixed daily, weekly, or monthly payment during a hospital stay. Paid directly to the insured. Not based on medical bills</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prescription drug policy</strong></td><td>Drugs outside the hospital, organized by formulary (generic, brand, specialty). Usually excludes non-prescription drugs (except injectable insulin)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Special risk policies:</strong> Cover unusual hazards (examples: a musician's hands, a race car driver for high-speed races).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medical expense need:</strong> Without enough medical coverage, a serious claim can wipe out savings.</li></ul>","text":"Policy What it pays Specified disease (dread disease) Fixed dollar amount for named diseases (examples: multiple sclerosis, kidney failure) Critical illness Lump sum on diagnosis of a covered critical illness (organ transplant, heart attack, stroke). The insured must survive a set period (for example, 30 days) Hospital indemnity Fixed daily, weekly, or monthly payment during a hospital stay. Paid directly to the insured. Not based on medical bills Prescription drug policy Drugs outside the hospital, organized by formulary (generic, brand, specialty). Usually excludes non-prescription drugs (except injectable insulin) Special risk policies: Cover unusual hazards (examples: a musician's hands, a race car driver for high-speed races). Medical expense need: Without enough medical coverage, a serious claim can wipe out savings."},{"id":"ch12-s20","title":"19. AD&D (accidental death and dismemberment)","html":"<h4>Principal sum vs. capital sum</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>PRINCIPAL SUM</th><th>CAPITAL SUM</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Paid for</td><td>Accidental death</td><td>Dismemberment</td></tr><tr><td>Amount</td><td>100% of the policy's AD&amp;D amount</td><td>A percentage of the principal sum set by the policy schedule</td></tr><tr><td>Also paid for</td><td>Loss of TWO limbs, or sight in BOTH eyes</td><td>One limb or one eye: a percentage of the principal sum set by the policy schedule</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Accidental means vs. accidental results</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Accidental means:</strong> BOTH the cause AND the result must be unintended. More restrictive.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Accidental results:</strong> Only the RESULT must be unintended. Less restrictive. Most states require the accidental-results definition.</li></ul><h4>Time limits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Death must occur within <strong>90 days</strong> of the accident. Some states allow later payment if the insured had total, continuous disability from the accident until death.</li></ul><h4>Multiple indemnity</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Double (2x principal), triple (3x), and quadruple (4x) indemnity can apply under specific conditions.</li></ul><h4>Group AD&amp;D</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Often offered with group life. Voluntary plans are employee-paid and optional.</li><li class=\"l0\">Cover occupational and nonoccupational losses, or only nonoccupational.</li><li class=\"l0\">Some plans increase the death benefit if death occurs on company business.</li><li class=\"l0\">Usually do NOT offer a conversion privilege.</li></ul><h4>Beneficiary provisions (AD&amp;D)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary / secondary (contingent) / tertiary</td><td>First, second, and third in line</td></tr><tr><td>Revocable / irrevocable</td><td>Changeable without consent / only with written approval</td></tr><tr><td>Per stirpes</td><td>To the beneficiary's heirs (bloodline) if the beneficiary dies first</td></tr><tr><td>Per capita</td><td>Split equally among living beneficiaries</td></tr><tr><td>Simultaneous death act</td><td>If the insured and primary die together, the primary is presumed to have died first</td></tr><tr><td>Common disaster</td><td>Benefit goes to the contingent if the insured and primary die within a short period; primary must survive a set period</td></tr><tr><td>Spendthrift clause</td><td>Pays in fixed installments to prevent squandering. Does NOT apply to a lump sum</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Principal sum vs. capital sum PRINCIPAL SUM CAPITAL SUM Paid for Accidental death Dismemberment Amount 100% of the policy's AD&D amount A percentage of the principal sum set by the policy schedule Also paid for Loss of TWO limbs, or sight in BOTH eyes One limb or one eye: a percentage of the principal sum set by the policy schedule Accidental means vs. accidental results Accidental means: BOTH the cause AND the result must be unintended. More restrictive. Accidental results: Only the RESULT must be unintended. Less restrictive. Most states require the accidental-results definition. Time limits Death must occur within 90 days of the accident. Some states allow later payment if the insured had total, continuous disability from the accident until death. Multiple indemnity Double (2x principal), triple (3x), and quadruple (4x) indemnity can apply under specific conditions. Group AD&D Often offered with group life. Voluntary plans are employee-paid and optional. Cover occupational and nonoccupational losses, or only nonoccupational. Some plans increase the death benefit if death occurs on company business. Usually do NOT offer a conversion privilege. Beneficiary provisions (AD&D) Term Meaning Primary / secondary (contingent) / tertiary First, second, and third in line Revocable / irrevocable Changeable without consent / only with written approval Per stirpes To the beneficiary's heirs (bloodline) if the beneficiary dies first Per capita Split equally among living beneficiaries Simultaneous death act If the insured and primary die together, the primary is presumed to have died first Common disaster Benefit goes to the contingent if the insured and primary die within a short period; primary must survive a set period Spendthrift clause Pays in fixed installments to prevent squandering. Does NOT apply to a lump sum"},{"id":"ch12-s21","title":"20. Federal and civil acts","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ERISA (1974)</strong></td><td>Protects pension and benefit plans. Sets fiduciary standards, contribution rules, and reporting to the IRS and Department of Labor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pregnancy Discrimination Act (1978)</strong></td><td>Amends Title VII (Civil Rights Act of 1964). Pregnancy is treated like other disabilities. Abortion coverage may be excluded, except where the mother's life is endangered or medical complications arise</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ADEA</strong> (Age Discrimination in Employment Act)</td><td>Employers with 20+ employees. Protects workers 40+. Generally bars mandatory retirement. Benefits for older workers must continue. Employees 65+ who work get the same benefits</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ADA</strong> (Americans with Disabilities Act)</td><td>Prohibits discrimination against people with disabilities. Disability is assessed case by case</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nondiscrimination rules</strong></td><td>Plans with retirement benefits cannot favor owners or officers. Plans must cover a broad cross-section of employees for tax-deductibility</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Workers' compensation</strong> is covered separately below.</li></ul>","text":"Law Key facts ERISA (1974) Protects pension and benefit plans. Sets fiduciary standards, contribution rules, and reporting to the IRS and Department of Labor Pregnancy Discrimination Act (1978) Amends Title VII (Civil Rights Act of 1964). Pregnancy is treated like other disabilities. Abortion coverage may be excluded, except where the mother's life is endangered or medical complications arise ADEA (Age Discrimination in Employment Act) Employers with 20+ employees. Protects workers 40+. Generally bars mandatory retirement. Benefits for older workers must continue. Employees 65+ who work get the same benefits ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) Prohibits discrimination against people with disabilities. Disability is assessed case by case Nondiscrimination rules Plans with retirement benefits cannot favor owners or officers. Plans must cover a broad cross-section of employees for tax-deductibility Workers' compensation is covered separately below."},{"id":"ch12-s22","title":"21. Wellness programs","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Offered by many large employers to improve health and productivity and reduce costs.</li><li class=\"l0\">Address drug abuse and stress management.</li><li class=\"l0\">Common incentives: gym memberships, smoking cessation, weight management.</li></ul>","text":"Offered by many large employers to improve health and productivity and reduce costs. Address drug abuse and stress management. Common incentives: gym memberships, smoking cessation, weight management."},{"id":"ch12-s23","title":"22. Workers' compensation","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>State-mandated</strong> insurance for job-related injuries and illnesses.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Pays:</strong> medical expenses, partial wage replacement (temporary or permanent disability), rehabilitation (physical therapy, retraining), and death benefits for dependents.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employer pays.</strong> Employees do not pay premiums.</li><li class=\"l0\">Covers only job-related injuries. Differs from health insurance on this point.</li><li class=\"l0\">Typically no deductibles or copayments.</li><li class=\"l0\">Employees generally give up the right to sue the employer.</li><li class=\"l0\">Laws and benefits vary by state. Independent contractors are usually not covered unless the policy includes them.</li></ul>","text":"State-mandated insurance for job-related injuries and illnesses. Pays: medical expenses, partial wage replacement (temporary or permanent disability), rehabilitation (physical therapy, retraining), and death benefits for dependents. Employer pays. Employees do not pay premiums. Covers only job-related injuries. Differs from health insurance on this point. Typically no deductibles or copayments. Employees generally give up the right to sue the employer. Laws and benefits vary by state. Independent contractors are usually not covered unless the policy includes them."},{"id":"ch12-s24","title":"23. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accidental means vs. accidental results</td><td>Cause AND result unintended vs. only result unintended</td></tr><tr><td>Principal sum vs. capital sum</td><td>Full amount for accidental death vs. percentage for dismemberment</td></tr><tr><td>Short-term vs. long-term disability</td><td>13 or 26 weeks vs. 2+ years</td></tr><tr><td>Own occupation vs. any occupation</td><td>Can't do own job vs. can't do any job</td></tr><tr><td>Guaranteed renewable vs. noncancellable</td><td>Renewal promised, premium can change by class vs. terms locked in</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting period vs. probationary period</td><td>Preexisting conditions (older rule) vs. sickness coverage delay</td></tr><tr><td>Dependent coverage vs. COBRA</td><td>Child to 26 (ACA) vs. departing employee's continuation</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare primary vs. secondary</td><td>Employer under 20: Medicare primary. 20+: group primary</td></tr><tr><td>Specified disease vs. critical illness</td><td>Fixed amount for named diseases vs. lump sum on a critical illness after a survival period</td></tr><tr><td>Hospital indemnity vs. medical expense</td><td>Fixed daily payment vs. reimbursement of actual costs</td></tr><tr><td>Dental basic vs. comprehensive</td><td>No deductible (scheduled) vs. UCR, deductibles and coinsurance</td></tr><tr><td>Workers' comp vs. health insurance</td><td>Job-related only, employer pays vs. all illness, employee pays part</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Accidental means vs. accidental results Cause AND result unintended vs. only result unintended Principal sum vs. capital sum Full amount for accidental death vs. percentage for dismemberment Short-term vs. long-term disability 13 or 26 weeks vs. 2+ years Own occupation vs. any occupation Can't do own job vs. can't do any job Guaranteed renewable vs. noncancellable Renewal promised, premium can change by class vs. terms locked in Waiting period vs. probationary period Preexisting conditions (older rule) vs. sickness coverage delay Dependent coverage vs. COBRA Child to 26 (ACA) vs. departing employee's continuation Medicare primary vs. secondary Employer under 20: Medicare primary. 20+: group primary Specified disease vs. critical illness Fixed amount for named diseases vs. lump sum on a critical illness after a survival period Hospital indemnity vs. medical expense Fixed daily payment vs. reimbursement of actual costs Dental basic vs. comprehensive No deductible (scheduled) vs. UCR, deductibles and coinsurance Workers' comp vs. health insurance Job-related only, employer pays vs. all illness, employee pays part"},{"id":"ch12-s25","title":"24. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Rank the five renewability types from least to most favorable to the insured.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Which renewability type is most common for disability income?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Under COBRA, how long does an employee who is terminated (not for gross misconduct) get coverage? How long if disabled?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> How many days does an employee have to elect COBRA?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> What is the maximum a COBRA participant can be charged?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Name the ten ACA essential health benefits.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> How long can a child stay on a parent's plan under the ACA?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What is the minimum age for the HSA catch-up contribution?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the difference between accidental means and accidental results?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> How many days after an accident must death occur for the AD&amp;D death benefit to apply?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Carla's employer, with 35 employees, ends her job. She has a family. How long can she keep group health coverage, and what is the maximum cost?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Matt's disability policy cannot be cancelled and its premium cannot change. What renewability type is it?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A surgeon's accident causes the loss of one hand. Her AD&amp;D policy pays a capital sum. Is the payout a percentage of the principal sum?</li></ul>","text":"Rank the five renewability types from least to most favorable to the insured. Which renewability type is most common for disability income? Under COBRA, how long does an employee who is terminated (not for gross misconduct) get coverage? How long if disabled? How many days does an employee have to elect COBRA? What is the maximum a COBRA participant can be charged? Name the ten ACA essential health benefits. How long can a child stay on a parent's plan under the ACA? What is the minimum age for the HSA catch-up contribution? What is the difference between accidental means and accidental results? How many days after an accident must death occur for the AD&D death benefit to apply? Scenario questions Scenario A: Carla's employer, with 35 employees, ends her job. She has a family. How long can she keep group health coverage, and what is the maximum cost? Scenario B: Matt's disability policy cannot be cancelled and its premium cannot change. What renewability type is it? Scenario C: A surgeon's accident causes the loss of one hand. Her AD&D policy pays a capital sum. Is the payout a percentage of the principal sum?"},{"id":"ch12-s26","title":"25. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Cancellable (least favorable to insured, lowest cost) → optionally renewable → conditionally renewable → guaranteed renewable → noncancellable (most favorable, highest cost).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Noncancellable.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> 18 months (29 if disabled).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> 60 days.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> 102% of the premium.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Ambulatory care, emergency, hospitalization, pregnancy and newborn, mental health and substance use, prescription drugs, rehabilitative and habilitative, laboratory, preventive and wellness, pediatric services.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Until age 26.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> 55.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Accidental means: both cause and result must be unintended. Accidental results: only the result must be unintended.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> 90 days.</p><p>Scenario A: 18 months of COBRA (the employee's own termination). Maximum cost: 102% of the premium. The employer has 20+ employees, so COBRA applies.</p><p>Scenario B: Noncancellable (noncancelable) disability income.</p><p>Scenario C: Yes. The capital sum is a percentage of the principal sum, set by the schedule in the policy.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 12:</strong> Make two cards. Card 1: COBRA (18/29 employee, 36 family, 60 days, 102%, 20+ employees). Card 2: ACA (10 benefits, no caps, age 26, metal tiers, SEP 60 days before). Recite both before the exam.</p></div>","text":"Cancellable (least favorable to insured, lowest cost) → optionally renewable → conditionally renewable → guaranteed renewable → noncancellable (most favorable, highest cost). Noncancellable. 18 months (29 if disabled). 60 days. 102% of the premium. Ambulatory care, emergency, hospitalization, pregnancy and newborn, mental health and substance use, prescription drugs, rehabilitative and habilitative, laboratory, preventive and wellness, pediatric services. Until age 26. 55. Accidental means: both cause and result must be unintended. Accidental results: only the result must be unintended. 90 days. Scenario A: 18 months of COBRA (the employee's own termination). Maximum cost: 102% of the premium. The employer has 20+ employees, so COBRA applies. Scenario B: Noncancellable (noncancelable) disability income. Scenario C: Yes. The capital sum is a percentage of the principal sum, set by the schedule in the policy. Memory tip for Chapter 12: Make two cards. Card 1: COBRA (18/29 employee, 36 family, 60 days, 102%, 20+ employees). Card 2: ACA (10 benefits, no caps, age 26, metal tiers, SEP 60 days before). Recite both before the exam."}]},{"id":"ch13","short":"Ch 13","title":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","label":"Chapter 13","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Two big sections. (1) <strong>Who provides health care coverage</strong>: commercial insurers (reimbursement), service providers (prepaid), HMO, PPO, POS, EPO. (2) <strong>Government programs</strong>: Medicare (Parts A, B, C, D), Medicaid, Social Security disability, workers' compensation, TRICARE, FEHB. <strong>Medicare Part A vs. Part B</strong> and <strong>HMO vs. PPO</strong> are heavily tested.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch13-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>HMO</strong> = prepaid, PCP gatekeeper, referrals required, no out-of-network (except emergency). Cheapest.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>PPO</strong> = fee-for-service, no gatekeeper, out-of-network covered at a reduced benefit. More expensive.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare Part A</strong> = hospital (usually premium-free). <strong>Part B</strong> = medical (optional, premium). <strong>Medicaid</strong> = low income (state and federal). <strong>Medicare</strong> = age 65+ and certain disabilities (federal).</li></ul>","text":"HMO = prepaid, PCP gatekeeper, referrals required, no out-of-network (except emergency). Cheapest. PPO = fee-for-service, no gatekeeper, out-of-network covered at a reduced benefit. More expensive. Medicare Part A = hospital (usually premium-free). Part B = medical (optional, premium). Medicaid = low income (state and federal). Medicare = age 65+ and certain disabilities (federal)."},{"id":"ch13-s2","title":"1. Commercial insurers vs. service providers","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>COMMERCIAL INSURERS</th><th>SERVICE PROVIDERS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Payment model</td><td><strong>Reimbursement</strong>: insured pays first, then files a claim</td><td><strong>Service-based (prepaid)</strong>: provider is paid directly; no upfront payment</td></tr><tr><td>Alternative</td><td>Right of assignment: the insured can assign benefits so the provider is paid directly</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Life, casualty, and monoline companies</td><td>Blue Cross/Blue Shield, HMOs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Blue Cross and Blue Shield</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Leading U.S. health insurers, mostly nonprofit. Loosely affiliated through a national association.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Blue Cross</strong> covers hospital expenses. <strong>Blue Shield</strong> covers surgical and medical expenses.</li><li class=\"l0\">Subscribers (members) pay a prepaid fee, usually monthly.</li><li class=\"l0\">Operate through HMOs and PPOs. Participating providers agree to set costs.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>BLUE CROSS = Hospital. BLUE SHIELD = Surgery and doctors (Shield protects the body in surgery).</p></div>","text":"COMMERCIAL INSURERS SERVICE PROVIDERS Payment model Reimbursement: insured pays first, then files a claim Service-based (prepaid): provider is paid directly; no upfront payment Alternative Right of assignment: the insured can assign benefits so the provider is paid directly — Examples Life, casualty, and monoline companies Blue Cross/Blue Shield, HMOs Blue Cross and Blue Shield Leading U.S. health insurers, mostly nonprofit. Loosely affiliated through a national association. Blue Cross covers hospital expenses. Blue Shield covers surgical and medical expenses. Subscribers (members) pay a prepaid fee, usually monthly. Operate through HMOs and PPOs. Participating providers agree to set costs. Memory trick: BLUE CROSS = Hospital. BLUE SHIELD = Surgery and doctors (Shield protects the body in surgery)."},{"id":"ch13-s3","title":"2. Managed care cost-control tools","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it is</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Higher deductibles</td><td>More cost-sharing</td><td>Limits claims</td></tr><tr><td>Coinsurance</td><td>Insured pays a percentage</td><td>Cost sharing</td></tr><tr><td>Shortened benefit periods</td><td>Shorter coverage duration</td><td>Cost containment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mandatory second opinion</strong></td><td>Second doctor must confirm elective surgery</td><td>Higher out-of-pocket cost if skipped</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Precertification (prior authorization)</strong></td><td>Approval BEFORE elective surgery or hospital admission</td><td>Benefits reduced or eliminated if skipped (emergencies excepted)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Case management (utilization review)</strong></td><td>Specialists (often RNs) manage high-cost claims</td><td>Three types: <strong>prospective</strong> (before admission), <strong>concurrent</strong> (during the stay), <strong>retrospective</strong> (after treatment; for trends, NOT to deny claims)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ambulatory surgery</strong></td><td>Outpatient surgery; no overnight stay</td><td>Lower cost</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (case management):</strong> PRO = before (prospective). CON-current = during (concurrent). RETRO = after (retrospective).</p></div>","text":"Tool What it is Key facts Higher deductibles More cost-sharing Limits claims Coinsurance Insured pays a percentage Cost sharing Shortened benefit periods Shorter coverage duration Cost containment Mandatory second opinion Second doctor must confirm elective surgery Higher out-of-pocket cost if skipped Precertification (prior authorization) Approval BEFORE elective surgery or hospital admission Benefits reduced or eliminated if skipped (emergencies excepted) Case management (utilization review) Specialists (often RNs) manage high-cost claims Three types: prospective (before admission), concurrent (during the stay), retrospective (after treatment; for trends, NOT to deny claims) Ambulatory surgery Outpatient surgery; no overnight stay Lower cost Memory trick (case management): PRO = before (prospective). CON-current = during (concurrent). RETRO = after (retrospective)."},{"id":"ch13-s4","title":"3. Provider organizations (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>HMO</th><th>PPO</th><th>POS</th><th>EPO</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Payment</td><td><strong>Prepaid</strong></td><td>Fee-for-service</td><td>Hybrid</td><td>Hybrid</td></tr><tr><td>Primary care physician (PCP) required?</td><td><strong>YES</strong> (gatekeeper)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Referrals</td><td>Required</td><td>Not required</td><td>Required for in-network</td><td>Not required</td></tr><tr><td>Out-of-network</td><td>NOT covered (except emergency)</td><td>Covered, reduced benefits</td><td>Covered, higher cost</td><td>NOT covered (except emergency)</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td><strong>Lowest</strong></td><td><strong>Higher</strong></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Moderate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>HMO = Hold My Option (you're locked into the network and need referrals). PPO = Preferred (you can go outside, and pay more).</p></div><h4>Network terms</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>In-network:</strong> Providers with a contract accepting negotiated rates.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Out-of-network:</strong> Providers without an agreement with the insurer.</li></ul>","text":"HMO PPO POS EPO Payment Prepaid Fee-for-service Hybrid Hybrid Primary care physician (PCP) required? YES (gatekeeper) No Yes No Referrals Required Not required Required for in-network Not required Out-of-network NOT covered (except emergency) Covered, reduced benefits Covered, higher cost NOT covered (except emergency) Cost Lowest Higher Moderate Moderate Memory trick: HMO = Hold My Option (you're locked into the network and need referrals). PPO = Preferred (you can go outside, and pay more). Network terms In-network: Providers with a contract accepting negotiated rates. Out-of-network: Providers without an agreement with the insurer."},{"id":"ch13-s5","title":"4. HMO structures","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>How physicians are paid or organized</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Staff</strong></td><td>Physicians are directly employed by the HMO</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Group</strong></td><td>HMO pays a capitation amount to a medical group, which pays its physicians</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Network</strong></td><td>Multiple physician groups, paid by capitation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IPA</strong> (independent practice association)</td><td>Physicians keep their own offices and see HMO patients part-time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Open vs. closed panel</strong></td><td>Open: physicians work from their own offices. Closed: physicians are salaried employees working in HMO facilities</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Key HMO terms</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Capitation:</strong> Fixed payment per member assigned to a physician.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>PCP (primary care physician) = &quot;gatekeeper&quot;</strong> who coordinates all care.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fixed periodic fee</strong> (service-incurred plan): members pay a regular fee, not per service.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Co-payments:</strong> Often required for certain services.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Emergency:</strong> Use the nearest emergency room and notify the PCP as soon as possible.</li></ul><h4>HMO coverage (typical)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Hospital services, surgery, outpatient care, diagnostics, therapy, inpatient mental health.</li><li class=\"l0\">Often also: prescriptions, vision and dental, home health, long-term care, substance abuse treatment, nursing services.</li></ul><h4>HMO federal rules</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Employers must contribute to the HMO plan.</li><li class=\"l0\">HMO cost cannot exceed a comparable commercial plan.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must prioritize preventive care and keep minimum reserves.</li><li class=\"l0\">Employer HMO premiums use <strong>community rating</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Regulated by both state and federal authorities.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Open enrollment:</strong> at least 30 days.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>24/7 access</strong> for referrals, consultations, and authorizations.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cannot deny</strong> coverage based on preexisting conditions (some state exceptions for chronic diseases).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cannot cancel</strong> based on current health status.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must resolve complaints efficiently. Cannot imply it offers commercial insurance.</li></ul>","text":"Model How physicians are paid or organized Staff Physicians are directly employed by the HMO Group HMO pays a capitation amount to a medical group, which pays its physicians Network Multiple physician groups, paid by capitation IPA (independent practice association) Physicians keep their own offices and see HMO patients part-time Open vs. closed panel Open: physicians work from their own offices. Closed: physicians are salaried employees working in HMO facilities Key HMO terms Capitation: Fixed payment per member assigned to a physician. PCP (primary care physician) = \"gatekeeper\" who coordinates all care. Fixed periodic fee (service-incurred plan): members pay a regular fee, not per service. Co-payments: Often required for certain services. Emergency: Use the nearest emergency room and notify the PCP as soon as possible. HMO coverage (typical) Hospital services, surgery, outpatient care, diagnostics, therapy, inpatient mental health. Often also: prescriptions, vision and dental, home health, long-term care, substance abuse treatment, nursing services. HMO federal rules Employers must contribute to the HMO plan. HMO cost cannot exceed a comparable commercial plan. Must prioritize preventive care and keep minimum reserves. Employer HMO premiums use community rating. Regulated by both state and federal authorities. Open enrollment: at least 30 days. 24/7 access for referrals, consultations, and authorizations. Cannot deny coverage based on preexisting conditions (some state exceptions for chronic diseases). Cannot cancel based on current health status. Must resolve complaints efficiently. Cannot imply it offers commercial insurance."},{"id":"ch13-s6","title":"5. PPOs","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Networks of providers offering services to groups at <strong>discounted rates</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\">Contracting groups: unions, commercial insurers, Blue Cross/Blue Shield, HMOs, employers.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fee-for-service</strong> (NOT prepaid).</li><li class=\"l0\">In-network: better coverage, lower out-of-pocket costs. Out-of-network: reduced benefits, higher costs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Emergency services are covered in full</strong>, in or out of network.</li><li class=\"l0\">Providers get discounted fees in exchange for a larger patient base.</li></ul>","text":"Networks of providers offering services to groups at discounted rates. Contracting groups: unions, commercial insurers, Blue Cross/Blue Shield, HMOs, employers. Fee-for-service (NOT prepaid). In-network: better coverage, lower out-of-pocket costs. Out-of-network: reduced benefits, higher costs. Emergency services are covered in full, in or out of network. Providers get discounted fees in exchange for a larger patient base."},{"id":"ch13-s7","title":"6. Medicare (EXAM CRITICAL)","html":"<h4>Overview</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Established:</strong> 1965 (implemented 1966). Federal program.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Hospital and medical coverage for people 65 and older and certain disabled people.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Administration:</strong> The Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services (CMS). The Social Security Administration (SSA) handles enrollment and public information.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Intermediaries</strong> handle hospital, home health, and facility services. <strong>Carriers</strong> process claims for physician and other services.</li></ul><h4>Eligibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Age 65+</strong> and eligible for Social Security benefits: eligible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Age 65+ but not eligible for Social Security:</strong> Can still apply by paying a premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Under 65 and disabled:</strong> Must have received <strong>Social Security disability benefits for 24 months.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">The 5-month waiting period applies before SSDI benefits start.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exceptions with immediate eligibility:</strong> <strong>ESRD</strong> (end-stage renal disease) and <strong>ALS</strong> (Lou Gehrig's disease).</li></ul><h4>Part A vs. Part B</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>PART A (Hospital Insurance)</th><th>PART B (Medical Insurance)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Funded by</td><td>Federal payroll and self-employment taxes</td><td>General tax revenue plus enrollee premiums</td></tr><tr><td>Premium</td><td>Usually FREE (if eligible)</td><td>Monthly premium REQUIRED</td></tr><tr><td>Enrollment</td><td>Automatic if eligible</td><td>OPTIONAL (can opt out)</td></tr><tr><td>Covers</td><td>Inpatient hospital (60 days per benefit period); skilled nursing (100 days per benefit period: days 1 to 20 fully covered, days 21 to 100 with a daily coinsurance); home health; hospice; psychiatric care (190 days lifetime max)</td><td>Outpatient services; doctor visits; medical supplies; preventive services. After the deductible, pays 80% and you pay 20%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Part A details</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A <strong>benefit period</strong> begins on the day of admission. A readmission within 60 days is the same benefit period. After 60 days it is a new one.</li><li class=\"l0\">Covers food, semiprivate rooms, nursing, medications, diagnostics, operating room, and supplies during a covered stay.</li></ul><h4>Part B covered services</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Doctor services; X-rays and diagnostic tests; office visits; medical and surgical services; medical supplies and anesthesia.</li><li class=\"l0\">Therapy (speech, physical, occupational); radiology and pathology.</li><li class=\"l0\">Blood transfusions (after the first 3 pints); drugs and biologicals; outpatient clinical care.</li><li class=\"l0\">Ambulance and emergency room treatment.</li><li class=\"l0\">Screenings: mammograms, Pap tests, colorectal exams.</li><li class=\"l0\">Prosthetic devices; flu vaccinations; diabetes management; medical devices (casts, splints, braces, surgical dressings).</li><li class=\"l0\">Home health care when Part A does not apply (20% coinsurance on durable medical equipment).</li><li class=\"l0\">Part B covers flu and several preventive vaccines (including certain pneumococcal, hepatitis B, and COVID-19 vaccines).</li></ul><h4>Enrollment periods</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Initial enrollment:</strong> Ends 3 months after the 65th birthday month.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>General enrollment period:</strong> January 1 to March 31 each year; coverage begins July 1.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Automatic enrollment:</strong> People eligible for Part A are automatically enrolled in Part B unless they opt out.</li></ul><h4>Assignment and excess charges</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare assignment:</strong> The doctor accepts the Medicare-approved amount as full payment. The doctor cannot charge more than Medicare pays (beyond the insured's cost sharing).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Excess charge:</strong> The difference between the doctor's actual charge and the Medicare-approved amount, for doctors who do not accept assignment.</li></ul><h4>Medicare cost assistance</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare Savings Programs</strong> (state-run) can help pay Part A and B premiums, deductibles, and coinsurance for those who qualify.</li></ul><h4>Claims and appeals</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A denied claim can be appealed. Complete the Medicare claim form, attach the doctor's bill, and submit it to the Medicare carrier or intermediary.</li><li class=\"l0\">Appeals can also go through the Social Security office.</li></ul><h4>Medicare exclusions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Parts A and B both exclude</th><th>Part B additionally excludes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Items</td><td>Private-duty nurses and personal attendants; private room accommodations; first 3 pints of blood; personal items (telephone, TV rental); custodial care beyond limits</td><td>Vision, hearing, and dental services; most prescription medications (except Part B-covered drugs); routine exams; cosmetic procedures; orthopedic care; injuries caused by war; care outside the U.S.; physician fees above Medicare's limit; home nursing beyond 100 days per benefit period</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Medicare primary vs. secondary</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Medicare is PRIMARY when</th><th>Medicare is SECONDARY when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Age 65+, retired, with no group plan</td><td>Employer group plan with <strong>20+ employees</strong> (age-based)</td></tr><tr><td>Employer with <strong>fewer than 20</strong> employees (age-based)</td><td>Disability-based group plan with <strong>100+</strong> employees</td></tr><tr><td>Disabled with a small group plan (fewer than 100 employees)</td><td>Workers' compensation or no-fault insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Covered under Medicare only</td><td>COBRA continuation coverage</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>VA benefits (depending on service and setting)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>Age-based: the small-employer line is 20. Disability-based: the line is 100.</p></div>","text":"Overview Established: 1965 (implemented 1966). Federal program. Purpose: Hospital and medical coverage for people 65 and older and certain disabled people. Administration: The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS). The Social Security Administration (SSA) handles enrollment and public information. Intermediaries handle hospital, home health, and facility services. Carriers process claims for physician and other services. Eligibility Age 65+ and eligible for Social Security benefits: eligible. Age 65+ but not eligible for Social Security: Can still apply by paying a premium. Under 65 and disabled: Must have received Social Security disability benefits for 24 months. The 5-month waiting period applies before SSDI benefits start. Exceptions with immediate eligibility: ESRD (end-stage renal disease) and ALS (Lou Gehrig's disease). Part A vs. Part B PART A (Hospital Insurance) PART B (Medical Insurance) Funded by Federal payroll and self-employment taxes General tax revenue plus enrollee premiums Premium Usually FREE (if eligible) Monthly premium REQUIRED Enrollment Automatic if eligible OPTIONAL (can opt out) Covers Inpatient hospital (60 days per benefit period); skilled nursing (100 days per benefit period: days 1 to 20 fully covered, days 21 to 100 with a daily coinsurance); home health; hospice; psychiatric care (190 days lifetime max) Outpatient services; doctor visits; medical supplies; preventive services. After the deductible, pays 80% and you pay 20% Part A details A benefit period begins on the day of admission. A readmission within 60 days is the same benefit period. After 60 days it is a new one. Covers food, semiprivate rooms, nursing, medications, diagnostics, operating room, and supplies during a covered stay. Part B covered services Doctor services; X-rays and diagnostic tests; office visits; medical and surgical services; medical supplies and anesthesia. Therapy (speech, physical, occupational); radiology and pathology. Blood transfusions (after the first 3 pints); drugs and biologicals; outpatient clinical care. Ambulance and emergency room treatment. Screenings: mammograms, Pap tests, colorectal exams. Prosthetic devices; flu vaccinations; diabetes management; medical devices (casts, splints, braces, surgical dressings). Home health care when Part A does not apply (20% coinsurance on durable medical equipment). Part B covers flu and several preventive vaccines (including certain pneumococcal, hepatitis B, and COVID-19 vaccines). Enrollment periods Initial enrollment: Ends 3 months after the 65th birthday month. General enrollment period: January 1 to March 31 each year; coverage begins July 1. Automatic enrollment: People eligible for Part A are automatically enrolled in Part B unless they opt out. Assignment and excess charges Medicare assignment: The doctor accepts the Medicare-approved amount as full payment. The doctor cannot charge more than Medicare pays (beyond the insured's cost sharing). Excess charge: The difference between the doctor's actual charge and the Medicare-approved amount, for doctors who do not accept assignment. Medicare cost assistance Medicare Savings Programs (state-run) can help pay Part A and B premiums, deductibles, and coinsurance for those who qualify. Claims and appeals A denied claim can be appealed. Complete the Medicare claim form, attach the doctor's bill, and submit it to the Medicare carrier or intermediary. Appeals can also go through the Social Security office. Medicare exclusions Parts A and B both exclude Part B additionally excludes Items Private-duty nurses and personal attendants; private room accommodations; first 3 pints of blood; personal items (telephone, TV rental); custodial care beyond limits Vision, hearing, and dental services; most prescription medications (except Part B-covered drugs); routine exams; cosmetic procedures; orthopedic care; injuries caused by war; care outside the U.S.; physician fees above Medicare's limit; home nursing beyond 100 days per benefit period Medicare primary vs. secondary Medicare is PRIMARY when Medicare is SECONDARY when Age 65+, retired, with no group plan Employer group plan with 20+ employees (age-based) Employer with fewer than 20 employees (age-based) Disability-based group plan with 100+ employees Disabled with a small group plan (fewer than 100 employees) Workers' compensation or no-fault insurance Covered under Medicare only COBRA continuation coverage VA benefits (depending on service and setting) Memory trick: Age-based: the small-employer line is 20. Disability-based: the line is 100."},{"id":"ch13-s8","title":"7. Social Security disability (OASDI)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Provides retirement, survivor, and disability benefits. Funded by payroll taxes.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Insured status:</strong> Use the Chapter 10 rules: <strong>fully insured = 40 quarters; currently insured = 6 of the last 13 quarters.</strong> SSDI requires fully insured status and 20 of the last 40 quarters.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Disability:</strong> Mental or physical; prevents substantial gainful work; lasts 12+ months or results in death.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>5-month waiting period</strong> before benefits begin (must remain disabled through it).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Benefit:</strong> Up to 100% of PIA for the worker. Spouse and dependents: 50% of the worker's PIA. Continues as long as the disability lasts.</li></ul>","text":"Provides retirement, survivor, and disability benefits. Funded by payroll taxes. Insured status: Use the Chapter 10 rules: fully insured = 40 quarters; currently insured = 6 of the last 13 quarters. SSDI requires fully insured status and 20 of the last 40 quarters. Disability: Mental or physical; prevents substantial gainful work; lasts 12+ months or results in death. 5-month waiting period before benefits begin (must remain disabled through it). Benefit: Up to 100% of PIA for the worker. Spouse and dependents: 50% of the worker's PIA. Continues as long as the disability lasts."},{"id":"ch13-s9","title":"8. Medicaid","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Established:</strong> 1965, Title XIX of the Social Security Act.</li><li class=\"l0\">Federal matching funds to states for medical help for low-income people, regardless of age.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Joint federal-state program,</strong> administered by the states.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Eligibility:</strong> Low income and limited assets. Also covers the blind, aged, disabled, people under 21, and welfare recipients.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Can cover</strong> Medicare deductibles and copayments for qualified people.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Spousal impoverishment protections:</strong> Protect part of the income and resources for the community (non-institutionalized) spouse.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Medicare vs. Medicaid:</strong> Medicare = age 65+ or disabled, federal, based on work history. Medicaid = low income, state-administered, based on need.</p></div>","text":"Established: 1965, Title XIX of the Social Security Act. Federal matching funds to states for medical help for low-income people, regardless of age. Joint federal-state program, administered by the states. Eligibility: Low income and limited assets. Also covers the blind, aged, disabled, people under 21, and welfare recipients. Can cover Medicare deductibles and copayments for qualified people. Spousal impoverishment protections: Protect part of the income and resources for the community (non-institutionalized) spouse. Medicare vs. Medicaid: Medicare = age 65+ or disabled, federal, based on work history. Medicaid = low income, state-administered, based on need."},{"id":"ch13-s10","title":"9. Workers' compensation","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Required benefits for work-related injury, illness, or death.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Varies by state.</strong> Most states require it (compulsory). Some allow it as an elective option.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employer options:</strong> state programs, private insurers, or self-insurance.</li><li class=\"l0\">Many states exclude farmworkers and domestic servants.</li></ul><h4>Four types of disability benefits (memorize)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Total permanent</strong></td><td>Cannot return to work (for example, dismemberment)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Permanent partial</strong></td><td>Can work, but not all previous duties</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Temporary total</strong></td><td>Cannot work temporarily, full recovery expected</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Temporary partial</strong></td><td>Can work with reduced capacity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Other workers' comp features</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medical benefits:</strong> Necessary treatment and hospital care, no time limit.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Survivor benefits:</strong> Support for dependents and burial expenses. Usually lasts until remarriage or dependents reach adulthood.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Rehabilitation:</strong> Services to help return to work.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Eligibility:</strong> Injury must be accidental, job-related, and during employment.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Coordination:</strong> With medical plans, group disability, and Medicare.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Extraterritorial:</strong> Covers employees working abroad, including transport home for treatment.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Second Injury Fund:</strong> Pays extra compensation for a later injury that combines with an earlier one.</li></ul>","text":"Purpose: Required benefits for work-related injury, illness, or death. Varies by state. Most states require it (compulsory). Some allow it as an elective option. Employer options: state programs, private insurers, or self-insurance. Many states exclude farmworkers and domestic servants. Four types of disability benefits (memorize) Type Meaning Total permanent Cannot return to work (for example, dismemberment) Permanent partial Can work, but not all previous duties Temporary total Cannot work temporarily, full recovery expected Temporary partial Can work with reduced capacity Other workers' comp features Medical benefits: Necessary treatment and hospital care, no time limit. Survivor benefits: Support for dependents and burial expenses. Usually lasts until remarriage or dependents reach adulthood. Rehabilitation: Services to help return to work. Eligibility: Injury must be accidental, job-related, and during employment. Coordination: With medical plans, group disability, and Medicare. Extraterritorial: Covers employees working abroad, including transport home for treatment. Second Injury Fund: Pays extra compensation for a later injury that combines with an earlier one."},{"id":"ch13-s11","title":"10. Other programs and arrangements","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>TRICARE</strong></td><td>Federal health plan for active and retired military and their families. Three options: <strong>Prime</strong> (military treatment facilities first), <strong>Extra</strong> (preferred provider network), <strong>Standard</strong> (fee-for-service; formerly CHAMPUS)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FEHB</strong> (Federal Employees Health Benefits)</td><td>Civilian federal employees and retirees. &quot;Managed competition&quot;: fee-for-service plans and prepaid HMOs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Consumer cooperative</strong></td><td>Incorporated or unincorporated. In an unincorporated organization, an attorney-in-fact manages operations, and members insure each other. Example: Blue Cross/Blue Shield</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Self-insurance (self-funding)</strong></td><td>Employer pays claims directly and carries the risk. <strong>Partially self-insured:</strong> employer buys <strong>stop-loss insurance</strong> to limit large claims. The employer remains liable for funding claims even with stop-loss</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Multiple employer trust (MET)</strong></td><td>Group benefits for small employers, often from the same industry. Employers join through a joinder agreement. Can be self-funded or insured. Contributions go into a 501(c)(9) trust. Premiums often based on the group's claims experience</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MEWA</strong> (multiple employer welfare arrangement)</td><td>A MET for small employers banding together. Often self-insured. Employees must share a common employment bond</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Program Key facts TRICARE Federal health plan for active and retired military and their families. Three options: Prime (military treatment facilities first), Extra (preferred provider network), Standard (fee-for-service; formerly CHAMPUS) FEHB (Federal Employees Health Benefits) Civilian federal employees and retirees. \"Managed competition\": fee-for-service plans and prepaid HMOs Consumer cooperative Incorporated or unincorporated. In an unincorporated organization, an attorney-in-fact manages operations, and members insure each other. Example: Blue Cross/Blue Shield Self-insurance (self-funding) Employer pays claims directly and carries the risk. Partially self-insured: employer buys stop-loss insurance to limit large claims. The employer remains liable for funding claims even with stop-loss Multiple employer trust (MET) Group benefits for small employers, often from the same industry. Employers join through a joinder agreement. Can be self-funded or insured. Contributions go into a 501(c)(9) trust. Premiums often based on the group's claims experience MEWA (multiple employer welfare arrangement) A MET for small employers banding together. Often self-insured. Employees must share a common employment bond"},{"id":"ch13-s12","title":"11. Tax treatment (health programs)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Part A</strong> is funded by Social Security payroll taxes. <strong>Part B</strong> is funded by general tax revenue and enrollee premiums.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Social Security benefits</strong> are generally not subject to federal income tax, but can be taxable: individual income over <strong>$25,000</strong> or joint income over <strong>$32,000</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employer</strong> premiums are deductible as a business expense. <strong>Employee</strong> premiums paid with after-tax dollars are not deductible.</li></ul>","text":"Part A is funded by Social Security payroll taxes. Part B is funded by general tax revenue and enrollee premiums. Social Security benefits are generally not subject to federal income tax, but can be taxable: individual income over $25,000 or joint income over $32,000. Employer premiums are deductible as a business expense. Employee premiums paid with after-tax dollars are not deductible."},{"id":"ch13-s13","title":"12. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Commercial insurer vs. service provider</td><td>Reimbursement (insured pays first) vs. prepaid (provider paid directly)</td></tr><tr><td>HMO vs. PPO</td><td>Prepaid, PCP and referrals, no out-of-network vs. fee-for-service, no referrals, out-of-network covered</td></tr><tr><td>POS vs. EPO</td><td>POS: PCP and referrals, out-of-network allowed at higher cost. EPO: no PCP, no out-of-network (except emergency)</td></tr><tr><td>Staff vs. group vs. IPA model</td><td>Employed by HMO vs. paid through a medical group vs. independent offices</td></tr><tr><td>Open vs. closed panel</td><td>Own offices vs. salaried in HMO facilities</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare Part A vs. Part B</td><td>Hospital, usually free, automatic vs. medical, premium, optional</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare vs. Medicaid</td><td>Federal, age 65+/disabled, work-based vs. state-run, low income</td></tr><tr><td>Primary vs. secondary Medicare</td><td>Medicare pays first vs. after the group plan</td></tr><tr><td>Total vs. partial workers' comp</td><td>Cannot return to work vs. can work with limits</td></tr><tr><td>MET vs. MEWA</td><td>Multiple employers in a trust vs. small employers with a common employment bond</td></tr><tr><td>Precertification vs. second opinion</td><td>Approval before admission vs. second doctor's confirmation</td></tr><tr><td>Prospective vs. concurrent vs. retrospective case management</td><td>Before, during, or after care</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Commercial insurer vs. service provider Reimbursement (insured pays first) vs. prepaid (provider paid directly) HMO vs. PPO Prepaid, PCP and referrals, no out-of-network vs. fee-for-service, no referrals, out-of-network covered POS vs. EPO POS: PCP and referrals, out-of-network allowed at higher cost. EPO: no PCP, no out-of-network (except emergency) Staff vs. group vs. IPA model Employed by HMO vs. paid through a medical group vs. independent offices Open vs. closed panel Own offices vs. salaried in HMO facilities Medicare Part A vs. Part B Hospital, usually free, automatic vs. medical, premium, optional Medicare vs. Medicaid Federal, age 65+/disabled, work-based vs. state-run, low income Primary vs. secondary Medicare Medicare pays first vs. after the group plan Total vs. partial workers' comp Cannot return to work vs. can work with limits MET vs. MEWA Multiple employers in a trust vs. small employers with a common employment bond Precertification vs. second opinion Approval before admission vs. second doctor's confirmation Prospective vs. concurrent vs. retrospective case management Before, during, or after care"},{"id":"ch13-s14","title":"13. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Which provider type is prepaid, and which is fee-for-service?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> What is the role of the PCP in an HMO?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Does a PPO require referrals?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> What is capitation?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> What is the difference between Medicare Part A and Part B funding?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What is the Medicare waiting period for disabled people who receive SSDI?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Which two conditions give immediate Medicare eligibility without the 24-month wait?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> How many employees make a group plan primary for an age-based Medicare claim?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is Medicaid, and who runs it?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> What is the difference between a MET and a MEWA?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Ruth wants to see a specialist without a referral and does not mind paying more. Which HMO or PPO structure fits?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Greg is 67, works for a company with 15 employees, and is covered by the company's group plan. Which program pays first?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A small employer with 30 employees in the same industry as other small employers wants group health coverage. What arrangement could help?</li></ul>","text":"Which provider type is prepaid, and which is fee-for-service? What is the role of the PCP in an HMO? Does a PPO require referrals? What is capitation? What is the difference between Medicare Part A and Part B funding? What is the Medicare waiting period for disabled people who receive SSDI? Which two conditions give immediate Medicare eligibility without the 24-month wait? How many employees make a group plan primary for an age-based Medicare claim? What is Medicaid, and who runs it? What is the difference between a MET and a MEWA? Scenario questions Scenario A: Ruth wants to see a specialist without a referral and does not mind paying more. Which HMO or PPO structure fits? Scenario B: Greg is 67, works for a company with 15 employees, and is covered by the company's group plan. Which program pays first? Scenario C: A small employer with 30 employees in the same industry as other small employers wants group health coverage. What arrangement could help?"},{"id":"ch13-s15","title":"14. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Service providers (BC/BS, HMO) are prepaid. Commercial insurers are reimbursement/fee-for-service.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Gatekeeper: coordinates all care and gives referrals.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> No.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> A fixed payment per member assigned to a physician.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Part A is funded by payroll taxes. Part B is funded by general revenue plus premiums.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> 24 months of SSDI benefits.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> ESRD and ALS.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> 20 or more employees (group plan is primary; Medicare secondary). With fewer than 20, Medicare is primary.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> A joint federal-state program for low-income people, run by the states.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> A MET is a trust for multiple employers in an association or industry. A MEWA is a MET-type arrangement for small employers with a common employment bond, often self-insured.</p><p>Scenario A: A PPO. It does not require referrals and covers out-of-network care at a reduced benefit.</p><p>Scenario B: Greg's employer has fewer than 20 employees, so Medicare is PRIMARY and the group plan is secondary.</p><p>Scenario C: A multiple employer trust (MET) or MEWA, which lets small employers in the same industry pool risk.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 13:</strong> Draw a 4-column table: HMO, PPO, POS, EPO. Fill in PCP, referrals, out-of-network, cost. Then draw Medicare Part A and Part B side by side with funding, premium, and enrollment.</p></div>","text":"Service providers (BC/BS, HMO) are prepaid. Commercial insurers are reimbursement/fee-for-service. Gatekeeper: coordinates all care and gives referrals. No. A fixed payment per member assigned to a physician. Part A is funded by payroll taxes. Part B is funded by general revenue plus premiums. 24 months of SSDI benefits. ESRD and ALS. 20 or more employees (group plan is primary; Medicare secondary). With fewer than 20, Medicare is primary. A joint federal-state program for low-income people, run by the states. A MET is a trust for multiple employers in an association or industry. A MEWA is a MET-type arrangement for small employers with a common employment bond, often self-insured. Scenario A: A PPO. It does not require referrals and covers out-of-network care at a reduced benefit. Scenario B: Greg's employer has fewer than 20 employees, so Medicare is PRIMARY and the group plan is secondary. Scenario C: A multiple employer trust (MET) or MEWA, which lets small employers in the same industry pool risk. Memory tip for Chapter 13: Draw a 4-column table: HMO, PPO, POS, EPO. Fill in PCP, referrals, out-of-network, cost. Then draw Medicare Part A and Part B side by side with funding, premium, and enrollment."}]},{"id":"ch14","short":"Ch 14","title":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","label":"Chapter 14","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Medical expense policies pay after the insured's share. Learn <strong>basic vs. major medical</strong>, the <strong>6 deductible types</strong>, <strong>coinsurance and stop-loss</strong>, the <strong>claims math</strong> (deductible first, then coinsurance), the <strong>surgical payment methods</strong>, and the <strong>HSA / HRA / MSA / FSA</strong> accounts.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch14-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Basic</strong> = small, first-dollar, no coinsurance. <strong>Major</strong> = large, after a deductible, with coinsurance.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Claims order:</strong> Deductible first. Then coinsurance on what remains. Then the stop-loss cap.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Comprehensive</strong> = ONE policy. <strong>Supplementary</strong> = TWO policies (basic + major).</li></ul>","text":"Basic = small, first-dollar, no coinsurance. Major = large, after a deductible, with coinsurance. Claims order: Deductible first. Then coinsurance on what remains. Then the stop-loss cap. Comprehensive = ONE policy. Supplementary = TWO policies (basic + major)."},{"id":"ch14-s2","title":"1. Basic vs. major medical (CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>BASIC medical</th><th>MAJOR medical</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deductible</td><td>First-dollar (none)</td><td>Applies (after deductible)</td></tr><tr><td>Benefit limits</td><td>LOW</td><td>HIGH</td></tr><tr><td>Benefit period</td><td>SHORT</td><td>Typically 1 year</td></tr><tr><td>Coinsurance</td><td>NONE</td><td>YES</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Routine care</td><td>Catastrophic protection</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"BASIC medical MAJOR medical Deductible First-dollar (none) Applies (after deductible) Benefit limits LOW HIGH Benefit period SHORT Typically 1 year Coinsurance NONE YES Best for Routine care Catastrophic protection"},{"id":"ch14-s3","title":"2. Six types of deductibles (MEMORIZE)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Flat (initial)</strong></td><td>Fixed dollar amount per occurrence, per person (example: $1,000)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Corridor</strong></td><td>Applied AFTER the basic coverage is used up. Bridges basic to major</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Integrated</strong></td><td>Basic benefits can count toward the major medical deductible</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Per-cause</strong></td><td>A separate deductible for each illness or accident</td></tr><tr><td><strong>All-cause</strong></td><td>One deductible per benefit period (calendar year or cumulative)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Family maximum</strong></td><td>Once a set number of family members meet their deductibles, the rest of the family's deductibles are waived</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Carryover provision:</strong> Expenses from the last 3 months of the year count toward the NEXT year's deductible.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>FLAT = first. CORRIDOR = in the middle (between basic and major). INTEGRATED = basic counts toward major. PER-CAUSE = per problem. ALL-CAUSE = one bill for all problems per year. FAMILY = the family cap.</p></div>","text":"Type How it works Flat (initial) Fixed dollar amount per occurrence, per person (example: $1,000) Corridor Applied AFTER the basic coverage is used up. Bridges basic to major Integrated Basic benefits can count toward the major medical deductible Per-cause A separate deductible for each illness or accident All-cause One deductible per benefit period (calendar year or cumulative) Family maximum Once a set number of family members meet their deductibles, the rest of the family's deductibles are waived Carryover provision: Expenses from the last 3 months of the year count toward the NEXT year's deductible. Memory trick: FLAT = first. CORRIDOR = in the middle (between basic and major). INTEGRATED = basic counts toward major. PER-CAUSE = per problem. ALL-CAUSE = one bill for all problems per year. FAMILY = the family cap."},{"id":"ch14-s4","title":"3. Coinsurance and stop-loss","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Coinsurance:</strong> The insurer and insured share costs AFTER the deductible. Written as insurer/insured, such as 80/20.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Reduce overuse of services.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Typical:</strong> 70% to 90% insurer share in-network; at least 60% out-of-network.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>To qualify as insurance:</strong> The insurer must pay at least 50% of the expense.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Stop-loss provision:</strong> Caps the insured's total out-of-pocket costs. After the limit, the insurer pays 100%.</li></ul><h4>Claims calculation (EXAM CRITICAL, memorize the steps)</h4><p>Policy: $750 deductible, 70/30 coinsurance. Expenses: $2,100.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Expenses − deductible: $2,100 − $750</td><td>$1,350 remaining</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Insurer pays 70% × $1,350</td><td><strong>$945</strong></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Insured pays 30% × $1,350</td><td>$405</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>Insured pays deductible + share: $750 + $405</td><td><strong>$1,155</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Check:</strong> $945 (insurer) + $1,155 (insured) = $2,100. ✔</p></div>","text":"Coinsurance: The insurer and insured share costs AFTER the deductible. Written as insurer/insured, such as 80/20. Purpose: Reduce overuse of services. Typical: 70% to 90% insurer share in-network; at least 60% out-of-network. To qualify as insurance: The insurer must pay at least 50% of the expense. Stop-loss provision: Caps the insured's total out-of-pocket costs. After the limit, the insurer pays 100%. Claims calculation (EXAM CRITICAL, memorize the steps) Policy: $750 deductible, 70/30 coinsurance. Expenses: $2,100. Step Calculation Result 1 Expenses − deductible: $2,100 − $750 $1,350 remaining 2 Insurer pays 70% × $1,350 $945 3 Insured pays 30% × $1,350 $405 Total Insured pays deductible + share: $750 + $405 $1,155 Check: $945 (insurer) + $1,155 (insured) = $2,100. ✔"},{"id":"ch14-s5","title":"4. Three surgical expense methods","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How the payment is set</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Surgical schedule</strong></td><td>Fixed dollar amount for each procedure</td><td>Appendectomy = $2,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relative value</strong></td><td>Units × a conversion factor</td><td>20 units × $50 = $1,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UCR (usual, customary, reasonable; nonscheduled)</strong></td><td>Based on the geographic area; sets the maximum payment</td><td>Insurer pays up to the typical charge in the area</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Method How the payment is set Example Surgical schedule Fixed dollar amount for each procedure Appendectomy = $2,000 Relative value Units × a conversion factor 20 units × $50 = $1,000 UCR (usual, customary, reasonable; nonscheduled) Based on the geographic area; sets the maximum payment Insurer pays up to the typical charge in the area"},{"id":"ch14-s6","title":"5. Hospital expense policies","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Coverage:</strong> Room and board; miscellaneous services (labs, x-rays, drugs, operating room).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Intensive care:</strong> Pays <strong>double</strong> the room-and-board benefit.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Indemnity basis:</strong> Fixed dollars per day. No deductible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Reimbursement basis:</strong> Actual charges (or a percentage). No deductible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Miscellaneous:</strong> Typically <strong>15 times</strong> the room-and-board benefit.</li></ul>","text":"Coverage: Room and board; miscellaneous services (labs, x-rays, drugs, operating room). Intensive care: Pays double the room-and-board benefit. Indemnity basis: Fixed dollars per day. No deductible. Reimbursement basis: Actual charges (or a percentage). No deductible. Miscellaneous: Typically 15 times the room-and-board benefit."},{"id":"ch14-s7","title":"5A. Comprehensive vs. supplementary major medical","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>SUPPLEMENTARY major medical</th><th>COMPREHENSIVE major medical</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>TWO separate policies (basic + major)</td><td>ONE combined policy</td></tr><tr><td>Supplements the basic policy</td><td>Complete on its own</td></tr><tr><td>Pays after basic benefits are used up</td><td>Covers nearly all expenses</td></tr><tr><td>Uses a corridor deductible (bridges basic to major)</td><td>Uses a standard deductible</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"SUPPLEMENTARY major medical COMPREHENSIVE major medical TWO separate policies (basic + major) ONE combined policy Supplements the basic policy Complete on its own Pays after basic benefits are used up Covers nearly all expenses Uses a corridor deductible (bridges basic to major) Uses a standard deductible"},{"id":"ch14-s8","title":"6. Tax-advantaged accounts","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Account</th><th>Funded by</th><th>Requires HDHP?</th><th>Rollover?</th><th>Owner</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>HSA</strong> (health savings account)</td><td>Individual or employer</td><td>YES</td><td>YES</td><td>Individual</td></tr><tr><td><strong>HRA</strong> (health reimbursement arrangement)</td><td>EMPLOYER ONLY</td><td>No</td><td>YES</td><td>Employer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MSA</strong> (medical savings account)</td><td>Individual</td><td>—</td><td>YES</td><td>Individual (small employers, 50 or fewer)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FSA</strong> (flexible spending account)</td><td>Employee (through a cafeteria plan)</td><td>No</td><td>Generally NO</td><td>Employee (use it or lose it)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> FSAs generally cannot roll over, although an employer may allow a limited carryover or grace period.</p></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>HSA = Individual owns it, needs HDHP. HRA = Employer funds it. FSA = Use it or lose it. MSA = the older, small-employer version.</p></div>","text":"Account Funded by Requires HDHP? Rollover? Owner HSA (health savings account) Individual or employer YES YES Individual HRA (health reimbursement arrangement) EMPLOYER ONLY No YES Employer MSA (medical savings account) Individual — YES Individual (small employers, 50 or fewer) FSA (flexible spending account) Employee (through a cafeteria plan) No Generally NO Employee (use it or lose it) Note: FSAs generally cannot roll over, although an employer may allow a limited carryover or grace period. Memory trick: HSA = Individual owns it, needs HDHP. HRA = Employer funds it. FSA = Use it or lose it. MSA = the older, small-employer version."},{"id":"ch14-s9","title":"7. Specialty policies","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Hospital indemnity</strong></td><td>Fixed benefit per day, week, or month. Paid to the INSURED. Based on days confined, NOT on actual expenses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Limited benefit</strong></td><td>Limited to specific illnesses or accidents (travel, a specified disease). Requires a &quot;Notice to Insured&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dread disease</strong></td><td>Fixed dollar amounts for specific serious illnesses (cancer, heart disease)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Critical illness</strong></td><td>LUMP SUM on diagnosis. Paid to the insured. Insured must survive a set period (for example, 30 days)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Policy Key facts Hospital indemnity Fixed benefit per day, week, or month. Paid to the INSURED. Based on days confined, NOT on actual expenses Limited benefit Limited to specific illnesses or accidents (travel, a specified disease). Requires a \"Notice to Insured\" Dread disease Fixed dollar amounts for specific serious illnesses (cancer, heart disease) Critical illness LUMP SUM on diagnosis. Paid to the insured. Insured must survive a set period (for example, 30 days)"},{"id":"ch14-s10","title":"8. Physician expense policies","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Also called basic physician nonsurgical expense coverage.</li><li class=\"l0\">Covers maternity, nursing fees, critical care, mental health, home health, hospice, outpatient, and emergency care.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Physician assistants (PAs):</strong> Payment cannot be denied solely because a PA provided the service.</li></ul>","text":"Also called basic physician nonsurgical expense coverage. Covers maternity, nursing fees, critical care, mental health, home health, hospice, outpatient, and emergency care. Physician assistants (PAs): Payment cannot be denied solely because a PA provided the service."},{"id":"ch14-s11","title":"9. Prescription drug coverage","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Drug formulary:</strong> The list of covered medications.</li><li class=\"l0\">Excludes hospital or extended-care dispensing.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Non-prescription drugs are NOT covered EXCEPT injectable insulin.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Not covered: devices, appliances, needles, administration fees.</li><li class=\"l0\">Usually excluded: sexual dysfunction and infertility (unless a state requires coverage).</li></ul>","text":"Drug formulary: The list of covered medications. Excludes hospital or extended-care dispensing. Non-prescription drugs are NOT covered EXCEPT injectable insulin. Not covered: devices, appliances, needles, administration fees. Usually excluded: sexual dysfunction and infertility (unless a state requires coverage)."},{"id":"ch14-s12","title":"10. Other key concepts","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Internal limits:</strong> Restrictions on specific services (example: $50 per visit, 60 sessions a year).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Benefit period:</strong> The time in which benefits can be received. Starts when the accident or illness occurs, or after the deductible is met.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Restoration of benefits:</strong> For lifetime or annual maximum policies. Restores a percentage of benefits used after a period without claims.</li></ul>","text":"Internal limits: Restrictions on specific services (example: $50 per visit, 60 sessions a year). Benefit period: The time in which benefits can be received. Starts when the accident or illness occurs, or after the deductible is met. Restoration of benefits: For lifetime or annual maximum policies. Restores a percentage of benefits used after a period without claims."},{"id":"ch14-s13","title":"11. Tax treatment","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reimbursed expenses</td><td>NOT deductible</td></tr><tr><td>Unreimbursed expenses</td><td>Deductible only if they exceed a percentage of AGI set by the IRS</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term care premiums</td><td>Count as medical expenses if they exceed the threshold, within age-based limits</td></tr><tr><td>Benefits received</td><td>NOT taxable (compensation for a loss)</td></tr><tr><td>Self-employed (sole proprietor)</td><td>Can deduct 100% of health premiums</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Item Treatment Reimbursed expenses NOT deductible Unreimbursed expenses Deductible only if they exceed a percentage of AGI set by the IRS Long-term care premiums Count as medical expenses if they exceed the threshold, within age-based limits Benefits received NOT taxable (compensation for a loss) Self-employed (sole proprietor) Can deduct 100% of health premiums"},{"id":"ch14-s14","title":"12. Consumer-driven health plans (CDHPs)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">High-deductible plans paired with tax-advantaged accounts.</li><li class=\"l0\">Three types: <strong>HSA</strong> (portable, individual-owned), <strong>HRA</strong> (employer-funded), <strong>FSA</strong> (use it or lose it).</li><li class=\"l0\">Best for: healthy people who want lower premiums and are comfortable managing expenses.</li></ul>","text":"High-deductible plans paired with tax-advantaged accounts. Three types: HSA (portable, individual-owned), HRA (employer-funded), FSA (use it or lose it). Best for: healthy people who want lower premiums and are comfortable managing expenses."},{"id":"ch14-s15","title":"13. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Basic vs. major medical</td><td>First-dollar, low limits, no coinsurance vs. after deductible, high limits, with coinsurance</td></tr><tr><td>Comprehensive vs. supplementary</td><td>One policy vs. two policies</td></tr><tr><td>Corridor vs. integrated deductible</td><td>Applied after basic is used up vs. basic counts toward it</td></tr><tr><td>Per-cause vs. all-cause</td><td>Each problem vs. one per year</td></tr><tr><td>Hospital indemnity vs. reimbursement</td><td>Fixed daily payment vs. actual expenses</td></tr><tr><td>Critical illness vs. dread disease</td><td>Lump sum on critical diagnosis vs. fixed amounts for named diseases</td></tr><tr><td>HSA vs. HRA vs. FSA</td><td>Individual, HDHP, portable vs. employer-only vs. use-it-or-lose-it</td></tr><tr><td>Schedule vs. relative value vs. UCR</td><td>Fixed per procedure vs. units times factor vs. area-based</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Basic vs. major medical First-dollar, low limits, no coinsurance vs. after deductible, high limits, with coinsurance Comprehensive vs. supplementary One policy vs. two policies Corridor vs. integrated deductible Applied after basic is used up vs. basic counts toward it Per-cause vs. all-cause Each problem vs. one per year Hospital indemnity vs. reimbursement Fixed daily payment vs. actual expenses Critical illness vs. dread disease Lump sum on critical diagnosis vs. fixed amounts for named diseases HSA vs. HRA vs. FSA Individual, HDHP, portable vs. employer-only vs. use-it-or-lose-it Schedule vs. relative value vs. UCR Fixed per procedure vs. units times factor vs. area-based"},{"id":"ch14-s16","title":"14. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the difference between basic and major medical coverage?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Name the six deductible types.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What is the carryover provision?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Who receives hospital indemnity benefits, and what are they based on?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> What must a critical illness insured do to receive the lump sum?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What is the insurer's minimum share for a policy to qualify as insurance?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> How much does the insurer pay on a $2,100 expense with a $750 deductible and 70/30 coinsurance?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Which surgical method uses units and a conversion factor?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Which account is employer-funded only?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Is injectable insulin covered when other non-prescription drugs are not?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Ben has $5,000 of medical bills. His policy has a $1,000 deductible and 80/20 coinsurance. How much does Ben pay, and how much does the insurer pay?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Lisa wants a plan with a separate policy for catastrophic costs that sits on top of her basic plan. Which type is this?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A worker wants a health account she owns, that rolls over, and that works with a high-deductible plan. Which account fits?</li></ul>","text":"What is the difference between basic and major medical coverage? Name the six deductible types. What is the carryover provision? Who receives hospital indemnity benefits, and what are they based on? What must a critical illness insured do to receive the lump sum? What is the insurer's minimum share for a policy to qualify as insurance? How much does the insurer pay on a $2,100 expense with a $750 deductible and 70/30 coinsurance? Which surgical method uses units and a conversion factor? Which account is employer-funded only? Is injectable insulin covered when other non-prescription drugs are not? Scenario questions Scenario A: Ben has $5,000 of medical bills. His policy has a $1,000 deductible and 80/20 coinsurance. How much does Ben pay, and how much does the insurer pay? Scenario B: Lisa wants a plan with a separate policy for catastrophic costs that sits on top of her basic plan. Which type is this? Scenario C: A worker wants a health account she owns, that rolls over, and that works with a high-deductible plan. Which account fits?"},{"id":"ch14-s17","title":"15. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Basic: first-dollar, low limits, short period, no coinsurance. Major: after deductible, high limits, coinsurance.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Flat, corridor, integrated, per-cause, all-cause, family maximum.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Expenses from the last 3 months of the year count toward the next year's deductible.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> The insured, based on days confined (not on expenses).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Survive a set period after diagnosis (for example, 30 days).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> 50% (to qualify as insurance).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> $945 (70% of $1,350).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Relative value.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> HRA.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Yes.</p><p>Scenario A: Deductible $1,000 → remaining $4,000 → insurer 80% = $3,200 → Ben pays $1,000 + 20% × $4,000 = $1,000 + $800 = $1,800. Insurer pays $3,200. (Check: $1,800 + $3,200 = $5,000.)</p><p>Scenario B: Supplementary major medical (two policies).</p><p>Scenario C: An HSA (individual-owned, needs an HDHP, rollover allowed).</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 14:</strong> Write the claims math on an index card and redo it three times with different numbers. Then make a four-column card for HSA / HRA / MSA / FSA.</p></div>","text":"Basic: first-dollar, low limits, short period, no coinsurance. Major: after deductible, high limits, coinsurance. Flat, corridor, integrated, per-cause, all-cause, family maximum. Expenses from the last 3 months of the year count toward the next year's deductible. The insured, based on days confined (not on expenses). Survive a set period after diagnosis (for example, 30 days). 50% (to qualify as insurance). $945 (70% of $1,350). Relative value. HRA. Yes. Scenario A: Deductible $1,000 → remaining $4,000 → insurer 80% = $3,200 → Ben pays $1,000 + 20% × $4,000 = $1,000 + $800 = $1,800. Insurer pays $3,200. (Check: $1,800 + $3,200 = $5,000.) Scenario B: Supplementary major medical (two policies). Scenario C: An HSA (individual-owned, needs an HDHP, rollover allowed). Memory tip for Chapter 14: Write the claims math on an index card and redo it three times with different numbers. Then make a four-column card for HSA / HRA / MSA / FSA."}]},{"id":"ch15","short":"Ch 15","title":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","label":"Chapter 15","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Disability income insurance replaces income when you can't work. The most tested idea is <strong>own occupation vs. any occupation</strong>. Next: total vs. partial disability, the two waiting periods (probationary vs. elimination), flat vs. residual benefits, and the riders.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch15-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Disability has been called a &quot;living death&quot;: the financial damage can be worse than death.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Own-occupation</strong> pays if you can't do YOUR job (better for the insured, costs more).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Any-occupation</strong> pays only if you can't do ANY job you're reasonably suited for (cheaper).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Longer elimination (waiting) period = LOWER premium.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Disability has been called a \"living death\": the financial damage can be worse than death. Own-occupation pays if you can't do YOUR job (better for the insured, costs more). Any-occupation pays only if you can't do ANY job you're reasonably suited for (cheaper). Longer elimination (waiting) period = LOWER premium."},{"id":"ch15-s2","title":"1. Purpose and importance","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Provides income if the insured is unable to work because of disability.</li><li class=\"l0\">Covers nonoccupational conditions primarily.</li><li class=\"l0\">Common renewability types for individual disability contracts include <strong>noncancellable</strong> and <strong>guaranteed renewable</strong> (guaranteed renewable lets the insurer adjust premiums by class).</li></ul>","text":"Provides income if the insured is unable to work because of disability. Covers nonoccupational conditions primarily. Common renewability types for individual disability contracts include noncancellable and guaranteed renewable (guaranteed renewable lets the insurer adjust premiums by class)."},{"id":"ch15-s3","title":"2. Own occupation vs. any occupation (MOST TESTED)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>OWN OCCUPATION (own-occ)</th><th>ANY OCCUPATION (any-occ)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Definition</td><td>Unable to perform the duties of THEIR OWN occupation</td><td>Unable to perform ANY job reasonably suited by education, training, or experience</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>A surgeon who can no longer operate gets benefits, even if she can still consult</td><td>A carpenter who can still teach or consult gets NO benefits</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>MORE expensive; harder to qualify</td><td>LESS expensive</td></tr><tr><td>Advantage</td><td>Better for the policyowner (broader coverage)</td><td>Lower premium</td></tr><tr><td>Also called</td><td>—</td><td>Combination definitions (sometimes)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Long-term group policies:</strong> Own-occupation for an initial period, then any-occupation.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>OWN = &quot;Only my job.&quot; ANY = &quot;Any job at all.&quot; Own is better for the insured, and costs more.</p></div>","text":"OWN OCCUPATION (own-occ) ANY OCCUPATION (any-occ) Definition Unable to perform the duties of THEIR OWN occupation Unable to perform ANY job reasonably suited by education, training, or experience Example A surgeon who can no longer operate gets benefits, even if she can still consult A carpenter who can still teach or consult gets NO benefits Cost MORE expensive; harder to qualify LESS expensive Advantage Better for the policyowner (broader coverage) Lower premium Also called — Combination definitions (sometimes) Long-term group policies: Own-occupation for an initial period, then any-occupation. Memory trick: OWN = \"Only my job.\" ANY = \"Any job at all.\" Own is better for the insured, and costs more."},{"id":"ch15-s4","title":"3. Total disability definitions","html":"<h4>Presumptive disability</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Automatic total disability, regardless of ability to work.</li><li class=\"l0\">Qualifying conditions: <strong>total blindness, total deafness, loss of speech, loss of two or more limbs.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Pays the <strong>FULL</strong> benefit.</li></ul><h4>Nonoccupational coverage</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Excludes losses from the insured's employment or a hazardous occupation.</li><li class=\"l0\">No benefits if the insured is eligible for workers' compensation or similar social insurance; in practice, benefits are coordinated with workers' compensation.</li></ul>","text":"Presumptive disability Automatic total disability, regardless of ability to work. Qualifying conditions: total blindness, total deafness, loss of speech, loss of two or more limbs. Pays the FULL benefit. Nonoccupational coverage Excludes losses from the insured's employment or a hazardous occupation. No benefits if the insured is eligible for workers' compensation or similar social insurance; in practice, benefits are coordinated with workers' compensation."},{"id":"ch15-s5","title":"4. Partial disability","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> Cannot perform one or more (but not all) job duties, or cannot work full time. Result: lower income.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Generally must have been totally disabled first.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Typical benefit:</strong> 50% of the monthly benefit for 3 to 6 months.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Encourages a return to work without losing all benefits.</li></ul><h4>Flat vs. residual benefit</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>FLAT amount</th><th>RESIDUAL amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How it pays</td><td>Fixed (typically 50% of the full benefit)</td><td>Based on the percentage of income lost</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Full benefit $4,000 → partial = $2,000, regardless of other income</td><td>30% income loss → 30% of the total disability benefit</td></tr><tr><td>Formula</td><td>—</td><td>% of income lost × monthly benefit</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>NOTE:</strong> Partial and residual riders are NOT combined in the same policy.</p></div>","text":"Definition: Cannot perform one or more (but not all) job duties, or cannot work full time. Result: lower income. Prerequisite: Generally must have been totally disabled first. Typical benefit: 50% of the monthly benefit for 3 to 6 months. Purpose: Encourages a return to work without losing all benefits. Flat vs. residual benefit FLAT amount RESIDUAL amount How it pays Fixed (typically 50% of the full benefit) Based on the percentage of income lost Example Full benefit $4,000 → partial = $2,000, regardless of other income 30% income loss → 30% of the total disability benefit Formula — % of income lost × monthly benefit NOTE: Partial and residual riders are NOT combined in the same policy."},{"id":"ch15-s6","title":"5. Accidental means vs. accidental results","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>ACCIDENTAL MEANS</th><th>ACCIDENTAL RESULTS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Strictness</td><td>MORE restrictive</td><td>LESS restrictive (standard today)</td></tr><tr><td>Requirement</td><td>BOTH cause AND result must be accidental</td><td>Only the RESULT must be unexpected</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Intentionally climbing a tree, then falling: NO coverage</td><td>Playing tennis, slipping, spraining an ankle: coverage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"ACCIDENTAL MEANS ACCIDENTAL RESULTS Strictness MORE restrictive LESS restrictive (standard today) Requirement BOTH cause AND result must be accidental Only the RESULT must be unexpected Example Intentionally climbing a tree, then falling: NO coverage Playing tennis, slipping, spraining an ankle: coverage"},{"id":"ch15-s7","title":"6. Probationary vs. elimination period (CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>PROBATIONARY period</th><th>ELIMINATION (waiting) period</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>When</td><td>AFTER the policy issue date</td><td>AFTER the disability begins</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>15, 30, or 60 days</td><td>Varies; 30 to 90 days common</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>SICKNESS only (not accidents)</td><td>EVERY disability claim</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Prevent adverse selection; exclude preexisting conditions</td><td>Like a &quot;time deductible&quot;; excludes short-term claims</td></tr><tr><td>How often</td><td>ONE time only</td><td>Each claim</td></tr><tr><td>Effect on premium</td><td>—</td><td>LONGER = LOWER premium</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>PROBATIONARY = at the START of the policy. ELIMINATION = at the START of each DISABILITY.</p></div>","text":"PROBATIONARY period ELIMINATION (waiting) period When AFTER the policy issue date AFTER the disability begins Duration 15, 30, or 60 days Varies; 30 to 90 days common Applies to SICKNESS only (not accidents) EVERY disability claim Purpose Prevent adverse selection; exclude preexisting conditions Like a \"time deductible\"; excludes short-term claims How often ONE time only Each claim Effect on premium — LONGER = LOWER premium Memory trick: PROBATIONARY = at the START of the policy. ELIMINATION = at the START of each DISABILITY."},{"id":"ch15-s8","title":"7. Benefit period","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Definition:</strong> The maximum time benefits are paid.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Short-term:</strong> 6 months to 2 years.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Long-term:</strong> More than 2 years (5, 10, or 20 years, or to age 65).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Longer benefit period = HIGHER premium.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">For substandard risks, the insurer may shorten the benefit period instead of raising the premium.</li></ul>","text":"Definition: The maximum time benefits are paid. Short-term: 6 months to 2 years. Long-term: More than 2 years (5, 10, or 20 years, or to age 65). Longer benefit period = HIGHER premium. For substandard risks, the insurer may shorten the benefit period instead of raising the premium."},{"id":"ch15-s9","title":"8. Delayed and recurrent disability","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Delayed disability</strong></td><td>The disability does not start right after the accident. It must develop within a set time (typically 20, 30, 60, or 90 days) to qualify</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Recurrent disability</strong></td><td>The same disability returns after recovery. Within the set period (typically 90 days or 6 months): treated as a continuation with NO new elimination period. After that period: a new disability with a NEW elimination period</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Provision Key facts Delayed disability The disability does not start right after the accident. It must develop within a set time (typically 20, 30, 60, or 90 days) to qualify Recurrent disability The same disability returns after recovery. Within the set period (typically 90 days or 6 months): treated as a continuation with NO new elimination period. After that period: a new disability with a NEW elimination period"},{"id":"ch15-s10","title":"9. Benefit calculation methods","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Percent-of-earnings</strong></td><td>Usually 60% of pre-disability earnings. Considers other disability income. Example: 60% of $5,000 a month = $3,000 a month</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Flat amount</strong></td><td>Fixed benefit, stated in the policy, regardless of other income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Loss of earning test</strong></td><td>Counts ACTIVE income (wages, salary, commissions, fees). Does NOT count earned-but-passive income (rent, interest, dividends). Income must drop by <strong>at least 20%</strong> to qualify</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Method How it works Percent-of-earnings Usually 60% of pre-disability earnings. Considers other disability income. Example: 60% of $5,000 a month = $3,000 a month Flat amount Fixed benefit, stated in the policy, regardless of other income Loss of earning test Counts ACTIVE income (wages, salary, commissions, fees). Does NOT count earned-but-passive income (rent, interest, dividends). Income must drop by at least 20% to qualify"},{"id":"ch15-s11","title":"10. Major riders and provisions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rider</th><th>What it does</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Waiver of premium</strong></td><td>Waives premiums during total disability; keeps the policy in force</td><td>Usually after 3 or 6 months of total disability. Usually ends at age 60 or 65. NOT a loan; premiums are waived, not deferred</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Social Security rider</strong></td><td>Pays income when Social Security is not yet started, denied, or less than the rider amount</td><td>Fills the gap that Social Security may leave</td></tr><tr><td><strong>COLA rider</strong></td><td>Automatic increases tied to the CPI</td><td>Adjusted annually on the disability anniversary. Offsets inflation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guaranteed insurability rider</strong></td><td>Buy more coverage at set intervals WITHOUT proof of insurability</td><td>Also called future increase option</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rehabilitation benefit</strong></td><td>Pays for vocational training for a new occupation</td><td>Insurer covers approved program costs. The insured must stay totally disabled and actively participate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hospital confinement rider</strong></td><td>Waives the elimination period if the insured is hospitalized</td><td>Benefits start on the first day of hospitalization. Usually 6 to 12 months</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Other provisions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Change of occupation:</strong> Moving to a more hazardous job REDUCES the benefit. A less hazardous job may lower the premium, with the excess refunded.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Nondisabling injury:</strong> Injury that does not cause disability. A medical expense benefit covers actual treatment, with proof.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Confining vs. nonconfining:</strong> Confining = must stay indoors to qualify. Nonconfining = can go outdoors during recovery.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Coverage after age 65:</strong> Usually requires working a set number of hours per week.</li></ul><h4>Additional riders</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Lifetime extension:</strong> Extends benefits beyond age 65 if disabled before 65.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Elective indemnity:</strong> One-time lump sum for specific injuries (short-term policies).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Additional monthly benefit (AMB):</strong> Supplemental benefits for the first 6 to 12 months.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Return of premium:</strong> Refund of a percentage of premiums if claims are favorable.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cash surrender value:</strong> Refund of premiums at 65, less benefits paid.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Credit disability:</strong> Covers loan payments during disability.</li></ul>","text":"Rider What it does Key facts Waiver of premium Waives premiums during total disability; keeps the policy in force Usually after 3 or 6 months of total disability. Usually ends at age 60 or 65. NOT a loan; premiums are waived, not deferred Social Security rider Pays income when Social Security is not yet started, denied, or less than the rider amount Fills the gap that Social Security may leave COLA rider Automatic increases tied to the CPI Adjusted annually on the disability anniversary. Offsets inflation Guaranteed insurability rider Buy more coverage at set intervals WITHOUT proof of insurability Also called future increase option Rehabilitation benefit Pays for vocational training for a new occupation Insurer covers approved program costs. The insured must stay totally disabled and actively participate Hospital confinement rider Waives the elimination period if the insured is hospitalized Benefits start on the first day of hospitalization. Usually 6 to 12 months Other provisions Change of occupation: Moving to a more hazardous job REDUCES the benefit. A less hazardous job may lower the premium, with the excess refunded. Nondisabling injury: Injury that does not cause disability. A medical expense benefit covers actual treatment, with proof. Confining vs. nonconfining: Confining = must stay indoors to qualify. Nonconfining = can go outdoors during recovery. Coverage after age 65: Usually requires working a set number of hours per week. Additional riders Lifetime extension: Extends benefits beyond age 65 if disabled before 65. Elective indemnity: One-time lump sum for specific injuries (short-term policies). Additional monthly benefit (AMB): Supplemental benefits for the first 6 to 12 months. Return of premium: Refund of a percentage of premiums if claims are favorable. Cash surrender value: Refund of premiums at 65, less benefits paid. Credit disability: Covers loan payments during disability."},{"id":"ch15-s12","title":"11. Business uses of disability insurance","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Business overhead expense (BOE)</strong></td><td>Pays operating expenses (rent, utilities, employee salaries, equipment leases) if the owner is disabled. Does NOT pay the disabled owner's salary. Premiums deductible; benefits taxable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disability buy-sell</strong></td><td>Buys out a disabled owner's or partner's interest. Paid to the company or other shareholder. <strong>Elimination period often up to 2 years</strong> (to ensure the disability is permanent). Lump sum or periodic payments.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key person disability</strong></td><td>Monthly benefit to the business for a key employee's loss. Elimination period 30 to 90 days. Benefit period 1 to 2 years. Benefits tax-free; premiums NOT deductible</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reducing term insurance</strong></td><td>Pays off a debt if the owner is disabled. Monthly benefit for the remaining debt period. Premiums NOT deductible; benefits tax-free</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Plan Key facts Business overhead expense (BOE) Pays operating expenses (rent, utilities, employee salaries, equipment leases) if the owner is disabled. Does NOT pay the disabled owner's salary. Premiums deductible; benefits taxable Disability buy-sell Buys out a disabled owner's or partner's interest. Paid to the company or other shareholder. Elimination period often up to 2 years (to ensure the disability is permanent). Lump sum or periodic payments. Key person disability Monthly benefit to the business for a key employee's loss. Elimination period 30 to 90 days. Benefit period 1 to 2 years. Benefits tax-free; premiums NOT deductible Reducing term insurance Pays off a debt if the owner is disabled. Monthly benefit for the remaining debt period. Premiums NOT deductible; benefits tax-free"},{"id":"ch15-s13","title":"12. Common exclusions","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">War and military service</li><li class=\"l0\">Attempted suicide</li><li class=\"l0\">Overseas residence</li><li class=\"l0\">Aviation (pilots and crew)</li><li class=\"l0\">Injuries while committing a felony</li></ul>","text":"War and military service Attempted suicide Overseas residence Aviation (pilots and crew) Injuries while committing a felony"},{"id":"ch15-s14","title":"13. Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA): major life activities","html":"<p>Major life activities include: working; caring for oneself; performing manual tasks; learning, thinking, reading, concentrating, and communicating; seeing, eating, hearing, and sleeping; walking, standing, lifting, and bending; speaking and breathing.</p>","text":"Major life activities include: working; caring for oneself; performing manual tasks; learning, thinking, reading, concentrating, and communicating; seeing, eating, hearing, and sleeping; walking, standing, lifting, and bending; speaking and breathing."},{"id":"ch15-s15","title":"14. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Own-occ vs. any-occ</td><td>Your own job (more expensive) vs. any suitable job (cheaper)</td></tr><tr><td>Probationary vs. elimination</td><td>One time at issue (sickness only) vs. every claim (all disabilities)</td></tr><tr><td>Flat vs. residual</td><td>Fixed amount (usually 50%) vs. percent of income lost</td></tr><tr><td>Accidental means vs. accidental results</td><td>Cause AND result unintended vs. only result unintended</td></tr><tr><td>Waiver of premium vs. disability income</td><td>Premiums waived (keeps policy) vs. income paid to the insured</td></tr><tr><td>Benefit period vs. elimination period</td><td>How long benefits last vs. how long you wait before benefits start</td></tr><tr><td>Recurrent vs. delayed disability</td><td>Same disability returns vs. disability develops later after an accident</td></tr><tr><td>Business overhead vs. key person</td><td>Covers the business's operating costs vs. covers the business's loss of a key person</td></tr><tr><td>Presumptive vs. standard total disability</td><td>Automatic (blindness, deafness, speech, 2+ limbs) vs. must prove inability to work</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Own-occ vs. any-occ Your own job (more expensive) vs. any suitable job (cheaper) Probationary vs. elimination One time at issue (sickness only) vs. every claim (all disabilities) Flat vs. residual Fixed amount (usually 50%) vs. percent of income lost Accidental means vs. accidental results Cause AND result unintended vs. only result unintended Waiver of premium vs. disability income Premiums waived (keeps policy) vs. income paid to the insured Benefit period vs. elimination period How long benefits last vs. how long you wait before benefits start Recurrent vs. delayed disability Same disability returns vs. disability develops later after an accident Business overhead vs. key person Covers the business's operating costs vs. covers the business's loss of a key person Presumptive vs. standard total disability Automatic (blindness, deafness, speech, 2+ limbs) vs. must prove inability to work"},{"id":"ch15-s16","title":"15. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the difference between own-occupation and any-occupation coverage?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Which conditions are presumptive total disabilities?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What is a typical partial disability benefit, and how long does it usually last?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> What is the difference between flat and residual partial benefits?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Under accidental means, must both the cause and the result be accidental?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> When does the probationary period occur, and how often?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> How long is a typical elimination period, and how does it affect premium?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What is the minimum income drop under the loss of earning test?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Does a waiver of premium rider provide an income?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> What is the typical elimination period for a disability buy-sell agreement?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> A dentist loses the use of her hands. She can still teach dental students. Under an own-occupation policy, does she get benefits? Under an any-occupation policy?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Paul buys a policy with a 90-day elimination period instead of 30 days. What does this do to his premium?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A business owner wants to keep the business running and cover rent and employee salaries during his disability. Which policy fits?</li></ul>","text":"What is the difference between own-occupation and any-occupation coverage? Which conditions are presumptive total disabilities? What is a typical partial disability benefit, and how long does it usually last? What is the difference between flat and residual partial benefits? Under accidental means, must both the cause and the result be accidental? When does the probationary period occur, and how often? How long is a typical elimination period, and how does it affect premium? What is the minimum income drop under the loss of earning test? Does a waiver of premium rider provide an income? What is the typical elimination period for a disability buy-sell agreement? Scenario questions Scenario A: A dentist loses the use of her hands. She can still teach dental students. Under an own-occupation policy, does she get benefits? Under an any-occupation policy? Scenario B: Paul buys a policy with a 90-day elimination period instead of 30 days. What does this do to his premium? Scenario C: A business owner wants to keep the business running and cover rent and employee salaries during his disability. Which policy fits?"},{"id":"ch15-s17","title":"16. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Own-occ pays if you cannot do your own occupation. Any-occ pays only if you cannot do any job you are suited for. Own-occ costs more.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Total blindness, total deafness, loss of speech, and loss of two or more limbs.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> About 50% of the monthly benefit for 3 to 6 months (after total disability).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Flat: a fixed amount (usually 50%) regardless of other income. Residual: the percentage of income lost, multiplied by the total benefit.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Yes. Accidental MEANS requires both the cause and the result to be accidental.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> After the policy issue date, one time only, and it applies to sickness only.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Commonly 30 to 90 days. A longer elimination period lowers the premium.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> 20%.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> No. Premiums are waived. Income is not paid.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Up to 2 years.</p><p>Scenario A: Own-occupation: yes, she gets benefits (she cannot do dental work, her own occupation). Any-occupation: no, because she can still teach.</p><p>Scenario B: The longer elimination period (90 days) lowers the premium.</p><p>Scenario C: Business overhead expense insurance. It does not pay the owner's salary.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 15:</strong> Build a one-page &quot;definition ladder&quot;: Presumptive → Own-occ → Any-occ → Partial (flat vs residual). Then draw the timeline: Policy issue (probationary) → Disability starts (elimination) → Benefits paid (benefit period).</p></div>","text":"Own-occ pays if you cannot do your own occupation. Any-occ pays only if you cannot do any job you are suited for. Own-occ costs more. Total blindness, total deafness, loss of speech, and loss of two or more limbs. About 50% of the monthly benefit for 3 to 6 months (after total disability). Flat: a fixed amount (usually 50%) regardless of other income. Residual: the percentage of income lost, multiplied by the total benefit. Yes. Accidental MEANS requires both the cause and the result to be accidental. After the policy issue date, one time only, and it applies to sickness only. Commonly 30 to 90 days. A longer elimination period lowers the premium. 20%. No. Premiums are waived. Income is not paid. Up to 2 years. Scenario A: Own-occupation: yes, she gets benefits (she cannot do dental work, her own occupation). Any-occupation: no, because she can still teach. Scenario B: The longer elimination period (90 days) lowers the premium. Scenario C: Business overhead expense insurance. It does not pay the owner's salary. Memory tip for Chapter 15: Build a one-page \"definition ladder\": Presumptive → Own-occ → Any-occ → Partial (flat vs residual). Then draw the timeline: Policy issue (probationary) → Disability starts (elimination) → Benefits paid (benefit period)."}]},{"id":"ch16","short":"Ch 16","title":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","label":"Chapter 16","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Three private products for people 65+: <strong>Medigap</strong> (fills Medicare gaps), <strong>Medicare Advantage / Part C</strong> (private alternative to Original Medicare), and <strong>Part D</strong> (prescription drugs). Then <strong>long-term care insurance</strong>, which is NOT covered by Medicare. Focus on the time periods, the ADL trigger, and the enrollment windows.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch16-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medigap</strong> = private add-on to Original Medicare (Parts A and B). Sold by private insurers.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare Advantage (Part C)</strong> = private plan that replaces Parts A and B. Can't be combined with Medigap.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Long-term care (LTC)</strong> = help with daily living. <strong>Medicare does NOT cover it.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Key trigger:</strong> Unable to perform <strong>2 of 6 ADLs</strong> for at least <strong>90 days</strong> (or severe cognitive impairment).</li></ul>","text":"Medigap = private add-on to Original Medicare (Parts A and B). Sold by private insurers. Medicare Advantage (Part C) = private plan that replaces Parts A and B. Can't be combined with Medigap. Long-term care (LTC) = help with daily living. Medicare does NOT cover it. Key trigger: Unable to perform 2 of 6 ADLs for at least 90 days (or severe cognitive impairment)."},{"id":"ch16-s2","title":"1. Medicare supplement (Medigap) policies","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Cover gaps left by Medicare Parts A and B (deductibles and coinsurance).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Sold by:</strong> Private insurers. <strong>Eligibility:</strong> Anyone enrolled in Medicare. No minimum age.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Standardized:</strong> Since June 2010, plans are identified by letters: <strong>A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, N.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Regulation:</strong> The Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1990 (OBRA) requires conformance to NAIC minimum standards.</li></ul><h4>Open enrollment (guaranteed issue) (CRITICAL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Length:</strong> 6 months.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Begins:</strong> When the person FIRST enrolls in Medicare Part B at age 65 or older.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Guaranteed issue:</strong> The insurer CANNOT refuse coverage or rate the applicant up during this period.</li></ul><h4>Five minimum standards (memorize)</h4><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Must provide supplementary benefits for BOTH Part A and Part B.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Benefits must adjust automatically with Medicare law changes.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Preexisting condition exclusion cannot exceed <strong>6 months</strong>.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Must cover the Part B <strong>20%</strong> coinsurance (may have a deductible).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Must include a <strong>30-day free-look</strong> period.</p><h4>Core benefits (in every plan, A through N)</h4><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Part A hospital coinsurance, plus <strong>365 extra days</strong> after Medicare's hospital days run out.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Part B coinsurance or copayment.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> <strong>First 3 pints of blood.</strong></p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Hospice care coinsurance or copayment (includes family counseling).</p><h4>Key policy features</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Guaranteed renewable.</strong> The only allowed reason for cancellation is <strong>nonpayment of premium.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Cannot duplicate Medicare benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\">Losses from sickness are treated the same as accidents.</li><li class=\"l0\">The insurer cannot cancel a spouse's coverage except for nonpayment.</li><li class=\"l0\">Cannot have exclusions MORE restrictive than Medicare's.</li><li class=\"l0\">Conversion privilege required (individual or continued from group).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Consumer documents:</strong> Outline of coverage (given to the applicant) and the Buyer's Guide.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> Foreign-travel emergency coverage is an optional benefit in certain plans (it is NOT in Plan A). It is not one of the core benefits.</p></div><h4>Plans F and G (Part B excess charges)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan F</th><th>Plan G</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Covers Part B excess charges</td><td>Covers Part B excess charges</td></tr><tr><td>NOT available to NEW beneficiaries after 2020 (MACRA)</td><td>Available to new beneficiaries</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Excess charge:</strong> The part of a doctor's charge above the Medicare-approved amount, for doctors who do not accept assignment.</li></ul><h4>Plans K and L (high cost-sharing)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan K (50%)</th><th>Plan L (75%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Covers 50% of most cost sharing; you pay 50%</td><td>Covers 75% of most cost sharing; you pay 25%</td></tr><tr><td>Has an annual out-of-pocket limit</td><td>LOWER out-of-pocket limit</td></tr><tr><td>100% coverage after the limit</td><td>100% coverage after the limit</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Plans M and N</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Plan M:</strong> Skilled nursing, foreign travel emergency, and 50% of the Part A deductible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Plan N:</strong> Skilled nursing, foreign travel emergency, and 100% of the Part A deductible.</li></ul><h4>Optional benefits (some plans)</h4><p>Preventive care; prescription drugs (50% after a $250 deductible); at-home recovery (up to 8 weeks); Part B excess charges; Part A and B deductibles; skilled nursing copayments on days 21 to 100; foreign travel emergency (80% of charges).</p><h4>Medicaid suspension</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A Medigap policy can be suspended for up to <strong>2 years</strong> while the insured qualifies for Medicaid.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must request the suspension within <strong>90 days</strong> of qualifying for Medicaid.</li><li class=\"l0\">If Medicaid coverage ends, notify the insurer within <strong>90 days</strong> for automatic reinstatement.</li></ul><h4>Replacement and commission rules</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A terminated policy's benefits for an ongoing loss continue until the benefit limit is reached.</li><li class=\"l0\">A replacement group policy must cover the same individuals.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Notice of replacement</strong> must be signed by BOTH the agent and the applicant.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>First-year commission cannot exceed 200%</strong> of the subsequent-year commission.</li></ul><h4>Part D restriction</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A person enrolled in Part D cannot be sold or renew Medigap <strong>Plans H, I, or J</strong> (they overlap with drug coverage).</li></ul>","text":"Purpose: Cover gaps left by Medicare Parts A and B (deductibles and coinsurance). Sold by: Private insurers. Eligibility: Anyone enrolled in Medicare. No minimum age. Standardized: Since June 2010, plans are identified by letters: A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, N. Regulation: The Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1990 (OBRA) requires conformance to NAIC minimum standards. Open enrollment (guaranteed issue) (CRITICAL) Length: 6 months. Begins: When the person FIRST enrolls in Medicare Part B at age 65 or older. Guaranteed issue: The insurer CANNOT refuse coverage or rate the applicant up during this period. Five minimum standards (memorize) Must provide supplementary benefits for BOTH Part A and Part B. Benefits must adjust automatically with Medicare law changes. Preexisting condition exclusion cannot exceed 6 months. Must cover the Part B 20% coinsurance (may have a deductible). Must include a 30-day free-look period. Core benefits (in every plan, A through N) Part A hospital coinsurance, plus 365 extra days after Medicare's hospital days run out. Part B coinsurance or copayment. First 3 pints of blood. Hospice care coinsurance or copayment (includes family counseling). Key policy features Guaranteed renewable. The only allowed reason for cancellation is nonpayment of premium. Cannot duplicate Medicare benefits. Losses from sickness are treated the same as accidents. The insurer cannot cancel a spouse's coverage except for nonpayment. Cannot have exclusions MORE restrictive than Medicare's. Conversion privilege required (individual or continued from group). Consumer documents: Outline of coverage (given to the applicant) and the Buyer's Guide. Note: Foreign-travel emergency coverage is an optional benefit in certain plans (it is NOT in Plan A). It is not one of the core benefits. Plans F and G (Part B excess charges) Plan F Plan G Covers Part B excess charges Covers Part B excess charges NOT available to NEW beneficiaries after 2020 (MACRA) Available to new beneficiaries Excess charge: The part of a doctor's charge above the Medicare-approved amount, for doctors who do not accept assignment. Plans K and L (high cost-sharing) Plan K (50%) Plan L (75%) Covers 50% of most cost sharing; you pay 50% Covers 75% of most cost sharing; you pay 25% Has an annual out-of-pocket limit LOWER out-of-pocket limit 100% coverage after the limit 100% coverage after the limit Plans M and N Plan M: Skilled nursing, foreign travel emergency, and 50% of the Part A deductible. Plan N: Skilled nursing, foreign travel emergency, and 100% of the Part A deductible. Optional benefits (some plans) Preventive care; prescription drugs (50% after a $250 deductible); at-home recovery (up to 8 weeks); Part B excess charges; Part A and B deductibles; skilled nursing copayments on days 21 to 100; foreign travel emergency (80% of charges). Medicaid suspension A Medigap policy can be suspended for up to 2 years while the insured qualifies for Medicaid. Must request the suspension within 90 days of qualifying for Medicaid. If Medicaid coverage ends, notify the insurer within 90 days for automatic reinstatement. Replacement and commission rules A terminated policy's benefits for an ongoing loss continue until the benefit limit is reached. A replacement group policy must cover the same individuals. Notice of replacement must be signed by BOTH the agent and the applicant. First-year commission cannot exceed 200% of the subsequent-year commission. Part D restriction A person enrolled in Part D cannot be sold or renew Medigap Plans H, I, or J (they overlap with drug coverage)."},{"id":"ch16-s3","title":"2. Medicare Select","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">A Medigap plan that works like managed care: the insured must use specific hospitals and doctors for full benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exception:</strong> Emergencies are covered outside the network.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Advantage:</strong> LOWER premiums than standard Medigap.</li><li class=\"l0\">Offered by hospitals, provider groups, insurers, or third-party administrators.</li></ul>","text":"A Medigap plan that works like managed care: the insured must use specific hospitals and doctors for full benefits. Exception: Emergencies are covered outside the network. Advantage: LOWER premiums than standard Medigap. Offered by hospitals, provider groups, insurers, or third-party administrators."},{"id":"ch16-s4","title":"3. Medicare and employer coverage","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Payor</th><th>Who pays first</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Private or employer insurance pays FIRST</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Secondary</strong></td><td>Medicare pays the remaining costs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Payor Who pays first Primary Private or employer insurance pays FIRST Secondary Medicare pays the remaining costs"},{"id":"ch16-s5","title":"4. Medicare Part C (Medicare Advantage)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Also known as Medicare Advantage (formerly Medicare+Choice).</li><li class=\"l0\">Provided through <strong>private health plans approved by Medicare.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Expanded under the <strong>Medicare Modernization Act of 2003.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Medicare pays a fixed amount to the private plan for each beneficiary.</li></ul><h4>Types of Part C plans</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>HMO</strong></td><td>Health maintenance organization (network, referrals)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PPO</strong></td><td>Preferred provider organization (more flexibility than an HMO)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PSO</strong></td><td>Provider-sponsored organization. Run by health care providers, not insurers. Providers take on financial risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PFFS</strong></td><td>Private fee-for-service. Can visit ANY Medicare-approved provider that accepts the plan's terms. Not all providers accept PFFS terms. The private plan sets payment amounts. May offer extra benefits</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Part C eligibility</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Must be enrolled in BOTH Part A and Part B, and must pay the Part B premium.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must live in the plan's service area.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cannot have end-stage renal disease (kidney failure).</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">No proof of insurability if enrolling within 6 months of the 65th birthday.</li></ul><h4>Part C key points</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Only ONE Medicare Advantage plan at a time.</li><li class=\"l0\">May reduce the need for a separate Medigap plan.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Does NOT cover long-term care.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Health Savings Account (HSA): a specialized trust for qualified medical expenses. Contributions tax-deductible; growth tax-free when used for qualified expenses.</li></ul><h4>Managed care availability</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Medicare managed care plans may be statewide or limited to certain counties.</li><li class=\"l0\">Companies decide each year whether to continue participating.</li></ul>","text":"Also known as Medicare Advantage (formerly Medicare+Choice). Provided through private health plans approved by Medicare. Expanded under the Medicare Modernization Act of 2003. Medicare pays a fixed amount to the private plan for each beneficiary. Types of Part C plans Plan Key facts HMO Health maintenance organization (network, referrals) PPO Preferred provider organization (more flexibility than an HMO) PSO Provider-sponsored organization. Run by health care providers, not insurers. Providers take on financial risk PFFS Private fee-for-service. Can visit ANY Medicare-approved provider that accepts the plan's terms. Not all providers accept PFFS terms. The private plan sets payment amounts. May offer extra benefits Part C eligibility Must be enrolled in BOTH Part A and Part B, and must pay the Part B premium. Must live in the plan's service area. Cannot have end-stage renal disease (kidney failure). No proof of insurability if enrolling within 6 months of the 65th birthday. Part C key points Only ONE Medicare Advantage plan at a time. May reduce the need for a separate Medigap plan. Does NOT cover long-term care. Health Savings Account (HSA): a specialized trust for qualified medical expenses. Contributions tax-deductible; growth tax-free when used for qualified expenses. Managed care availability Medicare managed care plans may be statewide or limited to certain counties. Companies decide each year whether to continue participating."},{"id":"ch16-s6","title":"5. Medicare Part D (prescription drugs)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Drug plans run by private insurers, through Medicare Advantage OR standalone prescription drug plans (PDPs).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Eligibility:</strong> Must be enrolled in Part A or Part B.</li><li class=\"l0\">Requires a premium and a deductible.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Late enrollment penalty:</strong> <strong>1% per month</strong> of delay if the person does not join during the initial enrollment period.</li><li class=\"l0\">All Part D plans provide basic and core benefits. Extra benefits cost extra premium.</li></ul>","text":"Drug plans run by private insurers, through Medicare Advantage OR standalone prescription drug plans (PDPs). Eligibility: Must be enrolled in Part A or Part B. Requires a premium and a deductible. Late enrollment penalty: 1% per month of delay if the person does not join during the initial enrollment period. All Part D plans provide basic and core benefits. Extra benefits cost extra premium."},{"id":"ch16-s7","title":"6. MACRA (2015)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare Access and CHIP Reauthorization Act</strong> of 2015: pay-for-performance model for physicians.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>MIPS</strong> categories: meaningful use (electronic health records), quality reporting, cost, and clinical practice improvement.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Key impact for this chapter:</strong> Plans C and F were phased out for NEW Medicare beneficiaries after 2020.</li></ul>","text":"Medicare Access and CHIP Reauthorization Act of 2015: pay-for-performance model for physicians. MIPS categories: meaningful use (electronic health records), quality reporting, cost, and clinical practice improvement. Key impact for this chapter: Plans C and F were phased out for NEW Medicare beneficiaries after 2020."},{"id":"ch16-s8","title":"7. Long-term care (LTC) insurance","html":"<h4>Purpose and basics</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Helps people who can no longer live independently.</li><li class=\"l0\">Covers medical, personal, and environmental services.</li><li class=\"l0\">LTC typically means care lasting more than 90 days, at home, in an adult care center, or in a nursing home.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Common misconception: Medicare covers LTC. It does not.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Most policies pay a <strong>fixed daily dollar amount.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Covers cognitive impairments (Alzheimer's, dementia).</li><li class=\"l0\">Typical application age: <strong>18 to 89.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">The NAIC Long-Term Care Insurance Model Act sets minimum standards.</li></ul><h4>Benefit trigger (EXAM CRITICAL)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Unable to perform at least 2 of 6 ADLs</strong> for at least <strong>90 days</strong>, OR</li><li class=\"l0\">Severe <strong>cognitive impairment</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>The six ADLs:</strong></p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Bathing</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Dressing</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Toileting</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Transferring (bed to chair)</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Continence</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Eating</p><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Substantial assistance (NAIC definition):</strong> Physical help, OR stand-by assistance (a caregiver present for immediate hands-on help if needed).</li></ul><h4>Cognitive impairment trigger</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Decline in short- or long-term memory, awareness of person/place/time, logical reasoning, or judgment (especially safety).</li><li class=\"l0\">Covered conditions include Alzheimer's, Parkinson's, non-Alzheimer's senile dementia, stroke, and brain injury.</li></ul><h4>Three levels of long-term care</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Skilled nursing</th><th>Intermediate</th><th>Custodial</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Daily medical or rehab care by skilled professionals; 24-hour nursing; physician-ordered; SHORT-TERM focus</td><td>Occasional or intermittent care by trained medical personnel; physician-ordered</td><td>Help with daily activities by non-medical caregivers; physician-prescribed; RN oversight. <strong>MOST COMMON</strong> for the elderly</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Skilled nursing facility (SNF):</strong> Short-term. <strong>Nursing home:</strong> Long-term.</li></ul><h4>Home and community-based services</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Home health care</strong></td><td>Part-time skilled or unskilled care at home (typically 50% of the daily benefit). Skilled care must be doctor-ordered</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Adult day care</strong></td><td>Care in a center when the primary caregiver is unavailable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Respite care</strong></td><td>Temporary relief for family caregivers (typically a few days)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Continuing care</strong></td><td>Benefits for people in continuing care retirement communities</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Assisted living</strong></td><td>Housing plus services (nursing care, housekeeping, meals). Qualifies if the person needs help with 1 or 2 ADLs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Elimination period</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Range: <strong>0 to 365 days.</strong> No benefits are paid during it.</li></ul><h4>Calendar days vs. service days (CRITICAL)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>CALENDAR days</th><th>SERVICE days</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Every consecutive day counts, whether or not care is received</td><td>Only days in which care is actually received count</td></tr><tr><td>FASTER access to benefits</td><td>SLOWER access to benefits</td></tr><tr><td>Example: a 30-day period starting March 1 ends March 30</td><td>Example: 30 service days at 3 visits per week = about 10 weeks</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Key LTC policy features</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Guaranteed renewable.</strong> Cannot be canceled while premiums are paid (rates can rise).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Preexisting conditions:</strong> Treatment or advice within 6 months before the policy; exclusion limited to 6 months.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Minimum benefit period:</strong> 1 year (a longer period costs more).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Free-look period:</strong> 30 days.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Post-claims underwriting: PROHIBITED.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Prior hospitalization is NOT required for nursing home benefits in modern policies.</li></ul><h4>LTC application and underwriting</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Applications must use clear, straightforward questions (except guaranteed-issue policies).</li><li class=\"l0\">The applicant receives a copy of the application at delivery (it is part of the complete contract).</li><li class=\"l0\">Older applicants may need medical records, a physician statement, a physical exam, or an ADL assessment.</li><li class=\"l0\">Cost factors: age, health, and benefit level. Younger applicants pay lower premiums.</li></ul><h4>LTC riders and options</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rider</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Inflation protection</strong></td><td>Benefits increase periodically with rising costs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guaranteed insurability</strong></td><td>Buy more coverage later without proving health</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Waiver of premium</strong></td><td>Premiums suspended during a hospital stay of 90 or 180 days</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Return of premium</strong></td><td>Refund if the policy lapses, or if death occurs before benefits are used</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>LTC exclusions (common)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Addictive behaviors (alcohol or drug dependency); acts of war; self-inflicted injuries; nervous disorders and mental health conditions; acute illness hospital care (usually NOT covered by comprehensive LTC).</li></ul><h4>Acute vs. chronic conditions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>ACUTE</th><th>CHRONIC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pneumonia, influenza. The body can fully recover. NOT typically covered by LTC</td><td>Arthritis, heart disease, hypertension. Treatable but not curable. Affects independence over time. This is what LTC covers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Purpose and basics Helps people who can no longer live independently. Covers medical, personal, and environmental services. LTC typically means care lasting more than 90 days, at home, in an adult care center, or in a nursing home. Common misconception: Medicare covers LTC. It does not. Most policies pay a fixed daily dollar amount. Covers cognitive impairments (Alzheimer's, dementia). Typical application age: 18 to 89. The NAIC Long-Term Care Insurance Model Act sets minimum standards. Benefit trigger (EXAM CRITICAL) Unable to perform at least 2 of 6 ADLs for at least 90 days, OR Severe cognitive impairment. The six ADLs: Bathing Dressing Toileting Transferring (bed to chair) Continence Eating Substantial assistance (NAIC definition): Physical help, OR stand-by assistance (a caregiver present for immediate hands-on help if needed). Cognitive impairment trigger Decline in short- or long-term memory, awareness of person/place/time, logical reasoning, or judgment (especially safety). Covered conditions include Alzheimer's, Parkinson's, non-Alzheimer's senile dementia, stroke, and brain injury. Three levels of long-term care Skilled nursing Intermediate Custodial Daily medical or rehab care by skilled professionals; 24-hour nursing; physician-ordered; SHORT-TERM focus Occasional or intermittent care by trained medical personnel; physician-ordered Help with daily activities by non-medical caregivers; physician-prescribed; RN oversight. MOST COMMON for the elderly Skilled nursing facility (SNF): Short-term. Nursing home: Long-term. Home and community-based services Service Key facts Home health care Part-time skilled or unskilled care at home (typically 50% of the daily benefit). Skilled care must be doctor-ordered Adult day care Care in a center when the primary caregiver is unavailable Respite care Temporary relief for family caregivers (typically a few days) Continuing care Benefits for people in continuing care retirement communities Assisted living Housing plus services (nursing care, housekeeping, meals). Qualifies if the person needs help with 1 or 2 ADLs Elimination period Range: 0 to 365 days. No benefits are paid during it. Calendar days vs. service days (CRITICAL) CALENDAR days SERVICE days Every consecutive day counts, whether or not care is received Only days in which care is actually received count FASTER access to benefits SLOWER access to benefits Example: a 30-day period starting March 1 ends March 30 Example: 30 service days at 3 visits per week = about 10 weeks Key LTC policy features Guaranteed renewable. Cannot be canceled while premiums are paid (rates can rise). Preexisting conditions: Treatment or advice within 6 months before the policy; exclusion limited to 6 months. Minimum benefit period: 1 year (a longer period costs more). Free-look period: 30 days. Post-claims underwriting: PROHIBITED. Prior hospitalization is NOT required for nursing home benefits in modern policies. LTC application and underwriting Applications must use clear, straightforward questions (except guaranteed-issue policies). The applicant receives a copy of the application at delivery (it is part of the complete contract). Older applicants may need medical records, a physician statement, a physical exam, or an ADL assessment. Cost factors: age, health, and benefit level. Younger applicants pay lower premiums. LTC riders and options Rider What it does Inflation protection Benefits increase periodically with rising costs Guaranteed insurability Buy more coverage later without proving health Waiver of premium Premiums suspended during a hospital stay of 90 or 180 days Return of premium Refund if the policy lapses, or if death occurs before benefits are used LTC exclusions (common) Addictive behaviors (alcohol or drug dependency); acts of war; self-inflicted injuries; nervous disorders and mental health conditions; acute illness hospital care (usually NOT covered by comprehensive LTC). Acute vs. chronic conditions ACUTE CHRONIC Pneumonia, influenza. The body can fully recover. NOT typically covered by LTC Arthritis, heart disease, hypertension. Treatable but not curable. Affects independence over time. This is what LTC covers"},{"id":"ch16-s9","title":"8. Long-term care partnership programs","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">State-operated, federally supported.</li><li class=\"l0\">Purpose: protect assets from the <strong>Medicaid spend-down</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Dollar-for-dollar</strong> asset protection equal to the insurance benefits paid. Example: if the policy pays $100,000, the insured can protect $100,000 of assets when applying for Medicaid.</li></ul>","text":"State-operated, federally supported. Purpose: protect assets from the Medicaid spend-down. Dollar-for-dollar asset protection equal to the insurance benefits paid. Example: if the policy pays $100,000, the insured can protect $100,000 of assets when applying for Medicaid."},{"id":"ch16-s10","title":"9. Association and group LTC requirements","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The association must <strong>educate</strong> members on LTC issues, with objective, balanced information.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must disclose ALL compensation (fees, commissions, administrative fees) and any interlocking directorates with the insurer.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must briefly describe how the policies and insurers were selected.</li><li class=\"l0\">The board must approve the policies AND the compensation arrangements.</li><li class=\"l0\">Non-compliance is an <strong>unfair trade practice.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Insurers must submit policy documents, outlines of coverage, and advertisements to the state's Superintendent (commissioner) and provide annual certification of association compliance.</li><li class=\"l0\">Policies from other states must show approval by a regulator with comparable requirements.</li></ul><h4>Required LTC disclosures to applicants</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Outline of coverage</li><li class=\"l0\">Shopper's guide</li><li class=\"l0\">Personal worksheet</li><li class=\"l0\">Rating practices</li></ul><h4>LTC marketing procedures</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Fair comparisons with adequate information.</li><li class=\"l0\">Avoid over-insurance (no more coverage than needed).</li><li class=\"l0\">Clarify that the policy may not cover all LTC expenses.</li></ul>","text":"The association must educate members on LTC issues, with objective, balanced information. Must disclose ALL compensation (fees, commissions, administrative fees) and any interlocking directorates with the insurer. Must briefly describe how the policies and insurers were selected. The board must approve the policies AND the compensation arrangements. Non-compliance is an unfair trade practice. Insurers must submit policy documents, outlines of coverage, and advertisements to the state's Superintendent (commissioner) and provide annual certification of association compliance. Policies from other states must show approval by a regulator with comparable requirements. Required LTC disclosures to applicants Outline of coverage Shopper's guide Personal worksheet Rating practices LTC marketing procedures Fair comparisons with adequate information. Avoid over-insurance (no more coverage than needed). Clarify that the policy may not cover all LTC expenses."},{"id":"ch16-s11","title":"10. LTC in life insurance and hybrid plans","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Living benefits / accelerated benefits rider:</strong> Can cover home health and nursing home care. The benefit is deducted from the death benefit. Uses the same triggers as individual LTC policies.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>LTC hybrid (linked) plans:</strong> Combine LTC with an annuity or life insurance. The benefit can go to LTC costs or to the insurance or annuity payout. If LTC is not needed, the benefit goes to the beneficiary.</li></ul>","text":"Living benefits / accelerated benefits rider: Can cover home health and nursing home care. The benefit is deducted from the death benefit. Uses the same triggers as individual LTC policies. LTC hybrid (linked) plans: Combine LTC with an annuity or life insurance. The benefit can go to LTC costs or to the insurance or annuity payout. If LTC is not needed, the benefit goes to the beneficiary."},{"id":"ch16-s12","title":"11. Taxation of LTC","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Qualified LTC insurance is treated as health or accident insurance.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Premiums:</strong> Deductible as medical expenses if total medical expenses exceed a percentage of adjusted gross income set by the IRS.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Benefits:</strong> Generally NOT taxed, subject to inflation-adjusted daily caps.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Employer-paid premiums:</strong> Usually excluded from the employee's taxable income.</li></ul>","text":"Qualified LTC insurance is treated as health or accident insurance. Premiums: Deductible as medical expenses if total medical expenses exceed a percentage of adjusted gross income set by the IRS. Benefits: Generally NOT taxed, subject to inflation-adjusted daily caps. Employer-paid premiums: Usually excluded from the employee's taxable income."},{"id":"ch16-s13","title":"12. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medigap vs. Medicare Advantage</td><td>Add-on to Original Medicare vs. replaces Parts A and B. Cannot be combined</td></tr><tr><td>Plan F vs. Plan G</td><td>Both cover Part B excess charges. F is closed to new beneficiaries after 2020 (with Plan C). G is available</td></tr><tr><td>Plan K vs. Plan L</td><td>K pays 50%. L pays 75% (lower out-of-pocket limit)</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare Select vs. standard Medigap</td><td>Network-based, lower premium vs. any provider</td></tr><tr><td>HMO vs. PPO vs. PFFS vs. PSO (Part C)</td><td>Network and referrals vs. more flexibility vs. any approved provider at plan rates vs. provider-run</td></tr><tr><td>Calendar vs. service days</td><td>Every day counts vs. only care days count</td></tr><tr><td>Skilled vs. custodial care</td><td>Medical, 24-hour, short-term vs. daily-living help, most common</td></tr><tr><td>Acute vs. chronic conditions</td><td>Recoverable (not covered by LTC) vs. long-term, affects independence (covered by LTC)</td></tr><tr><td>Nursing home vs. SNF</td><td>Long-term vs. short-term skilled</td></tr><tr><td>Primary vs. secondary payor</td><td>First vs. second</td></tr><tr><td>Medicaid suspension vs. reinstatement</td><td>Request within 90 days when you qualify vs. notify within 90 days when coverage ends</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Medigap vs. Medicare Advantage Add-on to Original Medicare vs. replaces Parts A and B. Cannot be combined Plan F vs. Plan G Both cover Part B excess charges. F is closed to new beneficiaries after 2020 (with Plan C). G is available Plan K vs. Plan L K pays 50%. L pays 75% (lower out-of-pocket limit) Medicare Select vs. standard Medigap Network-based, lower premium vs. any provider HMO vs. PPO vs. PFFS vs. PSO (Part C) Network and referrals vs. more flexibility vs. any approved provider at plan rates vs. provider-run Calendar vs. service days Every day counts vs. only care days count Skilled vs. custodial care Medical, 24-hour, short-term vs. daily-living help, most common Acute vs. chronic conditions Recoverable (not covered by LTC) vs. long-term, affects independence (covered by LTC) Nursing home vs. SNF Long-term vs. short-term skilled Primary vs. secondary payor First vs. second Medicaid suspension vs. reinstatement Request within 90 days when you qualify vs. notify within 90 days when coverage ends"},{"id":"ch16-s14","title":"13. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the open enrollment period for Medigap, and when does it begin?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> How many Medigap letters are there, and which is closed to new beneficiaries after 2020?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What are the five minimum standards for Medigap?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> What is the Part D late enrollment penalty?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Which Part C plan type is run by health care providers?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What is the LTC benefit trigger?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What are the six ADLs?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What is the difference between calendar days and service days?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> What is the maximum preexisting condition exclusion period for LTC?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> What does a partnership program protect?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Ellen turns 65 and enrolls in Medicare Part B. She applies for Medigap within her first 6 months. Can the insurer refuse her because of a heart condition?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Frank can no longer bathe or dress himself and needs hands-on help. His policy requires 2 of 6 ADLs for 90 days. Does he qualify if the help lasts 90 days?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> Maria wants a policy that starts paying as soon as she receives care three times a week. Should she choose calendar days or service days?</li></ul>","text":"What is the open enrollment period for Medigap, and when does it begin? How many Medigap letters are there, and which is closed to new beneficiaries after 2020? What are the five minimum standards for Medigap? What is the Part D late enrollment penalty? Which Part C plan type is run by health care providers? What is the LTC benefit trigger? What are the six ADLs? What is the difference between calendar days and service days? What is the maximum preexisting condition exclusion period for LTC? What does a partnership program protect? Scenario questions Scenario A: Ellen turns 65 and enrolls in Medicare Part B. She applies for Medigap within her first 6 months. Can the insurer refuse her because of a heart condition? Scenario B: Frank can no longer bathe or dress himself and needs hands-on help. His policy requires 2 of 6 ADLs for 90 days. Does he qualify if the help lasts 90 days? Scenario C: Maria wants a policy that starts paying as soon as she receives care three times a week. Should she choose calendar days or service days?"},{"id":"ch16-s15","title":"14. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Six months, beginning when the person first enrolls in Part B at age 65 or older.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Ten (A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, N). Plans C and F are closed to people who became Medicare beneficiaries after 2020 (MACRA).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> (1) Covers both Part A and Part B; (2) benefits adjust with Medicare law changes; (3) preexisting exclusion no more than 6 months; (4) covers the 20% Part B coinsurance; (5) 30-day free look.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> 1% per month of delay (if not enrolled during the initial enrollment period).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> PSO (provider-sponsored organization).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Unable to perform at least 2 of 6 ADLs for at least 90 days, or severe cognitive impairment.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Bathing, dressing, toileting, transferring, continence, eating.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Calendar days count every consecutive day. Service days count only days in which care is received.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> 6 months.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> The Medicaid spend-down. It protects assets on a dollar-for-dollar basis equal to the benefits paid.</p><p>Scenario A: No. Within the 6-month open enrollment period, the insurer must issue the policy (guaranteed issue) and cannot rate her up.</p><p>Scenario B: Yes. He cannot bathe and dress without help (2 ADLs), for at least 90 days, so he meets the benefit trigger.</p><p>Scenario C: Calendar days. Service days would only count the days she receives care, which slows access to benefits. Calendar days count every day, so benefits start sooner.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 16:</strong> Make three cards: (1) Medigap letters and the 6-month window; (2) Part A/B/C/D one line each; (3) LTC trigger: 2 of 6 ADLs, 90 days, elimination 0–365, calendar vs. service. Quiz yourself before bed.</p></div>","text":"Six months, beginning when the person first enrolls in Part B at age 65 or older. Ten (A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, N). Plans C and F are closed to people who became Medicare beneficiaries after 2020 (MACRA). (1) Covers both Part A and Part B; (2) benefits adjust with Medicare law changes; (3) preexisting exclusion no more than 6 months; (4) covers the 20% Part B coinsurance; (5) 30-day free look. 1% per month of delay (if not enrolled during the initial enrollment period). PSO (provider-sponsored organization). Unable to perform at least 2 of 6 ADLs for at least 90 days, or severe cognitive impairment. Bathing, dressing, toileting, transferring, continence, eating. Calendar days count every consecutive day. Service days count only days in which care is received. 6 months. The Medicaid spend-down. It protects assets on a dollar-for-dollar basis equal to the benefits paid. Scenario A: No. Within the 6-month open enrollment period, the insurer must issue the policy (guaranteed issue) and cannot rate her up. Scenario B: Yes. He cannot bathe and dress without help (2 ADLs), for at least 90 days, so he meets the benefit trigger. Scenario C: Calendar days. Service days would only count the days she receives care, which slows access to benefits. Calendar days count every day, so benefits start sooner. Memory tip for Chapter 16: Make three cards: (1) Medigap letters and the 6-month window; (2) Part A/B/C/D one line each; (3) LTC trigger: 2 of 6 ADLs, 90 days, elimination 0–365, calendar vs. service. Quiz yourself before bed."}]},{"id":"ch17","short":"Ch 17","title":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","label":"Chapter 17","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> Every individual health policy must include a set of <strong>12 mandatory provisions</strong> from the NAIC model law, and may include <strong>11 optional provisions</strong>. This chapter is almost entirely NUMBERS: grace periods, notice of claim, proof of loss, legal action timing, free look, and cancellation. Memorize the time limits table and you will answer most questions.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch17-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>12 mandatory</strong> provisions (must be in every policy) and <strong>11 optional</strong> (insurer's choice).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Grace period (NAIC model law):</strong> 7 days (weekly), 10 days (monthly), 31 days (quarterly or longer). Louisiana uses 30 days.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Clock rules:</strong> Notice of claim 20 days. Claim forms 15 days. Proof of loss 90 days. Wait 60 days before suing. Sue within 3 years.</li></ul>","text":"12 mandatory provisions (must be in every policy) and 11 optional (insurer's choice). Grace period (NAIC model law): 7 days (weekly), 10 days (monthly), 31 days (quarterly or longer). Louisiana uses 30 days. Clock rules: Notice of claim 20 days. Claim forms 15 days. Proof of loss 90 days. Wait 60 days before suing. Sue within 3 years."},{"id":"ch17-s2","title":"1. NAIC model law","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The <strong>Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provisions Law</strong> created by the NAIC.</li><li class=\"l0\">Purpose: consistent terms and language across individual health policies.</li><li class=\"l0\">Most states have adopted it.</li></ul>","text":"The Uniform Individual Accident and Sickness Policy Provisions Law created by the NAIC. Purpose: consistent terms and language across individual health policies. Most states have adopted it."},{"id":"ch17-s3","title":"2. The 12 mandatory provisions","html":"<h4>1. Entire contract</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The contract = policy + application + endorsements + riders. No outside documents or statements count.</li><li class=\"l0\">Agents and producers CANNOT change the policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">Only an authorized executive officer of the insurer can change it, documented on the policy or as an attached rider.</li></ul><h4>2. Time limit on certain defenses (incontestability)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Contestable period: usually <strong>2 to 3 years</strong> from issue.</li><li class=\"l0\">After the period, the insurer cannot contest the policy based on application misstatements.</li><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana: life insurance incontestability is 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)); health and accident is 3 years (R.S. 22:975).</li><li class=\"l0\">After the time limit, the insurer cannot void, deny, or reduce claims based on preexisting conditions.</li></ul><h4>3. Grace period (CRITICAL)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Payment frequency</th><th>Grace period (NAIC model law)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Weekly (industrial policies)</td><td><strong>7 days</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Monthly</td><td><strong>10 days</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Quarterly, semiannual, or annual</td><td><strong>31 days</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">If a claim is paid during the grace period, the unpaid premium is deducted from the payment.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Louisiana:</strong> Individual life policies: a grace period of <strong>30 days, or one month at the insurer's option</strong> (R.S. 22:931(A)(1)). Health and accident policies: <strong>30 days</strong> from the due date (R.S. 22:977). For health, if the premium is still unpaid 15 days before the grace period ends, the insurer must mail a first-class notice that the policy will lapse and can be reinstated.</li><li class=\"l0\">Death during the grace period (life insurance): the beneficiary receives the face amount MINUS any outstanding premiums.</li><li class=\"l0\">Failure to pay before the grace period ends = the policy LAPSES.</li></ul><h4>4. Reinstatement</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Puts a lapsed policy back in force. Time allowed: <strong>up to 3 years</strong>. Louisiana life: within 3 years of default, with evidence of insurability and payment (R.S. 22:931(A)(9)). Louisiana health: accepting the defaulted premium reinstates the policy, and sickness is covered again after 10 days (R.S. 22:975(A)(2)).</li><li class=\"l0\">Requirements: a reinstatement application, a statement of good health (proof of insurability), and payment of all back premiums, loans, and accrued interest.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Automatic reinstatement:</strong> If the insurer does not respond within <strong>45 days</strong> of the application.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Coverage on reinstatement:</strong> Accidents resume <strong>immediately</strong>. Sickness resumes after <strong>10 days</strong> (to prevent adverse selection).</li></ul><h4>5. Notice of claim</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Within <strong>20 days</strong> of the loss (or as soon as reasonably possible).</li><li class=\"l0\">Ongoing disability: notice may be required every <strong>6 months</strong> to confirm continuing disability.</li></ul><h4>6. Claim forms</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The insurer must provide claim forms within <strong>15 days</strong> of receiving notice.</li><li class=\"l0\">If the insurer fails to, the claimant may submit proof in any format that contains the necessary information.</li></ul><h4>7. Proof of loss</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Must be submitted within <strong>90 days</strong> from the date of loss.</li><li class=\"l0\">Extended to <strong>1 year</strong> if the claimant is legally incapacitated.</li><li class=\"l0\">For non-periodic payments, the insurer must begin payment immediately after receiving proof.</li></ul><h4>8. Time of payment of claims</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Paid immediately after the insurer receives notice and proof of loss.</li><li class=\"l0\">Disability income: paid at least <strong>monthly</strong>.</li></ul><h4>9. Payment of claims</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Health benefits: paid to the insured OR directly to the provider.</li><li class=\"l0\">Death benefits: paid to the named beneficiary; if none, to the estate.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Minor beneficiary:</strong> payment may be made to a relative by blood or marriage, capped at <strong>$1,000</strong>.</li></ul><h4>10. Physical exam and autopsy</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The insurer may require an exam at its own expense at reasonable intervals while a claim is active.</li><li class=\"l0\">The insurer may request an autopsy after death, unless state law prohibits it.</li></ul><h4>11. Legal actions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Must wait at least <strong>60 days</strong> after proof of loss before suing.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must file suit within <strong>3 years</strong>.</li></ul><h4>12. Change of beneficiary</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Revocable beneficiary: can be changed at any time without consent.</li><li class=\"l0\">Irrevocable beneficiary: changes require <strong>WRITTEN CONSENT</strong>.</li></ul><h4>Quick reference: the 12 mandatory provisions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>Key number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Grace period</td><td>7 / 10 / 31 days (NAIC model); Louisiana 30 days</td></tr><tr><td>Incontestability</td><td>2 to 3 years (Louisiana: life 2 years, health 3 years)</td></tr><tr><td>Notice of claim</td><td>20 days</td></tr><tr><td>Claim forms</td><td>Insurer provides within 15 days</td></tr><tr><td>Proof of loss</td><td>90 days (1 year if incapacitated)</td></tr><tr><td>Legal actions</td><td>Wait 60 days; file within 3 years</td></tr><tr><td>Reinstatement</td><td>Auto-reinstated if no response in 45 days; sickness coverage starts after 10 days</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (grace periods):</strong> Weekly = a WEEK (7). Monthly = 10 days. Everything else = a month and a day (31).</p></div>","text":"1. Entire contract The contract = policy + application + endorsements + riders. No outside documents or statements count. Agents and producers CANNOT change the policy. Only an authorized executive officer of the insurer can change it, documented on the policy or as an attached rider. 2. Time limit on certain defenses (incontestability) Contestable period: usually 2 to 3 years from issue. After the period, the insurer cannot contest the policy based on application misstatements. Louisiana: life insurance incontestability is 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)); health and accident is 3 years (R.S. 22:975). After the time limit, the insurer cannot void, deny, or reduce claims based on preexisting conditions. 3. Grace period (CRITICAL) Payment frequency Grace period (NAIC model law) Weekly (industrial policies) 7 days Monthly 10 days Quarterly, semiannual, or annual 31 days If a claim is paid during the grace period, the unpaid premium is deducted from the payment. Louisiana: Individual life policies: a grace period of 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option (R.S. 22:931(A)(1)). Health and accident policies: 30 days from the due date (R.S. 22:977). For health, if the premium is still unpaid 15 days before the grace period ends, the insurer must mail a first-class notice that the policy will lapse and can be reinstated. Death during the grace period (life insurance): the beneficiary receives the face amount MINUS any outstanding premiums. Failure to pay before the grace period ends = the policy LAPSES. 4. Reinstatement Puts a lapsed policy back in force. Time allowed: up to 3 years. Louisiana life: within 3 years of default, with evidence of insurability and payment (R.S. 22:931(A)(9)). Louisiana health: accepting the defaulted premium reinstates the policy, and sickness is covered again after 10 days (R.S. 22:975(A)(2)). Requirements: a reinstatement application, a statement of good health (proof of insurability), and payment of all back premiums, loans, and accrued interest. Automatic reinstatement: If the insurer does not respond within 45 days of the application. Coverage on reinstatement: Accidents resume immediately. Sickness resumes after 10 days (to prevent adverse selection). 5. Notice of claim Within 20 days of the loss (or as soon as reasonably possible). Ongoing disability: notice may be required every 6 months to confirm continuing disability. 6. Claim forms The insurer must provide claim forms within 15 days of receiving notice. If the insurer fails to, the claimant may submit proof in any format that contains the necessary information. 7. Proof of loss Must be submitted within 90 days from the date of loss. Extended to 1 year if the claimant is legally incapacitated. For non-periodic payments, the insurer must begin payment immediately after receiving proof. 8. Time of payment of claims Paid immediately after the insurer receives notice and proof of loss. Disability income: paid at least monthly. 9. Payment of claims Health benefits: paid to the insured OR directly to the provider. Death benefits: paid to the named beneficiary; if none, to the estate. Minor beneficiary: payment may be made to a relative by blood or marriage, capped at $1,000. 10. Physical exam and autopsy The insurer may require an exam at its own expense at reasonable intervals while a claim is active. The insurer may request an autopsy after death, unless state law prohibits it. 11. Legal actions Must wait at least 60 days after proof of loss before suing. Must file suit within 3 years. 12. Change of beneficiary Revocable beneficiary: can be changed at any time without consent. Irrevocable beneficiary: changes require WRITTEN CONSENT. Quick reference: the 12 mandatory provisions Provision Key number Grace period 7 / 10 / 31 days (NAIC model); Louisiana 30 days Incontestability 2 to 3 years (Louisiana: life 2 years, health 3 years) Notice of claim 20 days Claim forms Insurer provides within 15 days Proof of loss 90 days (1 year if incapacitated) Legal actions Wait 60 days; file within 3 years Reinstatement Auto-reinstated if no response in 45 days; sickness coverage starts after 10 days Memory trick (grace periods): Weekly = a WEEK (7). Monthly = 10 days. Everything else = a month and a day (31)."},{"id":"ch17-s4","title":"3. The 11 optional provisions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Provision</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Change of occupation</strong></td><td>More hazardous job: maximum benefit REDUCED. Less hazardous job: premium REDUCED</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Misstatement of age or sex</strong></td><td>Older than stated: benefits REDUCED. Younger than stated: benefits INCREASED</td></tr><tr><td>3-5</td><td><strong>Other insurance</strong></td><td>(a) Other insurance with THIS insurer: limits total coverage from one company. (b) Insurance with another insurer: benefits prorated if the insurer did not know about the other coverage. (c) Pro-rata payments when benefits are not based on expenses</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Relation of earnings (average earnings clause)</strong></td><td>If total disability benefits would exceed the insured's earnings, the insurer pays only a proportionate amount. Prevents over-insurance</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Unpaid premiums</strong></td><td>Unpaid premiums can be deducted from claim payments</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><strong>Cancellation</strong></td><td>Insurer cancellation notice: <strong>45 days</strong> written notice. Non-payment cancellation: <strong>10 days</strong> notice. The insured can cancel any time after the initial term</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><strong>Conformity with state statutes</strong></td><td>The policy is automatically amended to meet state law</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><strong>Illegal occupation</strong></td><td>No coverage for losses while engaged in illegal activity or felonies</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><strong>Intoxicants and narcotics</strong></td><td>No coverage if intoxicated or using narcotics without a prescription. Some policies exclude extended stays overseas</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory trick (change of occupation):</strong> MORE hazard = LESS benefit. LESS hazard = LESS premium. (Both changes lower the insurer's exposure.)</p></div>","text":"# Provision What it does 1 Change of occupation More hazardous job: maximum benefit REDUCED. Less hazardous job: premium REDUCED 2 Misstatement of age or sex Older than stated: benefits REDUCED. Younger than stated: benefits INCREASED 3-5 Other insurance (a) Other insurance with THIS insurer: limits total coverage from one company. (b) Insurance with another insurer: benefits prorated if the insurer did not know about the other coverage. (c) Pro-rata payments when benefits are not based on expenses 6 Relation of earnings (average earnings clause) If total disability benefits would exceed the insured's earnings, the insurer pays only a proportionate amount. Prevents over-insurance 7 Unpaid premiums Unpaid premiums can be deducted from claim payments 8 Cancellation Insurer cancellation notice: 45 days written notice. Non-payment cancellation: 10 days notice. The insured can cancel any time after the initial term 9 Conformity with state statutes The policy is automatically amended to meet state law 10 Illegal occupation No coverage for losses while engaged in illegal activity or felonies 11 Intoxicants and narcotics No coverage if intoxicated or using narcotics without a prescription. Some policies exclude extended stays overseas Memory trick (change of occupation): MORE hazard = LESS benefit. LESS hazard = LESS premium. (Both changes lower the insurer's exposure.)"},{"id":"ch17-s5","title":"4. Other important provisions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>No loss, no gain</strong></td><td>Principle of indemnity. Insurance restores you to your pre-loss position. No profit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Free look (right to examine)</strong></td><td>Standard: <strong>10 days</strong> to review and return for a full refund. Senior policies (LTC and Medicare supplement): <strong>30 days</strong>. Starts when the policyholder RECEIVES the policy. If canceled during the free look, the insurer has no liability for claims</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Policy form filing and approval</strong></td><td>Insurance commissioner must approve health policy forms. Auto-approval if not rejected within <strong>90 days</strong>. If rejected, the insurer may request a hearing within <strong>20 days</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Insuring clause</strong></td><td>The insurer's promise to pay specified benefits for a covered loss. Defines scope, limits, and conditions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Consideration clause</strong></td><td>Owner's consideration: application and premiums. Insurer's consideration: promise to cover. Includes the start date, the initial coverage period, the minimum premium, and application statements</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Benefit payment provision</strong></td><td>Payment methods: lump-sum reimbursement, one-time indemnity, or regular income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Owner's rights provision</strong></td><td>Defines the policyholder's authority: name or change beneficiaries, choose options, receive proceeds</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accumulation benefits</strong></td><td>Reward for continuous renewal (example: a 10% increase after 5 consecutive years)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Right of assignment</strong></td><td>Lets benefits be paid directly to the health provider. The insured does not have to pay first and get reimbursed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Availability of coverage</strong></td><td>Lets insurers offer group health to small employers (2 to 50 employees)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Probationary period</strong></td><td>Begins at the START of the policy term. Applies to SICKNESS only, not accidents</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Assignment types</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Absolute</th><th>Collateral</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FULL transfer of all rights; permanent</td><td>PARTIAL transfer; used to secure a loan; temporary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Provision Key facts No loss, no gain Principle of indemnity. Insurance restores you to your pre-loss position. No profit Free look (right to examine) Standard: 10 days to review and return for a full refund. Senior policies (LTC and Medicare supplement): 30 days. Starts when the policyholder RECEIVES the policy. If canceled during the free look, the insurer has no liability for claims Policy form filing and approval Insurance commissioner must approve health policy forms. Auto-approval if not rejected within 90 days. If rejected, the insurer may request a hearing within 20 days Insuring clause The insurer's promise to pay specified benefits for a covered loss. Defines scope, limits, and conditions Consideration clause Owner's consideration: application and premiums. Insurer's consideration: promise to cover. Includes the start date, the initial coverage period, the minimum premium, and application statements Benefit payment provision Payment methods: lump-sum reimbursement, one-time indemnity, or regular income Owner's rights provision Defines the policyholder's authority: name or change beneficiaries, choose options, receive proceeds Accumulation benefits Reward for continuous renewal (example: a 10% increase after 5 consecutive years) Right of assignment Lets benefits be paid directly to the health provider. The insured does not have to pay first and get reimbursed Availability of coverage Lets insurers offer group health to small employers (2 to 50 employees) Probationary period Begins at the START of the policy term. Applies to SICKNESS only, not accidents Assignment types Absolute Collateral FULL transfer of all rights; permanent PARTIAL transfer; used to secure a loan; temporary"},{"id":"ch17-s6","title":"5. Policy renewability types","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Can the insurer cancel?</th><th>Can premiums increase?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cancellable</strong></td><td>YES, at the insurer's discretion</td><td>YES</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guaranteed renewable</strong></td><td>NO (only for nonpayment)</td><td>YES, by CLASS, not for an individual</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Noncancellable</strong></td><td>NO (only for nonpayment)</td><td>NO. Premiums are locked</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nonrenewable</strong></td><td>Expires at the end of the term</td><td>Not applicable (fixed term)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Noncancellable:</strong> Highest premiums. Common in disability income policies, not in medical expense policies.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Noncancellable purchased at age 54:</strong> Term must extend at least 5 years.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Nonrenewable:</strong> Usually short-term health insurance, a fixed term of a year or less. The policy cannot be altered until a specified age (usually 65).</li></ul>","text":"Type Can the insurer cancel? Can premiums increase? Cancellable YES, at the insurer's discretion YES Guaranteed renewable NO (only for nonpayment) YES, by CLASS, not for an individual Noncancellable NO (only for nonpayment) NO. Premiums are locked Nonrenewable Expires at the end of the term Not applicable (fixed term) Noncancellable: Highest premiums. Common in disability income policies, not in medical expense policies. Noncancellable purchased at age 54: Term must extend at least 5 years. Nonrenewable: Usually short-term health insurance, a fixed term of a year or less. The policy cannot be altered until a specified age (usually 65)."},{"id":"ch17-s7","title":"6. Preexisting conditions","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Policies often exclude losses linked to preexisting conditions (ongoing illnesses, diseases, impairments).</li><li class=\"l0\">Governed by the time-limit (incontestability) provision.</li><li class=\"l0\">If disclosed on the application, the condition is generally NOT excluded.</li><li class=\"l0\">If excluded, the condition is named in an exclusion or waiver rider.</li></ul>","text":"Policies often exclude losses linked to preexisting conditions (ongoing illnesses, diseases, impairments). Governed by the time-limit (incontestability) provision. If disclosed on the application, the condition is generally NOT excluded. If excluded, the condition is named in an exclusion or waiver rider."},{"id":"ch17-s8","title":"7. Common riders","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rider</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Waiver of premium</strong></td><td>Waives premiums during total and permanent disability. Elimination period typically 90 days or 6 months. Ends around age 60 or 65. Covers only the primary insured; no cash payments. NOT a loan. Premiums resume if disability ends. Exclusions: self-inflicted injury, military or wartime service, injuries while committing a crime</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Payor rider</strong></td><td>Waives premiums on a JUVENILE life policy if the payor dies or becomes disabled</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accelerated benefit rider</strong></td><td>Access to part of the death benefit early if diagnosed with a terminal illness (1 to 2 years to live). The amount withdrawn reduces the death benefit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accidental death benefit (multiple indemnity)</strong></td><td>Extra payment, often double or triple, if death is caused by an accident</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guaranteed insurability (future increase option)</strong></td><td>Buy more coverage on future dates WITHOUT proof of insurability. Typically every 3 years or at life events (marriage, birth). Only age matters when the option is exercised. For a child's life policy, ages 25, 28, 31, 34, 37, and 40</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cost of living</strong></td><td>Adjusts the face amount based on the CPI</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Return of premium</strong></td><td>If death occurs within the period: face amount plus all premiums paid. If the insured outlives the period: refund of all premiums</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Social Security rider</strong></td><td>Income when Social Security benefits are delayed, denied, or less than expected</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Impairment rider (exclusion rider; waiver for impairments)</strong></td><td>Excludes coverage for a specific preexisting condition. The agent MUST explain the limitation. May be removed if the condition improves. May be with or without extra premium</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Rider Key facts Waiver of premium Waives premiums during total and permanent disability. Elimination period typically 90 days or 6 months. Ends around age 60 or 65. Covers only the primary insured; no cash payments. NOT a loan. Premiums resume if disability ends. Exclusions: self-inflicted injury, military or wartime service, injuries while committing a crime Payor rider Waives premiums on a JUVENILE life policy if the payor dies or becomes disabled Accelerated benefit rider Access to part of the death benefit early if diagnosed with a terminal illness (1 to 2 years to live). The amount withdrawn reduces the death benefit Accidental death benefit (multiple indemnity) Extra payment, often double or triple, if death is caused by an accident Guaranteed insurability (future increase option) Buy more coverage on future dates WITHOUT proof of insurability. Typically every 3 years or at life events (marriage, birth). Only age matters when the option is exercised. For a child's life policy, ages 25, 28, 31, 34, 37, and 40 Cost of living Adjusts the face amount based on the CPI Return of premium If death occurs within the period: face amount plus all premiums paid. If the insured outlives the period: refund of all premiums Social Security rider Income when Social Security benefits are delayed, denied, or less than expected Impairment rider (exclusion rider; waiver for impairments) Excludes coverage for a specific preexisting condition. The agent MUST explain the limitation. May be removed if the condition improves. May be with or without extra premium"},{"id":"ch17-s9","title":"8. Nonforfeiture options (when terminating a policy with cash value)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cash surrender</strong></td><td>Cash value paid in a lump sum; coverage ends</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Delayed payment provision</strong></td><td>The insurer may defer payment up to <strong>6 months</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Extended term</strong></td><td>Cash value buys term insurance for a set period, with the same death benefit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reduced paid-up</strong></td><td>Premiums stop; face amount is reduced</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Option What happens Cash surrender Cash value paid in a lump sum; coverage ends Delayed payment provision The insurer may defer payment up to 6 months Extended term Cash value buys term insurance for a set period, with the same death benefit Reduced paid-up Premiums stop; face amount is reduced"},{"id":"ch17-s10","title":"9. Dividend options (participating policies)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Dividends are a refund of overpaid premium. <strong>NOT taxable.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\">Paid only if the insurer has a <strong>divisible surplus</strong> (it operated more efficiently than expected).</li><li class=\"l0\">Typically paid annually while the policy is in force.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cash</strong></td><td>Cash payment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reduced premiums</strong></td><td>Applied to the next premium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accumulate interest</strong></td><td>Left with the insurer; only the interest is taxable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Paid-up additions</strong></td><td>Buys additional whole life coverage</td></tr><tr><td><strong>One-year term</strong></td><td>Buys one-year term insurance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Dividends are a refund of overpaid premium. NOT taxable. Paid only if the insurer has a divisible surplus (it operated more efficiently than expected). Typically paid annually while the policy is in force. Option What happens Cash Cash payment Reduced premiums Applied to the next premium Accumulate interest Left with the insurer; only the interest is taxable Paid-up additions Buys additional whole life coverage One-year term Buys one-year term insurance"},{"id":"ch17-s11","title":"10. Policy loans and cash value","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Found in whole life and some LTC policies.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cash value typically begins after 3 years</strong> in most states.</li><li class=\"l0\">Loans cannot exceed the guaranteed cash value (or the policy becomes invalid).</li><li class=\"l0\">Loans are NOT taxable.</li><li class=\"l0\">Outstanding loans plus interest are deducted from the death benefit.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Automatic premium loan:</strong> The insurer uses cash value to pay an overdue premium by the end of the grace period. Continues until the cash value is used up.</li></ul>","text":"Found in whole life and some LTC policies. Cash value typically begins after 3 years in most states. Loans cannot exceed the guaranteed cash value (or the policy becomes invalid). Loans are NOT taxable. Outstanding loans plus interest are deducted from the death benefit. Automatic premium loan: The insurer uses cash value to pay an overdue premium by the end of the grace period. Continues until the cash value is used up."},{"id":"ch17-s12","title":"11. Common exclusions","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Exclusion</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Suicide</strong></td><td>Within 2 years of issue, the insurer refunds premiums and pays no death benefit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aviation</strong></td><td>Pilots and crew of aircraft (military capacity)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>War and military service</strong></td><td>Deaths or injuries during service or acts of war. A military suspension clause may halt disability coverage</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Felony or illegal occupation</strong></td><td>Injuries while committing a crime</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alcohol and narcotics</strong></td><td>Injuries while intoxicated or using drugs without a prescription</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hazardous occupation or hobby</strong></td><td>High-risk activities</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Additional exclusions</h4><p>Self-inflicted injuries; foreign travel (some policies); noncommercial air travel; hernia (treated as accidental injury); participation in riots; workers' compensation-covered injuries; Veterans Administration-covered injuries; cosmetic or experimental procedures; vision correction and eye exams; services not medically necessary.</p>","text":"Exclusion Key facts Suicide Within 2 years of issue, the insurer refunds premiums and pays no death benefit Aviation Pilots and crew of aircraft (military capacity) War and military service Deaths or injuries during service or acts of war. A military suspension clause may halt disability coverage Felony or illegal occupation Injuries while committing a crime Alcohol and narcotics Injuries while intoxicated or using drugs without a prescription Hazardous occupation or hobby High-risk activities Additional exclusions Self-inflicted injuries; foreign travel (some policies); noncommercial air travel; hernia (treated as accidental injury); participation in riots; workers' compensation-covered injuries; Veterans Administration-covered injuries; cosmetic or experimental procedures; vision correction and eye exams; services not medically necessary."},{"id":"ch17-s13","title":"12. Beneficiary types","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Specific</strong></td><td>A named individual (for example, &quot;Jane Doe, spouse&quot;)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>A group, not named (for example, &quot;children of the insured&quot;)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Estate</strong></td><td>Used if no beneficiary is named or all predecease the insured</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Order of succession:</strong> Primary (first), secondary or contingent (if the primary dies first), tertiary (third). If none survive, the estate receives the proceeds.</li></ul>","text":"Type Meaning Specific A named individual (for example, \"Jane Doe, spouse\") Class A group, not named (for example, \"children of the insured\") Estate Used if no beneficiary is named or all predecease the insured Order of succession: Primary (first), secondary or contingent (if the primary dies first), tertiary (third). If none survive, the estate receives the proceeds."},{"id":"ch17-s14","title":"13. Dependent coverage","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Plans must cover dependent children until a specified age.</li><li class=\"l0\">Coverage can extend for children unable to support themselves because of a mental or physical disability.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>ACA dependent rule:</strong> Coverage until age <strong>26</strong>. Includes adopted children, stepchildren, and foster children. Coverage continues until the dependent marries or reaches the limiting age.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> The ACA dependent rule is a dependent coverage requirement (to age 26), not a conversion privilege. Remember it as &quot;dependent coverage to age 26.&quot;</p></div>","text":"Plans must cover dependent children until a specified age. Coverage can extend for children unable to support themselves because of a mental or physical disability. ACA dependent rule: Coverage until age 26. Includes adopted children, stepchildren, and foster children. Coverage continues until the dependent marries or reaches the limiting age. Note: The ACA dependent rule is a dependent coverage requirement (to age 26), not a conversion privilege. Remember it as \"dependent coverage to age 26.\""},{"id":"ch17-s15","title":"14. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mandatory vs. optional provisions</td><td>12 required in every policy vs. 11 the insurer may include</td></tr><tr><td>Grace periods</td><td>7 days (weekly), 10 days (monthly), 31 days (quarterly or longer) under the NAIC model; Louisiana 30 days</td></tr><tr><td>Free look: standard vs. senior</td><td>10 days vs. 30 days (LTC and Medicare supplement)</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute vs. collateral assignment</td><td>Full and permanent vs. partial and temporary (loan security)</td></tr><tr><td>Guaranteed renewable vs. noncancellable</td><td>Premium can rise by class vs. premium locked</td></tr><tr><td>Accidents vs. sickness (reinstatement)</td><td>Accidents covered immediately vs. sickness after 10 days</td></tr><tr><td>Notice of claim vs. proof of loss</td><td>Notice within 20 days vs. proof within 90 days</td></tr><tr><td>Revocable vs. irrevocable beneficiary</td><td>Change anytime vs. needs written consent</td></tr><tr><td>Cancellation by insurer vs. nonpayment</td><td>45 days' notice vs. 10 days' notice</td></tr><tr><td>Impairment rider vs. waiver of premium</td><td>Excludes a preexisting condition vs. waives premiums during total disability</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Mandatory vs. optional provisions 12 required in every policy vs. 11 the insurer may include Grace periods 7 days (weekly), 10 days (monthly), 31 days (quarterly or longer) under the NAIC model; Louisiana 30 days Free look: standard vs. senior 10 days vs. 30 days (LTC and Medicare supplement) Absolute vs. collateral assignment Full and permanent vs. partial and temporary (loan security) Guaranteed renewable vs. noncancellable Premium can rise by class vs. premium locked Accidents vs. sickness (reinstatement) Accidents covered immediately vs. sickness after 10 days Notice of claim vs. proof of loss Notice within 20 days vs. proof within 90 days Revocable vs. irrevocable beneficiary Change anytime vs. needs written consent Cancellation by insurer vs. nonpayment 45 days' notice vs. 10 days' notice Impairment rider vs. waiver of premium Excludes a preexisting condition vs. waives premiums during total disability"},{"id":"ch17-s16","title":"15. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the grace period for a monthly premium under the NAIC model law, and what does Louisiana use?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> How many days after a loss must notice of claim be given?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Within what time must the insurer provide claim forms?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> How long is the proof of loss deadline, and what extends it?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> How long must a claimant wait before suing after proof of loss?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What is the standard free-look period, and what is the senior free-look period?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> How long does a sickness coverage delay last after reinstatement?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Which optional provision reduces the benefit when the insured moves to a more hazardous job?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Can an agent change the policy?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> What is the ACA dependent coverage age?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Ben's quarterly premium is due March 1. He does not pay. When does the grace period end, and what happens if he dies on March 20?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> Rita has a waiver of premium rider that ends at age 60. She becomes totally and permanently disabled at 61. Does the waiver apply?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> The insurer never responds to Carl's reinstatement application. How does reinstatement work, and when does his sickness coverage start?</li></ul>","text":"What is the grace period for a monthly premium under the NAIC model law, and what does Louisiana use? How many days after a loss must notice of claim be given? Within what time must the insurer provide claim forms? How long is the proof of loss deadline, and what extends it? How long must a claimant wait before suing after proof of loss? What is the standard free-look period, and what is the senior free-look period? How long does a sickness coverage delay last after reinstatement? Which optional provision reduces the benefit when the insured moves to a more hazardous job? Can an agent change the policy? What is the ACA dependent coverage age? Scenario questions Scenario A: Ben's quarterly premium is due March 1. He does not pay. When does the grace period end, and what happens if he dies on March 20? Scenario B: Rita has a waiver of premium rider that ends at age 60. She becomes totally and permanently disabled at 61. Does the waiver apply? Scenario C: The insurer never responds to Carl's reinstatement application. How does reinstatement work, and when does his sickness coverage start?"},{"id":"ch17-s17","title":"16. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> 10 days under the NAIC model law. Louisiana uses 30 days (life: 30 days or one month; health: 30 days).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> 20 days (or as soon as reasonably possible).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Within 15 days of receiving notice.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> 90 days from the date of loss. Extended to 1 year if the claimant is legally incapacitated.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> At least 60 days.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> 10 days standard. 30 days for senior policies (LTC and Medicare supplement).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> 10 days (accidents are covered immediately).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> The change of occupation provision.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> No. Only an authorized executive officer of the insurer can change the policy.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Age 26.</p><p>Scenario A: The quarterly grace period is about a month (31 days under the NAIC model law; Louisiana uses 30 days), so it ends around April 1. Death on March 20 is within the grace period. The death benefit is paid minus the unpaid premium.</p><p>Scenario B: No. The rider ended at 60, and the disability began at 61. If the rider had ended at 65, the waiver would apply.</p><p>Scenario C: If the insurer doesn't respond within 45 days, the policy is automatically reinstated. Sickness coverage starts 10 days after reinstatement.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 17:</strong> Make a single timeline card with every number: 7/10/31 (grace), 20 (notice), 15 (forms), 90 (proof), 60 (wait), 3 (suit), 45 (reinstatement and cancel), 10 (sick delay), 10/30 (free look), 26 (dependents). Read it aloud three times a day for a week.</p></div>","text":"10 days under the NAIC model law. Louisiana uses 30 days (life: 30 days or one month; health: 30 days). 20 days (or as soon as reasonably possible). Within 15 days of receiving notice. 90 days from the date of loss. Extended to 1 year if the claimant is legally incapacitated. At least 60 days. 10 days standard. 30 days for senior policies (LTC and Medicare supplement). 10 days (accidents are covered immediately). The change of occupation provision. No. Only an authorized executive officer of the insurer can change the policy. Age 26. Scenario A: The quarterly grace period is about a month (31 days under the NAIC model law; Louisiana uses 30 days), so it ends around April 1. Death on March 20 is within the grace period. The death benefit is paid minus the unpaid premium. Scenario B: No. The rider ended at 60, and the disability began at 61. If the rider had ended at 65, the waiver would apply. Scenario C: If the insurer doesn't respond within 45 days, the policy is automatically reinstated. Sickness coverage starts 10 days after reinstatement. Memory tip for Chapter 17: Make a single timeline card with every number: 7/10/31 (grace), 20 (notice), 15 (forms), 90 (proof), 60 (wait), 3 (suit), 45 (reinstatement and cancel), 10 (sick delay), 10/30 (free look), 26 (dependents). Read it aloud three times a day for a week."}]},{"id":"ch18","short":"Ch 18","title":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","label":"Chapter 18","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> This chapter is the health version of Chapter 4. Know the <strong>application</strong> (3 parts), <strong>representations vs. warranties</strong>, the <strong>MIB</strong>, the <strong>risk classes</strong>, the <strong>premium receipt types</strong>, <strong>delivery</strong> (constructive delivery, free look), and <strong>premium and rating</strong> basics. Expect questions on receipts and on representation vs. warranty.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch18-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Underwriting protects against adverse selection.</strong> The insurer decides who is insurable and at what price.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Application statements are representations</strong> in most states. A warranty must be completely true.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Receipt type decides when coverage starts:</strong> Conditional (if insurable), Binding (until denied), Inspection (look first, obsolete-ish).</li></ul>","text":"Underwriting protects against adverse selection. The insurer decides who is insurable and at what price. Application statements are representations in most states. A warranty must be completely true. Receipt type decides when coverage starts: Conditional (if insurable), Binding (until denied), Inspection (look first, obsolete-ish)."},{"id":"ch18-s2","title":"1. Underwriting overview","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evaluate and select risks. The primary goal is protection against <strong>adverse selection</strong> (more high-risk people seeking coverage).</li><li class=\"l0\">Each insurer sets its own guidelines for insurable vs. uninsurable risk.</li><li class=\"l0\">Larger policies get more thorough research.</li></ul><h4>Seven sources of underwriting information</h4><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Application form</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Special questionnaires</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> Medical reports</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Inspection reports</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Credit reports</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> Medical Information Bureau (MIB) reports</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> Attending physician statement (APS)</p>","text":"Purpose: Evaluate and select risks. The primary goal is protection against adverse selection (more high-risk people seeking coverage). Each insurer sets its own guidelines for insurable vs. uninsurable risk. Larger policies get more thorough research. Seven sources of underwriting information Application form Special questionnaires Medical reports Inspection reports Credit reports Medical Information Bureau (MIB) reports Attending physician statement (APS)"},{"id":"ch18-s3","title":"2. The application","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The PRIMARY source. It is the first document reviewed.</li><li class=\"l0\">Becomes part of the legal contract when attached to the policy.</li><li class=\"l0\">The agent must make sure the information is accurate and complete. Incomplete applications go back to the agent.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Applicant:</strong> The person completing the application (may differ from the proposed insured).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Proposed insured:</strong> The person whose life or health is being considered.</li></ul><h4>The three parts</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Part I: General</strong></td><td>Name, age, date of birth, address, sex, marital status, occupation, income; amount and type of insurance; beneficiary; other pending applications and existing policies; hazardous activities, aviation, military, foreign travel; smoking status</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part II: Medical</strong></td><td>Health history questions. MUST be completed for EVERY application. May require an exam, blood or urine samples, depending on the face amount. Women may need specific tests (for example, a Pap test)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Part III: Agent's report</strong></td><td>The agent's observations; financial status and character; the reason for the sale; how long the agent has known the applicant; whether the policy replaces existing coverage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"The PRIMARY source. It is the first document reviewed. Becomes part of the legal contract when attached to the policy. The agent must make sure the information is accurate and complete. Incomplete applications go back to the agent. Applicant: The person completing the application (may differ from the proposed insured). Proposed insured: The person whose life or health is being considered. The three parts Part Contents Part I: General Name, age, date of birth, address, sex, marital status, occupation, income; amount and type of insurance; beneficiary; other pending applications and existing policies; hazardous activities, aviation, military, foreign travel; smoking status Part II: Medical Health history questions. MUST be completed for EVERY application. May require an exam, blood or urine samples, depending on the face amount. Women may need specific tests (for example, a Pap test) Part III: Agent's report The agent's observations; financial status and character; the reason for the sale; how long the agent has known the applicant; whether the policy replaces existing coverage"},{"id":"ch18-s4","title":"3. Representations vs. warranties (CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>REPRESENTATIONS</th><th>WARRANTIES</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>True to the BEST OF KNOWLEDGE</td><td>Must be COMPLETELY TRUE</td></tr><tr><td>Not expected to be precise in every detail</td><td>Breach can void the policy</td></tr><tr><td>Must be accurate only if MATERIAL to the risk</td><td>Even if the error is unintentional</td></tr><tr><td>Most states treat application statements this way</td><td>Even if not material to the loss</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>REPRESENTATION = &quot;to the best of my knowledge.&quot; WARRANTY = &quot;I guarantee every word.&quot;</p></div><h4>Misrepresentation</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Misrepresentation:</strong> A misleading or untrue statement on the application.</li><li class=\"l0\">It affects coverage only if it is MATERIAL to the risk. Material means it would have caused denial if known at the time.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Fraud:</strong> A material misrepresentation made DELIBERATELY.</li></ul><h4>Signatures and application rules</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Required signatures: the proposed insured (if an adult); the policyholder (if different); the agent soliciting the application.</li><li class=\"l0\">The application must show the insurer's name, the agent's name, and the agent's license ID number.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>No changes without the applicant's WRITTEN consent.</strong> The agent may correct errors, but the applicant must INITIAL the corrections.</li></ul>","text":"REPRESENTATIONS WARRANTIES True to the BEST OF KNOWLEDGE Must be COMPLETELY TRUE Not expected to be precise in every detail Breach can void the policy Must be accurate only if MATERIAL to the risk Even if the error is unintentional Most states treat application statements this way Even if not material to the loss Memory trick: REPRESENTATION = \"to the best of my knowledge.\" WARRANTY = \"I guarantee every word.\" Misrepresentation Misrepresentation: A misleading or untrue statement on the application. It affects coverage only if it is MATERIAL to the risk. Material means it would have caused denial if known at the time. Fraud: A material misrepresentation made DELIBERATELY. Signatures and application rules Required signatures: the proposed insured (if an adult); the policyholder (if different); the agent soliciting the application. The application must show the insurer's name, the agent's name, and the agent's license ID number. No changes without the applicant's WRITTEN consent. The agent may correct errors, but the applicant must INITIAL the corrections."},{"id":"ch18-s5","title":"4. Medical Information Bureau (MIB)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Provides medical history of applicants and shows life policies applied for with other insurers.</li><li class=\"l0\">Reveals lifestyle habits: drug use, alcohol, smoking, overeating.</li><li class=\"l0\">Helps uncover omissions and fraud.</li><li class=\"l0\">Uses <strong>code numbers</strong> for medical conditions.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Access:</strong> Member companies only. Can be used ONLY for underwriting and claims processing.</li><li class=\"l0\">Information may be shared with the proposed insured's doctor.</li><li class=\"l0\">Helps keep costs down for all policyholders by preventing fraud.</li></ul>","text":"Provides medical history of applicants and shows life policies applied for with other insurers. Reveals lifestyle habits: drug use, alcohol, smoking, overeating. Helps uncover omissions and fraud. Uses code numbers for medical conditions. Access: Member companies only. Can be used ONLY for underwriting and claims processing. Information may be shared with the proposed insured's doctor. Helps keep costs down for all policyholders by preventing fraud."},{"id":"ch18-s6","title":"5. Other underwriting sources","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Key facts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Attending physician statement (APS)</strong></td><td>Requested when more detail is needed about a medical condition. Requires the applicant's SIGNED authorization</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Medical report</strong></td><td>Completed by a doctor, paramedic, or registered nurse. Reviewed by the medical director or designee</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Inspection report</strong></td><td>For LARGE policies. Covers lifestyle, hazards, character, reputation, and finances. May interview neighbors, employees, associates, and the applicant. The applicant is entitled to a copy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Credit report</strong></td><td>Evaluates financial risk. Poor credit suggests the policy may lapse. Sources: Equifax, Experian, TransUnion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Consumer (investigative) report</strong></td><td>Detailed background investigation; interviews coworkers, neighbors, friends. Assesses personality, reputation, and way of life</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Special questionnaires</strong></td><td>In-depth questions on higher-risk activities (aviation, hazardous hobbies, foreign residence, finances, military, occupation). The most common is the <strong>aviation questionnaire</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Source Key facts Attending physician statement (APS) Requested when more detail is needed about a medical condition. Requires the applicant's SIGNED authorization Medical report Completed by a doctor, paramedic, or registered nurse. Reviewed by the medical director or designee Inspection report For LARGE policies. Covers lifestyle, hazards, character, reputation, and finances. May interview neighbors, employees, associates, and the applicant. The applicant is entitled to a copy Credit report Evaluates financial risk. Poor credit suggests the policy may lapse. Sources: Equifax, Experian, TransUnion Consumer (investigative) report Detailed background investigation; interviews coworkers, neighbors, friends. Assesses personality, reputation, and way of life Special questionnaires In-depth questions on higher-risk activities (aviation, hazardous hobbies, foreign residence, finances, military, occupation). The most common is the aviation questionnaire"},{"id":"ch18-s7","title":"6. Laws and requirements","html":"<h4>FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act), 1970</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Lets insurers obtain consumer reports.</li><li class=\"l0\">The applicant must be INFORMED of the purpose of the inquiry.</li><li class=\"l0\">If coverage is denied because of a report, the insurer must give the name and address of the reporting agency.</li><li class=\"l0\">The applicant can CONTEST adverse information.</li><li class=\"l0\">Promotes fairness, accuracy, and confidentiality.</li></ul><h4>USA PATRIOT Act (2001)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Requires insurers to combat money laundering and help prevent terrorism.</li><li class=\"l0\">Cash-surrender life insurance can be used for money laundering.</li><li class=\"l0\">Requires a <strong>Know Your Customer (KYC)</strong> process.</li></ul><h4>Privacy and HIPAA</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Insurers must comply with state and federal privacy laws.</li><li class=\"l0\">Cannot make decisions based ONLY on a prior adverse MIB result.</li><li class=\"l0\">The agent must provide privacy notices when the application is submitted.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>HIV consent:</strong> Required where a blood test is part of underwriting.</li></ul>","text":"FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act), 1970 Lets insurers obtain consumer reports. The applicant must be INFORMED of the purpose of the inquiry. If coverage is denied because of a report, the insurer must give the name and address of the reporting agency. The applicant can CONTEST adverse information. Promotes fairness, accuracy, and confidentiality. USA PATRIOT Act (2001) Requires insurers to combat money laundering and help prevent terrorism. Cash-surrender life insurance can be used for money laundering. Requires a Know Your Customer (KYC) process. Privacy and HIPAA Insurers must comply with state and federal privacy laws. Cannot make decisions based ONLY on a prior adverse MIB result. The agent must provide privacy notices when the application is submitted. HIV consent: Required where a blood test is part of underwriting."},{"id":"ch18-s8","title":"7. Risk classifications","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Classification</th><th>Description</th><th>Premium</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Preferred</strong></td><td>Better than average: superior health, ideal weight, non-smoker, no alcohol</td><td>LOWEST</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Standard</strong></td><td>Meets guidelines without restriction; the basis for premium rates</td><td>STANDARD</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Substandard (impaired)</strong></td><td>Below average: poor health, hazardous work, risky hobbies</td><td>RATED UP, or exclusions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Special class</strong></td><td>Does not qualify for standard. May get coverage with an exclusion rider for a preexisting condition</td><td>Higher premium or modified policy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Uninsurable (declined)</strong></td><td>Too risky to cover profitably</td><td>Application DECLINED</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Factors affecting risk class</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Age:</strong> Older = higher risk = higher premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Sex:</strong> Gender alone cannot be used to reject an applicant.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medical history:</strong> Likelihood of a condition returning.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Family history:</strong> May show predisposition.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Occupation:</strong> Hazardous jobs = higher risk.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Avocation (hobbies):</strong> Rock climbing, scuba diving = higher risk.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Moral hazard:</strong> Behaviors suggesting higher risk (heavy drinking, drug use, unethical conduct).</li></ul><h4>Occupation rules</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Two jobs</td><td>Evaluate based on the MORE hazardous job</td></tr><tr><td>Change to a LESS hazardous job</td><td>Refund the unearned premium</td></tr><tr><td>Change to a MORE hazardous job</td><td>Benefits reduced; premium stays the same</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Insurable interest (health)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Financial connection in which the applicant would suffer a loss.</li><li class=\"l0\">In health insurance: a financial impact from medical expenses or disability.</li><li class=\"l0\">Required for a health policy to be issued.</li></ul><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> Insurable interest requires a FINANCIAL interest. Sentimental attachment alone does NOT establish it.</p></div>","text":"Classification Description Premium Preferred Better than average: superior health, ideal weight, non-smoker, no alcohol LOWEST Standard Meets guidelines without restriction; the basis for premium rates STANDARD Substandard (impaired) Below average: poor health, hazardous work, risky hobbies RATED UP, or exclusions Special class Does not qualify for standard. May get coverage with an exclusion rider for a preexisting condition Higher premium or modified policy Uninsurable (declined) Too risky to cover profitably Application DECLINED Factors affecting risk class Age: Older = higher risk = higher premium. Sex: Gender alone cannot be used to reject an applicant. Medical history: Likelihood of a condition returning. Family history: May show predisposition. Occupation: Hazardous jobs = higher risk. Avocation (hobbies): Rock climbing, scuba diving = higher risk. Moral hazard: Behaviors suggesting higher risk (heavy drinking, drug use, unethical conduct). Occupation rules Change Result Two jobs Evaluate based on the MORE hazardous job Change to a LESS hazardous job Refund the unearned premium Change to a MORE hazardous job Benefits reduced; premium stays the same Insurable interest (health) Financial connection in which the applicant would suffer a loss. In health insurance: a financial impact from medical expenses or disability. Required for a health policy to be issued. Note: Insurable interest requires a FINANCIAL interest. Sentimental attachment alone does NOT establish it."},{"id":"ch18-s9","title":"8. The agent's role (field underwriter)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The agent acts in a <strong>fiduciary</strong> capacity.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Field underwriting:</strong> The producer determines which risks are suitable, provides disclosures, and submits the application.</li></ul><h4>Agent responsibilities</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Solicit appropriately.</li><li class=\"l0\">Complete the application thoroughly and accurately.</li><li class=\"l0\">Obtain the necessary signatures.</li><li class=\"l0\">Collect the first premium and issue a receipt.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Deliver the policy in PERSON</strong> and explain provisions, exclusions, and riders.</li><li class=\"l0\">Provide the Buyer's Guide and the Policy Summary.</li></ul><h4>Errors and omissions (E&amp;O)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">An unintentional error or honest mistake (a tort).</li><li class=\"l0\">E&amp;O coverage is strongly recommended for agents.</li></ul><h4>Buyer's guide vs. policy summary</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>BUYER'S GUIDE</th><th>POLICY SUMMARY</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A GENERAL resource on insurance concepts</td><td>SPECIFIC to the product being sold</td></tr><tr><td>Does NOT focus on a specific product</td><td>Includes the agent's name, the company, the policy, coverage amounts, premiums, dividends, and cost indexes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Outline of coverage:</strong> A plain-language description of basic benefits, terms, and conditions. Avoids jargon.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Disclosure form:</strong> A comparison form required when REPLACING an existing policy. Required by state regulators.</li></ul>","text":"The agent acts in a fiduciary capacity. Field underwriting: The producer determines which risks are suitable, provides disclosures, and submits the application. Agent responsibilities Solicit appropriately. Complete the application thoroughly and accurately. Obtain the necessary signatures. Collect the first premium and issue a receipt. Deliver the policy in PERSON and explain provisions, exclusions, and riders. Provide the Buyer's Guide and the Policy Summary. Errors and omissions (E&O) An unintentional error or honest mistake (a tort). E&O coverage is strongly recommended for agents. Buyer's guide vs. policy summary BUYER'S GUIDE POLICY SUMMARY A GENERAL resource on insurance concepts SPECIFIC to the product being sold Does NOT focus on a specific product Includes the agent's name, the company, the policy, coverage amounts, premiums, dividends, and cost indexes Outline of coverage: A plain-language description of basic benefits, terms, and conditions. Avoids jargon. Disclosure form: A comparison form required when REPLACING an existing policy. Required by state regulators."},{"id":"ch18-s10","title":"9. Premium receipts (CRITICAL)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Receipt</th><th>Key features</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Conditional receipt</strong> (most common)</td><td>Coverage starts IF the applicant is INSURABLE. Effective on the application date OR the medical exam date, whichever is later. If uninsurable: no coverage, premium refunded</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Binding receipt</strong> (unconditional)</td><td>Coverage GUARANTEED until the application is denied, even if the applicant is later found uninsurable. HIGH RISK for the insurer. Usually limited to the company's most seasoned agents. Also called a temporary insurance agreement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Inspection receipt</strong></td><td>Temporary delivery of the policy WITHOUT payment. Lets the applicant review before buying. Less used because of the free-look period</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Without a premium receipt</h4><p>Coverage does NOT begin until: the policy is issued, delivered, accepted, and the initial premium is paid.</p><h4>Trial application</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Submitted WITHOUT the first premium.</li><li class=\"l0\">Not a formal offer. The applicant is inviting an offer from the insurer.</li></ul><h4>Other terms</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Evidence of insurability:</strong> Proof of current health and medical history. May include a questionnaire and an exam.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Payor:</strong> The person responsible for paying premiums (usually the policyholder).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Policy fee:</strong> A nominal fee some insurers charge in the first or later years.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Policy term:</strong> The period the policy is in effect while premiums are paid. Can also mean one renewal term.</li></ul>","text":"Receipt Key features Conditional receipt (most common) Coverage starts IF the applicant is INSURABLE. Effective on the application date OR the medical exam date, whichever is later. If uninsurable: no coverage, premium refunded Binding receipt (unconditional) Coverage GUARANTEED until the application is denied, even if the applicant is later found uninsurable. HIGH RISK for the insurer. Usually limited to the company's most seasoned agents. Also called a temporary insurance agreement Inspection receipt Temporary delivery of the policy WITHOUT payment. Lets the applicant review before buying. Less used because of the free-look period Without a premium receipt Coverage does NOT begin until: the policy is issued, delivered, accepted, and the initial premium is paid. Trial application Submitted WITHOUT the first premium. Not a formal offer. The applicant is inviting an offer from the insurer. Other terms Evidence of insurability: Proof of current health and medical history. May include a questionnaire and an exam. Payor: The person responsible for paying premiums (usually the policyholder). Policy fee: A nominal fee some insurers charge in the first or later years. Policy term: The period the policy is in effect while premiums are paid. Can also mean one renewal term."},{"id":"ch18-s11","title":"10. Policy delivery","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Constructive delivery:</strong> Delivery occurs WITHOUT a physical transfer to the policyowner. The insurer gives up ALL control to someone acting for the owner (for example, the agent). It does NOT occur if the agent was told to hold the policy until good health is confirmed.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Delivery receipt:</strong> A signed document proving delivery. It marks the START of the free-look period.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Statement of good health:</strong> Required if the premium was NOT paid with the application. The agent collects the premium and a signed statement at delivery. The insurer verifies the owner's health has not changed since the application.</li></ul><h4>Free look</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Standard: at least <strong>10 days</strong> to review and return the policy for a full refund.</li><li class=\"l0\">Direct response and mail-order insurers: at least <strong>30 days.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Constructive delivery: Delivery occurs WITHOUT a physical transfer to the policyowner. The insurer gives up ALL control to someone acting for the owner (for example, the agent). It does NOT occur if the agent was told to hold the policy until good health is confirmed. Delivery receipt: A signed document proving delivery. It marks the START of the free-look period. Statement of good health: Required if the premium was NOT paid with the application. The agent collects the premium and a signed statement at delivery. The insurer verifies the owner's health has not changed since the application. Free look Standard: at least 10 days to review and return the policy for a full refund. Direct response and mail-order insurers: at least 30 days."},{"id":"ch18-s12","title":"11. Key dates","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Effective date</strong></td><td>When coverage begins; sets the premium due dates</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Issue date</strong></td><td>When the insurer completes underwriting and issues the contract</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Age change</strong></td><td>Happens halfway between birthdays. The age moves to the next higher age</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Backdating</strong></td><td>Setting an effective date BEFORE the application date to reduce the stated age and the premium. Limit: <strong>6 months.</strong> NOT allowed for variable contracts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Term Meaning Effective date When coverage begins; sets the premium due dates Issue date When the insurer completes underwriting and issues the contract Age change Happens halfway between birthdays. The age moves to the next higher age Backdating Setting an effective date BEFORE the application date to reduce the stated age and the premium. Limit: 6 months. NOT allowed for variable contracts"},{"id":"ch18-s13","title":"12. Premium calculations","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Health premium formula:</strong> Morbidity (risk of disease or disability, like mortality for death) + expenses (commissions, admin, reserves, profit) − interest (investment earnings reduce the premium).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Premium mode:</strong> Options are annual, semiannual, quarterly, monthly, and weekly. Rates are calculated assuming ANNUAL payment. More frequent payments = HIGHER total cost.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Collection methods:</strong> Electronic funds transfer (EFT) is the most common. Also ACH (one-time or recurring) and credit or debit cards. Many states prohibit extra fees for EFT.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Non-medical application:</strong> Processed WITHOUT a medical exam. Usually for healthy applicants seeking smaller amounts.</li></ul><h4>Earned vs. unearned premium</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>EARNED premium</th><th>UNEARNED premium</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The portion the insurer has earned</td><td>The portion NOT yet earned</td></tr><tr><td>Matches the elapsed term (6 months of a 12-month policy = 50% earned)</td><td>Matches the remaining term</td></tr><tr><td>Income for the insurer</td><td>A LIABILITY on the insurer's balance sheet. Must be REFUNDED if the policy is canceled</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Rating methods</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Claims experience:</strong> The insured's claims history. Higher claims = higher premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Experience tables:</strong> Claims tables from past experience, used to estimate future costs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Experience rating:</strong> Estimates a group's likely medical costs from its own history.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Community rating:</strong> Everyone in a geographic area pays the same price. NO medical underwriting.</li></ul>","text":"Health premium formula: Morbidity (risk of disease or disability, like mortality for death) + expenses (commissions, admin, reserves, profit) − interest (investment earnings reduce the premium). Premium mode: Options are annual, semiannual, quarterly, monthly, and weekly. Rates are calculated assuming ANNUAL payment. More frequent payments = HIGHER total cost. Collection methods: Electronic funds transfer (EFT) is the most common. Also ACH (one-time or recurring) and credit or debit cards. Many states prohibit extra fees for EFT. Non-medical application: Processed WITHOUT a medical exam. Usually for healthy applicants seeking smaller amounts. Earned vs. unearned premium EARNED premium UNEARNED premium The portion the insurer has earned The portion NOT yet earned Matches the elapsed term (6 months of a 12-month policy = 50% earned) Matches the remaining term Income for the insurer A LIABILITY on the insurer's balance sheet. Must be REFUNDED if the policy is canceled Rating methods Claims experience: The insured's claims history. Higher claims = higher premium. Experience tables: Claims tables from past experience, used to estimate future costs. Experience rating: Estimates a group's likely medical costs from its own history. Community rating: Everyone in a geographic area pays the same price. NO medical underwriting."},{"id":"ch18-s14","title":"13. Policy replacement","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Replacing an existing policy with a new one is LEGAL.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must provide a &quot;Notice Regarding Replacement&quot; to the consumer.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must inform the insurer when a replacement occurs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>The agent must explain:</strong> new underwriting may be stricter; benefits may be restricted for preexisting conditions; new limitations and exclusions may apply.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Failure to explain = E&amp;O liability.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Replacing an existing policy with a new one is LEGAL. Must provide a \"Notice Regarding Replacement\" to the consumer. Must inform the insurer when a replacement occurs. The agent must explain: new underwriting may be stricter; benefits may be restricted for preexisting conditions; new limitations and exclusions may apply. Failure to explain = E&O liability."},{"id":"ch18-s15","title":"14. Counter-offers and rated policies","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Counter-offer:</strong> The insurer declines the standard rate and offers a substandard policy at a HIGHER premium.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Rated policy (rating up):</strong> Issued with an extra charge for above-average risk, often due to poor health or a dangerous job.</li></ul>","text":"Counter-offer: The insurer declines the standard rate and offers a substandard policy at a HIGHER premium. Rated policy (rating up): Issued with an extra charge for above-average risk, often due to poor health or a dangerous job."},{"id":"ch18-s16","title":"15. Tax treatment (health)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Employer-paid premiums</strong></td><td>NOT in the employee's taxable income. Employer may deduct</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Employee contributions</strong></td><td>Deducted pre-tax; NOT deductible again</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Individual health policy</strong></td><td>Generally NOT deductible unless medical expenses exceed a percentage of adjusted gross income set by the IRS</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Personal disability premiums</strong></td><td>NOT deductible</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Health insurance benefits</strong></td><td>Generally NOT taxable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Personal disability benefits</strong></td><td>TAX-FREE (you paid the premiums)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Employer-paid disability benefits</strong></td><td>TAXABLE to the employee</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Shared premium</strong></td><td>Tax-free in proportion to the employee's contribution</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Item Treatment Employer-paid premiums NOT in the employee's taxable income. Employer may deduct Employee contributions Deducted pre-tax; NOT deductible again Individual health policy Generally NOT deductible unless medical expenses exceed a percentage of adjusted gross income set by the IRS Personal disability premiums NOT deductible Health insurance benefits Generally NOT taxable Personal disability benefits TAX-FREE (you paid the premiums) Employer-paid disability benefits TAXABLE to the employee Shared premium Tax-free in proportion to the employee's contribution"},{"id":"ch18-s17","title":"16. Commonly confused pairs","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Key difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Representation vs. warranty</td><td>Best of knowledge, material only vs. completely true, breach voids</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional vs. binding receipt</td><td>Covered IF insurable vs. covered until denied</td></tr><tr><td>Inspection vs. conditional receipt</td><td>Policy delivered without payment vs. covered if insurable</td></tr><tr><td>Buyer's guide vs. policy summary</td><td>General vs. product-specific</td></tr><tr><td>Earned vs. unearned premium</td><td>Insurer's income vs. liability refunded on cancellation</td></tr><tr><td>Experience rating vs. community rating</td><td>Group's own history vs. same price for an area</td></tr><tr><td>Effective date vs. issue date</td><td>When coverage starts vs. when the contract is issued</td></tr><tr><td>Standard vs. substandard</td><td>Standard premium vs. rated up or excluded</td></tr><tr><td>Trial application vs. application with premium</td><td>No offer; no coverage vs. receipt may start coverage (depends on receipt type)</td></tr><tr><td>Misrepresentation vs. fraud</td><td>Untrue statement vs. deliberate untrue statement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Pair Key difference Representation vs. warranty Best of knowledge, material only vs. completely true, breach voids Conditional vs. binding receipt Covered IF insurable vs. covered until denied Inspection vs. conditional receipt Policy delivered without payment vs. covered if insurable Buyer's guide vs. policy summary General vs. product-specific Earned vs. unearned premium Insurer's income vs. liability refunded on cancellation Experience rating vs. community rating Group's own history vs. same price for an area Effective date vs. issue date When coverage starts vs. when the contract is issued Standard vs. substandard Standard premium vs. rated up or excluded Trial application vs. application with premium No offer; no coverage vs. receipt may start coverage (depends on receipt type) Misrepresentation vs. fraud Untrue statement vs. deliberate untrue statement"},{"id":"ch18-s18","title":"17. Check yourself","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> What is the primary goal of underwriting?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> How many parts does the application have, and which one must be completed for every application?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> What is the difference between a representation and a warranty?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Who can access the MIB, and what can it be used for?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Which receipt is most common, and when does its coverage start?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> What is the maximum backdating period?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> What is the standard free-look period, and what starts it?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> What is constructive delivery?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Who must sign a replacement notice?</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> How many main risk classes are there, and which one has the lowest premium?</p><h4>Scenario questions</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario A:</strong> Nina applies for health coverage, and her application says she has never been treated for asthma. She was treated 2 years ago and forgot. The insurer finds out after the claim. Is the statement a representation or a warranty? Is it material?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario B:</strong> An agent gives Owen his policy but tells him to hold it until he confirms he is still in good health. Has constructive delivery occurred?</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Scenario C:</strong> A 42-year-old with a history of heart bypass surgery applies. The insurer offers coverage at a higher premium. What type of offer is this?</li></ul>","text":"What is the primary goal of underwriting? How many parts does the application have, and which one must be completed for every application? What is the difference between a representation and a warranty? Who can access the MIB, and what can it be used for? Which receipt is most common, and when does its coverage start? What is the maximum backdating period? What is the standard free-look period, and what starts it? What is constructive delivery? Who must sign a replacement notice? How many main risk classes are there, and which one has the lowest premium? Scenario questions Scenario A: Nina applies for health coverage, and her application says she has never been treated for asthma. She was treated 2 years ago and forgot. The insurer finds out after the claim. Is the statement a representation or a warranty? Is it material? Scenario B: An agent gives Owen his policy but tells him to hold it until he confirms he is still in good health. Has constructive delivery occurred? Scenario C: A 42-year-old with a history of heart bypass surgery applies. The insurer offers coverage at a higher premium. What type of offer is this?"},{"id":"ch18-s19","title":"18. Answer key","html":"<p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> Protect against adverse selection (evaluate and select risks).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> Three parts: Part I (general), Part II (medical), Part III (agent's report). Part II must be completed for every application.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> A representation is true to the best of knowledge and must be accurate only if material. A warranty must be completely true, and breach can void the policy.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> Member insurers only. Use is limited to underwriting and claims processing.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">5.</span> Conditional receipt. Coverage starts on the application date or medical exam date (whichever is later), if the applicant is insurable.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">6.</span> 6 months.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">7.</span> 10 days (standard). It starts at delivery (the delivery receipt).</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">8.</span> Delivery without physical transfer. The insurer gives up all control to someone acting for the owner, such as the agent. Does not occur if the agent is told to hold the policy.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">9.</span> Both the agent and the applicant.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">10.</span> Four main classes (preferred, standard, substandard, uninsurable/declined). A special class is a variant of substandard. Preferred has the lowest premium.</p><p>Scenario A: It is a representation (the applicant's statement is judged to the best of knowledge). It is material only if it would have changed the insurer's decision. A knowing false statement could be fraud. It matters only if the omission would have caused denial or a higher rate.</p><p>Scenario B: Probably not. The agent was told to withhold delivery until health is confirmed, which does not qualify as constructive delivery.</p><p>Scenario C: A substandard (counter-) offer: a higher premium for an impaired risk.</p><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for Chapter 18:</strong> Make a receipt card: CONDITIONAL = if insurable. BINDING = until denied. INSPECTION = look first. Then a representations-vs-warranties card with two words each.</p></div>","text":"Protect against adverse selection (evaluate and select risks). Three parts: Part I (general), Part II (medical), Part III (agent's report). Part II must be completed for every application. A representation is true to the best of knowledge and must be accurate only if material. A warranty must be completely true, and breach can void the policy. Member insurers only. Use is limited to underwriting and claims processing. Conditional receipt. Coverage starts on the application date or medical exam date (whichever is later), if the applicant is insurable. 6 months. 10 days (standard). It starts at delivery (the delivery receipt). Delivery without physical transfer. The insurer gives up all control to someone acting for the owner, such as the agent. Does not occur if the agent is told to hold the policy. Both the agent and the applicant. Four main classes (preferred, standard, substandard, uninsurable/declined). A special class is a variant of substandard. Preferred has the lowest premium. Scenario A: It is a representation (the applicant's statement is judged to the best of knowledge). It is material only if it would have changed the insurer's decision. A knowing false statement could be fraud. It matters only if the omission would have caused denial or a higher rate. Scenario B: Probably not. The agent was told to withhold delivery until health is confirmed, which does not qualify as constructive delivery. Scenario C: A substandard (counter-) offer: a higher premium for an impaired risk. Memory tip for Chapter 18: Make a receipt card: CONDITIONAL = if insurable. BINDING = until denied. INSPECTION = look first. Then a representations-vs-warranties card with two words each."}]},{"id":"ch19","short":"Bonus","title":"Bonus - Social Insurance Study Tool Slides (25 screenshots)","label":"Bonus: Study Tool Slides","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>What this is:</strong> A summary of the 25-slide &quot;Social Insurance Study Tool&quot; (Medicare Parts A to D, Medicaid, and Social Security). The topics overlap with other chapters, so this section doubles as a fast review.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"ch19-s1","title":"Big picture in 30 seconds","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare</strong> = federal, age 65+ or disabled (24 months on SSDI) or ESRD/ALS. <strong>Medicaid</strong> = joint federal-state, low income, year-round, covers long-term nursing home care. <strong>Social Security</strong> = income benefits funded by FICA.</li><li class=\"l0\">Most useful lines: <strong>Part A usually free; Part B optional with a 20% coinsurance; Part C = all-in-one from private insurers; Part D = drugs.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare enrollment window = 7 months</strong> (3 before the 65th birthday month, the birthday month, and 3 after).</li></ul>","text":"Medicare = federal, age 65+ or disabled (24 months on SSDI) or ESRD/ALS. Medicaid = joint federal-state, low income, year-round, covers long-term nursing home care. Social Security = income benefits funded by FICA. Most useful lines: Part A usually free; Part B optional with a 20% coinsurance; Part C = all-in-one from private insurers; Part D = drugs. Medicare enrollment window = 7 months (3 before the 65th birthday month, the birthday month, and 3 after)."},{"id":"ch19-s2","title":"1. Slides 1 to 2: Welcome and Medicare introduction","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">The tool covers three study guides: Module 1 (Medicare Parts A, B, C, D), Module 2 (Medicaid), and Module 3 (Social Security benefits).</li><li class=\"l0\">Medicare is a federal health insurance program for: people age <strong>65 or older</strong>; people <strong>under 65 with qualifying disabilities</strong>; and people of <strong>any age with ESRD or ALS</strong>.</li></ul>","text":"The tool covers three study guides: Module 1 (Medicare Parts A, B, C, D), Module 2 (Medicaid), and Module 3 (Social Security benefits). Medicare is a federal health insurance program for: people age 65 or older; people under 65 with qualifying disabilities; and people of any age with ESRD or ALS."},{"id":"ch19-s3","title":"2. Slides 3 to 6: Medicare Parts A, B, C, D","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Eligibility and costs</th><th>Key facts from the slides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A</strong> (hospital)</td><td>Inpatient hospital, skilled nursing facility (SNF), hospice, some home health</td><td>Usually free if you or your spouse paid Medicare taxes for <strong>10+ years</strong></td><td>Deductible per benefit period. Coinsurance after <strong>60 days</strong> in a hospital or <strong>20 days</strong> in a SNF. Automatic at 65 if receiving Social Security</td></tr><tr><td><strong>B</strong> (medical)</td><td>Doctor services, outpatient care, preventive services, durable medical equipment</td><td>OPTIONAL; most people pay a monthly premium (income-adjusted)</td><td>Annual deductible. <strong>20% coinsurance</strong> after the deductible. <strong>Late enrollment penalty: 10% increase for each 12-month delay</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>C</strong> (Medicare Advantage)</td><td>An alternative to Original Medicare, offered by private insurers</td><td>Must already be enrolled in A and B. Premiums vary by plan</td><td>Must cover everything in A and B EXCEPT hospice (Original Medicare still pays hospice). Usually includes Part D and extras (dental, vision, hearing, fitness). Copays and networks apply</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D</strong> (prescription drugs)</td><td>Prescription medications through private plans approved by Medicare</td><td>Must be enrolled in A or B (or in a Part C plan that doesn't include drugs)</td><td>Monthly premium, deductible, copay or coinsurance. <strong>Annual out-of-pocket cap</strong>: the plan pays 100% for the rest of the year after the cap. The coverage gap (&quot;donut hole&quot;) was <strong>eliminated January 1, 2025</strong>. Late penalty if not enrolled when first eligible without other creditable coverage</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Medicare comparison chart (slide 7)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Part A</th><th>Part B</th><th>Part C</th><th>Part D</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Provider</td><td>Federal government</td><td>Federal government</td><td>Private insurer</td><td>Private insurer</td></tr><tr><td>Enrollment</td><td>Automatic if receiving Social Security at 65</td><td>Optional</td><td>Voluntary (must have A and B)</td><td>Voluntary</td></tr><tr><td>Premium</td><td>Usually free</td><td>Monthly premium</td><td>Varies by plan</td><td>Varies</td></tr><tr><td>Late penalty</td><td>No (if qualified for free)</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Extras</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Often included</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Exam tips (slide 8)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Medicare Advantage (Part C) = &quot;all-in-one&quot; (may include A, B, D, plus extras).</li><li class=\"l0\">Part D = drugs.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Enrollment window = 7 months</strong> (3 before, the month of, and 3 after the 65th birthday).</li><li class=\"l0\">Late penalties apply to Parts B and D if enrollment is delayed without creditable coverage.</li></ul>","text":"Part What it covers Eligibility and costs Key facts from the slides A (hospital) Inpatient hospital, skilled nursing facility (SNF), hospice, some home health Usually free if you or your spouse paid Medicare taxes for 10+ years Deductible per benefit period. Coinsurance after 60 days in a hospital or 20 days in a SNF. Automatic at 65 if receiving Social Security B (medical) Doctor services, outpatient care, preventive services, durable medical equipment OPTIONAL; most people pay a monthly premium (income-adjusted) Annual deductible. 20% coinsurance after the deductible. Late enrollment penalty: 10% increase for each 12-month delay C (Medicare Advantage) An alternative to Original Medicare, offered by private insurers Must already be enrolled in A and B. Premiums vary by plan Must cover everything in A and B EXCEPT hospice (Original Medicare still pays hospice). Usually includes Part D and extras (dental, vision, hearing, fitness). Copays and networks apply D (prescription drugs) Prescription medications through private plans approved by Medicare Must be enrolled in A or B (or in a Part C plan that doesn't include drugs) Monthly premium, deductible, copay or coinsurance. Annual out-of-pocket cap: the plan pays 100% for the rest of the year after the cap. The coverage gap (\"donut hole\") was eliminated January 1, 2025. Late penalty if not enrolled when first eligible without other creditable coverage Medicare comparison chart (slide 7) Feature Part A Part B Part C Part D Provider Federal government Federal government Private insurer Private insurer Enrollment Automatic if receiving Social Security at 65 Optional Voluntary (must have A and B) Voluntary Premium Usually free Monthly premium Varies by plan Varies Late penalty No (if qualified for free) Yes No Yes Extras No No Often included — Exam tips (slide 8) Medicare Advantage (Part C) = \"all-in-one\" (may include A, B, D, plus extras). Part D = drugs. Enrollment window = 7 months (3 before, the month of, and 3 after the 65th birthday). Late penalties apply to Parts B and D if enrollment is delayed without creditable coverage."},{"id":"ch19-s4","title":"3. Slides 9 to 17: Medicaid","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicaid</strong> is a <strong>joint federal and state program</strong> for low-income individuals and families: children, pregnant women, elderly adults, and people with disabilities.</li><li class=\"l0\">States must follow minimum federal guidelines, but eligibility and benefits <strong>vary by state</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Eligibility:</strong> means-tested (income and limited assets). Asset limits mainly apply to the aged, blind, and disabled. Varies by state of residence, household size, and category. States may expand eligibility under the Affordable Care Act.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Mandatory benefits (federal law):</strong> inpatient and outpatient hospital; physician services; laboratory and X-ray; nursing home and home health care; family planning; pediatric and maternity care.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Optional benefits (states choose):</strong> prescription drugs; dental and vision; physical therapy.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Enrollment:</strong> <strong>year-round</strong> (unlike Medicare). Through state Medicaid agencies or healthcare.gov in some states. Children and pregnant women often qualify quickly.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cost:</strong> usually very low or no cost. Some states have nominal copays or premiums for certain higher-income groups.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Long-term care:</strong> Medicaid is the <strong>primary payer of long-term nursing home care</strong> in the U.S. and covers custodial care that Medicare typically does not pay for.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Dual eligibility:</strong> People who qualify for BOTH Medicare and Medicaid are &quot;dual eligibles.&quot; Medicaid may help pay Medicare premiums, deductibles, and copayments.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Comparison chart (slide 16):</strong> Type = joint federal-state. Target = low-income individuals and families. Administered by each state, following federal rules. Costs minimal or none. Covers long-term care: yes. Prescription drugs: often yes (optional). Dual eligible support: yes.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exam tips (slide 17):</strong></li><li class=\"l1\">&quot;Medicaid = Aid for the poor&quot; (the mnemonic on the slide).</li><li class=\"l1\">States run Medicaid, so rules vary by location.</li><li class=\"l1\">Medicaid covers long-term care; Medicare does not.</li><li class=\"l1\">Dual eligibles get coverage from both programs. Medicaid often pays what Medicare doesn't.</li><li class=\"l1\">ACA expansion lets many low-income adults qualify, especially in expansion states.</li></ul>","text":"Medicaid is a joint federal and state program for low-income individuals and families: children, pregnant women, elderly adults, and people with disabilities. States must follow minimum federal guidelines, but eligibility and benefits vary by state. Eligibility: means-tested (income and limited assets). Asset limits mainly apply to the aged, blind, and disabled. Varies by state of residence, household size, and category. States may expand eligibility under the Affordable Care Act. Mandatory benefits (federal law): inpatient and outpatient hospital; physician services; laboratory and X-ray; nursing home and home health care; family planning; pediatric and maternity care. Optional benefits (states choose): prescription drugs; dental and vision; physical therapy. Enrollment: year-round (unlike Medicare). Through state Medicaid agencies or healthcare.gov in some states. Children and pregnant women often qualify quickly. Cost: usually very low or no cost. Some states have nominal copays or premiums for certain higher-income groups. Long-term care: Medicaid is the primary payer of long-term nursing home care in the U.S. and covers custodial care that Medicare typically does not pay for. Dual eligibility: People who qualify for BOTH Medicare and Medicaid are \"dual eligibles.\" Medicaid may help pay Medicare premiums, deductibles, and copayments. Comparison chart (slide 16): Type = joint federal-state. Target = low-income individuals and families. Administered by each state, following federal rules. Costs minimal or none. Covers long-term care: yes. Prescription drugs: often yes (optional). Dual eligible support: yes. Exam tips (slide 17): \"Medicaid = Aid for the poor\" (the mnemonic on the slide). States run Medicaid, so rules vary by location. Medicaid covers long-term care; Medicare does not. Dual eligibles get coverage from both programs. Medicaid often pays what Medicare doesn't. ACA expansion lets many low-income adults qualify, especially in expansion states."},{"id":"ch19-s5","title":"4. Slides 18 to 19: Social Security retirement","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Social Security is a federal insurance program paying retired workers, disabled workers, survivors of deceased workers, and dependents of beneficiaries. Funded by <strong>FICA</strong> payroll taxes.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Retirement eligibility:</strong> <strong>40 work credits</strong> (typically 10 years of work). Earliest age <strong>62</strong> (reduced benefit). Benefit increases for each year you delay, up to age <strong>70</strong>.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Full retirement age (FRA):</strong> <strong>65 for those born before 1938; gradually rising for 1938 to 1959; 67 for 1960 and later.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Benefit amount:</strong> Based on the average indexed monthly earnings (AIME) over the <strong>35 highest-earning years.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Spousal benefit:</strong> A spouse can receive up to <strong>50%</strong> of the worker's benefit. Divorced spouses may qualify under certain conditions.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p>The 35 highest-earning years are the basis of AIME.</p></div>","text":"Social Security is a federal insurance program paying retired workers, disabled workers, survivors of deceased workers, and dependents of beneficiaries. Funded by FICA payroll taxes. Retirement eligibility: 40 work credits (typically 10 years of work). Earliest age 62 (reduced benefit). Benefit increases for each year you delay, up to age 70. Full retirement age (FRA): 65 for those born before 1938; gradually rising for 1938 to 1959; 67 for 1960 and later. Benefit amount: Based on the average indexed monthly earnings (AIME) over the 35 highest-earning years. Spousal benefit: A spouse can receive up to 50% of the worker's benefit. Divorced spouses may qualify under certain conditions. Memory trick: The 35 highest-earning years are the basis of AIME."},{"id":"ch19-s6","title":"5. Slides 20 to 21: Disability and survivor benefits (Social Security)","html":"<h4>Disability (SSDI)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">For people who cannot work because of a severe, long-term condition.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must meet SSA's definition of disability: lasts <strong>12 months or more</strong>, or is terminal.</li><li class=\"l0\">Must have enough recent work credits for age.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>5-month waiting period</strong> before benefits begin. <strong>Waived for ALS.</strong></li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medicare eligibility begins 24 months</strong> after disability benefits start. <strong>Except ALS.</strong></li></ul><h4>Survivor benefits</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Paid to family members of a deceased worker who earned enough credits.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Eligible survivors:</strong></li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Spouses</strong>: especially if over 60, or age 50 if disabled, or caring for a child under 16.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Dependent children</strong> under age 18 (or 19 if still in high school).</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Disabled children</strong> of any age if disabled before age 22.</li><li class=\"l1\"><strong>Dependent parents</strong> age 62 or older.</li></ul><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Study tip:</strong> A widow or widower can start survivor benefits at age 60, or at age <strong>50 if disabled</strong>. A spouse caring for a child under 16 may also qualify.</p></div>","text":"Disability (SSDI) For people who cannot work because of a severe, long-term condition. Must meet SSA's definition of disability: lasts 12 months or more, or is terminal. Must have enough recent work credits for age. 5-month waiting period before benefits begin. Waived for ALS. Medicare eligibility begins 24 months after disability benefits start. Except ALS. Survivor benefits Paid to family members of a deceased worker who earned enough credits. Eligible survivors: Spouses: especially if over 60, or age 50 if disabled, or caring for a child under 16. Dependent children under age 18 (or 19 if still in high school). Disabled children of any age if disabled before age 22. Dependent parents age 62 or older. Study tip: A widow or widower can start survivor benefits at age 60, or at age 50 if disabled. A spouse caring for a child under 16 may also qualify."},{"id":"ch19-s7","title":"6. Slide 22: SSI (Supplemental Security Income)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Needs-based</strong> benefit for the elderly, blind, or disabled with limited income and resources.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Not based on work history.</strong> Funded by <strong>general tax revenues</strong>, not FICA.</li><li class=\"l0\">Often confused with SSDI. Know the difference: <strong>SSDI = work history. SSI = need.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Needs-based benefit for the elderly, blind, or disabled with limited income and resources. Not based on work history. Funded by general tax revenues, not FICA. Often confused with SSDI. Know the difference: SSDI = work history. SSI = need."},{"id":"ch19-s8","title":"7. Slides 23 to 24: Social Security comparison chart and exam tips","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Eligibility</th><th>When it begins</th><th>Key notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Retirement</strong></td><td>Age 62+ with 40 work credits</td><td>As early as 62 (reduced)</td><td>Full benefit at FRA. Delayed credits up to 70</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disability (SSDI)</strong></td><td>Disability plus enough work credits</td><td>After the 5-month waiting period</td><td>Medicare after 24 months</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Survivor</strong></td><td>Family of a deceased worker with enough credits</td><td>Depends on age and relationship</td><td>Spouse, children, and parents may qualify</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SSI</strong></td><td>Aged, blind, or disabled with low income and resources</td><td>Upon approval</td><td>Funded by general taxes. No work history required</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Exam tips (slide 24)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">SSDI = disability benefits based on work history.</li><li class=\"l0\">SSI = needs-based benefits, with no work history required.</li><li class=\"l0\">A widow or widower can receive benefits as early as age <strong>60</strong> (<strong>50</strong> if disabled).</li><li class=\"l0\">Children under <strong>18</strong> (or <strong>19</strong> if in school) may get survivor benefits.</li><li class=\"l0\">Medicare eligibility starts <strong>2 years</strong> after SSDI begins.</li><li class=\"l0\">Work credits: most people earn <strong>4 per year</strong>. <strong>40 credits = fully insured.</strong></li></ul>","text":"Benefit Eligibility When it begins Key notes Retirement Age 62+ with 40 work credits As early as 62 (reduced) Full benefit at FRA. Delayed credits up to 70 Disability (SSDI) Disability plus enough work credits After the 5-month waiting period Medicare after 24 months Survivor Family of a deceased worker with enough credits Depends on age and relationship Spouse, children, and parents may qualify SSI Aged, blind, or disabled with low income and resources Upon approval Funded by general taxes. No work history required Exam tips (slide 24) SSDI = disability benefits based on work history. SSI = needs-based benefits, with no work history required. A widow or widower can receive benefits as early as age 60 (50 if disabled). Children under 18 (or 19 if in school) may get survivor benefits. Medicare eligibility starts 2 years after SSDI begins. Work credits: most people earn 4 per year. 40 credits = fully insured."},{"id":"ch19-s9","title":"8. Slide 25: Closing screen","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Completion screen only. No new content.</li></ul>","text":"Completion screen only. No new content."},{"id":"ch19-s10","title":"9. Quick comparison","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Key fact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part A premium</td><td>Usually free (10+ years of Medicare taxes)</td></tr><tr><td>Part B late penalty</td><td>10% increase for each 12-month delay</td></tr><tr><td>Part D donut hole</td><td>Eliminated January 1, 2025</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare enrollment</td><td>7-month window (3 months before, birth month, 3 months after)</td></tr><tr><td>FRA</td><td>65 for those born before 1938; gradually rising for 1938 to 1959; 67 for 1960 and later</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI waiting period</td><td>5 months, waived for ALS</td></tr><tr><td>Widow(er) early benefits</td><td>Age 60, or 50 if disabled</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare Advantage</td><td>Must cover A and B except hospice; hospice stays with Original Medicare</td></tr><tr><td>Medicaid</td><td>Primary payer of long-term nursing home care</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout memory\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Memory trick</div><p><strong>Memory tip for the slides:</strong> Read all 25 slides once more as a single flow (Medicare A, B, C, D, then Medicaid, then Social Security). Then do the comparison charts from memory. They show up in every module.</p></div>","text":"Topic Key fact Part A premium Usually free (10+ years of Medicare taxes) Part B late penalty 10% increase for each 12-month delay Part D donut hole Eliminated January 1, 2025 Medicare enrollment 7-month window (3 months before, birth month, 3 months after) FRA 65 for those born before 1938; gradually rising for 1938 to 1959; 67 for 1960 and later SSDI waiting period 5 months, waived for ALS Widow(er) early benefits Age 60, or 50 if disabled Medicare Advantage Must cover A and B except hospice; hospice stays with Original Medicare Medicaid Primary payer of long-term nursing home care Memory tip for the slides: Read all 25 slides once more as a single flow (Medicare A, B, C, D, then Medicaid, then Social Security). Then do the comparison charts from memory. They show up in every module."}]},{"id":"addendum","short":"Addendum","title":"Addendum: Exam Blueprint and Louisiana Regulation","label":"Addendum: Louisiana Regulation and Exam Blueprint","intro":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>About this addendum:</strong> Louisiana regulation is the largest single domain on the combined Series 103 exam. This section gives the exam blueprint and the Louisiana rules you need to know.</p></div>","sections":[{"id":"addendum-s1","title":"1. Exam blueprint (PSI)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Exam</th><th>Covers</th><th>Questions</th><th>Time</th><th>Pass mark</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Series 101</strong></td><td>Life</td><td>100</td><td>120 minutes</td><td>70%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Series 102</strong></td><td>Health and accident</td><td>100</td><td>120 minutes</td><td>70%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Series 103</strong></td><td>Life, health, and accident (combined)</td><td>150</td><td>160 minutes</td><td>70%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Unscored experimental questions (about 5 to 10) may be added. They count against your time.</li><li class=\"l0\">Life and health taken separately = Series 101 plus Series 102. Combined = Series 103.</li><li class=\"l0\">Fees: Series 101 $36; Series 102 $36; Series 103 $58.</li><li class=\"l0\">Testing: PSI test centers only. Remote testing ended July 8, 2024.</li><li class=\"l0\">Allowed: scratch paper, pencil, and a silent non-programmable calculator. No reference materials or electronics.</li><li class=\"l0\">Retakes: unlimited within one year, but not on the same day.</li><li class=\"l0\">Fingerprinting is required separately through IdentoGO.</li></ul><h4>Series 103 domain weights (combined exam)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Weight</th><th>Chapters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Louisiana insurance regulation</strong></td><td><strong>23%</strong></td><td>Section 2 below (plus Chapter 1 and Chapter 4)</td></tr><tr><td>Licensing and regulation</td><td>1%</td><td>Section 2.2 below</td></tr><tr><td>General insurance concepts</td><td>7%</td><td>Chapters 1 and 2</td></tr><tr><td>Life, health, and accident insurance basics</td><td>14%</td><td>Chapters 2, 3, and 4</td></tr><tr><td>Types of life insurance policies</td><td>8%</td><td>Chapters 5.1 and 6</td></tr><tr><td>Life insurance policy provisions, options, and riders</td><td>13%</td><td>Chapters 5.2 and 7</td></tr><tr><td>Annuities</td><td>7%</td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr><td>Individual health and accident policy provisions</td><td>7%</td><td>Chapters 17 and 18</td></tr><tr><td>Disability income and related insurance</td><td>4%</td><td>Chapter 15</td></tr><tr><td>Medical plans</td><td>4%</td><td>Chapters 12 and 14</td></tr><tr><td>Group health insurance</td><td>3%</td><td>Chapters 6 and 12</td></tr><tr><td>Health insurance for senior citizens and special needs</td><td>7%</td><td>Chapters 13 and 16</td></tr><tr><td>Federal tax considerations (life and health)</td><td>2%</td><td>Section 3.5 below and Chapter 7</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Series 101 and 102 domain weights (for separate exams)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Series 101 (Life)</th><th>Weight</th><th>Series 102 (Health and accident)</th><th>Weight</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Louisiana insurance regulation</td><td>10%</td><td>Louisiana insurance regulation</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>Licensing and regulation</td><td>2%</td><td>Licensing and regulation</td><td>2%</td></tr><tr><td>General insurance concepts</td><td>10%</td><td>General insurance concepts</td><td>11%</td></tr><tr><td>Life insurance basics</td><td>16%</td><td>Health and accident insurance basics</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>Life insurance policy provisions, options, and riders</td><td>26%</td><td>Individual health and accident policy provisions</td><td>14%</td></tr><tr><td>Types of life insurance policies</td><td>17%</td><td>Disability income and related insurance</td><td>15%</td></tr><tr><td>Federal tax considerations for life insurance</td><td>8%</td><td>Group health insurance</td><td>9%</td></tr><tr><td>Annuities</td><td>11%</td><td>Medical plans</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td>Senior citizens and special-needs health insurance</td><td>14%</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td>Federal tax considerations for health insurance</td><td>5%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Exam Covers Questions Time Pass mark Series 101 Life 100 120 minutes 70% Series 102 Health and accident 100 120 minutes 70% Series 103 Life, health, and accident (combined) 150 160 minutes 70% Unscored experimental questions (about 5 to 10) may be added. They count against your time. Life and health taken separately = Series 101 plus Series 102. Combined = Series 103. Fees: Series 101 $36; Series 102 $36; Series 103 $58. Testing: PSI test centers only. Remote testing ended July 8, 2024. Allowed: scratch paper, pencil, and a silent non-programmable calculator. No reference materials or electronics. Retakes: unlimited within one year, but not on the same day. Fingerprinting is required separately through IdentoGO. Series 103 domain weights (combined exam) Domain Weight Chapters Louisiana insurance regulation 23% Section 2 below (plus Chapter 1 and Chapter 4) Licensing and regulation 1% Section 2.2 below General insurance concepts 7% Chapters 1 and 2 Life, health, and accident insurance basics 14% Chapters 2, 3, and 4 Types of life insurance policies 8% Chapters 5.1 and 6 Life insurance policy provisions, options, and riders 13% Chapters 5.2 and 7 Annuities 7% Chapter 9 Individual health and accident policy provisions 7% Chapters 17 and 18 Disability income and related insurance 4% Chapter 15 Medical plans 4% Chapters 12 and 14 Group health insurance 3% Chapters 6 and 12 Health insurance for senior citizens and special needs 7% Chapters 13 and 16 Federal tax considerations (life and health) 2% Section 3.5 below and Chapter 7 Series 101 and 102 domain weights (for separate exams) Series 101 (Life) Weight Series 102 (Health and accident) Weight Louisiana insurance regulation 10% Louisiana insurance regulation 10% Licensing and regulation 2% Licensing and regulation 2% General insurance concepts 10% General insurance concepts 11% Life insurance basics 16% Health and accident insurance basics 10% Life insurance policy provisions, options, and riders 26% Individual health and accident policy provisions 14% Types of life insurance policies 17% Disability income and related insurance 15% Federal tax considerations for life insurance 8% Group health insurance 9% Annuities 11% Medical plans 10% Senior citizens and special-needs health insurance 14% Federal tax considerations for health insurance 5%"},{"id":"addendum-s2","title":"2. Louisiana regulation (Series 103: 23%)","html":"<h4>2.1 Where the rules live</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana's insurance statutes are in <strong>Title 22 of the Louisiana Revised Statutes</strong> (the Insurance Code). Administrative rules are in <strong>Title 37 of the Louisiana Administrative Code</strong>, Part XIII (insurance regulations).</li><li class=\"l0\">The <strong>Louisiana Department of Insurance (LDI)</strong> administers the code.</li></ul><h4>2.2 Licensing and continuing education</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Exam:</strong> A resident applicant must pass an examination for each line of authority. Passing score <strong>70%</strong> (R.S. 22:1545(A)).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Pre-licensing education:</strong> None required by statute.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Lines of authority:</strong> R.S. 22:1547(A)(3) lists <strong>variable life and variable annuity</strong> as a separate line of authority.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Continuing education (life, accident, health producers):</strong> <strong>24 hours</strong> every <strong>2 years</strong>, including <strong>at least 3 ethics hours</strong> (R.S. 22:1573(C); cycle R.S. 22:1547(C)). From renewals in <strong>July 2027</strong>, <strong>2 of the 24 hours</strong> must cover legislative updates.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annuity training:</strong> Producers who sell annuities complete a <strong>one-time 4-hour</strong> annuity training course. This is separate from CE.</li></ul><h4>2.3 Louisiana policy rules (life and health)</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Louisiana value</th><th>Statute</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Life free look</td><td><strong>10 days from receipt</strong> of the policy</td><td>R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a)</td></tr><tr><td>Life incontestability</td><td><strong>2 years</strong> in force during the insured's lifetime</td><td>R.S. 22:931(A)(2)</td></tr><tr><td>Life grace period</td><td><strong>30 days, or one month</strong> at the insurer's option</td><td>R.S. 22:931(A)(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Life reinstatement</td><td>Within <strong>3 years</strong> of default, with evidence of insurability and payment</td><td>R.S. 22:931(A)(9)</td></tr><tr><td>Health incontestability</td><td><strong>3 years</strong></td><td>R.S. 22:975</td></tr><tr><td>Health grace period</td><td><strong>30 days</strong></td><td>R.S. 22:977</td></tr><tr><td>Health premium notice</td><td>If unpaid <strong>15 days</strong> before the grace period ends, the insurer mails a first-class notice of lapse and reinstatement rights</td><td>R.S. 22:977</td></tr><tr><td>Health reinstatement</td><td>Accepting the defaulted premium reinstates; sickness covered again after 10 days</td><td>R.S. 22:975(A)(2)</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum age to contract</td><td>Age of majority <strong>18</strong> (Civil Code art. 29). A minor <strong>15 or older</strong> may contract for life, health, or accident insurance</td><td>R.S. 22:852(B)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>2.4 Guaranty association</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>R.S. 22:2083(C)(2), per life:</strong> death benefits <strong>$300,000</strong>; cash surrender and withdrawal values <strong>$100,000</strong>; health benefits <strong>$500,000</strong>; annuity present value <strong>$250,000</strong>. Aggregate cap <strong>$500,000</strong> for any one individual.</li></ul><h4>2.5 Replacement and annuity sales</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Replacement:</strong> Louisiana Regulation 70 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 89) requires a notice regarding replacement, signed by the applicant and the producer.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annuity sales standard:</strong> Louisiana Regulation 89 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 117) sets a <strong>best-interest</strong> standard for annuity sales and recommendations. It was amended effective <strong>September 20, 2024</strong>.</li></ul><h4>2.6 Unfair and deceptive practices</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>R.S. 22:1964</strong> lists unfair or deceptive acts. Examples: misrepresenting policy provisions, false advertising, coercion, unfair discrimination, illegal rebates, and unfair claim-settlement practices (paragraph 14).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Claim-settlement practices (paragraph 14, examples):</strong> not responding promptly to communications; denying a claim without a reasonable investigation; not affirming or denying coverage within a reasonable time after proof of loss; not explaining a denial; not providing claim forms within 15 days; not giving the claim file on written request.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>R.S. 22:1973 was repealed effective July 1, 2024.</strong> The good-faith duty is now in <strong>R.S. 22:1892(I)</strong>: the insurer owes its insured a duty of good faith and fair dealing.</li></ul><h4>2.7 Federal tax items</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Standard federal tax points for life and annuities are summarized in Section 3.5 below.</li></ul>","text":"2.1 Where the rules live Louisiana's insurance statutes are in Title 22 of the Louisiana Revised Statutes (the Insurance Code). Administrative rules are in Title 37 of the Louisiana Administrative Code, Part XIII (insurance regulations). The Louisiana Department of Insurance (LDI) administers the code. 2.2 Licensing and continuing education Exam: A resident applicant must pass an examination for each line of authority. Passing score 70% (R.S. 22:1545(A)). Pre-licensing education: None required by statute. Lines of authority: R.S. 22:1547(A)(3) lists variable life and variable annuity as a separate line of authority. Continuing education (life, accident, health producers): 24 hours every 2 years, including at least 3 ethics hours (R.S. 22:1573(C); cycle R.S. 22:1547(C)). From renewals in July 2027, 2 of the 24 hours must cover legislative updates. Annuity training: Producers who sell annuities complete a one-time 4-hour annuity training course. This is separate from CE. 2.3 Louisiana policy rules (life and health) Rule Louisiana value Statute Life free look 10 days from receipt of the policy R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a) Life incontestability 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime R.S. 22:931(A)(2) Life grace period 30 days, or one month at the insurer's option R.S. 22:931(A)(1) Life reinstatement Within 3 years of default, with evidence of insurability and payment R.S. 22:931(A)(9) Health incontestability 3 years R.S. 22:975 Health grace period 30 days R.S. 22:977 Health premium notice If unpaid 15 days before the grace period ends, the insurer mails a first-class notice of lapse and reinstatement rights R.S. 22:977 Health reinstatement Accepting the defaulted premium reinstates; sickness covered again after 10 days R.S. 22:975(A)(2) Minimum age to contract Age of majority 18 (Civil Code art. 29). A minor 15 or older may contract for life, health, or accident insurance R.S. 22:852(B) 2.4 Guaranty association R.S. 22:2083(C)(2), per life: death benefits $300,000; cash surrender and withdrawal values $100,000; health benefits $500,000; annuity present value $250,000. Aggregate cap $500,000 for any one individual. 2.5 Replacement and annuity sales Replacement: Louisiana Regulation 70 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 89) requires a notice regarding replacement, signed by the applicant and the producer. Annuity sales standard: Louisiana Regulation 89 (LAC 37:XIII, Chapter 117) sets a best-interest standard for annuity sales and recommendations. It was amended effective September 20, 2024. 2.6 Unfair and deceptive practices R.S. 22:1964 lists unfair or deceptive acts. Examples: misrepresenting policy provisions, false advertising, coercion, unfair discrimination, illegal rebates, and unfair claim-settlement practices (paragraph 14). Claim-settlement practices (paragraph 14, examples): not responding promptly to communications; denying a claim without a reasonable investigation; not affirming or denying coverage within a reasonable time after proof of loss; not explaining a denial; not providing claim forms within 15 days; not giving the claim file on written request. R.S. 22:1973 was repealed effective July 1, 2024. The good-faith duty is now in R.S. 22:1892(I): the insurer owes its insured a duty of good faith and fair dealing. 2.7 Federal tax items Standard federal tax points for life and annuities are summarized in Section 3.5 below."},{"id":"addendum-s3","title":"3. Additional topics","html":"<p>Each topic below has a pointer to the chapter or a short summary.</p><h4>3.1 Louisiana regulation and producer ethics</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Covered in Section 2 above. Producer ethics and fiduciary duty: see Chapter 1 (&quot;Ethical agent practices&quot;) and Chapter 3 (agency law).</li></ul><h4>3.2 Replacement rules and annuity best interest</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Covered in Section 2.5 above.</li></ul><h4>3.3 Affordable Care Act basics (not Louisiana-specific)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Individual and small-group health plans cannot deny coverage or charge more because of health status (guaranteed issue and no preexisting-condition exclusions). This is why preexisting-condition waiting periods are no longer used (Chapter 12).</li><li class=\"l0\">Marketplace premium tax credits depend on income. Essential health benefits and metal tiers are in Chapter 12.</li></ul><h4>3.4 Group health participation and funding</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Participation rules (100% noncontributory, 75% contributory) are in Chapter 12. Self-funding and stop-loss are in Chapter 13. COBRA and HIPAA are in Chapter 12.</li></ul><h4>3.5 Tax summary (standard federal rules)</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Life insurance death proceeds:</strong> generally income-tax-free to the beneficiary (exceptions: transfer for value; interest on installments is taxable).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Cash value growth:</strong> tax-deferred. Withdrawals and loans from a standard policy: generally not taxable until gains exceed premiums paid.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Modified endowment contracts (MECs):</strong> withdrawals and loans taxed <strong>LIFO</strong> (gains first), plus a <strong>10% penalty</strong> before age 59½.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Section 1035 exchanges:</strong> tax-free transfers, such as life to annuity. Annuity to life insurance is not allowed.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Annuities:</strong> earnings taxed first on withdrawal (LIFO); exclusion ratio on annuitized payments.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Group term life:</strong> the first <strong>$50,000</strong> of coverage is tax-free to the employee (Chapter 6).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Business-owned life insurance:</strong> the $50,000 figure does not apply to COLI (Chapter 8).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Medical expense deduction:</strong> expenses above a percentage of adjusted gross income set by the IRS.</li></ul><h4>3.6 Claims handling</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Louisiana claim-settlement duties are in Section 2.6. Claim forms within 15 days and proof-of-loss timing are in Chapter 17.</li></ul><h4>3.7 Exam blueprint and exam-day</h4><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\">Domain weights and format are in Section 1. Practice under timed conditions and review every miss.</li></ul>","text":"Each topic below has a pointer to the chapter or a short summary. 3.1 Louisiana regulation and producer ethics Covered in Section 2 above. Producer ethics and fiduciary duty: see Chapter 1 (\"Ethical agent practices\") and Chapter 3 (agency law). 3.2 Replacement rules and annuity best interest Covered in Section 2.5 above. 3.3 Affordable Care Act basics (not Louisiana-specific) Individual and small-group health plans cannot deny coverage or charge more because of health status (guaranteed issue and no preexisting-condition exclusions). This is why preexisting-condition waiting periods are no longer used (Chapter 12). Marketplace premium tax credits depend on income. Essential health benefits and metal tiers are in Chapter 12. 3.4 Group health participation and funding Participation rules (100% noncontributory, 75% contributory) are in Chapter 12. Self-funding and stop-loss are in Chapter 13. COBRA and HIPAA are in Chapter 12. 3.5 Tax summary (standard federal rules) Life insurance death proceeds: generally income-tax-free to the beneficiary (exceptions: transfer for value; interest on installments is taxable). Cash value growth: tax-deferred. Withdrawals and loans from a standard policy: generally not taxable until gains exceed premiums paid. Modified endowment contracts (MECs): withdrawals and loans taxed LIFO (gains first), plus a 10% penalty before age 59½. Section 1035 exchanges: tax-free transfers, such as life to annuity. Annuity to life insurance is not allowed. Annuities: earnings taxed first on withdrawal (LIFO); exclusion ratio on annuitized payments. Group term life: the first $50,000 of coverage is tax-free to the employee (Chapter 6). Business-owned life insurance: the $50,000 figure does not apply to COLI (Chapter 8). Medical expense deduction: expenses above a percentage of adjusted gross income set by the IRS. 3.6 Claims handling Louisiana claim-settlement duties are in Section 2.6. Claim forms within 15 days and proof-of-loss timing are in Chapter 17. 3.7 Exam blueprint and exam-day Domain weights and format are in Section 1. Practice under timed conditions and review every miss."},{"id":"addendum-s4","title":"Louisiana producer requirements and fraud (verified from statute)","html":"<ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Long-term care training:</strong> A one-time training of at least 8 hours, then at least 4 hours every 2 years. Insurers must confirm completion (R.S. 22:1575).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Appointments:</strong> The insurer files a notice of appointment within 15 days of signing the agency contract (R.S. 22:1558).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Temporary licenses:</strong> A temporary producer license lasts up to 180 days and needs no exam (R.S. 22:1553).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Discipline:</strong> The commissioner may probate, suspend, revoke, or refuse a license, and may fine up to $500 per violation and $10,000 per year (R.S. 22:1554).</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Insurance fraud:</strong> A felony carrying up to 5 years in prison or a $5,000 fine per count. If the benefit is $1,000 or less, up to 6 months or a $1,000 fine (R.S. 22:1924). Suspected fraud must be reported to the fraud office within 60 days (R.S. 22:1926). Good-faith reporters have civil immunity (R.S. 22:1928).</li></ul>","text":"Long-term care training: A one-time training of at least 8 hours, then at least 4 hours every 2 years. Insurers must confirm completion (R.S. 22:1575). Appointments: The insurer files a notice of appointment within 15 days of signing the agency contract (R.S. 22:1558). Temporary licenses: A temporary producer license lasts up to 180 days and needs no exam (R.S. 22:1553). Discipline: The commissioner may probate, suspend, revoke, or refuse a license, and may fine up to $500 per violation and $10,000 per year (R.S. 22:1554). Insurance fraud: A felony carrying up to 5 years in prison or a $5,000 fine per count. If the benefit is $1,000 or less, up to 6 months or a $1,000 fine (R.S. 22:1924). Suspected fraud must be reported to the fraud office within 60 days (R.S. 22:1926). Good-faith reporters have civil immunity (R.S. 22:1928)."}]}];
window.REFERENCE = [{"id":"ref-how-to-use-this-guide","title":"How to Use This Guide","html":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Read this first:</strong> You have 27 days. You do not need to memorize everything. You need to recognize the traps, know the numbers, and recall the rules under mild pressure. This guide is built for that.</p></div><h3>Study tools inside each chapter</h3><ul class=\"bullets\"><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Chapter at a glance:</strong> A one-paragraph map of the chapter.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Big picture in 30 seconds:</strong> The 3 to 4 ideas that matter most.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Memory tricks:</strong> Short cues. Say them out loud.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Commonly confused pairs:</strong> The traps. Most exam misses come from these.</li><li class=\"l0\"><strong>Check yourself and Scenario questions:</strong> Answer before you read the answer key. Active recall beats rereading.</li></ul><h3>Study rhythm (ADHD-friendly)</h3><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">1.</span> <strong>25 minutes:</strong> Read ONE chapter section at a time. Stop at each heading.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">2.</span> <strong>5 minutes:</strong> Close the book. Say the big-picture points out loud, or write them on paper.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">3.</span> <strong>5 minutes:</strong> Do the check-yourself questions. Then review the answers.</p><p class=\"num\"><span class=\"n\">4.</span> <strong>Once per day:</strong> Recite the Master Cheat Sheet (Contents item 22) for 5 minutes.</p><p>Stop at 60 minutes per day. Long sessions reduce retention.</p>","text":"Study tools inside each chapter Chapter at a glance: A one-paragraph map of the chapter. Big picture in 30 seconds: The 3 to 4 ideas that matter most. Memory tricks: Short cues. Say them out loud. Commonly confused pairs: The traps. Most exam misses come from these. Check yourself and Scenario questions: Answer before you read the answer key. Active recall beats rereading. Study rhythm (ADHD-friendly) 25 minutes: Read ONE chapter section at a time. Stop at each heading. 5 minutes: Close the book. Say the big-picture points out loud, or write them on paper. 5 minutes: Do the check-yourself questions. Then review the answers. Once per day: Recite the Master Cheat Sheet (Contents item 22) for 5 minutes. Stop at 60 minutes per day. Long sessions reduce retention."},{"id":"ref-your-27-day-plan-november-25-to-december-22-2026","title":"Your 27-Day Plan (November 25 to December 22, 2026)","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dates</th><th>Focus</th><th>Tasks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Wed Nov 25 to Fri Nov 27</strong></td><td>Foundations</td><td>Chapters 1 and 2. Make the Agent/Broker/Public Adjuster card. Make the Peril/Hazard/Loss triangle</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sat Nov 28 to Tue Dec 1</strong></td><td>Contracts and life basics</td><td>Chapters 3 and 4. Build the contract checklist. Learn the 4 risk classes and the 4 receipts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wed Dec 2 to Fri Dec 4</strong></td><td>Life products</td><td>Chapters 5.1 and 5.2. Make the term/whole/universal three-column card. Learn the key numbers table</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sat Dec 5 to Sun Dec 6</strong></td><td>Group and estate</td><td>Chapters 6 and 7. Learn 100% vs 75%, 31 days, and the settlement options</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mon Dec 7 to Tue Dec 8</strong></td><td>Business and Social Security</td><td>Chapter 8 (cross-purchase math), Chapter 10 (2026 figures)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wed Dec 9 to Thu Dec 10</strong></td><td>Annuities and retirement</td><td>Chapter 9 and Chapter 11 (limits and RMD rules)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Dec 11 to Sat Dec 12</strong></td><td>Health basics</td><td>Chapters 12 and 13 (COBRA, ACA, Medicare A/B, HMO/PPO)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sun Dec 13 to Mon Dec 14</strong></td><td>Health detail</td><td>Chapters 14 and 15 (claims math, own vs any occupation)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tue Dec 15 to Wed Dec 16</strong></td><td>Seniors and provisions</td><td>Chapters 16, 17, 18 and the slides (Chapter 19)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thu Dec 17</strong></td><td>First full practice test</td><td>Contents item 23 (mixed practice test). Mark every miss. Redo the matching chapter</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fri Dec 18</strong></td><td>Louisiana review</td><td>Review the Louisiana regulation addendum (Contents item 21) and the state law section of the cheat sheet (Contents item 22)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sat Dec 19</strong></td><td>Weak spots</td><td>Redo chapters tied to your misses. Use the cheat sheet</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sun Dec 20</strong></td><td>Second practice test</td><td>Contents item 23 again. Target 80% or higher</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mon Dec 21</strong></td><td>Light review</td><td>Cheat sheet only. Sleep well. Prepare your ID and the exam location</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tue Dec 22</strong></td><td>EXAM DAY</td><td>Check the test center, time, and ID requirements the morning of the exam</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> Your course access expires on December 22, 2026 at 4:57 PM. Finish the course content by December 20 so you are not racing a deadline.</p></div>","text":""},{"id":"ref-master-cheat-sheet-key-numbers-and-rules","title":"Master Cheat Sheet (Key Numbers and Rules)","html":"<div class=\"callout info\"><p><strong>How to use:</strong> Recite this for 5 minutes a day. Cover the right column and say the answer.</p></div><h3>Contracts and underwriting</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Essential contract elements</td><td>Offer and acceptance, consideration, legal purpose, competent parties</td></tr><tr><td>Application without premium</td><td>An invitation, not an offer</td></tr><tr><td>Insurable interest timing (life and health)</td><td>At application only</td></tr><tr><td>Insurable interest timing (property and casualty)</td><td>At application AND at claim</td></tr><tr><td>Warranty vs. representation</td><td>Warranty = completely true. Representation = true to best knowledge; material only</td></tr><tr><td>Incontestable period</td><td>Life 2 years in force during the insured's lifetime (Louisiana: R.S. 22:931(A)(2)); health and accident 3 years (Louisiana: R.S. 22:975)</td></tr><tr><td>Suicide exclusion (life)</td><td>2 years; premiums refunded</td></tr><tr><td>Free look (standard individual life)</td><td>10 days (Louisiana: from receipt of the policy, R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a))</td></tr><tr><td>Free look (mail order or direct response)</td><td>30 days</td></tr><tr><td>Free look (LTC and Medicare supplement)</td><td>30 days</td></tr><tr><td>Backdating limit</td><td>6 months (not for variable contracts)</td></tr><tr><td>Replacement</td><td>Notice required; agent explains new limits</td></tr><tr><td>Risk classes</td><td>Preferred, standard, substandard, declined</td></tr><tr><td>Receipt that covers the applicant only if insurable</td><td>Conditional</td></tr><tr><td>Receipt that covers until formal denial</td><td>Binding (unconditional)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Life insurance and policy values</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Term renewal</td><td>Premium rises each renewal, no proof of insurability</td></tr><tr><td>Convertible term: attained vs original age</td><td>Attained = higher premium. Original = lower premium, may owe a difference</td></tr><tr><td>Modified premium</td><td>Lower first 5 years, one step-up in year 6</td></tr><tr><td>Single premium whole life</td><td>Always a MEC</td></tr><tr><td>7-pay test</td><td>Fails = MEC. Applies after June 20, 1988</td></tr><tr><td>MEC withdrawals</td><td>LIFO. 10% penalty before 59½</td></tr><tr><td>Nonforfeiture: highest face amount</td><td>Extended term (default option)</td></tr><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>Not taxable. Interest on accumulated dividends is taxable</td></tr><tr><td>Policy loans</td><td>Not taxable while in force. Unpaid loan reduces death benefit</td></tr><tr><td>Deferral of cash surrender payment</td><td>Up to 6 months</td></tr><tr><td>Accidental death (ADB)</td><td>Double (2x) or triple (3x); death within 90 days of accident</td></tr><tr><td>Waiver of premium</td><td>Usually after 6 months of total disability; ends at 60 to 65</td></tr><tr><td>Settlement option: default</td><td>Lump sum (chosen by owner, or beneficiary if owner chose none)</td></tr><tr><td>Viatical settlement: terminal illness</td><td>Expected death within 24 months</td></tr><tr><td>Accelerated benefit cap</td><td>Usually 50% of face value</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Group life and tax thresholds</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Noncontributory plan participation</td><td>100% of eligible employees</td></tr><tr><td>Contributory plan participation</td><td>75% of eligible employees</td></tr><tr><td>Enrollment period</td><td>31 days</td></tr><tr><td>Group conversion period</td><td>31 days after termination; no proof of insurability</td></tr><tr><td>Master policy ends</td><td>Members insured 5+ years may convert</td></tr><tr><td>Favorable group tax test</td><td>At least 70% of employees covered; no more than 15% key employees</td></tr><tr><td>Group term tax-free coverage</td><td>First $50,000</td></tr><tr><td>Probationary period</td><td>1 to 6 months</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Annuities</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fixed vs variable</td><td>General account, guaranteed vs separate account, owner risk</td></tr><tr><td>Variable annuity license</td><td>Insurance license + Series 6 or 7</td></tr><tr><td>Period certain vs refund</td><td>Period certain = guaranteed time. Refund = guaranteed money</td></tr><tr><td>Straight life payout</td><td>Highest payment, no refund, stops at death</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawal tax order</td><td>LIFO (earnings first)</td></tr><tr><td>10% penalty</td><td>Earnings before age 59½ (plus possible surrender charge)</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion ratio</td><td>Investment ÷ expected return</td></tr><tr><td>Beneficiary election (death during accumulation)</td><td>Within 60 days</td></tr><tr><td>1035 exchange</td><td>Life or endowment to annuity: yes. Annuity to life: NO</td></tr><tr><td>Corporate annuity with non-natural annuitant</td><td>Interest taxed yearly</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Retirement and savings (2026 figures)</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>401(k) employee limit</td><td>$24,500 (2026)</td></tr><tr><td>IRA limit</td><td>$7,500 (2026)</td></tr><tr><td>HSA limit</td><td>$4,400 self-only / $8,750 family (2026); catch-up $1,000 at 55+</td></tr><tr><td>RMD age</td><td>73 (born 1951 to 1959); 75 (born 1960 or later)</td></tr><tr><td>RMD penalty</td><td>25% (10% if corrected quickly)</td></tr><tr><td>Roth qualified withdrawal</td><td>5-year rule plus 59½, death, disability, or first home ($10,000)</td></tr><tr><td>Direct rollover</td><td>No withholding. Indirect = 20% withheld; replace within 60 days</td></tr><tr><td>SIMPLE plan</td><td>100 or fewer employees; immediate 100% vesting</td></tr><tr><td>403(b)</td><td>501(c)(3) nonprofits, public schools, some ministers</td></tr><tr><td>ERISA exemptions</td><td>Church and governmental plans (collectively bargained plans are generally NOT exempt)</td></tr><tr><td>Top-heavy plan</td><td>More than 60% of assets to key employees</td></tr><tr><td>Inherited IRA (non-spouse)</td><td>Generally 10 years to empty</td></tr><tr><td>Coverdell</td><td>$2,000 per child a year; child under 18</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Social Security (2026 figures)</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fully insured</td><td>40 quarters (max 4 per year)</td></tr><tr><td>Currently insured</td><td>6 of last 13 quarters (survivor benefits only)</td></tr><tr><td>Full retirement age</td><td>65 (before 1938); gradual 1938 to 1959; 67 (1960 or later)</td></tr><tr><td>Early retirement at 62</td><td>Permanent reduction (about 70% of PIA when FRA is 67)</td></tr><tr><td>Delayed credits</td><td>Up to age 70</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings test (under FRA)</td><td>$24,480 a year ($1 for $2)</td></tr><tr><td>Earnings test (year you reach FRA)</td><td>$65,160 a year ($1 for $3, months before FRA only)</td></tr><tr><td>Lump-sum death benefit</td><td>$255</td></tr><tr><td>Widow(er) early benefits</td><td>Age 60 (50 if disabled)</td></tr><tr><td>Child survivor age limit</td><td>18 (19 if in high school); disabled child before 22</td></tr><tr><td>Blackout period</td><td>Youngest child 16 until spouse 60</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI waiting period</td><td>5 months (waived for ALS)</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI disability test</td><td>Total: cannot do any SGA; 12+ months or death. SGA: $1,690 (non-blind)</td></tr><tr><td>SSDI work requirement</td><td>20 of last 40 quarters</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare after SSDI</td><td>24 months (except ALS and ESRD)</td></tr><tr><td>Social Security taxable max</td><td>$184,500 (2026)</td></tr><tr><td>Benefits taxable</td><td>Single over $25,000; joint over $32,000</td></tr><tr><td>FICA</td><td>7.65% employee, 7.65% employer, 15.3% self-employed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Medicare and Medicaid</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medicare enrollment window (initial)</td><td>7 months (3 before, birth month, 3 after)</td></tr><tr><td>General enrollment</td><td>January 1 to March 31; coverage starts July 1</td></tr><tr><td>Part A</td><td>Usually free; 60 days per hospital benefit period; SNF days 1 to 20 full, 21 to 100 coinsurance</td></tr><tr><td>Part B</td><td>Optional; 20% coinsurance; late penalty 10% per 12-month delay</td></tr><tr><td>Part C (Medicare Advantage)</td><td>Private; covers A and B except hospice; must have A and B</td></tr><tr><td>Part D</td><td>Private; late penalty 1% per month; out-of-pocket cap; donut hole gone 2025</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare primary (age-based)</td><td>Employer with fewer than 20 employees</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare secondary (age-based)</td><td>Employer with 20+ employees</td></tr><tr><td>Medicare primary (disability-based)</td><td>Group plan with fewer than 100 employees</td></tr><tr><td>ESRD and ALS</td><td>Immediate Medicare eligibility</td></tr><tr><td>Medicaid</td><td>Joint federal-state; low income; year-round; primary payer of nursing home care</td></tr><tr><td>Medigap open enrollment</td><td>6 months from Part B at 65+ (guaranteed issue)</td></tr><tr><td>Medigap letters</td><td>A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, N. Plans C and F closed to new Medicare beneficiaries after 2020</td></tr><tr><td>Medigap free look and renewal</td><td>30-day free look; guaranteed renewable (only nonpayment allows cancellation)</td></tr><tr><td>Medigap Medicaid suspension</td><td>Up to 2 years; request within 90 days</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Health insurance and COBRA</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Renewability (least to most favorable to insured)</td><td>Cancellable, optionally renewable, conditionally renewable, guaranteed renewable, noncancellable</td></tr><tr><td>COBRA: employee terminated</td><td>18 months (29 if disabled)</td></tr><tr><td>COBRA: family events (death, divorce, child aging out, Medicare)</td><td>36 months</td></tr><tr><td>COBRA election</td><td>60 days; up to 102% of premium</td></tr><tr><td>COBRA employer size</td><td>20 or more employees</td></tr><tr><td>ACA essential benefits</td><td>10 categories; no lifetime or annual dollar limits</td></tr><tr><td>Dependent coverage (ACA)</td><td>To age 26</td></tr><tr><td>Metal tiers</td><td>Bronze 60/40, silver 70/30, gold 80/20, platinum 90/10</td></tr><tr><td>HMO</td><td>Prepaid; PCP gatekeeper; referrals; no out-of-network except emergency</td></tr><tr><td>PPO</td><td>Fee-for-service; no referrals; out-of-network covered at reduced benefit</td></tr><tr><td>POS</td><td>Hybrid; PCP and referrals; out-of-network at higher cost</td></tr><tr><td>EPO</td><td>Hybrid; no PCP; no out-of-network except emergency</td></tr><tr><td>Medical expense deduction threshold</td><td>Expenses above a percentage of adjusted gross income set by the IRS</td></tr><tr><td>Health claims math</td><td>Deductible first, then coinsurance on the remainder</td></tr><tr><td>Non-prescription drugs covered</td><td>Only injectable insulin</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Disability and long-term care</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Own occupation vs any occupation</td><td>Own-occ = your own job (costs more)</td></tr><tr><td>Probationary period</td><td>After issue; sickness only; one time</td></tr><tr><td>Elimination period</td><td>After disability begins; each claim; longer = lower premium</td></tr><tr><td>Benefit period</td><td>Short-term 6 months to 2 years; long-term more than 2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Presumptive total disability</td><td>Blindness, deafness, loss of speech, loss of 2+ limbs</td></tr><tr><td>Loss of earning test</td><td>Income must drop 20%+</td></tr><tr><td>LTC trigger</td><td>2 of 6 ADLs for 90 days, or severe cognitive impairment</td></tr><tr><td>Six ADLs</td><td>Bathing, dressing, toileting, transferring, continence, eating</td></tr><tr><td>LTC elimination period</td><td>0 to 365 days</td></tr><tr><td>Calendar days vs service days</td><td>Calendar = every day counts (faster). Service = only care days (slower)</td></tr><tr><td>LTC free look</td><td>30 days</td></tr><tr><td>LTC partnership</td><td>Dollar-for-dollar asset protection</td></tr><tr><td>Business overhead expense</td><td>Pays business costs; does NOT pay owner salary</td></tr><tr><td>Key person disability</td><td>Elimination 30 to 90 days; benefit 1 to 2 years</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>State insurance law and policy provisions (health)</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Number or answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Grace period</td><td>7 days (weekly), 10 days (monthly), 31 days (quarterly or longer) in the NAIC model. Louisiana life: 30 days or one month at the insurer's option (R.S. 22:931(A)(1)); Louisiana health: 30 days from the due date (R.S. 22:977)</td></tr><tr><td>Notice of claim</td><td>20 days</td></tr><tr><td>Claim forms</td><td>Insurer provides within 15 days</td></tr><tr><td>Proof of loss</td><td>90 days (1 year if incapacitated)</td></tr><tr><td>Wait before suing</td><td>60 days after proof of loss</td></tr><tr><td>Time to sue</td><td>Within 3 years (some states 5)</td></tr><tr><td>Reinstatement</td><td>Louisiana life: within 3 years of default, with evidence of insurability and payment (R.S. 22:931(A)(9)). Louisiana health: accepting the defaulted premium reinstates; sickness covered again after 10 days (R.S. 22:975(A)(2))</td></tr><tr><td>Policy cancellation notice</td><td>45 days (10 days for nonpayment)</td></tr><tr><td>Policy form approval</td><td>Auto-approved if not rejected within 90 days</td></tr><tr><td>Minor beneficiary payment cap</td><td>$1,000 (to a relative)</td></tr><tr><td>Change of occupation</td><td>More hazardous = benefit reduced; less hazardous = premium reduced</td></tr><tr><td>Misstatement of age</td><td>Older = benefit reduced; younger = benefit increased</td></tr><tr><td>Free look (health standard)</td><td>10 days; senior policies 30 days</td></tr><tr><td>Louisiana producer exam pass mark</td><td>70% (R.S. 22:1545(A)); PSI: Series 101 and 102 are 100 questions each in 120 minutes; Series 103 is 150 questions in 160 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Louisiana continuing education</td><td>24 hours every 2 years, including at least 3 ethics hours (R.S. 22:1573(C); R.S. 22:1547(C)). From July 2027 renewals, 2 of the 24 hours must cover legislative updates</td></tr><tr><td>Louisiana annuity training</td><td>One-time 4-hour annuity training course (LDI). Regulation 89 best-interest standard, amended effective Sept 20, 2024</td></tr><tr><td>Louisiana guaranty association (per life)</td><td>Death benefits $300,000; cash surrender and withdrawal values $100,000; health benefits $500,000; annuity present value $250,000; aggregate $500,000 for one individual (R.S. 22:2083(C)(2))</td></tr><tr><td>Louisiana minors</td><td>Age of majority 18 (Civil Code art. 29). A minor 15 or older may contract for life or health insurance (R.S. 22:852(B))</td></tr><tr><td>Louisiana unfair practices</td><td>R.S. 22:1964(14) lists unfair claim practices, such as not responding promptly, denying without reasonable investigation, and not providing claim forms within 15 days. R.S. 22:1973 was repealed effective July 1, 2024; the insurer's duty of good faith and fair dealing is in R.S. 22:1892(I)</td></tr><tr><td>Federal anti-crime</td><td>18 U.S.C. 1033: 10 years (15 if conservation); 1034: civil penalty up to $50,000 or compensation received</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","text":"Contracts and underwriting Life insurance and policy values Group life and tax thresholds Annuities Retirement and savings (2026 figures) Social Security (2026 figures) Medicare and Medicaid Health insurance and COBRA Disability and long-term care State insurance law and policy provisions (health)"}];
window.RECALL = [{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"What are the two ways insurance can transfer risk?","a":"Transfer of risk through a legal contract, OR pooling/collecting funds."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"Who owns a mutual company, and who gets its dividends?","a":"Policyholders own a mutual. They receive dividends from divisible surplus."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"A company is headquartered in Ohio and is authorized in Ohio. Is it domestic, foreign, or alien in Ohio?","a":"Domestic (its home state is Ohio, where it is authorized)."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"A company in Germany sells policies in Texas. What is it?","a":"An alien insurer (principal office outside the U.S.)."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"An insurer wants to give part of a large risk to another insurer. What is the first company called?","a":"The ceding company."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"Who does a public adjuster represent?","a":"The insured, not the insurer."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"In a dispute, whom does the agent represent?","a":"The insurer."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"What are the three types of agent authority?","a":"Express, implied, apparent."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"What is the NAIC's four-part mission?","a":"(1) Uniformity, (2) efficiency, (3) consumer protection, (4) preserving state regulation."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"Can a producer use state guaranty association protection in a sales presentation?","a":"No."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"Scenario A: Maria buys a policy from Carl. Carl works only for one company and is paid by that company. Carl is a ______ agent. Maria, meanwhile, went to a different office and asked for quotes from several insurers. That person is a ______.","a":"Scenario A: Captive (career) agent for Carl; Maria's shopping is done through an independent agent or broker."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"Scenario B: Ray's insurer tells him his claim is denied. Ray hires someone to fight the denial and the person is paid by Ray. Who is this person working for?","a":"Scenario B: Ray hired a public adjuster, who works for the insured."},{"unit":"ch01","unitTitle":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","q":"Scenario C: A company says it is \"authorized\" in Florida but its headquarters is in Canada. What is the Florida classification?","a":"Scenario C: Alien insurer (principal office outside the U.S.)."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"What is the difference between a peril and a hazard?","a":"A peril is the event that causes loss (fire). A hazard is a condition that increases the chance the peril will happen (oily rags next to a stove)."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Which insurable-risk element means the insured cannot control the event?","a":"Chance of loss (the event happens by chance and is outside the insured's control)."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Why can an insurer predict total losses for 10,000 policies but not for one person?","a":"The law of large numbers: large groups become predictable, individuals do not."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"A driver texts while driving. Is this a moral or morale hazard?","a":"Morale hazard (careless, not dishonest)."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"A homeowner exaggerates a storm loss to get more money. Moral or morale?","a":"Moral hazard (intentional fraud)."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Which kind of risk can a casino gain from?","a":"Speculative risk (gain or loss)."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Under a named-perils policy, a fire is not listed. Is the loss covered?","a":"No. Named perils cover only listed perils."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Name the four steps of the risk management process.","a":"Identify, choose a method, implement, review and adjust."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Scenario A: Jim has a history of heart disease and applies for life insurance with a plan to leave out his doctor visits. Which concept is the insurer most worried about? (Hint: think adverse selection and moral hazard.)","a":"Scenario A: Adverse selection (high-risk applicant who hides information) and moral hazard (intentional concealment)."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Scenario B: Ann leaves her car door open with keys inside because she has full coverage. Which hazard is this?","a":"Scenario B: Morale hazard (careless because insurance is in place)."},{"unit":"ch02","unitTitle":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","q":"Scenario C: A business owner installs sprinklers and fire doors. Which risk method is this?","a":"Scenario C: Risk reduction (loss control)."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"Name the four essential elements of a valid contract.","a":"Offer and acceptance, consideration, legal purpose, competent parties."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"Is an application with no premium an offer?","a":"No. It is an invitation to make an offer."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"What happens when an applicant makes a counter-offer?","a":"The original offer is voided. There is no contract unless the applicant accepts the counter-offer."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"Which kinds of contracts are valued?","a":"Life insurance (and fixed-benefit products)."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"A homeowner's neighbor buys a property policy on the homeowner's house. Why is that invalid?","a":"The homeowner's neighbor has no insurable interest in the house, so it is a wagering contract."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"At what points must insurable interest exist for a life insurance policy?","a":"Life insurance: at the time of application (not at claim)."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"What is the difference between a warranty and a representation?","a":"A warranty must be true in all respects and is binding. A representation is believed true to the best of knowledge and is not binding."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"Name the three elements of estoppel.","a":"False representation, reliance, harm."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"Who represents the insured in a sale: agent or broker?","a":"The broker."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"What is the definition of subrogation?","a":"The insurer's right to recover what it paid from the third party who caused the loss."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"Scenario A: Donna's application says she is a non-smoker. She smokes but says it was \"to the best of my knowledge\" that she does not. Is this a warranty or a representation? If it is material and false, what can the insurer do?","a":"Scenario A: It is a representation (believed true, to the best of knowledge). If it is material and false, the insurer can rescind or void the contract, subject to the contestability period."},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"Scenario B: An insurer's agent tells Tom that his pre-existing condition will be covered. Tom relies on this and cancels his other coverage. The insurer then denies the claim. What doctrine helps Tom?","a":"Scenario B: Estoppel. The insurer is stopped from denying the claim because Tom relied on the agent's promise to his harm. (Also a waiver of the pre-existing exclusion is possible.)"},{"unit":"ch03","unitTitle":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","q":"Scenario C: A person wants a policy on a stranger's life and names himself as beneficiary, with no connection to the stranger. What is missing?","a":"Scenario C: Insurable interest. Without it, the policy is a wagering contract, and the scheme resembles STOLI."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"What are the four risk classifications, in order from best to worst?","a":"Preferred, standard, substandard, declined."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"Which premium receipt guarantees coverage until the insurer formally rejects the application?","a":"The binding receipt (unconditional receipt)."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"How much insurable interest can a creditor have in a debtor's life?","a":"Up to the debt plus accrued interest, and no more."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"Is an application statement a representation or a warranty?","a":"A representation."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"Who must sign the application, and what happens if the producer does not sign?","a":"The producer must sign (required). The proposed insured and policyowner also sign when applicable. Without the producer's signature the application will not be considered."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"What is the free-look period for an individual life policy in Louisiana, and when does it start?","a":"10 days. It starts from receipt of the policy."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"Which federal law requires notice when an inspection report is requested?","a":"The Fair Credit Reporting Act (1970)."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"Name three information sources an underwriter may use.","a":"Any three of: application, medical report, APS, MIB, inspection report, credit report, special questionnaire, mortality table."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"What is backdating, and what is its typical limit?","a":"Backdating sets an earlier effective date to lower the applicant's age and premium. Typical limit: 6 months, as limited by state regulation."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"Can an agent issue a policy?","a":"No. Only the home office underwriter (insurer) issues policies."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"Scenario A: Ellen pays the first premium with her application. The receipt says \"conditional.\" She dies three weeks later, before the underwriter decides. Is she covered? (Hint: think about the condition.)","a":"Scenario A: Ellen is probably covered as of the application or exam date, if she is found insurable. This is an insurability-type conditional receipt. If the underwriter later finds her uninsurable, there is no coverage and the premium is refunded. Review the actual receipt wording."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"Scenario B: Sam, a business owner, wants to insure his key employee. Does his company have insurable interest? What kind of coverage is this?","a":"Scenario B: Yes. A business has insurable interest in key employees. This is key person insurance."},{"unit":"ch04","unitTitle":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","q":"Scenario C: Grace has a heart condition and wants a policy. The underwriter offers coverage at a higher premium. What class is Grace in?","a":"Scenario C: Substandard (impaired) risk, with a rated policy."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"What are the three primary categories of life insurance?","a":"Ordinary, industrial, and group."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"Does term insurance accrue cash value?","a":"No. Term has no cash value."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"What are the three types of level, decreasing, and increasing term? Which one is used for a mortgage?","a":"Level (stays the same), decreasing (falls to $0), increasing (grows). Decreasing term is used for mortgage protection."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"What does a re-entry (revertible) term policy require at renewal?","a":"Evidence of insurability (the insured may need to prove insurability to keep the lowest premium)."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"What is the difference between the attained-age and original-age conversion methods?","a":"Attained-age: premium based on age at conversion (higher, no extra payment). Original-age: premium based on issue age (lower, may need an extra payment)."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"In a family income policy, when does the income start?","a":"The policy start date (not the death date)."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"Who receives the death benefit on a second-to-die policy, and when?","a":"The beneficiary receives the death benefit when the LAST surviving insured dies."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"What is the difference between Option A and Option B in universal life?","a":"Option A: total death benefit stays level; pure insurance shrinks as cash value grows. Option B: pure insurance stays level; total death benefit grows with cash value."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"What is the 7-pay test?","a":"A test of whether premiums in the first 7 years exceed the amount needed to fully pay up the policy in 7 level payments. Failing it makes the policy a MEC."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"Are all single premium whole life policies MECs?","a":"Yes."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"Scenario A: Ben wants the lowest possible premium for 20 years of protection while his kids are young, and he does not need cash value. Which product fits?","a":"Scenario A: Level term (20-year term), since no cash value is wanted."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"Scenario B: Carla is 40 and buys a 20-year term policy with a conversion option. At age 50, she wants permanent coverage. Her health has declined. Can she convert, and which conversion method is more expensive?","a":"Scenario B: Yes, she can convert without evidence of insurability. The attained-age method is more expensive (her current age)."},{"unit":"ch05_1","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","q":"Scenario C: A creditor tells a borrower, \"You must buy credit life insurance from our affiliated agency.\" What is the problem?","a":"Scenario C: The creditor cannot require the borrower to buy from a specific source. That is coercion and illegal."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"How long is the incontestable period, and what are its main exceptions?","a":"Two years. Exceptions: impersonation or identity fraud, no insurable interest at application, intent to murder the insured, age or sex misstatement, and nonpayment of premiums."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"What happens to premiums if the insured dies by suicide in month 18?","a":"Premiums are refunded (no interest). No death benefit, because the suicide exclusion applies in the first 2 years."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"Which nonforfeiture option gives the highest face amount?","a":"Extended term insurance."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"What is the difference between a waiver of premium rider and a disability income benefit rider?","a":"Waiver of premium waives the premiums and gives no income. Disability income pays a monthly income."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"How is an accidental death benefit different from double indemnity?","a":"ADB is a general term for the accidental-death rider. Double indemnity specifically pays 2x the face amount."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"Are dividends taxable? Is the interest on accumulated dividends taxable?","a":"Dividends are NOT taxable (return of overpaid premium). Interest on accumulated dividends IS taxable."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"Can a policy loan be repaid? What happens if the loan plus interest exceeds the cash value?","a":"Yes. Repayment is not required, but unpaid loans reduce the death benefit. If loan plus interest exceeds cash value, the policy lapses."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"In a settlement option, who pays interest on retained proceeds, and is it taxable?","a":"The insurer pays interest on the proceeds it holds under an installment settlement. That interest is taxable as ordinary income."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"What is the difference between a status and a results war clause?","a":"Status: no coverage while in military service. Results: no coverage only if death results from military activity."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"Which type of assignment is temporary?","a":"A collateral assignment."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"Scenario A: Tom's policy lapses after he stops paying. He has cash value and wants to keep the original face amount for as long as possible, without more premiums. Which nonforfeiture option? (Hint: \"same amount, limited time.\")","a":"Scenario A: Extended term insurance (same face amount, limited period)."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"Scenario B: Lisa's husband is injured in a car accident and dies 120 days later. The policy has an ADB rider. Does the rider pay double?","a":"Scenario B: No. The ADB requires death within 90 days of the accident. Death at 120 days is outside the window, so the rider does not pay double. The ordinary death benefit is still paid."},{"unit":"ch05_2","unitTitle":"Chapter 5.2 - Life Insurance Policy Provisions, Riders, and Exclusions","q":"Scenario C: Kevin's life insurance policy names his daughter as beneficiary. He wants to be able to change the beneficiary later without asking her. Is the designation revocable or irrevocable?","a":"Scenario C: Revocable. The owner can change the beneficiary at any time without consent."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"Who owns the master group policy, and who holds the certificate?","a":"The employer (contract holder) owns the master policy. The employee holds the certificate."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"What minimum participation applies to a contributory plan?","a":"75% of eligible employees."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"How long is the enrollment period for group life?","a":"31 days."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"How long is the probationary period for new employees?","a":"1 to 6 months."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"How long is the conversion period after employment ends?","a":"31 days after termination."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"Does conversion require proof of insurability?","a":"No."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"What is the largest tax-free amount of group term coverage per employee?","a":"$50,000. Coverage above that may be taxable."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"What must be true about the eligible-employee group for the plan to get favorable tax treatment?","a":"At least 70% of all employees covered, and no more than 15% key employees (at least 85% of participants must NOT be key employees)."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"What is the difference between blanket life and franchise life?","a":"Blanket life covers a group exposed to a common hazard with no names and no certificates. Franchise life issues an individual policy to each participant."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"Who receives the proceeds from a credit life policy?","a":"The creditor (the bank), up to the loan balance."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"Scenario A: Dana leaves her job in March. She has group life coverage and wants a permanent policy. The coverage was group term. What is her deadline, and does she need a medical exam?","a":"Scenario A: Dana has 31 days from termination to convert. No medical exam is required. She will convert to an individual whole life policy (most insurers limit conversion to whole life)."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"Scenario B: A company offers a noncontributory group plan. Only 80% of eligible employees sign up. Is this acceptable? Explain.","a":"Scenario B: Not acceptable for a noncontributory plan. Noncontributory plans require 100% participation."},{"unit":"ch06","unitTitle":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","q":"Scenario C: A bank loans money to Jordan for a car and requires a policy that pays off the loan if Jordan dies. Which type of group coverage is this?","a":"Scenario C: Credit life insurance (decreasing term, paid to the creditor)."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"Which premium factor is most significant?","a":"Mortality."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"Does a higher interest rate raise or lower the premium?","a":"Lower. Higher interest = lower premium."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"What is the cheapest premium mode?","a":"Annual."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"What is the difference between net premium and gross premium?","a":"Net premium covers mortality and interest. Gross premium is net premium plus expenses (what the owner pays)."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"Which NAIC index assumes the policy is surrendered?","a":"The surrender cost index."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"Who is the viator in a viatical settlement?","a":"The policyholder who sells the policy because of chronic or terminal illness."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"How much of its face value can a policy usually pay as an accelerated benefit?","a":"Usually up to 50%."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"Who chooses the settlement option if the owner made no choice?","a":"The beneficiary."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"Under which settlement option does the beneficiary receive payments for life?","a":"Life income (single or joint and survivor, depending on the sub-option)."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"If a beneficiary dies and there are two surviving children and two grandchildren from the deceased, how does per stirpes divide the share?","a":"Per stirpes: each surviving child gets one-third (33.3%). The deceased child's two children split their parent's one-third (16.65% each)."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"Scenario A: Helen is 35 and wants a policy she can surrender in 15 years for cash. Which cost index helps her compare policies?","a":"Scenario A: The surrender cost index (planning to surrender in 10 to 20 years)."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"Scenario B: Paul's policy has an irrevocable beneficiary. He wants to switch the beneficiary to his new spouse. What is required?","a":"Scenario B: The irrevocable beneficiary must consent to the change."},{"unit":"ch07","unitTitle":"Chapter 7 - Life Insurance Premiums, Proceeds, and Settlement Options","q":"Scenario C: Mark is terminally ill, with an expected life of 18 months. He sells his policy to an investor. Is this a viatical or life settlement?","a":"Scenario C: A viatical settlement, because Mark is terminally ill (expected death within 24 months)."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"What is the most effective method for determining the amount of life insurance?","a":"The Needs Approach."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"What is the human life value formula?","a":"(Annual earned income − expenses) × years until retirement."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"In the needs approach, what do you subtract from total capital needs?","a":"Liquid assets (savings, pensions, existing life proceeds, Social Security, income-producing assets)."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"Does a key person policy cover the business owner or the employee?","a":"The employee (the insured). The business is the owner and beneficiary."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"In a three-partner cross-purchase plan, how many policies are needed?","a":"Six policies. Each of the three partners buys policies on the other two (3 × 2 = 6)."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"In an entity plan with four partners, how many policies are needed?","a":"Four policies, one on each partner, owned by the partnership."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"What is the main purpose of a Section 303 redemption plan?","a":"To let the corporation redeem stock to pay estate taxes, so the business is not forced to sell assets."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"Who owns a key person policy?","a":"The business (third-party ownership)."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"What is the difference between an executive bonus plan and split-dollar insurance?","a":"Split-dollar is a method of sharing the cost of a policy. An executive bonus plan is a non-qualified bonus that the employee uses to pay premiums on a policy the employee owns."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"Are variable annuities sold with only an insurance license?","a":"No. They require a FINRA license and a state insurance license."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"Scenario A: Ana is the sole proprietor of a bakery. She wants the business to continue after her death. Her employee is named as buyer. Is a buy-sell plan the right tool? What happens to the business when a sole proprietor dies?","a":"Scenario A: Yes, a buy-sell can work, but a sole proprietorship usually ends when the owner dies. The plan would have an employee buy the business from the estate. Ana should confirm with an attorney."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"Scenario B: Three equal partners use a cross-purchase plan. Partner A dies. How do the survivors fund the buyout?","a":"Scenario B: The surviving partners each buy a share of the deceased partner's interest, using the policies they each own on the deceased partner (each survivor owns one policy on A)."},{"unit":"ch08","unitTitle":"Chapter 8 - Determining Life Insurance Needs and Business Uses of Life Insurance","q":"Scenario C: A corporation buys life insurance on its top salesperson to protect against the loss of her sales. What type of insurance is this, and who receives the proceeds?","a":"Scenario C: Key person insurance. The corporation is the owner and the beneficiary."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"What is the key difference between a life insurance policy and an annuity?","a":"Life insurance creates an estate (death benefit). An annuity liquidates an estate (turns savings into income)."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"What are the two phases of an annuity?","a":"Accumulation (pay-in) and annuity (payout)."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"Which account holds a variable annuity's assets?","a":"The separate account."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"Which license does a variable annuity seller need, in addition to the insurance license?","a":"Series 6 or Series 7."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"In what order are withdrawals taxed (LIFO)?","a":"Earnings first (taxable), then principal (tax-free)."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"What does the 10% penalty apply to?","a":"The earnings portion of withdrawals before age 59½."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"What is the exclusion ratio formula?","a":"Total investment ÷ expected return."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"Within what time must a beneficiary decide how to take the death proceeds from an annuity?","a":"Within 60 days."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"What is the difference between \"period certain\" and \"refund\" guarantees?","a":"Period certain guarantees the number of years. Refund guarantees the return of money."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"Can an annuity be exchanged for life insurance tax-free?","a":"No. Annuity-to-life-insurance exchanges are not allowed. Life insurance or an endowment to an annuity is allowed."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"Scenario A: Ruth is 68 and wants a lifetime income with no refund. Which payout option fits, and what is its drawback?","a":"Scenario A: Straight life annuity. Drawback: payments stop at death and there is no refund to beneficiaries."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"Scenario B: Dan, age 45, withdraws $20,000 from a deferred annuity. Half of that is earnings. He is penalized by the surrender charge and the IRS. Explain both.","a":"Scenario B: The surrender charge is the insurer's fee for withdrawing more than the free amount. The IRS 10% penalty applies to the earnings portion of a withdrawal before 59½. Dan may face both."},{"unit":"ch09","unitTitle":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","q":"Scenario C: A corporation buys an annuity. The annuitant is the corporation itself. How is the interest taxed?","a":"Scenario C: The interest is taxed as ordinary income each year, because a non-natural person is the annuitant."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"How many quarters are needed to be fully insured?","a":"40 quarters (about 10 years). Quarters need not be consecutive. Max 4 per year."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"What does a currently insured person receive?","a":"Survivor benefits only."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"What is the difference between AIME and PIA?","a":"AIME is the average indexed monthly earnings used as input. PIA is the full benefit at FRA."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"At what age does a surviving spouse with no children first get benefits?","a":"Age 60."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"What is the lump-sum death benefit?","a":"$255."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"When does the blackout period start?","a":"When the youngest child turns 16 (or immediately if no children)."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"Can a person collect both their own retirement and a survivor benefit?","a":"No. The dual benefit rule allows only the higher benefit."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"What is the SSA's definition of total disability?","a":"Inability to do any substantial gainful activity in the national economy, due to a medically determinable impairment expected to last at least 12 months or to result in death."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"What is the waiting period for SSDI?","a":"5 months. Benefits start in the 6th month."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"Which Medicare part is optional and covers outpatient care?","a":"Part B."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"Scenario A: Priya is 40 and has worked 30 quarters. Is she fully insured? Currently insured?","a":"Scenario A: She has 30 quarters, which is fewer than 40, so she is NOT fully insured. Currently insured status depends on whether she has 6 quarters in the last 13 quarters. If she has recent work, she is currently insured."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"Scenario B: A widow has a child who is 15. She is 45 and not working. Are her benefits paid now? When does the blackout end?","a":"Scenario B: Yes, she receives survivor benefits now, because she is caring for a child under 16. The blackout starts when the child turns 16 (no benefits for her then, although the child's benefits continue to 18). The blackout ends when she reaches age 60."},{"unit":"ch10","unitTitle":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","q":"Scenario C: Tom is 64 in 2026 (FRA 67) and earns $30,000 while receiving benefits. Is he under the earnings test, and what is the limit?","a":"Scenario C: Yes, he is under the earnings test because he is under FRA (67). The 2026 limit is $24,480. Earnings above the limit: $30,000 − $24,480 = $5,520. Withholding: $5,520 ÷ 2 = $2,760 for the year."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"What makes a plan qualified?","a":"It meets IRS requirements and follows ERISA, and gets favorable tax treatment."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"Can a nonqualified plan favor highly paid employees?","a":"Yes. Nonqualified plans can favor highly paid employees (qualified plans cannot)."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"Which plans are for nonprofits and public school employees only?","a":"403(b) plans."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"What is the difference between defined contribution and defined benefit?","a":"Defined contribution sets the contribution and the final benefit depends on investment results (employee bears risk). Defined benefit sets the benefit by formula (employer bears risk)."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"Which ERISA form is filed each year?","a":"Form 5500."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"How long after joining must a participant receive the summary plan description?","a":"Within 90 days of enrollment."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"What is the early withdrawal penalty for an IRA before age 59½?","a":"10%, plus income tax on the taxable portion."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"What must happen for a Roth withdrawal to be tax-free?","a":"The account must be at least 5 years old AND a qualifying event must occur (age 59½, death, disability, or first home purchase up to $10,000)."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"How does a direct rollover differ from an indirect rollover?","a":"A direct rollover moves money trustee to trustee with no withholding. An indirect rollover pays the money to the owner, who has 20% withheld and must replace it within 60 days to avoid tax."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"Which small-business plan has immediate 100% vesting?","a":"SIMPLE plans."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"Scenario A: Lee leaves his job and takes a check payable to himself for his 401(k). He puts the money into an IRA 30 days later. What happened to the 20%, and what should he have done?","a":"Scenario A: The plan withheld 20% for taxes. To keep the full amount tax-deferred, Lee had to replace the withheld 20% from other money within 60 days. He should have asked for a direct rollover (trustee to trustee), which has no withholding."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"Scenario B: A 45-year-old employee of a for-profit company asks whether she can use a 403(b) plan. What is the answer?","a":"Scenario B: No. A 403(b) is only for 501(c)(3) nonprofits, public schools, and some ministers. A for-profit employer uses a 401(k) or similar plan."},{"unit":"ch11","unitTitle":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","q":"Scenario C: A self-employed consultant with no employees wants a plan with high contribution limits and simple administration. Which plan fits best?","a":"Scenario C: A SEP or a Solo 401(k)-type plan. The Keogh is possible but more complex. The SEP is the simplest and has high limits."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Rank the five renewability types from least to most favorable to the insured.","a":"Cancellable (least favorable to insured, lowest cost) → optionally renewable → conditionally renewable → guaranteed renewable → noncancellable (most favorable, highest cost)."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Which renewability type is most common for disability income?","a":"Noncancellable."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Under COBRA, how long does an employee who is terminated (not for gross misconduct) get coverage? How long if disabled?","a":"18 months (29 if disabled)."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"How many days does an employee have to elect COBRA?","a":"60 days."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"What is the maximum a COBRA participant can be charged?","a":"102% of the premium."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Name the ten ACA essential health benefits.","a":"Ambulatory care, emergency, hospitalization, pregnancy and newborn, mental health and substance use, prescription drugs, rehabilitative and habilitative, laboratory, preventive and wellness, pediatric services."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"How long can a child stay on a parent's plan under the ACA?","a":"Until age 26."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"What is the minimum age for the HSA catch-up contribution?","a":"55."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"What is the difference between accidental means and accidental results?","a":"Accidental means: both cause and result must be unintended. Accidental results: only the result must be unintended."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"How many days after an accident must death occur for the AD&D death benefit to apply?","a":"90 days."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Scenario A: Carla's employer, with 35 employees, ends her job. She has a family. How long can she keep group health coverage, and what is the maximum cost?","a":"Scenario A: 18 months of COBRA (the employee's own termination). Maximum cost: 102% of the premium. The employer has 20+ employees, so COBRA applies."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Scenario B: Matt's disability policy cannot be cancelled and its premium cannot change. What renewability type is it?","a":"Scenario B: Noncancellable (noncancelable) disability income."},{"unit":"ch12","unitTitle":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","q":"Scenario C: A surgeon's accident causes the loss of one hand. Her AD&D policy pays a capital sum. Is the payout a percentage of the principal sum?","a":"Scenario C: Yes. The capital sum is a percentage of the principal sum, set by the schedule in the policy."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"Which provider type is prepaid, and which is fee-for-service?","a":"Service providers (BC/BS, HMO) are prepaid. Commercial insurers are reimbursement/fee-for-service."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"What is the role of the PCP in an HMO?","a":"Gatekeeper: coordinates all care and gives referrals."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"Does a PPO require referrals?","a":"No."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"What is capitation?","a":"A fixed payment per member assigned to a physician."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"What is the difference between Medicare Part A and Part B funding?","a":"Part A is funded by payroll taxes. Part B is funded by general revenue plus premiums."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"What is the Medicare waiting period for disabled people who receive SSDI?","a":"24 months of SSDI benefits."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"Which two conditions give immediate Medicare eligibility without the 24-month wait?","a":"ESRD and ALS."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"How many employees make a group plan primary for an age-based Medicare claim?","a":"20 or more employees (group plan is primary; Medicare secondary). With fewer than 20, Medicare is primary."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"What is Medicaid, and who runs it?","a":"A joint federal-state program for low-income people, run by the states."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"What is the difference between a MET and a MEWA?","a":"A MET is a trust for multiple employers in an association or industry. A MEWA is a MET-type arrangement for small employers with a common employment bond, often self-insured."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"Scenario A: Ruth wants to see a specialist without a referral and does not mind paying more. Which HMO or PPO structure fits?","a":"Scenario A: A PPO. It does not require referrals and covers out-of-network care at a reduced benefit."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"Scenario B: Greg is 67, works for a company with 15 employees, and is covered by the company's group plan. Which program pays first?","a":"Scenario B: Greg's employer has fewer than 20 employees, so Medicare is PRIMARY and the group plan is secondary."},{"unit":"ch13","unitTitle":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","q":"Scenario C: A small employer with 30 employees in the same industry as other small employers wants group health coverage. What arrangement could help?","a":"Scenario C: A multiple employer trust (MET) or MEWA, which lets small employers in the same industry pool risk."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"What is the difference between basic and major medical coverage?","a":"Basic: first-dollar, low limits, short period, no coinsurance. Major: after deductible, high limits, coinsurance."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Name the six deductible types.","a":"Flat, corridor, integrated, per-cause, all-cause, family maximum."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"What is the carryover provision?","a":"Expenses from the last 3 months of the year count toward the next year's deductible."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Who receives hospital indemnity benefits, and what are they based on?","a":"The insured, based on days confined (not on expenses)."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"What must a critical illness insured do to receive the lump sum?","a":"Survive a set period after diagnosis (for example, 30 days)."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"What is the insurer's minimum share for a policy to qualify as insurance?","a":"50% (to qualify as insurance)."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"How much does the insurer pay on a $2,100 expense with a $750 deductible and 70/30 coinsurance?","a":"$945 (70% of $1,350)."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Which surgical method uses units and a conversion factor?","a":"Relative value."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Which account is employer-funded only?","a":"HRA."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Is injectable insulin covered when other non-prescription drugs are not?","a":"Yes."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Scenario A: Ben has $5,000 of medical bills. His policy has a $1,000 deductible and 80/20 coinsurance. How much does Ben pay, and how much does the insurer pay?","a":"Scenario A: Deductible $1,000 → remaining $4,000 → insurer 80% = $3,200 → Ben pays $1,000 + 20% × $4,000 = $1,000 + $800 = $1,800. Insurer pays $3,200. (Check: $1,800 + $3,200 = $5,000.)"},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Scenario B: Lisa wants a plan with a separate policy for catastrophic costs that sits on top of her basic plan. Which type is this?","a":"Scenario B: Supplementary major medical (two policies)."},{"unit":"ch14","unitTitle":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","q":"Scenario C: A worker wants a health account she owns, that rolls over, and that works with a high-deductible plan. Which account fits?","a":"Scenario C: An HSA (individual-owned, needs an HDHP, rollover allowed)."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"What is the difference between own-occupation and any-occupation coverage?","a":"Own-occ pays if you cannot do your own occupation. Any-occ pays only if you cannot do any job you are suited for. Own-occ costs more."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"Which conditions are presumptive total disabilities?","a":"Total blindness, total deafness, loss of speech, and loss of two or more limbs."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"What is a typical partial disability benefit, and how long does it usually last?","a":"About 50% of the monthly benefit for 3 to 6 months (after total disability)."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"What is the difference between flat and residual partial benefits?","a":"Flat: a fixed amount (usually 50%) regardless of other income. Residual: the percentage of income lost, multiplied by the total benefit."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"Under accidental means, must both the cause and the result be accidental?","a":"Yes. Accidental MEANS requires both the cause and the result to be accidental."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"When does the probationary period occur, and how often?","a":"After the policy issue date, one time only, and it applies to sickness only."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"How long is a typical elimination period, and how does it affect premium?","a":"Commonly 30 to 90 days. A longer elimination period lowers the premium."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"What is the minimum income drop under the loss of earning test?","a":"20%."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"Does a waiver of premium rider provide an income?","a":"No. Premiums are waived. Income is not paid."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"What is the typical elimination period for a disability buy-sell agreement?","a":"Up to 2 years."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"Scenario A: A dentist loses the use of her hands. She can still teach dental students. Under an own-occupation policy, does she get benefits? Under an any-occupation policy?","a":"Scenario A: Own-occupation: yes, she gets benefits (she cannot do dental work, her own occupation). Any-occupation: no, because she can still teach."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"Scenario B: Paul buys a policy with a 90-day elimination period instead of 30 days. What does this do to his premium?","a":"Scenario B: The longer elimination period (90 days) lowers the premium."},{"unit":"ch15","unitTitle":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","q":"Scenario C: A business owner wants to keep the business running and cover rent and employee salaries during his disability. Which policy fits?","a":"Scenario C: Business overhead expense insurance. It does not pay the owner's salary."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What is the open enrollment period for Medigap, and when does it begin?","a":"Six months, beginning when the person first enrolls in Part B at age 65 or older."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"How many Medigap letters are there, and which is closed to new beneficiaries after 2020?","a":"Ten (A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, N). Plans C and F are closed to people who became Medicare beneficiaries after 2020 (MACRA)."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What are the five minimum standards for Medigap?","a":"(1) Covers both Part A and Part B; (2) benefits adjust with Medicare law changes; (3) preexisting exclusion no more than 6 months; (4) covers the 20% Part B coinsurance; (5) 30-day free look."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What is the Part D late enrollment penalty?","a":"1% per month of delay (if not enrolled during the initial enrollment period)."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"Which Part C plan type is run by health care providers?","a":"PSO (provider-sponsored organization)."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What is the LTC benefit trigger?","a":"Unable to perform at least 2 of 6 ADLs for at least 90 days, or severe cognitive impairment."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What are the six ADLs?","a":"Bathing, dressing, toileting, transferring, continence, eating."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What is the difference between calendar days and service days?","a":"Calendar days count every consecutive day. Service days count only days in which care is received."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What is the maximum preexisting condition exclusion period for LTC?","a":"6 months."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"What does a partnership program protect?","a":"The Medicaid spend-down. It protects assets on a dollar-for-dollar basis equal to the benefits paid."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"Scenario A: Ellen turns 65 and enrolls in Medicare Part B. She applies for Medigap within her first 6 months. Can the insurer refuse her because of a heart condition?","a":"Scenario A: No. Within the 6-month open enrollment period, the insurer must issue the policy (guaranteed issue) and cannot rate her up."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"Scenario B: Frank can no longer bathe or dress himself and needs hands-on help. His policy requires 2 of 6 ADLs for 90 days. Does he qualify if the help lasts 90 days?","a":"Scenario B: Yes. He cannot bathe and dress without help (2 ADLs), for at least 90 days, so he meets the benefit trigger."},{"unit":"ch16","unitTitle":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","q":"Scenario C: Maria wants a policy that starts paying as soon as she receives care three times a week. Should she choose calendar days or service days?","a":"Scenario C: Calendar days. Service days would only count the days she receives care, which slows access to benefits. Calendar days count every day, so benefits start sooner."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"What is the grace period for a monthly premium under the NAIC model law, and what does Louisiana use?","a":"10 days under the NAIC model law. Louisiana uses 30 days (life: 30 days or one month; health: 30 days)."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"How many days after a loss must notice of claim be given?","a":"20 days (or as soon as reasonably possible)."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Within what time must the insurer provide claim forms?","a":"Within 15 days of receiving notice."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"How long is the proof of loss deadline, and what extends it?","a":"90 days from the date of loss. Extended to 1 year if the claimant is legally incapacitated."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"How long must a claimant wait before suing after proof of loss?","a":"At least 60 days."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"What is the standard free-look period, and what is the senior free-look period?","a":"10 days standard. 30 days for senior policies (LTC and Medicare supplement)."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"How long does a sickness coverage delay last after reinstatement?","a":"10 days (accidents are covered immediately)."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Which optional provision reduces the benefit when the insured moves to a more hazardous job?","a":"The change of occupation provision."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Can an agent change the policy?","a":"No. Only an authorized executive officer of the insurer can change the policy."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"What is the ACA dependent coverage age?","a":"Age 26."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Scenario A: Ben's quarterly premium is due March 1. He does not pay. When does the grace period end, and what happens if he dies on March 20?","a":"Scenario A: The quarterly grace period is about a month (31 days under the NAIC model law; Louisiana uses 30 days), so it ends around April 1. Death on March 20 is within the grace period. The death benefit is paid minus the unpaid premium."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Scenario B: Rita has a waiver of premium rider that ends at age 60. She becomes totally and permanently disabled at 61. Does the waiver apply?","a":"Scenario B: No. The rider ended at 60, and the disability began at 61. If the rider had ended at 65, the waiver would apply."},{"unit":"ch17","unitTitle":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","q":"Scenario C: The insurer never responds to Carl's reinstatement application. How does reinstatement work, and when does his sickness coverage start?","a":"Scenario C: If the insurer doesn't respond within 45 days, the policy is automatically reinstated. Sickness coverage starts 10 days after reinstatement."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"What is the primary goal of underwriting?","a":"Protect against adverse selection (evaluate and select risks)."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"How many parts does the application have, and which one must be completed for every application?","a":"Three parts: Part I (general), Part II (medical), Part III (agent's report). Part II must be completed for every application."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"What is the difference between a representation and a warranty?","a":"A representation is true to the best of knowledge and must be accurate only if material. A warranty must be completely true, and breach can void the policy."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"Who can access the MIB, and what can it be used for?","a":"Member insurers only. Use is limited to underwriting and claims processing."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"Which receipt is most common, and when does its coverage start?","a":"Conditional receipt. Coverage starts on the application date or medical exam date (whichever is later), if the applicant is insurable."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"What is the maximum backdating period?","a":"6 months."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"What is the standard free-look period, and what starts it?","a":"10 days (standard). It starts at delivery (the delivery receipt)."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"What is constructive delivery?","a":"Delivery without physical transfer. The insurer gives up all control to someone acting for the owner, such as the agent. Does not occur if the agent is told to hold the policy."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"Who must sign a replacement notice?","a":"Both the agent and the applicant."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"How many main risk classes are there, and which one has the lowest premium?","a":"Four main classes (preferred, standard, substandard, uninsurable/declined). A special class is a variant of substandard. Preferred has the lowest premium."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"Scenario A: Nina applies for health coverage, and her application says she has never been treated for asthma. She was treated 2 years ago and forgot. The insurer finds out after the claim. Is the statement a representation or a warranty? Is it material?","a":"Scenario A: It is a representation (the applicant's statement is judged to the best of knowledge). It is material only if it would have changed the insurer's decision. A knowing false statement could be fraud. It matters only if the omission would have caused denial or a higher rate."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"Scenario B: An agent gives Owen his policy but tells him to hold it until he confirms he is still in good health. Has constructive delivery occurred?","a":"Scenario B: Probably not. The agent was told to withhold delivery until health is confirmed, which does not qualify as constructive delivery."},{"unit":"ch18","unitTitle":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","q":"Scenario C: A 42-year-old with a history of heart bypass surgery applies. The insurer offers coverage at a higher premium. What type of offer is this?","a":"Scenario C: A substandard (counter-) offer: a higher premium for an impaired risk."}];
